const QUESTIONS = [{"id":1,"s":"A 54-year-old woman has persistent MCP and PIP synovitis despite 20 mg weekly oral methotrexate and folate. Adherence is confirmed, renal function is normal and she has troublesome nausea after each dose. Which adjustment is the most appropriate before declaring methotrexate ineffective?","o":["Switch to subcutaneous methotrexate","Use methotrexate daily","Add long-term prednisolone alone","Stop all DMARDs","Switch immediately to cyclophosphamide"],"a":0,"e":"Subcutaneous methotrexate can improve tolerability and exposure; optimise methotrexate before moving to an alternative mechanism, with disease activity reassessed. Before switching mechanism, verify weekly dosing, folate prescription, adherence and disease activity. Splitting the weekly oral dose or using subcutaneous administration can improve gastrointestinal tolerability and bioavailability; monitor blood counts, liver tests and renal function. Persistent synovitis despite an optimised trial warrants treat-to-target escalation. The early arthritis modules emphasise assessing both swollen joints and function when judging response; persistent power-Doppler synovitis can help distinguish inflammation from non-inflammatory pain.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":2,"s":"A 62-year-old man with rheumatoid arthritis develops progressive exertional dyspnoea, bibasal crackles and a restrictive pattern on pulmonary function tests. High-resolution CT demonstrates a probable UIP pattern. Which comorbidity most strongly influences the safety discussion before a new biologic is prescribed?","o":["Primary hyperparathyroidism","Raynaud phenomenon","Interstitial lung disease","Calcium pyrophosphate deposition","Carpal tunnel syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"RA-associated interstitial lung disease requires integrated respiratory-rheumatology assessment and treatment selection based on pulmonary disease. RA-associated ILD should trigger joint rheumatology-respiratory assessment, including baseline and serial FVC and DLCO. UIP morphology can carry a poorer prognosis. Treatment choice must balance articular control against pulmonary disease, infection risk and progression; the presence of ILD is not in itself an automatic ban on every biologic. The course highlights crackles, exertional hypoxaemia and falling diffusion capacity as clues to inflammatory connective-tissue-disease lung involvement; HRCT pattern and serial physiology are complementary.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy","Connective Tissue Disease","Metabolic Bone Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":3,"s":"A 39-year-old woman has active rheumatoid arthritis despite an adequate trial of methotrexate. She has untreated latent tuberculosis documented by a positive IGRA and normal chest imaging. What should occur before initiating a TNF inhibitor?","o":["Assess and treat latent tuberculosis according to local protocol","Vaccinate with live BCG while immunosuppressed","Ignore IGRA if radiograph is normal","Begin biologic immediately and repeat IGRA in six months","Prescribe prophylactic fluconazole"],"a":0,"e":"TNF blockade can reactivate latent tuberculosis; appropriate evaluation and preventive treatment are needed before treatment. A positive IGRA with normal chest imaging can represent latent TB, but active TB must be excluded clinically. Arrange an appropriate preventive regimen and coordinate the biologic start with TB specialists according to local policy. TNF blockade particularly increases the risk of TB reactivation. The infection and immunosuppression sections distinguish a positive screening test from active tuberculosis, which must be excluded before preventive treatment and biologic exposure.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":4,"s":"A man receiving adalimumab for severe psoriatic arthritis develops blurred vision, optic neuritis and new periventricular demyelinating lesions on MRI. What is the most appropriate change to his arthritis treatment?","o":["Continue unchanged","Start infliximab immediately","Increase adalimumab frequency","Add another TNF inhibitor","Stop the TNF inhibitor and seek neurological evaluation"],"a":4,"e":"Suspected demyelinating disease is a major concern with TNF inhibition; choose a non-TNF approach after specialist assessment. New optic neuritis and MRI demyelination while using a TNF inhibitor require urgent neurology review and investigation for demyelinating disease. Do not substitute another drug from the same TNF-inhibitor class without specialist assessment; choose a non-TNF mechanism once the neurological diagnosis is clarified. The immunology module links TNF signalling to host defence and immune regulation; a new neurological syndrome during targeted therapy requires reassessment of drug-related and independent causes.","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":5,"s":"A 33-year-old man has inflammatory back pain, raised CRP, active sacroiliitis on MRI and persistent high disease activity despite adequate trials of two NSAIDs and a structured exercise programme. What is the most appropriate next therapeutic strategy?","o":["Start lifelong high-dose oral prednisolone","Continue antibiotics for six months","Add methotrexate for isolated axial symptoms","Offer intra-articular methotrexate","Consider a biologic or targeted therapy for active axial spondyloarthritis"],"a":4,"e":"Persistent active axial disease after conventional measures may justify targeted treatment; conventional DMARDs lack proven efficacy for isolated axial inflammation. Confirm persistent objectively active axial disease, adequate NSAID trials, physical therapy and alternative explanations for pain. Biologic selection is influenced by psoriasis, inflammatory bowel disease, uveitis, infection screening and local access criteria. Conventional synthetic DMARDs do not reliably improve isolated axial inflammation. The axial spondyloarthritis modules distinguish active inflammatory lesions from chronic structural damage; symptoms, CRP and sacroiliac MRI should be interpreted together. Questions 6-10","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Psoriatic Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":6,"s":"A 41-year-old woman with axial spondyloarthritis has recurrent anterior uveitis and active spinal disease despite NSAID therapy. Which biologic class and formulation is generally preferred when recurrent uveitis is an important comorbidity?","o":["Etanercept specifically","Monoclonal-antibody TNF inhibitor","Rituximab","Abatacept","Anakinra"],"a":1,"e":"Monoclonal TNF inhibitors have more favourable evidence for recurrent uveitis than etanercept in axial spondyloarthritis. When recurrent anterior uveitis coexists with axial disease, monoclonal TNF inhibitors such as adalimumab or infliximab are commonly preferred over etanercept. Review previous uveitis treatment with ophthalmology and screen for TB and viral hepatitis before starting a biologic. An episode of anterior uveitis may present with unilateral pain, photophobia and redness. Ophthalmological confirmation is important because extra-musculoskeletal disease changes biologic selection.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":7,"s":"A 47-year-old man with active psoriatic arthritis has severe chronic plaque psoriasis, dactylitis and several swollen peripheral joints despite methotrexate. Which treatment mechanism directly addresses both prominent skin disease and peripheral arthritis?","o":["Thrombin inhibition","Acetylcholinesterase inhibition","IL-17 pathway inhibition","Osteoclast activation","Dopamine agonism"],"a":2,"e":"IL-17 inhibitors treat both psoriasis and psoriatic arthritis; other biologic choices depend on uveitis and IBD comorbidity. IL-17 inhibition can address both joint and extensive skin psoriasis. An IL-23-pathway agent is another appropriate class for prominent cutaneous disease, and concomitant IBD or uveitis may alter selection. Screen for infection and review dermatology input rather than selecting solely on tender-joint count. The course describes dactylitis as a combined inflammatory process involving digits and peri-tendinous tissues, while enthesitis and nail disease increase the likelihood of psoriatic arthritis.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Psoriatic Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":8,"s":"A 52-year-old man with psoriatic arthritis and active Crohn disease requires escalation beyond conventional treatment. Which option is least suitable because it may worsen his bowel disease?","o":["Gastroenterology-led Crohn therapy","An appropriate IL-23-pathway agent","Monoclonal TNF inhibitor","A bowel-effective biologic","IL-17 inhibitor"],"a":4,"e":"IL-17 inhibition is generally avoided in active inflammatory bowel disease; coordinate joint and bowel treatment. IL-17 blockade can induce or worsen inflammatory bowel disease and is generally avoided in active Crohn disease. Prefer an agent effective for both joints and gut after discussion with gastroenterology; monoclonal TNF inhibitors are a common choice, while the specific IL-23 agent matters because bowel approvals differ. Always ask specifically about bloody diarrhoea, abdominal pain and known bowel inflammation before prescribing an IL-17 inhibitor for spondyloarthritis or psoriatic arthritis.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Psoriatic Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":9,"s":"A 28-year-old man has asymmetric oligoarthritis, conjunctivitis and inflammatory heel pain three weeks after microbiologically confirmed Campylobacter enteritis. Joint aspirate shows inflammatory sterile fluid. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Gonococcal septic arthritis","Osteoarthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis","Calcium pyrophosphate arthritis","Reactive arthritis"],"a":4,"e":"An antecedent gastrointestinal infection followed by sterile peripheral arthritis and enthesitis supports reactive arthritis. Reactive arthritis usually appears days to weeks after gastrointestinal or genitourinary infection and is frequently asymmetric with enthesitis or extra-articular inflammation. Exclude a septic joint when clinically indicated. Initial management centres on NSAIDs and local measures; persistent disease may require a DMARD. The course contrasts reactive arthritis with septic or disseminated gonococcal infection: preceding infection is supportive, but a new hot joint still requires consideration of aspiration.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Spondyloarthritis & AS","Crystal Arthropathy","Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":10,"s":"A 64-year-old woman with an acutely swollen knee has synovial fluid showing positively birefringent rhomboid crystals and a white cell count of 48,000/microlitre. She is febrile and diabetic. What is the most important additional test on the aspirate?","o":["Anti-CCP antibodies","Gram stain and bacterial culture","Rheumatoid factor","Serum urate alone","HLA-B27"],"a":1,"e":"Crystal identification does not exclude concomitant septic arthritis, particularly with systemic features. CPP crystals establish crystal deposition but cannot exclude concomitant bacterial infection. Send Gram stain and culture, including appropriate blood cultures if febrile. A high synovial leukocyte count and immunometabolic risk factors strengthen the need for urgent septic arthritis assessment. On polarised microscopy, CPP crystals are commonly rhomboid and weakly positively birefringent; monosodium urate crystals are needle-like and strongly negatively birefringent. Questions 11-15","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Spondyloarthritis & AS","Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":11,"s":"A 69-year-old patient presents with first acute monoarthritis of the wrist; radiographs show chondrocalcinosis and aspiration reveals weakly positively birefringent crystals. Which underlying metabolic disorder is a recognised association worth assessing in recurrent or atypical CPPD?","o":["Wilson disease","Addison disease","Type 1 diabetes","Hyperparathyroidism","Hypocortisolism"],"a":3,"e":"CPPD may accompany hyperparathyroidism, haemochromatosis, hypomagnesaemia or hypophosphatasia. CPPD associations include hyperparathyroidism, haemochromatosis, hypomagnesaemia and hypothyroidism. Investigations should be proportionate to age, recurrence and atypical distribution. Consider both calcium/phosphate/PTH and iron indices when the presentation suggests a secondary cause. Chondrocalcinosis may also occur with osteoarthritis or asymptomatic CPP deposition. A younger patient or unusual distribution particularly strengthens the case for secondary metabolic testing.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Metabolic Bone Disease"]},{"id":12,"s":"A 45-year-old man with gout and chronic kidney disease has recurrent flares and tophi despite taking 100 mg of allopurinol daily; serum urate is 510 micromol/L. What is the most appropriate long-term strategy?","o":["Aim for serum urate above 500 micromol/L","Stop allopurinol permanently because he has CKD","Treat flares only with antibiotics","Titrate urate-lowering treatment towards a target with monitoring","Change to aspirin monotherapy"],"a":3,"e":"Allopurinol can be started low and carefully titrated in CKD; aim below 360 micromol/L, and consider below 300 with tophi. NICE NG219 recommends treat-to-target urate-lowering therapy with dose adjustment guided by monthly urate testing. Aim below 360 micromol/L, and consider below 300 micromol/L in tophaceous or persistently symptomatic gout. CKD calls for lower starting doses and careful titration, not automatic permanent withdrawal. Hyperuricaemia alone does not diagnose gout, and serum urate can fall during a flare; treatment decisions should account for clinical burden, crystal evidence and long-term urate targets.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy"]},{"id":13,"s":"A 59-year-old man taking allopurinol for tophaceous gout presents with a typical flare. He is afebrile with no concern about infection. What should usually happen to established urate-lowering therapy during the flare?","o":["Replace it with high-dose aspirin","Continue allopurinol while treating the flare","Change to probenecid for one week","Stop allopurinol until all pain resolves","Double the dose immediately without review"],"a":1,"e":"Do not routinely interrupt established urate-lowering therapy during gout attacks; provide appropriate flare treatment. Stopping established allopurinol during a flare risks subsequent urate fluctuation and poorer long-term control. Treat acute inflammation with a suitable NSAID, low-dose colchicine or corticosteroid, adjusting for renal function, comorbidities and interactions. Review urate control after the episode. The crystal arthropathy section stresses that treating inflammation and dissolving urate deposits are distinct goals: continuing background urate lowering does not replace acute-flare therapy.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":14,"s":"A 36-year-old woman is starting urate-lowering treatment for frequent gout attacks. She asks about preventing early flares during titration. Which accompanying approach is appropriate when not contraindicated?","o":["Routine high-dose antibiotic prophylaxis","Avoid all anti-inflammatory agents","Long-term cyclophosphamide","Daily intravenous methylprednisolone","Low-dose colchicine prophylaxis during initiation and titration"],"a":4,"e":"Flare prophylaxis, commonly low-dose colchicine, reduces mobilisation flares when urate-lowering therapy begins. Starting urate-lowering treatment can mobilise deposits and precipitate early flares. NICE NG219 recommends considering colchicine while achieving target urate; if unsuitable, other anti-inflammatory prophylaxis may be considered. Tailor colchicine to kidney function and interacting drugs, and explain its temporary preventive purpose. Counselling should explain that mobilisation of stored urate after initiating treatment can transiently increase flares even while the long-term therapeutic strategy is working.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":15,"s":"A 72-year-old patient has severe acute knee pain, fever, CRP 220 mg/L and turbid aspirate. Gram stain is negative but the cell count is 110,000/microlitre with 94% neutrophils. What is the most appropriate next action?","o":["Wait two weeks for repeat CRP","Label as osteoarthritis","Inject steroid immediately before culture","Urgent management for suspected septic arthritis while cultures are pending","Exclude infection because Gram stain is negative"],"a":3,"e":"A negative Gram stain cannot exclude septic arthritis; urgent microbiological sampling, antibiotics and drainage planning are required. A negative Gram stain does not rule out septic arthritis. Obtain synovial cultures and blood cultures before antibiotics where this does not delay critical treatment; urgent orthopaedic/infectious disease review, antimicrobial therapy and drainage assessment are indicated. Never inject intra-articular steroid into an infected joint. The joint infection module cautions that inflammatory and septic synovial-fluid white-cell counts overlap; neither a threshold nor a negative Gram stain reliably rules out infection. Questions 16-20","c":"Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","t":["Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":16,"s":"A 30-year-old woman has migratory polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis and several painless pustular lesions on the extremities; she has a new sexual partner. Which diagnosis must be actively considered?","o":["Primary osteoarthritis","Fibromyalgia","Haemochromatosis","Disseminated gonococcal infection","Familial Mediterranean fever"],"a":3,"e":"The arthritis-dermatitis syndrome with tenosynovitis is characteristic of disseminated gonococcal infection. Disseminated gonococcal infection may present with migratory polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis and pustular dermatitis even without genitourinary symptoms. Obtain genital and extragenital NAAT samples and blood/synovial cultures as appropriate, and manage promptly with guideline-directed antibiotics and partner notification. Disseminated gonococcal infection can produce a dermatitis-tenosynovitis-polyarthralgia syndrome, while purulent monoarthritis may occur without obvious mucosal symptoms.","c":"Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","t":["Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":17,"s":"A 66-year-old man has recurrent haemarthroses and disproportionate ankle arthropathy. Ferritin and transferrin saturation are elevated; hand radiographs show hook-like osteophytes at the second and third MCP joints. Which diagnosis best explains the arthropathy?","o":["Reactive arthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis","Ochronosis","Primary hyperoxaluria","Hereditary haemochromatosis"],"a":4,"e":"MCP hook osteophytes, iron overload and atypical joint involvement suggest haemochromatosis arthropathy. Haemochromatosis arthropathy commonly involves MCP joints and can mimic inflammatory arthritis or CPPD. Hook-like osteophytes and high transferrin saturation are clues. Genetic testing and liver assessment may be appropriate; venesection treats iron overload but established arthropathy may persist. The course identifies second and third MCP disease with characteristic hook osteophytes as a clue to haemochromatosis arthropathy, often accompanied by CPP crystal deposition.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy"]},{"id":18,"s":"A 43-year-old man reports alternating buttock pain, waking in the second half of the night, and improvement on exercise. Pelvic X-ray is normal but CRP is raised. Which investigation is most useful if axial spondyloarthritis remains suspected?","o":["Routine lumbar CT alone","Hand ultrasonography","Repeat plain pelvis X-ray next day","Bone densitometry","MRI of sacroiliac joints using inflammatory sequences"],"a":4,"e":"MRI can detect active sacroiliitis in non-radiographic axial spondyloarthritis when plain films are unrevealing. MRI sacroiliac joints using inflammation-sensitive sequences helps identify active sacroiliitis when plain films remain normal. Imaging findings must be interpreted alongside clinical pattern and alternative causes of marrow oedema, including mechanical loading and postpartum changes. Inflammatory back pain features improve the pre-test probability but are not diagnostic; MRI bone-marrow oedema may also reflect mechanical stress, so imaging needs clinical correlation.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":19,"s":"A 48-year-old woman with erosive RA develops new gait instability, hand clumsiness and hyperreflexia. Cervical movement provokes paraesthesia. What is the most urgent investigation?","o":["MRI of the cervical spine with urgent spinal assessment","Plain chest radiograph","DEXA only","Elective foot ultrasound","Repeat rheumatoid factor"],"a":0,"e":"Signs of cervical cord compromise in RA require urgent MRI and specialist evaluation. Neurological deficit in RA with suspected cervical instability warrants urgent cervical spine MRI and specialist spinal assessment; immobilisation precautions may be appropriate. Atlantoaxial subluxation and pannus can threaten the cord, and plain flexion-extension radiographs alone cannot assess neural compression. Longstanding erosive disease may damage the transverse ligament and cervical joints; clinical signs of myelopathy take priority over reassuring appearances on earlier plain radiographs.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":20,"s":"A 57-year-old woman with poorly controlled seropositive RA develops neutropenia, recurrent infections and splenomegaly. What is the most likely syndrome?","o":["Behcet syndrome","Schnitzler syndrome","RS3PE","Adult-onset Still disease","Felty syndrome"],"a":4,"e":"The triad of longstanding RA, neutropenia and splenomegaly suggests Felty syndrome; exclude LGL leukaemia and other causes. Felty syndrome classically combines established RA, neutropenia and splenomegaly. Check infections, medication toxicity and other causes of cytopenia; large granular lymphocytic leukaemia is an important mimic requiring haematological evaluation, sometimes with flow cytometry and clonality testing. Neutropenia in RA requires a broad differential including medication toxicity, infection, hypersplenism and clonal lymphoproliferative disease, not just Felty syndrome. Questions 21-25","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Vasculitis"]},{"id":21,"s":"A 62-year-old man with RA develops progressive neutropenia and splenomegaly. Peripheral smear shows increased large granular lymphocytes. Which investigation best distinguishes an alternative haematological cause from Felty syndrome?","o":["Serum urate","Anti-dsDNA antibodies","HLA-B27","Plain hand radiograph alone","Peripheral blood flow cytometry with T-cell clonality testing"],"a":4,"e":"T-cell large granular lymphocytic leukaemia can mimic Felty syndrome and requires haematological immunophenotyping/clonality assessment. T-cell large granular lymphocytic leukaemia can coexist with rheumatoid arthritis and neutropenia. Peripheral blood immunophenotyping and T-cell receptor clonality testing help distinguish it from Felty syndrome; haematology input is appropriate, especially with severe neutropenia or recurrent infections. Large granular lymphocyte disorders may feature chronic neutropenia and autoimmune arthritis; peripheral blood flow cytometry and molecular clonality testing assess this important mimic.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Spondyloarthritis & AS","SLE & Lupus","Crystal Arthropathy"]},{"id":22,"s":"A 37-year-old man develops abrupt, recurrent attacks of painful MCP swelling lasting two days with complete return to normal function between attacks. Anti-CCP is strongly positive. Which is the most likely clinical syndrome?","o":["Palindromic rheumatism","Septic arthritis","Established destructive rheumatoid arthritis","Osteoarthritis","Polymyalgia rheumatica"],"a":0,"e":"Brief self-limited attacks with symptom-free intervals suggest palindromic rheumatism; anti-CCP positivity predicts RA risk. Palindromic rheumatism produces self-limited episodes with return to baseline between attacks. Anti-CCP positivity increases the likelihood of later persistent RA, so follow for sustained synovitis and radiographic progression. Hydroxychloroquine may be considered for frequent attacks after assessment. The episodic, fully reversible nature of palindromic rheumatism distinguishes it from persistent RA synovitis; ACPA positivity modifies future-risk counselling.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Vasculitis","Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":23,"s":"A 51-year-old woman with RA continues to have a high DAS28 and elevated CRP after six months of verified optimal csDMARD treatment. Which principle should direct subsequent treatment selection?","o":["Treat only if erosions appear","Accept persistent moderate activity indefinitely","Treat-to-target escalation to achieve remission or low disease activity","Use analgesics instead of disease modification","Monitor annually only"],"a":2,"e":"Active RA warrants systematic reassessment and escalation towards remission or low disease activity. Treat-to-target requires regular objective disease-activity assessment, timely treatment adjustment and a shared remission or low-activity target. The 2025 EULAR RA update incorporates comorbidity and safety considerations, including JAK-inhibitor risks, when choosing the next mechanism. Treat-to-target assessments should document swollen and tender joint counts plus inflammatory markers and patient assessment, while avoiding escalation based on non-inflammatory pain alone.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":24,"s":"A 44-year-old patient with longstanding seronegative peripheral arthritis has recurrent painful oral ulcers, genital ulcers and posterior uveitis with retinal vasculitis. Which diagnosis is most likely?","o":["Relapsing polychondritis","Behcet syndrome","Primary Sjogren syndrome","Psoriatic arthritis","Sarcoid arthropathy"],"a":1,"e":"Recurrent mucosal ulcers with retinal vasculitis strongly support Behcet syndrome. Behcet syndrome is a clinical diagnosis; there is no single diagnostic blood test. Retinal vasculitis is organ-threatening and requires urgent ophthalmological assessment and systemic immunosuppression tailored to severity. Recurrent oral ulcers alone are insufficient to establish the diagnosis. Behcet disease is a variable-vessel vasculitis; ocular, neurological and vascular disease carry greater organ-threatening implications than isolated mucocutaneous lesions.","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","Vasculitis","Connective Tissue Disease"]},{"id":25,"s":"A 58-year-old woman with severe destructive RA has red eyes with deep, boring ocular pain and scleral tenderness. Vision is reduced. What is the most concerning ocular complication?","o":["Allergic conjunctivitis","Necrotising scleritis","Dry-eye syndrome","Simple episcleritis","Presbyopia"],"a":1,"e":"Severe painful scleritis in RA may threaten vision and signal systemic vasculitis; urgent ophthalmological assessment is needed. Scleritis typically causes severe deep ocular pain and may threaten sight, whereas episcleritis is usually more superficial. Urgent ophthalmology referral is required. Necrotising scleritis can indicate severe systemic RA-associated vasculitis and may require systemic immunosuppression. The eye-symptoms module distinguishes superficial episcleritis from deep scleritis by severity of pain and potential visual risk; scleral inflammation merits urgent specialist assessment. Questions 26-30","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Vasculitis"]},{"id":26,"s":"A 26-year-old woman with SLE develops rising urine protein-creatinine ratio, haematuria with casts and declining complement despite few systemic symptoms. Which investigation most directly classifies renal disease to guide induction treatment?","o":["Skin biopsy","HLA-B27 testing","Plain renal radiograph","Anti-Ro titre alone","Kidney biopsy"],"a":4,"e":"Renal biopsy defines lupus nephritis class and activity/chronicity, guiding treatment decisions after contraindications are assessed. Proteinuria, active urinary sediment and falling complement support possible lupus nephritis, but histology determines class, activity, chronicity and relevant mimics. The ACR 2024 guideline recommends prompt biopsy when lupus nephritis is suspected unless contraindicated; treatment intensity should be pathology-guided. Urinary protein quantification, blood pressure, creatinine, sediment and complement trends inform suspicion, but kidney biopsy supplies the proliferative versus membranous histological distinction.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","SLE & Lupus","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":27,"s":"A woman with active proliferative lupus nephritis is already receiving hydroxychloroquine and glucocorticoids. Which drug is an established induction treatment backbone?","o":["Etanercept","Sulfasalazine","Allopurinol","Colchicine","Mycophenolate mofetil"],"a":4,"e":"Mycophenolate is an established treatment backbone for active class III/IV lupus nephritis. The 2024 ACR guideline conditionally recommends combination (triple) therapy comprising glucocorticoids plus either a mycophenolic-acid analogue and belimumab, a mycophenolic-acid analogue and a calcineurin inhibitor, or low-dose cyclophosphamide and belimumab followed by mycophenolate. Treatment selection depends on the kidney biopsy, proteinuria, renal function, extrarenal activity and patient factors.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":28,"s":"A 24-year-old with SLE taking 400 mg hydroxychloroquine daily weighs 48 kg and has eGFR 40 mL/min/1.73 m2. Which long-term safety concern needs particular attention?","o":["Ototoxicity caused by low-dose folate","Retinal toxicity due to high weight-adjusted dose and renal impairment","Rheumatoid nodules","Permanent neutrophilia","Rapid urate nephropathy"],"a":1,"e":"Hydroxychloroquine target dosing around 5 mg/kg actual weight/day requires individualisation; impaired renal function increases retinal risk. Retinal toxicity risk is associated with daily dose per actual body weight, renal impairment and duration of exposure. At 48 kg, 400 mg daily greatly exceeds the commonly advised 5 mg/kg/day threshold. Reassess dose and ensure risk-adapted ophthalmic monitoring. The lupus modules distinguish the benefits of continuing hydroxychloroquine from dose-dependent retinal toxicity; renal impairment and duration of exposure increase screening importance.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":29,"s":"A 31-year-old with SLE is in partial remission but requires prednisolone 12.5 mg daily for persistent arthritis and rash despite hydroxychloroquine. Which longer-term strategy aligns with contemporary lupus recommendations?","o":["Stop hydroxychloroquine routinely","Add steroid-sparing immunomodulatory therapy and reduce prednisolone","Maintain prednisolone 12.5 mg indefinitely without review","Use antibiotics continuously","Replace with high-dose aspirin"],"a":1,"e":"EULAR and ACR emphasise minimising chronic glucocorticoid exposure, using immunomodulatory agents to enable tapering. Long-term oral glucocorticoids carry cumulative infection, metabolic, cardiovascular and bone risks. The 2025 ACR and 2023 EULAR lupus recommendations support early steroid-sparing escalation when activity persists; choose agents according to manifestations and pregnancy plans, while maintaining hydroxychloroquine when suitable. Persistently requiring moderate-dose glucocorticoids suggests inadequate disease control and cumulative toxicity risk; steroid-sparing treatment should be assessed against objective disease activity.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":30,"s":"A 29-year-old woman has three early miscarriages and persistently positive lupus anticoagulant. She is now pregnant, with no prior thrombosis. Which is the usual antithrombotic strategy for obstetric APS?","o":["Low-dose aspirin plus prophylactic low-molecular-weight heparin","Dual antiplatelet therapy only","No treatment despite confirmed APS","Warfarin throughout pregnancy","Rivaroxaban"],"a":0,"e":"Obstetric APS generally requires aspirin plus prophylactic heparin during pregnancy, with individualised postpartum planning. Pregnancy management for obstetric APS generally combines low-dose aspirin with prophylactic low-molecular-weight heparin, with antenatal surveillance. Women with previous thrombosis usually require therapeutic anticoagulation instead. Confirm laboratory persistence and discuss postpartum thromboprophylaxis. Pregnancy management depends on whether antiphospholipid antibodies are associated with obstetric events, prior thrombosis, or neither; review medication safety and maternal-fetal surveillance.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":31,"s":"A woman with diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis has rapid skin progression and a new blood pressure of 190/110, acute kidney injury and microangiopathic haemolysis after recent high-dose glucocorticoids. What is the urgent treatment priority?","o":["Elective renal biopsy before treatment","Stop all blood pressure medication","Start NSAIDs and observe","High-dose prednisolone escalation","Start an ACE inhibitor promptly with specialist monitoring"],"a":4,"e":"Scleroderma renal crisis is an emergency requiring rapid ACE inhibition; glucocorticoids increase risk. Scleroderma renal crisis is defined by rapidly developing hypertension, AKI and often microangiopathic haemolysis. ACE inhibition is urgent even if creatinine initially rises; monitor potassium, renal function and blood pressure closely and involve nephrology. High-dose glucocorticoids are a recognised risk factor. Abrupt hypertension, acute kidney injury and microangiopathic haemolysis are classic scleroderma renal crisis clues; normal baseline blood pressure does not eliminate risk.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":32,"s":"A 54-year-old woman with systemic sclerosis develops increasing exertional dyspnoea, falling DLCO and basal inspiratory crackles. Which investigation is most appropriate for assessing suspected interstitial lung disease?","o":["Plain wrist radiograph","High-resolution CT chest","Arthroscopy","Bone scintigraphy","Serum urate"],"a":1,"e":"HRCT is central to assessing systemic-sclerosis ILD alongside pulmonary function testing and multidisciplinary review. HRCT characterises the pattern and extent of SSc-associated ILD; PFTs including FVC/DLCO provide complementary baseline and surveillance data. ACR/CHEST suggests monitoring PFTs every 3-6 months during the first year of SSc-ILD, adjusted for stability and severity. Systemic sclerosis lung disease assessment relies on HRCT and serial FVC/DLCO, because symptoms and auscultation alone may underestimate interstitial involvement.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Connective Tissue Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":33,"s":"A 46-year-old patient with limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis has progressive dyspnoea, normal spirometry and disproportionately reduced DLCO. Echocardiography suggests elevated pulmonary arterial pressure. What is required to confirm pulmonary hypertension haemodynamics?","o":["Right-heart catheterisation","Chest radiograph alone","Serum ACE level","Left ankle ultrasound","Repeat ANA only"],"a":0,"e":"Right-heart catheterisation confirms and characterises pulmonary hypertension, differentiating pre- from post-capillary disease. Right-heart catheterisation establishes haemodynamic pulmonary hypertension and discriminates precapillary disease from left-heart or other causes. Echocardiography is a screening assessment, not definitive classification. Systemic sclerosis patients require ongoing cardiopulmonary surveillance. A disproportionate fall in DLCO with relatively preserved lung volumes should raise suspicion of pulmonary vascular disease, while also prompting evaluation for concomitant ILD and other causes.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":34,"s":"A 45-year-old woman has proximal muscle weakness, mechanic's hands, Raynaud phenomenon and ILD; anti-Jo-1 is positive. Which unifying syndrome is most likely?","o":["Muscular dystrophy","Antisynthetase syndrome","Polymyalgia rheumatica","Sarcoidosis","Inclusion-body myositis"],"a":1,"e":"Antisynthetase antibodies with myositis, ILD and characteristic hands support antisynthetase syndrome. Antisynthetase syndrome may feature myositis, ILD, inflammatory arthritis, Raynaud phenomenon, fever and mechanic's hands. ILD can precede muscle disease, so HRCT and full PFT assessment are important. Autoantibody subtype informs phenotype but does not replace organ assessment. Mechanic hands, arthritis, Raynaud phenomenon and ILD together form an antisynthetase-spectrum presentation; myositis may be subtle or clinically absent. Questions 36-40","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Connective Tissue Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":35,"s":"A 58-year-old woman with dermatomyositis is positive for anti-TIF1-gamma and has progressive weight loss. Which additional assessment has particularly high priority?","o":["HLA-B27 typing","Routine urate estimation only","Risk-stratified malignancy screening","Immediate joint replacement","Screening for haemochromatosis alone"],"a":2,"e":"Anti-TIF1-gamma adult dermatomyositis is associated with increased cancer risk; screening should be risk-adapted. Adult dermatomyositis has a malignancy association, particularly with anti-TIF1-gamma. Follow current international risk-stratified cancer screening recommendations, with age-appropriate and enhanced investigations when indicated by high-risk phenotype, symptoms and local protocol. Myositis-specific antibodies help identify clinical phenotypes and malignancy risk, but screening intensity must be tailored to age, symptoms and the full antibody profile.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Crystal Arthropathy","Connective Tissue Disease"]},{"id":36,"s":"A 48-year-old woman with inflammatory myopathy has rapidly progressive ILD, painful palmar papules and minimal muscle weakness. Anti-MDA5 is strongly positive. What is the greatest immediate threat?","o":["Rapidly progressive interstitial lung disease","Carpal tunnel syndrome","Hyperparathyroidism","Secondary osteoarthritis","Asymptomatic hyperuricaemia"],"a":0,"e":"Anti-MDA5 dermatomyositis is associated with potentially fulminant ILD requiring urgent specialised management. Anti-MDA5 disease is strongly linked to rapidly progressive ILD even when overt myositis is minimal. Arrange immediate respiratory/rheumatology multidisciplinary evaluation, oxygenation assessment and HRCT/PFTs as feasible. Treatment often requires rapid combination immunomodulation rather than routine observation. Anti-MDA5-associated dermatomyositis may have little measurable muscle weakness despite severe lung involvement, making respiratory trajectory and oxygen requirement especially important.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Metabolic Bone Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":37,"s":"A 56-year-old woman with sicca symptoms, positive anti-Ro and parotid enlargement develops purpura, low C4 and monoclonal gammopathy. Which serious complication must be excluded?","o":["Osteoid osteoma","B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma","Osteonecrosis of femoral head","Reactive arthritis","Ankylosing spondylitis"],"a":1,"e":"Persistent salivary swelling, purpura, low complement and monoclonality are lymphoma risk features in Sjogren disease. Persistent gland enlargement, palpable purpura, low C4, cryoglobulinaemia or monoclonal gammopathy raise concern for B-cell lymphoma in Sjogren disease. Investigate with imaging and tissue sampling of suspicious lesions, rather than attributing enlarging glands to uncomplicated sicca disease. Sj\u00f6gren disease accompanied by persistent salivary gland enlargement, low complement, purpura or cryoglobulins calls for assessment of systemic activity and lymphoproliferative complications.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Connective Tissue Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":38,"s":"A 71-year-old woman has new temporal headache, scalp tenderness and transient monocular visual loss. ESR is 92 mm/h; temporal artery ultrasound cannot be organised today. Which immediate step is most appropriate?","o":["Begin high-dose glucocorticoids urgently and arrange rapid assessment","Wait for biopsy before any treatment","Repeat ESR in four weeks","Give only paracetamol","Use methotrexate monotherapy"],"a":0,"e":"Visual ischaemic symptoms in suspected GCA require immediate treatment without waiting for imaging or biopsy. Suspected GCA with amaurosis fugax is a time-critical sight-threatening emergency. Start high-dose glucocorticoids immediately and obtain urgent ophthalmology/vascular ultrasound assessment; imaging or biopsy must not delay treatment. Consider intravenous therapy for acute visual threat according to local protocols. Cranial ischaemic symptoms in suspected giant cell arteritis require immediate treatment; ultrasonography and biopsy support diagnosis but must not delay protection of vision. Questions 41-45","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":39,"s":"A 67-year-old patient has suspected giant cell arteritis but temporal artery ultrasound is negative after two weeks of glucocorticoids. She has limb claudication and unequal arm blood pressures. Which investigation best explores large-vessel involvement?","o":["Bone density scan","Repeat rheumatoid factor","Cross-sectional vascular imaging such as PET-CT, CTA or MRA","Heel MRI","Plain cervical X-ray"],"a":2,"e":"A negative temporal ultrasound does not exclude large-vessel GCA; choose vascular imaging based on context and availability. Temporal artery ultrasound loses sensitivity after treatment and can be negative in predominantly extracranial GCA. CTA, MRA or FDG-PET/CT can assess aortic branches and large-vessel inflammation depending on availability and steroid exposure. Clinical probability remains central. A negative temporal artery study does not completely exclude giant cell arteritis, particularly after treatment; consider large-vessel imaging when suspicion persists.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":40,"s":"A 74-year-old patient with classic bilateral shoulder and hip stiffness has persistent CRP elevation, fever, anaemia and a limited response to glucocorticoids. What is the most important clinical action?","o":["Escalate steroids indefinitely without reassessment","Attribute fever to osteoarthritis","Diagnose fibromyalgia automatically","Exclude disease because rheumatoid factor is negative","Re-evaluate for mimics including infection or malignancy"],"a":4,"e":"Atypical symptoms and poor steroid response warrant reassessment rather than automatic PMR dose escalation. Atypical or refractory PMR warrants reassessment for occult infection, malignancy, inflammatory arthritis, myopathy and GCA rather than indefinite glucocorticoid escalation. The 2026 BSR PMR guidance reinforces structured reassessment and steroid-sparing options in selected patients. The polymyalgia rheumatica module flags striking constitutional symptoms, refractory inflammation or atypical patterns as reasons to revisit malignancy, infection and large-vessel vasculitis.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":41,"s":"A 34-year-old man has recurrent sinusitis, otitis, cavitating lung nodules, haematuria and positive PR3-ANCA. Which diagnosis is most likely?","o":["Polyarteritis nodosa","Microscopic polyangiitis","Takayasu arteritis","IgA vasculitis","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis"],"a":4,"e":"Upper airway disease, cavitating pulmonary nodules, renal involvement and PR3-ANCA favour GPA. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis often combines ENT destruction, pulmonary nodules/cavitation and pauci-immune glomerulonephritis. PR3-ANCA supports but does not prove the diagnosis; tissue biopsy should be obtained where safe without delaying organ-saving therapy. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis combines ENT, pulmonary and renal disease; tissue confirmation should be sought where feasible without delaying treatment of rapidly progressive organ damage.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":42,"s":"A 60-year-old patient presents with pulmonary haemorrhage and rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis attributed to ANCA-associated vasculitis. Which induction approach is most appropriate?","o":["Hydroxychloroquine alone","Delayed treatment until all antibodies normalise","Colchicine monotherapy","Intra-articular steroid only","Glucocorticoids with rituximab or cyclophosphamide under urgent specialist care"],"a":4,"e":"Life- or organ-threatening ANCA vasculitis needs urgent remission induction with rituximab or cyclophosphamide plus glucocorticoids. Life- or organ-threatening AAV is treated urgently with glucocorticoids plus rituximab or cyclophosphamide. Steroid-sparing avacopan may be considered in selected GPA/MPA patients; plasma exchange is not routine for every pulmonary-renal presentation and depends on renal severity and clinical circumstances. Pulmonary-renal syndrome needs parallel investigation for ANCA-associated disease, anti-GBM antibodies and mimics; life-threatening haemorrhage and renal decline warrant urgent multidisciplinary management.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":43,"s":"A 52-year-old patient has adult-onset asthma, marked eosinophilia, mononeuritis multiplex, pulmonary infiltrates and sinus disease. Which diagnosis is most likely?","o":["IgG4-related pancreatitis","Polymyalgia rheumatica","Behcet syndrome","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Rheumatoid vasculitis"],"a":3,"e":"Asthma, eosinophilic inflammation and systemic vasculitic neuropathy suggest EGPA. EGPA commonly combines asthma, eosinophilia, sinus inflammation and vasculitic neuropathy. ANCA may be negative. Treatment is determined by severity: organ-threatening vasculitis warrants induction immunosuppression, whereas selected non-organ-threatening disease may benefit from anti-IL-5 approaches. In eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis, asthma and eosinophilia may precede overt vasculitis; peripheral neuropathy and cardiac disease help define severity. Questions 46-50","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis"]},{"id":44,"s":"A 42-year-old man has livedo racemosa, painful testicular swelling, hypertension, abdominal pain and multiple mononeuropathies. Urine sediment is bland and ANCA is negative. Which diagnosis is most likely?","o":["IgA nephropathy","Lupus nephritis","Microscopic polyangiitis","Anti-GBM disease","Polyarteritis nodosa"],"a":4,"e":"Medium-vessel PAN can cause mesenteric, testicular and neurologic ischaemia but typically spares glomeruli and lungs. Polyarteritis nodosa causes medium-vessel ischaemia and neuropathy without typical glomerulonephritis or pulmonary capillaritis. Assess hepatitis B status and look for visceral aneurysms on appropriate angiography. Disease severity and viral association govern treatment. Polyarteritis nodosa predominantly affects medium arteries and typically lacks glomerulonephritis or pulmonary capillaritis; vascular imaging may reveal microaneurysms.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Infection & Immunosuppression"]},{"id":45,"s":"A 25-year-old woman has arm claudication, asymmetrical upper limb pulses and an audible subclavian bruit; PET imaging shows aortic branch inflammation. Which disease is most likely?","o":["Takayasu arteritis","Giant cell arteritis","Kawasaki disease","Thromboangiitis obliterans","Polyarteritis nodosa"],"a":0,"e":"Large-vessel vasculitis in a young woman with pulse deficits is characteristic of Takayasu arteritis. Takayasu arteritis is a large-vessel vasculitis causing pulse discrepancy, limb claudication and bruits, often in younger patients. CTA/MRA or PET can document vessel involvement; exclude mimics and evaluate for stenosis-related hypertension and organ ischaemia. Large-vessel inflammation can cause pulse asymmetry, bruits and limb claudication before fixed stenoses become apparent; PET and angiographic techniques address different aspects of activity and anatomy.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Paediatric Rheumatology","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":46,"s":"A 30-year-old man with recurrent aphthae, genital ulcers and retinal vasculitis develops painful haemoptysis; CTA demonstrates pulmonary artery aneurysms. What is the most important management consideration?","o":["Urgent specialist immunosuppression; avoid uncritical anticoagulation","Observe for a year","Offer arthrocentesis","Treat as isolated oral candidiasis","Routine anticoagulation without vascular review"],"a":0,"e":"Pulmonary arterial aneurysms in Behcet disease are life-threatening; bleeding risk makes anticoagulation decisions complex. Pulmonary artery aneurysm in Behcet disease carries a major haemorrhage risk; anticoagulation can be hazardous and should not be instituted reflexively for presumed pulmonary embolism. Urgent multidisciplinary assessment and powerful immunosuppression are usually central, with intervention for selected vascular lesions. Behcet-associated pulmonary artery aneurysm can cause catastrophic haemorrhage; invasive vascular intervention requires coordination with systemic control of vessel-wall inflammation.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":47,"s":"A 46-year-old woman has steroid-responsive bilateral lacrimal gland enlargement, pancreatic swelling and retroperitoneal fibrosis; serum IgG4 is elevated. Which test best establishes the diagnosis while excluding mimics?","o":["Bone density","Appropriate tissue biopsy showing characteristic histopathology","ANA alone","Joint aspiration","Serum IgG4 level alone"],"a":1,"e":"IgG4-related disease requires clinicopathological assessment; serum IgG4 is neither sufficiently sensitive nor specific. Elevated serum IgG4 is not diagnostic by itself. Biopsy assesses dense lymphoplasmacytic infiltration, storiform fibrosis and obliterative phlebitis where applicable, while excluding lymphoma and malignancy. Organ context and the proportion of IgG4-positive plasma cells matter. IgG4-related disease can affect pancreas, lacrimal glands and retroperitoneum, but a serum IgG4 rise alone is insufficient; tissue pathology and malignancy exclusion are central.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":48,"s":"A 37-year-old woman with lupus has fever, cytopenias, ferritin 18,000 micrograms/L, hypofibrinogenaemia and rising liver enzymes. Which complication is most likely?","o":["Fibromyalgia","Isolated serositis","Uncomplicated iron deficiency","Osteoarthritis","Macrophage activation syndrome / secondary HLH"],"a":4,"e":"Extreme ferritin elevation with cytopenias, coagulopathy and organ dysfunction suggests hyperinflammatory HLH/MAS. Macrophage activation syndrome/secondary HLH causes hyperinflammation with marked ferritin elevation, cytopenias, falling fibrinogen and hepatic injury. It is life-threatening and demands urgent investigation for infection and triggers plus prompt specialist immunomodulatory treatment. Macrophage activation syndrome may be recognised by paradoxically falling ESR as fibrinogen is consumed, together with cytopenias, liver injury and rapidly rising ferritin. Questions 51-55","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain"]},{"id":49,"s":"A 40-year-old woman with systemic sclerosis has painful digital ulcers despite warming measures and a calcium channel blocker. No critical infection is present. Which therapy class can be considered for persistent ischaemic digital disease?","o":["Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor","Systemic beta blocker","Calcitonin","Allopurinol","Thiazide diuretic solely"],"a":0,"e":"PDE5 inhibitors can improve refractory Raynaud symptoms and digital ulcers; severe ischaemia may require intravenous prostanoids. Persistent systemic-sclerosis digital ulcers can benefit from phosphodiesterase-5 inhibition and, for severe refractory disease, intravenous prostanoid therapy. Assess for critical ischaemia, infection and local wound care needs; endothelin-receptor antagonists may reduce new ulcers in selected patients. Scleroderma digital ulcers are associated with structural microvascular disease as well as vasospasm; assess infection, tissue ischaemia and the need for specialist vasodilator escalation.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease"]},{"id":50,"s":"A 53-year-old man has recurrent auricular chondritis sparing the lobule, nasal bridge inflammation, episcleritis and progressive hoarseness. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Granuloma annulare","Rosacea","Gout","Relapsing polychondritis","Psoriatic arthritis"],"a":3,"e":"Inflammation of cartilage-rich structures with ocular and airway disease suggests relapsing polychondritis. Relapsing polychondritis affects cartilaginous structures; sparing of the soft ear lobule is characteristic. Airway involvement may cause dangerous stenosis or malacia, so hoarseness and respiratory symptoms warrant urgent airway assessment and appropriate imaging/bronchoscopy planning. Relapsing polychondritis affects cartilaginous structures of the ears, nose and airway; stridor, voice change or respiratory difficulty should trigger urgent airway assessment.","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy"]},{"id":51,"s":"A 35-year-old woman with recurrent lacrimal and salivary swelling, hypergammaglobulinaemia and suspected Sjogren syndrome is anti-Ro negative. Which investigation can provide objective histological support for Sjogren classification?","o":["Serum urate","Labial minor salivary gland biopsy","Temporal artery biopsy","HLA-B27 testing","Bone scintigraphy"],"a":1,"e":"Minor salivary gland focal lymphocytic sialadenitis supports classification when serology is uninformative. Labial salivary gland biopsy showing focal lymphocytic sialadenitis may help classify seronegative Sjogren disease when clinical suspicion remains strong. Correlate with objective tear/saliva testing and exclude alternative causes of sicca and gland enlargement. Recurrent gland swelling has a differential extending beyond Sj\u00f6gren disease, including IgG4-related disease, sarcoidosis and lymphoproliferative pathology; biopsy may be decisive.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Crystal Arthropathy","Connective Tissue Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":52,"s":"A 52-year-old man has unexplained skin tightening of the fingers, Raynaud phenomenon, abnormal nailfold capillaries and new dysphagia. Which autoantibody is associated particularly with diffuse skin involvement and interstitial lung disease in systemic sclerosis?","o":["Anti-mitochondrial M2","Anti-AChR","Anti-CCP","Anti-GBM","Anti-topoisomerase I (Scl-70)"],"a":4,"e":"Anti-topoisomerase I positivity is associated with diffuse systemic sclerosis and ILD risk, though assessment is clinical. Anti-topoisomerase I (Scl-70) is associated with diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis and increased ILD risk, whereas anticentromere antibodies associate more often with limited cutaneous disease and pulmonary vascular complications. Antibodies support phenotyping but do not determine an individual prognosis. Raynaud phenomenon with abnormal nailfold capillaroscopy and disease-specific antibodies supports a systemic-sclerosis spectrum even before extensive skin thickening develops.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Connective Tissue Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":53,"s":"A 58-year-old woman has glenohumeral pain and progressive restriction of both active and passive external rotation, with diabetes and a normal plain radiograph. Which diagnosis is most likely?","o":["Isolated supraspinatus tendinopathy","Subacromial bursitis","Thoracic outlet syndrome","Adhesive capsulitis","Cervical radiculopathy"],"a":3,"e":"Global restriction of passive shoulder motion, particularly external rotation, favours adhesive capsulitis. Adhesive capsulitis produces global loss of passive and active glenohumeral motion, classically external rotation. Diabetes and thyroid disease increase risk. Management usually involves education, analgesia, range-of-motion therapy and selected corticosteroid injection. Restriction of passive external rotation is particularly useful for distinguishing true capsular stiffness from isolated rotator cuff pain, where passive range is generally preserved. Questions 56-60","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":54,"s":"A 63-year-old woman has lateral hip pain and cannot lie on the affected side. Hip internal rotation is preserved and there is focal greater-trochanter tenderness. Which diagnosis is most likely?","o":["L3 radiculopathy","Avascular necrosis","Septic arthritis","Hip osteoarthritis","Greater trochanteric pain syndrome"],"a":4,"e":"Local trochanteric tenderness and preserved hip range support gluteal tendinopathy/GTPS. Greater trochanteric pain syndrome usually relates to gluteal tendinopathy rather than isolated bursitis. Lateral tenderness and painful side-lying favour the diagnosis over intra-articular hip disease, where restricted internal rotation and groin pain are more typical. Gluteal tendinopathy is suggested by lateral trochanteric tenderness and pain with single-leg loading; evaluate hip osteoarthritis and lumbar referred pain when features are atypical.","c":"Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","t":["Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Infection & Immunosuppression"]},{"id":55,"s":"A 49-year-old woman has medial ankle pain, hindfoot valgus, loss of the medial arch and cannot perform a single-leg heel raise. Which structure is most likely dysfunctional?","o":["Peroneus brevis","Tibialis posterior tendon","Extensor hallucis longus","Tibialis anterior tendon","Achilles tendon"],"a":1,"e":"Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction produces progressive collapsing flatfoot and impaired inversion on heel rise. Tibialis posterior dysfunction weakens hindfoot inversion and arch support, leading to progressive collapsing flatfoot. Examine single-leg heel rise and flexible versus fixed deformity; management includes support, strengthening and foot/ankle specialist referral when progressive. The lower-limb regional pain module links progressive arch collapse to failure of medial dynamic support; fixed deformity or loss of function warrants more urgent foot and ankle assessment.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":56,"s":"A 55-year-old woman after distal-radius fracture develops severe disproportionate hand pain, swelling, colour and temperature asymmetry and allodynia. Which syndrome is most likely?","o":["Palindromic rheumatism","Septic arthritis","Carpal tunnel syndrome","Complex regional pain syndrome","Cervical myelopathy"],"a":3,"e":"Disproportionate continuing pain with sensory, vasomotor, sudomotor and motor/trophic changes suggests CRPS. Complex regional pain syndrome is diagnosed clinically using Budapest criteria, requiring disproportionate pain and appropriate symptom/sign domains. Exclude infection, vascular compromise and mechanical complications. Early functional rehabilitation, desensitisation and pain management are important. The widespread pain module emphasises that disproportionate persistent pain can reflect altered nociceptive processing; physical signs and exclusion of important structural or vascular pathology remain essential.","c":"Infection & Immunosuppression","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":57,"s":"A 61-year-old man has shoulder pain on abduction with preserved passive external rotation. Pain is reproduced by resisted abduction, without systemic features. Which diagnosis is most likely?","o":["Rotator cuff-related shoulder pain","Septic glenohumeral arthritis","Adhesive capsulitis","Glenohumeral ankylosis","Cervical myelopathy"],"a":0,"e":"Painful resisted motion with relatively preserved passive range favours rotator cuff pathology over capsulitis. Rotator cuff-related shoulder pain causes painful resisted movement with relatively preserved passive range, unlike adhesive capsulitis. Assess for traumatic full-thickness tears and red flags; progressive graded exercise and analgesia are first-line for uncomplicated tendinopathy. A painful resisted abduction test is not specific for a rotator cuff tear; consider the combination of strength, passive movement, trauma history and function.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":58,"s":"A 68-year-old woman has symptomatic knee osteoarthritis, obesity and no inflammatory arthritis. She asks for the intervention most likely to offer durable functional improvement. Which intervention should be central to management?","o":["Hydroxychloroquine","Empirical oral prednisolone","Individualised therapeutic exercise with weight management","Long-term high-dose opioids","Arthroscopic lavage routinely"],"a":2,"e":"Exercise and weight management are core osteoarthritis treatments; arthroscopy and DMARDs are not routine treatments. NICE NG226 prioritises therapeutic exercise and weight management as core OA care. In overweight patients, even modest loss helps, while around 10% weight reduction generally offers greater benefit than 5%. Pharmacological analgesia supports participation but does not replace rehabilitation. Osteoarthritis education should integrate strengthening, aerobic activity, weight management and supported self-management; radiographic severity alone should not dictate disability assessment. Questions 61-65","c":"Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","t":["Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":59,"s":"A 57-year-old office worker has nocturnal paraesthesia in the thumb, index and middle fingers, thenar weakness and preserved little-finger sensation. Which nerve is principally affected?","o":["Ulnar nerve at Guyon canal","Median nerve at the carpal tunnel","Axillary nerve","Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"],"a":1,"e":"Median neuropathy at the wrist causes radial three-and-a-half digit symptoms and may cause thenar weakness. Median neuropathy at the carpal tunnel causes radial three-and-a-half digit symptoms and thenar weakness, while the little finger is spared. Severe weakness, persistent numbness or diagnostic uncertainty warrants nerve conduction assessment and possible decompression referral. Persistent thenar wasting or objective weakness suggests more advanced median nerve compromise and lowers the threshold for specialist assessment beyond night splinting alone.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":60,"s":"A 60-year-old woman treated with rituximab for vasculitis develops new fever and non-productive cough. CT reveals bilateral ground-glass infiltrates and oxygen saturations are falling. Which opportunistic infection must be considered?","o":["Parvovirus arthropathy","Scabies","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia","Clostridioides difficile colitis","Tetanus"],"a":2,"e":"Immunosuppressed patients with hypoxia and ground-glass opacities need urgent assessment for PJP among other infections. Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia is a key consideration in immunosuppressed patients with hypoxaemia and diffuse ground-glass change. Evaluate opportunistic and conventional infection urgently; bronchoalveolar sampling or PCR may assist, and timely treatment should not await all results in unstable patients. Opportunistic infection may present without marked inflammatory markers in immunosuppressed patients; consider treatment-associated antibody depletion and concurrent steroid exposure.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":61,"s":"A 67-year-old man has severe nocturnal back pain, weight loss, anaemia, elevated total protein and lytic vertebral lesions. Which diagnosis should be prioritised?","o":["Multiple myeloma","Polymyalgia rheumatica","Fibromyalgia","Paget disease alone","Ankylosing spondylitis"],"a":0,"e":"Anaemia, paraprotein clues and lytic bone lesions raise immediate concern for plasma cell malignancy. Multiple myeloma can present with lytic lesions, anaemia, renal dysfunction and monoclonal proteins. Request serum protein electrophoresis, immunofixation, serum free light chains and urine studies, with haematology assessment; infection and metastatic cancer remain differentials. Malignant bone disease may mimic mechanical and inflammatory spinal pain; persistent night pain, systemic symptoms and destructive imaging warrant urgent investigation.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Vasculitis","Metabolic Bone Disease","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":62,"s":"A 71-year-old man has fever, a new murmur, splinter haemorrhages and positive rheumatoid factor with low complement; he is referred for possible systemic vasculitis. What is the most important alternative diagnosis?","o":["Infective endocarditis","Osteoporosis","Rheumatoid arthritis solely","Polymyalgia rheumatica","Gout"],"a":0,"e":"Endocarditis can mimic immune-complex vasculitis; obtain blood cultures and echocardiographic assessment. Infective endocarditis can cause circulating immune complexes, positive rheumatoid factor, hypocomplementaemia and vasculitis-like manifestations. Obtain multiple blood cultures and echocardiography before immunosuppression where feasible, as steroids without treating infection may be harmful. Infective endocarditis and immune-complex disease can produce positive RF, low complement and renal abnormalities, so immunological tests cannot substitute for cultures and echocardiography.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Vasculitis","Crystal Arthropathy","Metabolic Bone Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":63,"s":"A 52-year-old man with a prosthetic knee has acute joint pain, warmth, raised CRP and a draining sinus tract communicating with the joint. Which diagnosis should be assumed until appropriately managed?","o":["Fibromyalgia","Palindromic rheumatism","Osteoarthritis flare alone","Prosthetic joint infection","Asymptomatic loosening"],"a":3,"e":"A sinus tract communicating with a prosthesis is highly concerning for prosthetic joint infection; urgent orthopaedic and microbiology input is warranted. A sinus tract communicating with a prosthesis strongly supports prosthetic joint infection. Urgent orthopaedic and microbiology input is required; obtain suitable deep specimens and avoid empiric antibiotics before sampling in a stable patient when this is safe. Prosthetic joint infection may be caused by biofilm-forming organisms and requires surgical planning as well as microbiology; a draining sinus is particularly important diagnostically.","c":"Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","t":["Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":64,"s":"A 73-year-old woman receiving denosumab for severe osteoporosis is scheduled to stop treatment without alternative therapy. What is the key risk that should drive a transition plan?","o":["Rapid improvement in bone density after cessation","Retinal toxicity","Addison crisis","Acute gout","Rebound vertebral fractures after denosumab cessation"],"a":4,"e":"Stopping denosumab without follow-on antiresorptive treatment can cause rebound bone turnover and multiple vertebral fractures. Stopping denosumab without effective antiresorptive follow-on therapy can cause rebound bone turnover and multiple vertebral fractures. Plan transition, commonly to a bisphosphonate, with specialist consideration of timing, prior duration and bone turnover monitoring. The osteoporosis modules emphasise rebound vertebral-fracture risk after denosumab cessation; the follow-on antiresorptive plan is part of safe prescribing from the outset.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Metabolic Bone Disease"]},{"id":65,"s":"A 74-year-old patient taking oral bisphosphonate for eight years develops weeks of prodromal thigh pain; X-ray shows lateral cortical thickening of the femoral shaft. What complication should be suspected?","o":["Osteogenesis imperfecta","Septic arthritis","Giant cell tumour","Atypical femoral fracture","Osteomalacia alone"],"a":3,"e":"Long-term antiresorptive treatment can be associated with atypical femoral fractures, often heralded by thigh pain. Atypical femoral fracture is associated with long-term antiresorptive exposure and often presents with prodromal thigh pain and lateral cortical thickening. Image the entire femur and contralateral side, review antiresorptive treatment and seek orthopaedic assessment. Prodromal thigh pain in long-term bisphosphonate use should prompt full-length femoral imaging; incomplete atypical fractures may precede a complete transverse fracture.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression"]},{"id":66,"s":"A 38-year-old woman with inflammatory bowel disease presents with proximal weakness, bone pain, low phosphate and elevated alkaline phosphatase; vitamin D is profoundly low. Which bone disorder best explains this pattern?","o":["Paget disease","Primary osteoarthritis","Osteomalacia","Osteopetrosis","Chondrocalcinosis"],"a":2,"e":"Hypophosphataemia, vitamin D deficiency, bone pain and muscle weakness support defective mineralisation. Vitamin D deficiency osteomalacia causes defective mineralisation, bone pain and proximal weakness with often high ALP and low phosphate. Investigate calcium, phosphate, PTH, vitamin D and malabsorption; replace deficiency while monitoring biochemical response. Osteomalacia reflects impaired mineralisation rather than simply reduced bone mass; low phosphate, secondary hyperparathyroidism and high alkaline phosphatase help distinguish it from osteoporosis.","c":"Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","t":["Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Metabolic Bone Disease"]},{"id":67,"s":"A 75-year-old man has pelvic bone pain, enlarged skull circumference, isolated high alkaline phosphatase and radiographs showing mixed lytic and sclerotic bone expansion. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Hypervitaminosis A","Paget disease of bone","Rheumatoid arthritis","Osteonecrosis","Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia"],"a":1,"e":"Paget disease causes focal increased bone remodelling, expansion, cortical thickening and elevated alkaline phosphatase. Paget disease features focal accelerated bone remodelling, bone enlargement, pain and elevated bone-derived ALP. Assess disease extent with imaging; symptomatic active disease generally responds to potent bisphosphonates, especially intravenous zoledronic acid when appropriate. Paget disease may be identified by an isolated ALP rise, characteristic bone expansion and cortical thickening; determine whether biochemical activity corresponds to symptomatic sites. Questions 71-75","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Metabolic Bone Disease"]},{"id":68,"s":"A 65-year-old woman with glucocorticoid-treated RA sustained a low-trauma vertebral fracture. Her DXA T-score is -1.8. What is the correct interpretation for fracture prevention?","o":["Bisphosphonates are always contraindicated","Only repeat DXA after ten years","She is at high fracture risk despite a T-score above -2.5","Normal T-score excludes fracture risk","No intervention can reduce future fractures"],"a":2,"e":"A fragility vertebral fracture indicates high future risk; management is not based solely on a densitometric osteoporosis threshold. A low-trauma vertebral fracture establishes clinically important skeletal fragility despite a T-score in the osteopenic range. Incorporate glucocorticoid exposure, prior fracture and other risks into a management plan; anti-osteoporosis treatment is usually indicated. A fragility vertebral fracture strongly affects risk classification even when DXA shows osteopenia rather than osteoporosis; glucocorticoids further increase fracture probability.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Metabolic Bone Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":69,"s":"A 7-year-old girl has persistent knee synovitis for four months and a painless reduction in visual acuity is not reported. She has ANA-positive oligoarticular JIA. Which surveillance is essential even without ocular symptoms?","o":["Serial serum urate only","Regular slit-lamp screening for anterior uveitis","Annual carotid Doppler","Monthly CT brain","Routine colonoscopy"],"a":1,"e":"Chronic JIA-associated anterior uveitis can be asymptomatic; risk-adapted slit-lamp surveillance is essential. JIA-associated anterior uveitis may be silent and threaten vision despite a quiet joint examination. ANA-positive young-onset oligoarticular disease is high risk; ophthalmology slit-lamp screening intervals depend on risk and should continue as advised. Children with oligoarticular JIA can develop asymptomatic chronic anterior uveitis; screening must continue even if joint symptoms improve and the child denies eye pain.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Paediatric Rheumatology","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":70,"s":"An 11-year-old with systemic JIA develops persistent fever, bruising, hepatosplenomegaly, ferritin 25,000, falling platelets and fibrinogen, and rising transaminases. Which complication is most likely?","o":["Macrophage activation syndrome","Simple iron overload","Isolated viral rhinitis","Osgood-Schlatter disease","Osteochondritis dissecans"],"a":0,"e":"Cytopenias, hypofibrinogenaemia and marked hyperferritinaemia in systemic JIA indicate MAS, a medical emergency. MAS in systemic JIA can present with falling platelets and fibrinogen despite active systemic inflammation, striking ferritin elevation, hepatitis and coagulopathy. Escalate immediately to paediatric rheumatology/critical care for trigger evaluation and targeted immunosuppressive treatment. Systemic JIA with persistent fever, falling platelets, reduced fibrinogen and hepatitis should prompt emergency evaluation for macrophage activation syndrome.","c":"Paediatric Rheumatology","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Paediatric Rheumatology"]},{"id":71,"s":"A 14-year-old boy has persistent inflammatory lower-back pain, heel enthesitis, asymmetric ankle synovitis and HLA-B27 positivity. Which JIA phenotype is most likely?","o":["Juvenile dermatomyositis","Systemic JIA","Enthesitis-related arthritis","Oligoarticular ANA-positive JIA","Polyarticular RF-positive JIA"],"a":2,"e":"Enthesitis, inflammatory axial symptoms and asymmetric lower-limb arthritis indicate an enthesitis-related phenotype. Enthesitis-related arthritis often combines lower-limb asymmetric arthritis, enthesitis and inflammatory axial symptoms, sometimes with HLA-B27 and acute anterior uveitis. Follow for sacroiliitis and bowel/skin disease and tailor therapy to axial versus peripheral involvement. Enthesitis and inflammatory back pain can occur in juvenile spondyloarthritis before unequivocal radiographic sacroiliitis; assess peripheral, axial, ocular and bowel manifestations.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Connective Tissue Disease","Paediatric Rheumatology","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":72,"s":"A 13-year-old girl presents with a violaceous heliotrope rash, Gottron papules, proximal weakness and raised CK. Which feature requires active assessment due to potentially serious extra-muscular involvement?","o":["Swallowing dysfunction and aspiration risk","Reduced serum urate","Hyperuricaemia alone","Isolated carpal tunnel symptoms","Increased foot arch"],"a":0,"e":"Juvenile dermatomyositis can cause bulbar weakness and dysphagia; evaluate swallowing and systemic complications. Bulbar weakness in juvenile dermatomyositis can impair swallowing and cause aspiration, while severe disease can affect respiratory muscles. Assess swallow safety urgently alongside strength, skin disease, cardiac and pulmonary manifestations; intensive multidisciplinary treatment may be required. Juvenile dermatomyositis can include vasculopathy, skin ulceration and swallowing dysfunction; swallowing safety and organ involvement should accompany routine strength measurement. Questions 76-78","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Connective Tissue Disease","Paediatric Rheumatology"]},{"id":73,"s":"A patient receiving methotrexate for RA is inadvertently prescribed trimethoprim for a urinary infection. She develops oral ulceration, pancytopenia and acute kidney injury. What drug interaction most likely explains the toxicity?","o":["Cytochrome P450 induction causing steroid withdrawal","Increased calcium excretion","Additive antifolate toxicity with methotrexate","Increased urate clearance","TNF-alpha antagonism"],"a":2,"e":"Trimethoprim can exacerbate methotrexate marrow and mucosal toxicity, especially with renal dysfunction. Trimethoprim acts as an antifolate and can markedly potentiate methotrexate toxicity, particularly with renal impairment, causing mucositis and marrow suppression. Stop interacting drugs, urgently assess blood counts, kidney function and infection, and arrange specialist consideration of folinic acid rescue. Methotrexate safety education must emphasise once-weekly dosing and interacting medications; severe cytopenias and mucositis require immediate medication review and urgent blood tests.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":74,"s":"A patient taking leflunomide develops severe drug-induced hepatitis and the medication is discontinued. Rapid removal of the active metabolite is indicated. Which intervention accelerates elimination?","o":["Vitamin K alone","Cholestyramine washout","Oral iron","Probenecid","Naloxone"],"a":1,"e":"Leflunomide's active metabolite undergoes enterohepatic recirculation; cholestyramine accelerates elimination. Leflunomide undergoes prolonged enterohepatic recycling, so simply stopping the tablets does not rapidly clear its active metabolite. Cholestyramine washout is used in serious toxicity or pregnancy planning, with follow-up drug-level testing where indicated. Leflunomide has a long effective elimination period because of enterohepatic recirculation; a formal washout regimen is needed for certain serious adverse events and conception planning.","c":"Pregnancy & Reproductive","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":75,"s":"A 64-year-old woman starting tofacitinib for RA has previous myocardial infarction, continues to smoke and has several cardiovascular risk factors. Which safety issue is most important in shared treatment selection?","o":["No cardiovascular evaluation is needed","All JAK inhibitors prevent thrombosis","No infection screening is necessary","Increased risks of major cardiovascular events, malignancy and thrombosis with JAK inhibitors","Live vaccines are always safer during treatment"],"a":3,"e":"Regulatory warnings highlight MACE, malignancy, VTE and serious infection concerns with JAK inhibition, especially in higher-risk patients. JAK inhibitors carry warnings about serious infections, malignancy, major adverse cardiovascular events and thrombosis, especially in patients with relevant risk factors. Document individual risk-benefit discussion, assess alternative DMARD options and manage modifiable cardiovascular risks. The pharmacology modules emphasise infection screening, vaccination review and comorbidity assessment before targeted therapy; JAK-inhibitor risk assessment is particularly important with cardiovascular risk factors.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":76,"s":"A 54-year-old woman with seropositive RA has been treated with rituximab. Six months later, peripheral CD19-positive B cells are undetectable, but rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies remain detectable at similar titres. What is the best explanation?","o":["Activated naive T lymphocytes continuously secrete rheumatoid factor","Memory B cells express no CD20 and therefore survive unchanged","Long-lived plasma cells do not express CD20 and continue to secrete antibody","Rituximab selectively eliminates all immunoglobulin-secreting plasma cells","CD20 is expressed only on neutrophils"],"a":2,"e":"Rituximab targets CD20, expressed from pre-B to memory B cells but not on most plasma cells. Pre-existing antibody (including protective vaccine immunity) therefore persists, whereas new humoral responses are impaired. IgG can fall with repeated courses. Other reasons autoantibodies persist after B-cell depletion: tissue-resident or splenic B cells escaping depletion; early B-cell repopulation (check CD19 count, often 6\u20139 months); human anti-chimeric antibodies reducing rituximab effect. Anti-Ro and anti-dsDNA titres may fall partially; vaccine antibody titres usually stay stable. CD20 is expressed from pre-B to memory B cells but not on pro-B cells, plasmablasts or most plasma cells. Clinical response in RA is best in seropositive patients, and poorer when synovium is B-cell-poor. Check immunoglobulins before each cycle. Retreat on clinical flare or at fixed intervals (usually \u20216 months). Give non-live vaccines \u20212 weeks before infusion. Options when the response is inadequate: switch mechanism (abatacept, IL-6R, JAKi); obinutuzumab (type II anti-CD20) in lupus nephritis. Memory B cells are CD20-positive and rapidly reactivated. Tissue-resident memory T cells may explain flares recurring at the same sites. Rituximab, high-dose GC, abatacept and methotrexate all reduce vaccine responses.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":77,"s":"A patient with RA is starting tocilizumab. Which molecular target does this drug block?","o":["TNF-alpha","Interleukin-1 receptor","Interleukin-6 receptor","Interleukin-17A","CD20"],"a":2,"e":"Tocilizumab and sarilumab block the IL-6 receptor. Monitoring includes neutrophils, platelets, transaminases and lipids, and the drug suppresses CRP and fever, which can mask infection. Other causes of an unexpectedly low CRP in sepsis: tocilizumab or sarilumab, JAK inhibitors (JAK1 relays IL-6 signalling), severe liver failure and very early infection. SLE flare with a normal CRP is typical, so a high CRP in SLE suggests infection or serositis. Tocilizumab is IV monthly or SC weekly; sarilumab is SC fortnightly. Adverse effects: neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, raised ALT, raised LDL (monitor lipids), diverticular perforation, injection-site reactions. Uses: RA (good as monotherapy), GCA (tocilizumab with a 26-week GC taper), relapsing PMR (sarilumab), systemic JIA and AOSD, early SSc-ILD (tocilizumab), ICI-arthritis, cytokine release syndrome. Withhold for serious infection and image early, because fever and CRP will not alert you.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":78,"s":"What are Janus kinases (JAKs), and why does their inhibition have broad effects?","o":["Secreted immunoglobulins that neutralise TNF","Nuclear transcription factors that directly bind DNA","Intracellular tyrosine kinases that transmit signals from type I and II cytokine receptors","Cell-surface integrins mediating leukocyte adhesion","Proteases responsible for collagen breakdown"],"a":2,"e":"JAK1, JAK2, JAK3 and TYK2 relay signals from many cytokine receptors. This breadth explains the class effects of JAK inhibitors, including infection (notably herpes zoster), venous thromboembolism, lipid changes and cardiovascular signals in at-risk patients. JAK Main cytokines Clue JAK1 IL-6, type I/II IFN, gc upadacitinib, filgotinib (JAK1selective) cytokines JAK2 EPO, TPO, GM-CSF, anaemia, thrombocytopenia IL-12/23 JAK3 gc cytokines (IL-2, lymphocyte function; 4, 7, 15, 21) tofacitinib (JAK1/3) TYK2 type I IFN, IL-12, ILdeucravacitinib (psoriasis; 23 SLE trials) TNF and IL-1 signal through NF-kB and MAP kinases, not JAK\u2013STAT. Before starting: recombinant zoster vaccine; lipids at 8\u201312 weeks; FBC (lymphopenia, neutropenia, anaemia); LFTs; CK (rises are common). ORAL Surveillance (tofacitinib vs TNFi in people \u202150 with \u20211 CV risk factor) showed more MACE, malignancy, VTE, serious infection and death. Use only if no suitable alternative in people \u202165, current or long-term past smokers, or those with CV or malignancy risk, and with caution where there are VTE risk factors. Licensed uses now span RA, PsA, axSpA, GCA (upadacitinib) and AOSD/JIA. Dermatomyositis has trial data (brepocitinib). Stop 3 days before elective surgery.","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":79,"s":"The anti-inflammatory effect of low-dose weekly methotrexate is thought to be mediated mainly by which mechanism?","o":["Direct cyclo-oxygenase-2 inhibition","Depletion of circulating CD20-positive B cells","Inhibition of calcineurin-dependent T-cell activation","Increased extracellular adenosine through AICAR transformylase inhibition","Irreversible inhibition of xanthine oxidase"],"a":3,"e":"Methotrexate polyglutamates inhibit AICAR transformylase, raising extracellular adenosine, which is antiinflammatory. Folate supplementation reduces toxicity without abolishing efficacy, which supports a mechanism beyond simple antifolate activity. Other antifolate or adenosine-related points: caffeine (an adenosine-receptor antagonist) may modestly blunt methotrexate efficacy at high intake (inconsistent data). Cytotoxic high-dose methotrexate works through DHFR inhibition and is a different pharmacology. Polyglutamation traps methotrexate intracellularly, so full effect takes 6\u201312 weeks. Bioavailability of oral methotrexate plateaus above about 15 mg; subcutaneous dosing avoids this. Usual RA regimen: start 15 mg/week and rise to 20\u201325 mg/week (or about 0.3 mg/kg) over 4\u20138 weeks, with folic acid 5 mg weekly. Switch to SC for an inadequate response or GI intolerance before judging failure.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":80,"s":"Leflunomide reduces lymphocyte proliferation by inhibiting which enzyme?","o":["Calcineurin","Janus kinase 1","Thiopurine methyltransferase","Dihydroorotate dehydrogenase","Phosphodiesterase-4"],"a":3,"e":"Leflunomide (via teriflunomide) inhibits dihydroorotate dehydrogenase and de novo pyrimidine synthesis in activated lymphocytes. Its active metabolite undergoes enterohepatic recirculation and persists for up to 2 years, which is why cholestyramine washout is used before conception or after serious toxicity. Other causes of raised ALT or cytopenias on leflunomide: alcohol, NAFLD, co-prescribed methotrexate (additive hepatotoxicity), viral hepatitis. Other leflunomide adverse effects: hypertension, diarrhoea, weight loss, alopecia, peripheral neuropathy, rare ILD (higher risk in Japanese populations and pre-existing ILD). Dose 10\u201320 mg daily (loading doses are usually omitted because of GI upset). Monitor FBC, LFTs and BP. Washout with cholestyramine 8 g three times daily for 11 days (or activated charcoal), then confirm two plasma teriflunomide levels below 0.02 mg/L 14 days apart. Use washout for pregnancy plans, serious toxicity or switching to another hepatotoxic drug.","c":"Pregnancy & Reproductive","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":81,"s":"What is the mechanism of action of abatacept?","o":["TNF-alpha neutralisation by a monoclonal antibody","IL-6 receptor blockade","JAK1/3 catalytic inhibition","B-cell depletion through CD20","A CTLA-4-immunoglobulin fusion protein that blocks CD80/86\u2013CD28 co-stimulation of T cells"],"a":4,"e":"CTLA-4-immunoglobulin fusion protein that blocks CD80/86\u2013CD28 co-stimulation of T cells Abatacept binds CD80/CD86 on antigen-presenting cells and prevents CD28-mediated T-cell activation. It has a relatively favourable serious-infection profile among biologics. Abatacept can be given IV monthly or SC weekly and is less immunogenic. Infection signal is lower than TNFi in observational data. It is effective in seropositive RA (best in high-titre ACPA) and JIA, and is used for the joints in RA- ILD. It also treats CTLA-4/LRBA deficiency (immune dysregulation with immunodeficiency). Prevention trials: 12 months of abatacept in ACPA-positive arthralgia delayed RA (about 6% vs 29% at 1 year), but the effect waned after stopping. Abatacept is not standard prevention; offer trials. Avoid in COPD (more respiratory adverse events in trials).","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":82,"s":"A 30-year-old man with axial spondyloarthritis is starting secukinumab. What does this drug neutralise?","o":["Interleukin-23 p19","Interleukin-17A","Interleukin-6 receptor","Tumour necrosis factor-alpha","B-cell activating factor"],"a":1,"e":"Secukinumab and ixekizumab are IL-17A inhibitors (bimekizumab inhibits IL-17A and IL-17F). They are effective in axial and peripheral spondyloarthritis and psoriasis but can provoke or worsen inflammatory bowel disease. Other drugs that can provoke or unmask IBD: IL-17A and IL- 17A/F inhibitors, etanercept (paradoxical IBD), isotretinoin (debated), NSAIDs (flare). Other IL-17i adverse effects: mucocutaneous Candida (oral thrush, more with bimekizumab), eczematous reactions, neutropenia. Secukinumab and ixekizumab neutralise IL-17A; bimekizumab neutralises IL-17A and IL-17F. Effective for axial and peripheral SpA, enthesitis, dactylitis, nails and skin. Less effective than mAb TNFi for uveitis. Ineffective (and harmful) in Crohn's. Take a history for bowel symptoms before starting (consider faecal calprotectin if suggestive). Prefer IL-17i when there is extensive psoriasis. Prefer mAb TNFi when there is IBD or recurrent uveitis.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Psoriatic Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":83,"s":"A patient with psoriatic arthritis has active spinal inflammation despite adequate NSAID therapy. Which cytokine-targeted pathway has not shown reliable efficacy for isolated axial disease?","o":["TNF-alpha inhibition","IL-17A inhibition","IL-23 pathway inhibition","JAK inhibition","IL-6 receptor inhibition"],"a":2,"e":"IL-23 inhibitors are useful for several peripheral and skin manifestations of psoriatic arthritis, but they have not demonstrated established efficacy for isolated axial spondyloarthritis. Ustekinumab targets the shared IL-12/23 p40 subunit; p19 inhibitors selectively target IL-23. They must not be regarded as equivalent to IL-17 or TNF inhibitors for established axial inflammation.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Psoriatic Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":84,"s":"Apremilast is used in psoriatic arthritis. What is its mechanism of action?","o":["Sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor modulation","Janus kinase inhibition","Phosphodiesterase-4 inhibition, increasing intracellular cAMP","Interleukin-6 receptor blockade","CD20-directed B-cell depletion"],"a":2,"e":"Apremilast is an oral PDE4 inhibitor. Common adverse effects are diarrhoea, nausea and weight loss; mood should be monitored because of rare depression. Mood change on apremilast vs other causes: active disease burden, steroid withdrawal, isotretinoin, chronic pain. Weight loss vs GI side effects vs undiagnosed malignancy or IBD. Apremilast needs no routine blood monitoring and no TB screening. Dose-titrate over 5 days to 30 mg twice daily (once daily if eGFR <30). It has modest efficacy (below TNFi) for joints, skin and enthesitis. It reduced oral ulcers in Beh\u00e7et's (RELIEF trial). Places in therapy: mild oligoarticular PsA or skinpredominant disease after csDMARD failure, where a b/tsDMARD is not appropriate; refractory Beh\u00e7et's mucocutaneous ulcers. Strong CYP3A4 inducers (rifampicin) reduce efficacy, so avoid co-prescription.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Psoriatic Arthritis","Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":85,"s":"Why is concomitant methotrexate advised when infliximab is used for rheumatoid arthritis?","o":["To prevent all methotrexate-associated cytopenias","To increase anti-infliximab antibody formation","To neutralise circulating TNF independently","To prevent infusion reactions through direct antihistamine action","It reduces formation of anti-drug antibodies, improving drug survival and efficacy"],"a":4,"e":"Chimeric and humanised monoclonal antibodies are immunogenic. Combining them with a csDMARD lowers antidrug antibody formation and improves outcomes. IL-6 receptor inhibitors and JAK inhibitors have the best evidence as monotherapy. Other causes of secondary loss of response to a TNF inhibitor: non-adherence; new non-inflammatory pain (OA, fibromyalgia); weight gain (volume of distribution); intercurrent infection; wrong diagnosis (e.g. PsA treated as RA, crystal disease). Immunogenicity order: infliximab (chimeric) > adalimumab > golimumab and certolizumab; etanercept forms nonneutralising antibodies. Low trough level with antibodies means switch within class. Adequate trough level without response means switch mechanism. If methotrexate is contraindicated, low-dose methotrexate (even 7.5\u201310 mg), leflunomide or azathioprine can still reduce immunogenicity, or choose IL-6R or JAK monotherapy. Therapeutic drug monitoring is reactive, not routine, in most UK practice.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":86,"s":"A patient is starting weekly methotrexate. What is the main purpose of co-prescribing folic acid?","o":["To increase efficacy through inhibition of urate synthesis","To prevent every infection during methotrexate therapy","To reduce gastrointestinal, mucocutaneous and hepatic adverse effects","To eliminate the need for blood monitoring","To enhance CD20-mediated B-cell depletion"],"a":2,"e":"Folic acid (commonly 5 mg once weekly on a different day from methotrexate, or per local regimen) reduces nausea, stomatitis and transaminase rises without meaningfully reducing efficacy. Methotrexate toxicities not prevented by folic acid: pneumonitis (idiosyncratic), teratogenicity, infection risk, accelerated nodulosis, and, largely, severe myelosuppression from overdose or interaction (which needs folinic acid, not folic acid). GI intolerance and \"methotrexate fog\" (malaise after dosing) are the commonest reasons for stopping. Alopecia and mouth ulcers respond to folate. Nausea also improves with SC dosing, taking it in the evening or an antiemetic. Folic acid 5 mg once weekly is usual. Increase to 5 mg up to 6 days/week (not on the methotrexate day) for persistent mucositis or LFT rises. Alcohol within national limits (\u00a314 units/week) is generally acceptable but individualised; heavy use is not.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":87,"s":"A 58-year-old woman taking methotrexate 20 mg weekly is prescribed trimethoprim for a urinary tract infection. Ten days later she presents with mouth ulcers, bruising and a neutrophil count of 0.4 x 10I/L. What is the most likely explanation?","o":["A drug interaction causing additive antifolate marrow toxicity","Stop methotrexate, admit for urgent assessment and give folinic acid rescue","Additive antifolate toxicity with methotrexate","Paternal low-dose methotrexate exposure is considered compatible with conception","At least 1 month before conception, continuing folic acid"],"a":0,"e":"drug interaction causing additive antifolate marrow toxicity Trimethoprim and co-trimoxazole add antifolate effects and reduce methotrexate clearance, causing potentially fatal myelosuppression. Both drugs should be stopped, FBC monitored and folinic acid rescue considered with urgent specialist advice. Other drugs that interact dangerously with methotrexate: co-trimoxazole and trimethoprim (antifolate), penicillins and probenecid (reduced renal clearance), PPIs (mainly highdose methotrexate), NSAIDs (small effect at low dose; take care in CKD), leflunomide (hepatotoxicity), and nitrous oxide anaesthesia (folate inactivation). Other causes of acute neutropenia: viral infection, sepsis, Felty's, LGL leukaemia, other drugs (sulfasalazine, clozapine, carbimazole). Patterns of toxicity: mucositis first, then cytopenias with a nadir about 7\u201310 days later. Severity is worse with renal impairment, hypoalbuminaemia, dehydration, older age and dosing errors. Stop both drugs. Check FBC, U&E and LFTs. Give folinic acid (calcium folinate), commonly 15 mg every 6 hours, adjusted to counts and renal function. Admit if febrile or neutrophils are below 1.0. Use G-CSF for profound neutropenia and treat neutropenic sepsis empirically. Use nitrofurantoin or another non-antifolate for UTI in patients on methotrexate.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":88,"s":"A 70-year-old woman with a recent diagnosis of RA attends with mouth ulcers, conjunctival irritation and pancytopenia. On review, she has been taking her methotrexate 10 mg tablet every morning. What is the most important immediate step?","o":["Paternal low-dose methotrexate exposure is considered compatible with conception","A drug interaction causing additive antifolate marrow toxicity","Stop methotrexate, admit for urgent assessment and give folinic acid rescue","Do not stop without follow-on therapy; plan a bisphosphonate (often zoledronate) about 6 months after the last dose","At least 1 month before conception, continuing folic acid"],"a":2,"e":"Inadvertent daily dosing is a well-recognised and potentially fatal error. Management is urgent: stop methotrexate, give folinic acid (calcium folinate), support counts and assess for sepsis. Clear written once-weekly instructions on a named day help prevent this. Differential of pancytopenia in RA: methotrexate toxicity (dose error, interaction, AKI), Felty's syndrome, T-LGL leukaemia, MAS, B12 or folate deficiency, myelodysplasia, sulfasalazine or azathioprine toxicity, hypersplenism, parvovirus, lymphoma. Mucositis (mouth ulcers, sore eyes or skin) with cytopenias in a new methotrexate user strongly suggests daily dosing. Ask to see the pill packet. AKI precipitates toxicity even at correct doses. Stop methotrexate. Give folinic acid rescue early (it is most effective within 24 hours of the last dose). Hydration; G- CSF and transfusion as needed; barrier nursing; broadspectrum antibiotics if febrile. Glucarpidase is used for high-dose methotrexate with renal failure (oncology). Prevention: a single strength (2.5 mg) tablet, a named day on the prescription, patient education and pharmacy checks.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":89,"s":"Six weeks after starting methotrexate, a 63-year-old woman develops fever, a dry cough and breathlessness. SpO2 is 89% on air and CT shows bilateral ground-glass change. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["High-dose glucocorticoids including IV methylprednisolone pulses, with IVIG","High-dose glucocorticoids (including pulses) with cyclophosphamide","Co-trimoxazole prophylaxis against Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, which is the infection to suspect","Stop methotrexate, exclude infection including Pneumocystis, and start high-dose glucocorticoids","Urgent assessment for GCA, with high-dose glucocorticoids if it is suspected"],"a":3,"e":"Acute methotrexate pneumonitis is an idiosyncratic hypersensitivity reaction, usually within the first year. The drug is withdrawn, infection (including opportunistic organisms) excluded and corticosteroids given. Rechallenge after confirmed pneumonitis is not advised. Fever, cough and ground-glass change on methotrexate: methotrexate pneumonitis vs PJP (lymphopenic, high-dose GC), viral pneumonia (including COVID-19, CMV), atypical bacterial infection, RA-ILD acute exacerbation, druginduced ILD from other agents (leflunomide, TNFi, rituximab), pulmonary oedema, alveolar haemorrhage. Risk factors: older age, diabetes, hypoalbuminaemia, preexisting lung disease, prior DMARD lung toxicity. BAL often shows lymphocytosis with a high CD4:CD8 ratio. Eosinophilia occurs in about a third. Pneumonitis is rare (<1%). Methotrexate does not cause chronic RA-ILD and may delay its onset. Stop methotrexate. Do BAL and induced sputum for PJP PCR, viral PCR and cultures. Cover with empirical cotrimoxazole and antibiotics until infection is excluded. Prednisolone about 1 mg/kg, or IV methylprednisolone if hypoxic. Do not rechallenge. Stable pre-existing ILD is not an absolute contraindication to methotrexate (manage with the respiratory team). The EULAR course states that methotrexate can be continued in most patients with stable RA-ILD.","c":"Infection & Immunosuppression","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":90,"s":"A patient on methotrexate 20 mg weekly has routine bloods showing ALT 118 U/L (previously 35 U/L) with normal bilirubin and albumin. She feels well. What is the most appropriate action?","o":["Add an IL-17 or IL-23 inhibitor","Withhold methotrexate, discuss with the rheumatology team and repeat the tests promptly","Switch to subcutaneous methotrexate","Reduce or withhold ciclosporin, manage blood pressure and discuss with the specialist team","Withhold further rituximab, consider an alternative DMARD and discuss immunoglobulin replacement with immunology if infections persist"],"a":1,"e":"ALT more than about three times the upper limit is a reason to withhold methotrexate and discuss with the rheumatology team while repeating tests promptly. The 2025 BSR csDMARD guideline moves away from fixed cutoffs: decisions weigh the trend and the absolute value, many isolated abnormalities are transient or unrelated, and alcohol, other hepatotoxins and fatty liver should be considered. Adults with liver-disease risk factors should have FIB-4 scoring (with elastography if indicated) when starting methotrexate. Causes of raised ALT on methotrexate other than drug hepatotoxicity: alcohol, NAFLD or metabolic syndrome (the commonest), statins, leflunomide, NSAIDs, isoniazid (latent TB treatment), viral hepatitis (HBV reactivation), autoimmune hepatitis (overlap with SLE or Sj\u00f6gren's), PBC (anti-mitochondrial antibody, especially in SSc or Sj\u00f6gren's), congestive hepatopathy, haemochromatosis. For a confirmed ALT above about 3 \u00d7 ULN: withhold, repeat in 1\u20132 weeks and restart at a lower dose once settled. For persistent smaller rises: reduce the dose, increase folate, address alcohol and weight, and do a liver screen plus FIB- 4 (with elastography if FIB-4 \u20211.3). Refer to hepatology if fibrosis risk is high. Liver biopsy is now rarely needed.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":91,"s":"Which monitoring schedule is most appropriate for a patient starting methotrexate?","o":["Start 100 mg daily and increase by 100 mg every 2\u20134 weeks to target urate; give low-dose colchicine","FBC, LFTs and renal function at week 2, then about monthly for the first 3-6 months, then every 3 months, with the interval individualised to toxicity risk","Treat latent TB (for example 3 months of isoniazid with rifampicin), starting the TNF inhibitor after at least 1 month if urgent, or on completion","Use a national risk tool that includes RA (such as QRISK3), reassess regularly, and control disease activity and traditional risk factors","Stop methotrexate, exclude infection including Pneumocystis, and start high-dose glucocorticoids"],"a":1,"e":"The 2025 BSR csDMARD guideline replaced the fixed 2-weekly schedule with risk-adapted monitoring: bloods at week 2, then roughly monthly for the first 3-6 months, then 3-monthly in stable patients without risk factors. The interval can be extended after an individual risk assessment, or shortened when there are toxicity risk factors. Extra tests are needed after dose increases, and risk is reassessed annually. Toxicity risk factors that justify closer monitoring: age >65, eGFR <60, alcohol excess, obesity or NAFLD, diabetes, coprescribed hepatotoxins or antifolates, recent dose escalation, combination csDMARDs. Note the different schedules. The EULAR course text quotes an older fixed schedule (2-weekly until stable for 6 weeks, then monthly for 3 months, then 3-monthly). For UK exams, the 2025 BSR risk-adapted schedule in the answer applies. Recheck about 2 weeks after any dose increase. Baseline: FBC, U&E, LFTs, CRP, HBV/HCV/HIV, chest radiograph (or recent imaging), and lung function if there is lung disease.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":92,"s":"Which DMARD can cause agranulocytosis, usually within the first 3 months, and therefore needs blood monitoring?","o":["NSAIDs","TNF inhibitors","Sulfasalazine","Chikungunya virus","Infective endocarditis"],"a":2,"e":"Sulfasalazine can cause neutropenia or agranulocytosis, hepatitis and hypersensitivity, so FBC and LFTs are monitored. It can also cause reversible oligospermia. Other sulfasalazine adverse effects: rash and DRESS, hepatitis, megaloblastic anaemia (folate), haemolysis (G6PD deficiency), orange urine and tears (stains contact lenses), reversible oligospermia, drug-induced lupus (rare). Sulfonamide allergy is a contraindication. Agranulocytosis is idiosyncratic and usually occurs within the first 3 months. Sudden sore throat, fever or mouth ulcers need an urgent FBC. Titrate 500 mg/day up to 2\u20133 g/day over 4 weeks. Monitoring is often reduced to 3-monthly after the first year if stable. Compatible with pregnancy (give with folic acid 5 mg) and breastfeeding (care in premature, jaundiced or G6PD-deficient infants). Best-evidenced csDMARD for peripheral SpA arthritis; not effective for axial disease.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Infection & Immunosuppression","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":93,"s":"A patient with severe RA is to start azathioprine. Which pre-treatment test is essential?","o":["HLA-B*58:01 genotyping","Therapeutic exercise with weight loss and education","Thiopurine methyltransferase (TPMT) activity or genotype","Treat-to-target escalation to achieve remission or low disease activity","Urgent management for suspected septic arthritis while cultures are pending"],"a":2,"e":"Low or absent TPMT activity causes accumulation of thioguanine nucleotides and severe myelotoxicity. Azathioprine is avoided with absent activity and dose-reduced with intermediate activity. NUDT15 variants are also relevant in some populations. Routine FBC monitoring is still required. Other causes of severe myelotoxicity on azathioprine: NUDT15 deficiency (East Asian and Hispanic populations), xanthine oxidase inhibitor co-prescription, ACE inhibitors (modest effect), co-trimoxazole, viral infection, hypersensitivity syndrome (fever, rash, hepatitis or pancreatitis, usually within the first weeks). Normal TPMT does not exclude toxicity. Absent TPMT activity: avoid azathioprine. Intermediate activity: about 50% dose. Normal: target about 2\u20132.5 mg/kg/day. Monitor FBC and LFTs. Azathioprine causes macrocytosis (MCV rise is expected). Long-term risks: non-melanoma skin cancer (sun protection) and lymphoma. Compatible with pregnancy and breastfeeding.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":94,"s":"A patient with severe psoriatic arthritis on ciclosporin has a sustained creatinine rise of 35% above baseline on two tests a week apart, with BP 152/94 mmHg. What is the most appropriate action?","o":["Withhold further rituximab, consider an alternative DMARD and discuss immunoglobulin replacement with immunology if infections persist","Reduce or withhold ciclosporin, manage blood pressure and discuss with the specialist team","Urgent management for suspected septic arthritis while cultures are pending","Stop the TNF inhibitor and seek neurological evaluation","Withhold methotrexate, discuss with the rheumatology team and repeat the tests promptly"],"a":1,"e":"Ciclosporin causes dose-related nephrotoxicity and hypertension. A sustained creatinine rise above 30% from baseline warrants dose reduction or withholding. NSAIDs and many drug interactions increase nephrotoxicity. Other causes of rising creatinine on ciclosporin: NSAIDs, dehydration, interacting drugs (CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels: clarithromycin, azoles, diltiazem, grapefruit; inducers lower them), ACE inhibitors or ARBs, active nephritis in SLE, TMA. Other ciclosporin adverse effects: hypertrichosis, gingival hyperplasia, tremor, hyperkalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hyperuricaemia (gout), raised lipids, PRES. Measure BP and creatinine twice at baseline. Withhold if creatinine rises >30% on two occasions, then restart at a lower dose once it is back within about 15% of baseline. Treat hypertension with a dihydropyridine CCB (amlodipine; avoid diltiazem and verapamil, which raise levels). Tacrolimus and voclosporin have the same class effects; voclosporin needs no level monitoring.","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":95,"s":"Which group of adverse effects is the main concern with intravenous cyclophosphamide, and which pretreatment discussion is particularly important in a patient who may want children?","o":["Peripheral neuropathy, requiring baseline nerve-conduction studies","Irreversible retinal toxicity, requiring a baseline ophthalmology review","Gonadal failure, haemorrhagic cystitis, marrow suppression and later malignancy; gonadotoxicity and fertility preservation must be discussed before treatment","Reversible hair loss and nausea only, with no long-term effect on fertility","Injection-site reactions and a small increase in diverticular perforation risk"],"a":2,"e":"Cyclophosphamide can cause ovarian and testicular failure, related to age and cumulative dose. Fertility preservation and GnRH analogue protection should be discussed without delaying life-saving therapy. Hydration, mesna where indicated, urinalysis, FBC monitoring and Pneumocystis prophylaxis are part of safe use. Cumulative dose relates to bladder and haematological cancer risk. Other causes of haematuria in a patient on cyclophosphamide: active glomerulonephritis (dysmorphic red cells, casts), UTI, bladder carcinoma (late, doserelated; lifelong surveillance), anticoagulation. Other cyclophosphamide toxicities: nausea, alopecia, hyponatraemia (SIADH), infection (PJP, zoster), teratogenicity, pulmonary fibrosis (rare). Ovarian failure risk rises steeply after about age 30 and with cumulative dose above about 10 g. Euro-Lupus (3 g total) carries a much lower risk than NIH high-dose regimens. The leucocyte nadir is at about 10\u201314 days after an IV pulse. Prefer IV pulses (lower cumulative dose) over oral daily dosing. Check FBC before each pulse and at 10\u201314 days, reducing the dose for low nadirs, older age or renal impairment. Use mesna with high doses. Give PJP prophylaxis. Offer oocyte or embryo cryopreservation (or sperm banking) where time allows; GnRH agonists (e.g. goserelin) offer partial ovarian protection. Rituximab is preferred when fertility matters (AAV).","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Infection & Immunosuppression","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":96,"s":"A 66-year-old woman with RA takes hydroxychloroquine. She is prescribed azithromycin and citalopram. Which risk is of greatest concern?","o":["Hepatotoxicity, requiring urgent liver function tests","Hereditary haemochromatosis","Increased bleeding risk, requiring INR monitoring","QT prolongation and ventricular arrhythmia","Acute pancreatitis, requiring serum amylase or lipase"],"a":3,"e":"Hydroxychloroquine prolongs the QT interval, and combination with macrolides and some SSRIs increases the risk of torsades de pointes. An ECG and review of interacting drugs is advisable. Long-term use is also rarely associated with cardiomyopathy. Other QT-prolonging drugs often co-prescribed in rheumatology: macrolides, fluoroquinolones, SSRIs (citalopram, escitalopram), ondansetron, domperidone, tricyclics, methadone, azole antifungals, amiodarone. Electrolyte drivers: low K, Mg or Ca. Other causes of syncope in SLE or RA: congenital heart block (adult), myocarditis, PE. Hydroxychloroquine cardiomyopathy is rare, appears after long exposure, and shows restrictive or hypertrophic physiology and conduction disease (curvilinear bodies on biopsy). Other hydroxychloroquine toxicities: retinopathy, hypoglycaemia, psoriasis flare, skin pigmentation, myopathy or neuromyopathy, and haemolysis (G6PD deficiency, rare). ECG at baseline when interacting drugs or cardiac risk are present. Choose doxycycline or amoxicillin over azithromycin where possible, and sertraline over citalopram. Keep the hydroxychloroquine dose \u00a35 mg/kg/day actual body weight.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Psoriatic Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":97,"s":"A patient with RA on tocilizumab has 3 days of left iliac fossa pain and feels unwell. Temperature is 37.4 \u00b0C and CRP is 4 mg/L. What is the most important consideration?","o":["Reactivation of latent tuberculosis presenting as mesenteric lymphadenopathy","Hepatitis B reactivation presenting with jaundice and deranged liver enzymes","Methotrexate-induced hepatotoxicity requiring immediate cessation","A flare of the underlying rheumatoid arthritis requiring DMARD escalation","IL-6 blockade suppresses CRP and fever, so diverticulitis or bowel perforation must be actively excluded with imaging"],"a":4,"e":"IL-6 inhibitors blunt the acute-phase response and fever, masking infection. Tocilizumab is associated with lower gastrointestinal perforation, especially with diverticular disease or concomitant glucocorticoids or NSAIDs. A low threshold for CT is needed. Acute abdomen on tocilizumab: diverticulitis or perforation (the main concern), appendicitis, cholecystitis, pancreatitis, mesenteric ischaemia (vasculitis), steroid-associated peptic perforation, opportunistic colitis (CMV). JAK inhibitors also carry a GI perforation signal (smaller). Risk factors: history of diverticulitis, concomitant glucocorticoids or NSAIDs, older age. Peritonism may be absent and fever, CRP and neutrophilia blunted; ESR may also be falsely low. Do an early CT abdomen and pelvis, involve surgeons early and withhold tocilizumab. Avoid IL-6R inhibitors in patients with prior diverticulitis or perforation.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Vasculitis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":98,"s":"A patient on azathioprine is to start a xanthine oxidase inhibitor (allopurinol or febuxostat) for gout. What is the main concern, and how would toxicity present?","o":["Allopurinol hypersensitivity syndrome, presenting with fever, rash and eosinophilia within weeks","Reduced azathioprine efficacy from induction of hepatic drug metabolism","Enhanced urate-lowering effect of allopurinol, requiring a dose reduction of allopurinol","Additive nephrotoxicity, requiring renal function monitoring","Xanthine oxidase inhibition raises 6-mercaptopurine levels, causing severe myelosuppression, which can present within weeks as fever, sore throat and mouth ulcers from neutropenia"],"a":4,"e":"Xanthine oxidase metabolises 6-mercaptopurine. Allopurinol or febuxostat with azathioprine causes toxic accumulation and potentially fatal marrow suppression; without major azathioprine dose reduction, profound neutropenia and sepsis can develop within weeks. If a patient presents this way, check the FBC urgently, stop azathioprine and treat neutropenic sepsis as an emergency. If the combination is unavoidable, azathioprine is reduced to about a quarter of the dose with close monitoring, or switched to a non-thiopurine agent. Alternatives that avoid the interaction: switch azathioprine to mycophenolate or methotrexate, or use a uricosuric (benzbromarone; probenecid if eGFR is adequate). Mercaptopurine has the same interaction. Other interactions of azathioprine: warfarin (reduced INR effect) and co-trimoxazole (additive marrow toxicity). The 6-thioguanine nucleotide pathway is TPMT-dependent; xanthine oxidase provides the alternative inactivation route. Blocking it shunts metabolism into active thioguanines. In gastroenterology, the combination is sometimes used deliberately (allopurinol 100 mg with azathioprine at 25\u2013 33% dose) under metabolite monitoring. In rheumatology, avoid it unless specialist-supervised with weekly FBC initially.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":99,"s":"A patient on a biologic DMARD develops sepsis requiring IV antibiotics. What is the most appropriate immediate DMARD plan?","o":["Continue the biologic unchanged, since sepsis does not alter infection risk on immunosuppression","Switch immediately to a biologic with a different mechanism of action","Increase the biologic dose to compensate for reduced efficacy during infection","Stop all treatment permanently, including hydroxychloroquine and glucocorticoids","Withhold the biologic and other non-essential immunosuppression until the infection is treated and the patient has recovered"],"a":4,"e":"Serious infection requires temporary withholding of biologic or targeted therapy, coordinated with the treating team. The 2025 BSR csDMARD guideline also advises pausing csDMARDs during severe infection needing admission or IV antibiotics. Long-term glucocorticoids must not be stopped abruptly because of adrenal suppression; sick-day dose increases may be needed. Deciding which drugs to pause in severe infection: biologics, JAK inhibitors and csDMARDs (2025 BSR) are paused. Hydroxychloroquine can usually continue. Glucocorticoids continue, with stress dosing. Restart once antibiotics are complete and the patient is clinically recovered (often about 1\u20132 weeks after resolution). Restart sooner for organ-threatening disease, with specialist discussion. Infection risk is highest in the first 6\u201312 months of a new advanced therapy and with concomitant GC above about 7.5\u201310 mg/day. Fever, CRP and neutrophilia may be blunted by IL-6 or JAK inhibition and by GC. Septic patients on long-term GC (\u20215 mg for \u20214 weeks): hydrocortisone 50 mg every 6 hours IV or 200 mg/24 h by infusion. Review vaccination and prophylaxis after recovery.","c":"Infection & Immunosuppression","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":100,"s":"A patient taking prednisolone 10 mg daily for 18 months stops it abruptly and presents with vomiting, hypotension and weakness. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Opioid withdrawal syndrome","Myxoedema (hypothyroid) crisis","Adrenal crisis due to hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis suppression","Acute pancreatitis secondary to glucocorticoid-induced hypertriglyceridaemia","Addisonian crisis from autoimmune primary adrenal failure, unrelated to the steroid course"],"a":2,"e":"Prolonged glucocorticoid use suppresses the HPA axis. Adrenal crisis requires immediate parenteral hydrocortisone and fluids. Patients on long-term steroids need a steroid card, sick-day rules and gradual tapering. Differential of vomiting, hypotension and weakness in a patient who has stopped steroids: adrenal crisis; sepsis (which may precipitate crisis); disease flare (e.g. PMR or GCA relapse); GC withdrawal syndrome (arthralgia, myalgia, fatigue, mood change with normal markers and BP); hypercalcaemia; hyponatraemia from other causes; primary adrenal failure (Addison's, which features hyperkalaemia and pigmentation). In GC-induced (secondary or tertiary) adrenal insufficiency the mineralocorticoid axis is preserved, so hyperkalaemia is unusual. Hyponatraemia and hypoglycaemia occur. Any route can suppress the HPA axis (oral, IA, inhaled, topical, nasal), and the risk is highest with \u20215 mg prednisolone for \u20214 weeks, evening dosing, dexamethasone and CYP3A4 inhibitors. Crisis: hydrocortisone 100 mg IV or IM immediately, then 200 mg/24 h (infusion or 50 mg every 6 hours), IV saline and glucose. Do not wait for cortisol results; take a sample before treatment only if it causes no delay. Then give a steroid emergency card, sick-day rules and an IM hydrocortisone kit. Testing HPA recovery: a 9 am cortisol \u202124 hours after the last prednisolone dose, once on 4\u20135 mg/day. >300 nmol/L: stop. 150\u2013300: continue and recheck. <150: likely adrenal insufficiency; endocrine referral. Use a short Synacthen test for indeterminate results. Recovery can take 6\u201312 months, and sick-day rules apply for up to a year after stopping.","c":"Drug Monitoring & Safety","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":101,"s":"What are the key principles of long-term glucocorticoid use in inflammatory rheumatic disease?","o":["Discontinue permanently after 3 months regardless of disease activity, to avoid cumulative toxicity","Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest time and taper gradually after prolonged use","Use the maximum tolerated dose until remission, then stop abruptly without tapering","Only influenza vaccination is relevant during glucocorticoid therapy","Switch all patients to alternate-day dosing from the outset to reduce adrenal suppression"],"a":1,"e":"Glucocorticoids carry dose-and duration-dependent harms including infection, diabetes, hypertension, cataract, osteoporosis and adrenal suppression. Exposure should be minimised and tapered carefully. GC toxicity by system: bone (osteoporosis, osteonecrosis), muscle (steroid myopathy with normal CK and type II fibre atrophy), metabolic (diabetes peaking in the afternoon, weight gain, dyslipidaemia), CV (hypertension, MI, AF, HF), eye (posterior subcapsular cataract, glaucoma, central serous chorioretinopathy), skin (thinning, purpura, striae, poor healing), neuropsychiatric (insomnia, mania, psychosis, often at >40 mg), infection (zoster, PJP, TB, Strongyloides hyperinfection), GI (ulcer with NSAIDs, diverticular perforation), growth suppression in children. \"Low dose is not no risk\": even \u00a35 mg/day long term modestly raises the risk of infection, fracture, cataract and CV events. In myositis, falling strength with a normal CK suggests steroid myopathy, while rising strength with a normal CK suggests disease control. Plan the taper at the start. Rough taper: reduce by 5\u201310 mg every 1\u20132 weeks above 20 mg, by 2.5 mg every 2\u20134 weeks from 20 to 10 mg, and by 1 mg every 4 weeks below 10 mg; slow down near the physiological dose (about 5 mg). Give as a single morning dose. Bone protection, BP and glucose checks (pre-evening-meal capillary glucose), an annual eye check, vaccination and a steroid card. Use GC-sparing agents early. Before long-term GC: weight, BP, HbA1c, lipids, bone profile, FRAX \u00b1 DXA, eye and mental-health history, HBV, HCV, HIV and IGRA where relevant, and Strongyloides serology (or empirical ivermectin) if from an endemic area. Calcium about 1000\u20131200 mg/day total intake (not routine 1\u20132 g supplements) and vitamin D 800\u20131000 IU/day. A PPI is needed only if combined with NSAIDs, antiplatelets or anticoagulants, or at high GI risk.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Metabolic Bone Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":102,"s":"A patient with severe vasculitis is starting high-dose prednisolone with cyclophosphamide. Which prophylaxis is most appropriate, and which infection should you suspect if a patient on this regimen without prophylaxis develops breathlessness, exertional hypoxia and diffuse ground-glass change?","o":["Low-dose colchicine prophylaxis during initiation and titration","High-dose glucocorticoids (including pulses) with cyclophosphamide","Urgent assessment for GCA, with high-dose glucocorticoids if it is suspected","Co-trimoxazole prophylaxis against Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, which is the infection to suspect","Stop methotrexate, exclude infection including Pneumocystis, and start high-dose glucocorticoids"],"a":3,"e":"Pneumocystis prophylaxis should be considered when prednisolone above about 15\u201330 mg/day is given for more than 2\u20134 weeks, particularly with cyclophosphamide or rituximab. Co-trimoxazole is first-line; check for G6PD deficiency and monitor counts, renal function and potassium. PJP typically causes subacute dyspnoea, desaturation on exertion and diffuse ground-glass change in immunosuppressed patients. It needs bronchoscopic or inducedsputum sampling and prompt high-dose co-trimoxazole, with adjunctive steroids if hypoxic. Diffuse ground-glass change in an immunosuppressed patient: PJP; CMV pneumonitis; COVID-19 or influenza; methotrexate or other drug pneumonitis; alveolar haemorrhage (falling Hb, rising DLCO, haemoptysis may be absent); pulmonary oedema; acute exacerbation of ILD; MDA5-associated rapidly progressive ILD. PJP clues: lymphopenia (CD4 <200), raised LDH, raised serum b-D-glucan, normal or near-normal chest radiograph early, marked exertional desaturation, pneumothorax. Non- HIV PJP is more fulminant and carries higher mortality than HIV-associated PJP. Prophylaxis: co-trimoxazole 480 mg daily or 960 mg three times weekly. Alternatives are atovaquone, dapsone (after a G6PD check) or nebulised pentamidine. Treatment: cotrimoxazole 15\u201320 mg/kg/day of the trimethoprim component in divided doses for 21 days, plus prednisolone if PaO2 <9.3 kPa. Never combine co-trimoxazole prophylaxis with methotrexate without considering the interaction. Many centres use atovaquone in patients on methotrexate.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":103,"s":"A patient with RA has had four courses of rituximab over 3 years and now has recurrent chest infections. Serum IgG is 3.1 g/L. What is the most appropriate action?","o":["Withhold methotrexate, discuss with the rheumatology team and repeat the tests promptly","Reduce or withhold ciclosporin, manage blood pressure and discuss with the specialist team","Prior infection history, glucocorticoid exposure, vaccination status, comorbidities and the infection profile of each option","Withhold further rituximab, consider an alternative DMARD and discuss immunoglobulin replacement with immunology if infections persist","Withhold the biologic and other non-essential immunosuppression until the infection is treated and the patient has recovered"],"a":3,"e":"Secondary hypogammaglobulinaemia is a recognised complication of repeated rituximab and increases serious infection risk. Immunoglobulins should be checked before each cycle. Other causes of low IgG in a rheumatology patient: prior cyclophosphamide, mycophenolate or high-dose GC; nephrotic syndrome or protein-losing enteropathy (IgG loss); CVID unmasked; CLL, lymphoma or myeloma (check paraprotein and free light chains); Good's syndrome (thymoma). Risk factors for rituximab hypogammaglobulinaemia: low baseline IgG, multiple courses, high cumulative dose, prior cyclophosphamide, concomitant GC, older age. Low IgM is common and less important than low IgG. Late-onset neutropenia occurs in about 5\u201310% at 1\u20136 months. Check IgG, IgA and IgM before each cycle. If IgG is low with recurrent or severe infections: stop or space rituximab, test specific antibody responses (pneumococcal or tetanus), give prophylactic antibiotics, and give immunoglobulin replacement (IVIG or SCIG) via immunology if infections persist. Switch to a non-B-cell drug.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":104,"s":"Before starting rituximab, screening shows HBsAg negative, anti-HBc positive, anti-HBs negative and undetectable HBV DNA. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Take part in cervical screening, with consideration of more frequent screening and HPV vaccination because of increased risk","Anti-histone","Start antiviral prophylaxis (such as entecavir or tenofovir) with hepatology input, then proceed","Hepatitis B and C serology, HIV testing, latent TB screening, and baseline FBC, renal and liver function","Cross-sectional vascular imaging such as PET-CT, CTA or MRA"],"a":2,"e":"Resolved (occult) HBV carries a meaningful reactivation risk with B-cell depletion, which can cause fulminant hepatitis. Antiviral prophylaxis is advised with rituximab; with most other DMARDs, HBV DNA monitoring is usually sufficient. HBsAg-positive patients need antiviral therapy with almost all immunosuppression. HBV serology interpretation: | HBsAg | anti-HBc | anti-HBs | Meaning | Action before rituximab | |---|---|---|---|---| | + | + | - | Chronic infection | Antiviral therapy (hepatology) for almost all immunosuppression | | - | + | - or + | Resolved / occult | Prophylaxis (entecavir or tenofovir) with rituximab or high-risk regimens; HBV DNA monitoring for most others | | - | - | + | Vaccinated | Nothing further | | - | - | - | Susceptible | Vaccinate | Reactivation can occur up to 12 months or more after the last rituximab dose. Presentation is a hepatitis flare and can be fulminant (fatal liver failure). HBV DNA rises weeks before ALT. Prophylaxis is usually continued for at least 12\u201318 months after the last rituximab dose. Screen everyone before biologics, JAK inhibitors, rituximab or high-dose GC. HBsAg-positive patients on \u202120 mg/day prednisolone for >4 weeks also need prophylaxis.","c":"Infection & Immunosuppression","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":105,"s":"Before starting a TNF inhibitor, a 40-year-old woman has a positive interferon-gamma release assay. Chest radiograph is normal and she has no symptoms. What is the most appropriate plan?","o":["ASDAS below 1.3 is inactive disease; a fall of 1.1 or more is a clinically important improvement, so a fall of","Treat latent TB (for example 3 months of isoniazid with rifampicin), starting the TNF inhibitor after at least 1 month if urgent, or on completion","Duodenal biopsy for PAS staining and T. whipplei PCR","Assess and treat latent tuberculosis according to local protocol","Consider gradual tapering of the TNF inhibitor rather than stopping it"],"a":1,"e":"Latent TB should be treated before TNF inhibition. When biologic therapy is urgent, it can usually start after at least a month of latent TB treatment. Etanercept carries a lower but not negligible reactivation risk and does not remove the need for treatment. TB screening pitfalls: false-negative IGRA on high-dose GC or with lymphopenia (indeterminate results); a positive IGRA from past treated TB; recent contact with a negative IGRA (repeat test). Active TB must be excluded first (symptoms, chest radiograph or CT, sputum) before treating latent infection. Reactivation risk ranks monoclonal TNFi (infliximab, adalimumab) above etanercept, which is above non-TNF biologics (abatacept, tocilizumab and rituximab carry low risk, but screening is still standard). Extrapulmonary and disseminated TB is common on TNFi. Latent TB regimens: 3 months of isoniazid plus rifampicin (with pyridoxine), or 6 months of isoniazid. Rifampicin interacts with GC (double the dose), tofacitinib and warfarin. Monitor LFTs, particularly with methotrexate.","c":"Infection & Immunosuppression","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":106,"s":"Which set of investigations is required before starting a biologic DMARD?","o":["HbA1c, lipid profile and 25-hydroxyvitamin D only","Baseline echocardiography in every patient, regardless of cardiac history","Serum ACE level and chest radiograph to screen for sarcoidosis","Serum free light chains and bone marrow biopsy in every patient","Hepatitis B and C serology, HIV testing, latent TB screening, and baseline FBC, renal and liver function"],"a":4,"e":"Pre-biologic assessment includes viral hepatitis and HIV serology, TB risk assessment with IGRA and chest imaging as indicated, baseline bloods (often including immunoglobulins), vaccination review and pregnancy considerations. Full pre-biologic checklist: TB (IGRA \u00b1 chest radiograph); HBsAg, anti-HBc and HCV antibodies; HIV; VZV IgG if no history; FBC, U&E, LFTs; immunoglobulins (rituximab); lipids (IL-6R, JAKi); pregnancy plans and contraception; cancer history (recent malignancy, skin checks); demyelination and NYHA III\u2013IV heart failure (avoid TNFi); diverticulitis (caution with IL-6R and JAKi); VTE and CV risk (JAKi); vaccinations up to date (non-live ideally \u20212 weeks before; live vaccines \u20214 weeks before starting). VZV serology is advised if there is no history of chickenpox or shingles. Lipids are checked for IL-6R and JAK inhibitors; immunoglobulins before rituximab.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":107,"s":"A patient is about to start, or is already on, rituximab, and her vaccinations (including COVID-19) are not up to date. How should vaccination be timed?","o":["Give indicated non-live vaccines at least 2 weeks before the first infusion where feasible; for patients already on rituximab, time vaccines late in the dosing cycle, before the next infusion, recognising that antibody","Delay all vaccination until at least 12 months after stopping rituximab, regardless of urgency","Give a live vaccine concurrently with the rituximab infusion","Do not vaccinate at all, since antibody responses will be blunted regardless of timing","Vaccinate only once B-cell depletion has fully resolved, with no earlier option available"],"a":0,"e":"responses may be blunted B-cell depletion impairs humoral vaccine responses for at least 6 months. Vaccines should be given beforehand where feasible. COVID-19 vaccines are non-live and recommended; anti-CD20 therapy blunts humoral responses, so vaccination is best timed late in the dosing cycle, and T-cell responses are relatively preserved. Boosters follow national schedules. Non-live vaccines are safe during immunosuppression; live vaccines are generally avoided on significant immunosuppression. For patients on methotrexate, current BSR guidance suggests withholding it for up to 2 weeks after influenza or COVID-19 vaccination if disease activity allows. Response hierarchy: rituximab and abatacept impair responses most, then methotrexate (influenza) and highdose GC. TNFi, IL-6R and IL-17i have little effect. Some JAKi (e.g. tofacitinib) modestly reduce pneumococcal responses. Where possible, vaccinate at least 6 months after the last rituximab dose and at least 2\u20134 weeks before the next one. Consider checking B-cell (CD19) repopulation. Recommend vaccinating household members. Offer early antivirals (and, where available, monoclonal antibodies) for COVID-19 in Bcell-depleted patients.","c":"Infection & Immunosuppression","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":108,"s":"A 64-year-old woman with RA is starting upadacitinib. She has never had shingles vaccination. Which is most appropriate?","o":["Live attenuated (Zostavax) zoster vaccine, given before starting upadacitinib","No vaccination is required, since JAK inhibitors do not increase zoster risk","Pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine alone; zoster vaccination is not indicated","Recombinant adjuvanted (non-live) zoster vaccine, two doses","Defer all vaccination until upadacitinib is eventually stopped"],"a":3,"e":"JAK inhibitors increase herpes zoster risk. The non-live recombinant vaccine is safe in immunosuppressed patients; the live vaccine should be avoided once significant immunosuppression has started. Zoster risk factors in rheumatology: JAK inhibitors (highest; dose-dependent, especially in Asian populations), anifrolumab, high-dose GC, cyclophosphamide, SLE itself, age >50. Disseminated or multidermatomal zoster needs IV aciclovir and a pause in immunosuppression. Recombinant zoster vaccine (Shingrix): two doses 2\u20136 months apart (0 and 1\u20132 months in the immunocompromised), ideally before starting a JAKi but safe during therapy. The live zoster vaccine (Zostavax) is contraindicated on significant immunosuppression and has now been withdrawn in the UK.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":109,"s":"In which patients are live attenuated vaccines generally contraindicated?","o":["Prednisolone 20 mg/day or more for at least 2 weeks, biologic DMARDs or combination immunosuppression","Hydroxychloroquine monotherapy at a standard dose","Low-dose methotrexate below 10 mg weekly","Sulfasalazine monotherapy for peripheral arthritis","Short courses of NSAIDs for symptom control"],"a":0,"e":"Live vaccines (MMR, varicella, yellow fever, oral typhoid, live zoster) carry a risk of vaccine-derived infection under significant immunosuppression. They need specialist risk assessment and timing, ideally before immunosuppression starts. Non-live vaccines are safe. Live vaccines: MMR, varicella, live zoster, yellow fever, BCG, oral typhoid, oral polio, intranasal influenza (LAIV), rotavirus (infants). Give live vaccines \u20214 weeks before starting immunosuppression. After stopping, wait \u20211 month after high-dose GC (up to 3 months after pulses or combined immunosuppression), \u20213 months for most csDMARDs and biologics, and 6\u201312 months after rituximab (per specialist guidance). Low-dose GC (<20 mg/day, or short courses <2 weeks), hydroxychloroquine and sulfasalazine alone are not usually a contraindication. Some guidelines allow live vaccines on low-dose methotrexate (\u00a30.4 mg/kg/week, \u00a325 mg) and azathioprine (\u00a33 mg/kg/day) after specialist risk assessment. For yellow fever, travellers on significant immunosuppression need an exemption certificate and protective advice.","c":"Infection & Immunosuppression","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":110,"s":"Which vaccines are particularly important for adults with inflammatory rheumatic disease on immunosuppression?","o":["Oral typhoid and yellow fever as routine vaccinations for every patient","BCG vaccination in adults with no prior tuberculosis exposure","Live attenuated intranasal influenza vaccine in preference to the inactivated vaccine","MMR booster vaccination regardless of immunosuppression status","Influenza, COVID-19, pneumococcal, recombinant zoster where eligible, and hepatitis B where indicated"],"a":4,"e":"Vaccination status should be reviewed at least annually. Non-live vaccines are generally safe and ideally given during quiescent disease and before immunosuppression is escalated. HPV vaccination should be considered where eligible. Practical schedule: annual influenza (inactivated); pneumococcal conjugate per current national schedule (e.g. PCV20 single dose, or PCV then PPV23); COVID-19 boosters; recombinant zoster (eligible age or on JAKi); hepatitis B if at risk or before rituximab in susceptible people; HPV (up to 45 in many schedules for immunosuppressed SLE); RSV for eligible older adults; tetanus as indicated. Vaccinate in quiescent disease and before escalation, but do not delay vaccination for this. Non-live vaccines are safe at any GC dose; very high doses may blunt responses. Ideally vaccinate before starting GC or when the dose is lower, but never delay.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":111,"s":"The child of a patient receiving a biologic is due routine childhood vaccines, including live vaccines. What is the best advice?","o":["Delay all of the child's vaccines, including non-live vaccines, until the parent's biologic is stopped","Household contacts should be fully vaccinated; with live vaccines, practical hygiene precautions apply","The child should avoid any contact with the patient after live vaccines, indefinitely","Household contacts should avoid vaccination entirely to minimise viral shedding near the patient","Only the child's influenza vaccine needs to be timed around the parent's biologic doses"],"a":1,"e":"(for example after rotavirus vaccine) Immunising household contacts protects the immunosuppressed patient. Live vaccines are not contraindicated for contacts, but precautions apply, such as careful hand hygiene and avoiding nappy changing for about 2 weeks after rotavirus vaccine. Contacts can receive MMR, varicella (if a vaccine rash develops, avoid close contact until the lesions crust) and rotavirus (careful hand hygiene; the immunosuppressed person should ideally avoid nappy changing for about 2 weeks). Live intranasal influenza is avoided only in contacts of very severely immunosuppressed people (e.g. isolated transplant recipients). Oral polio is no longer used in the UK because the vaccine virus can be shed and transmitted. Infants exposed in utero to TNFi (other than certolizumab) in the third trimester: defer live vaccines (rotavirus, BCG) until 6 months of age. Breastfeeding on biologics does not alter the infant schedule.","c":"Paediatric Rheumatology","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":112,"s":"Which immune cells are the principal source of rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies in rheumatoid arthritis?","o":["Neutrophils and mast cells","B lymphocytes and plasma cells","Dendritic cells and macrophages","CD8-positive cytotoxic T lymphocytes","Synovial fibroblasts"],"a":1,"e":"lymphocytes and plasma cells Autoantibodies are produced by autoreactive B cells and plasma cells, which provides the rationale for B-cell depletion with rituximab, particularly in seropositive disease. Synovial plasma cells make RF and ACPA locally, forming immune complexes that activate complement and macrophages. Ectopic lymphoid follicles are seen in the lympho-myeloid pathotype. ACPA can bind osteoclast precursors directly and drive bone loss before arthritis. Pathogenesis cascade: loss of tolerance to citrullinated proteins at mucosal sites (lung, gums, gut), then epitope spreading, then a cytokine surge (TNF, IL-6, GM-CSF), then synovitis. RANKL drives osteoclastic erosion and MMPs drive cartilage loss. Synovial fibroblasts (THY1+ sublining, PRG4+ lining) are not targeted by current drugs.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":113,"s":"What are the diagnostic and prognostic characteristics of anti-CCP (ACPA) antibodies, and which result best predicts progression to RA in a patient with inflammatory arthralgia but no synovitis?","o":["ACPA sensitivity exceeds 95%, so a negative result reliably excludes RA","ACPA titre tracks current swollen-joint count and can substitute for clinical examination","ACPA is an activity marker that falls reliably with successful treat-to-target therapy","ACPA specificity exceeds 95% and positivity is associated with erosive disease; high-titre ACPA","ACPA and rheumatoid factor are interchangeable and provide no additional prognostic information"],"a":3,"e":"(especially with RF) best predicts progression from arthralgia to RA ACPA has specificity of about 95% and sensitivity of about 60\u201375%. It may precede clinical arthritis by years and, especially at high titre with RF, predicts more aggressive erosive disease. In people with arthralgia, high-titre ACPA with RF co-positivity predicts progression to RA, and such patients merit early specialist review. ACPA-positive without RA: other inflammatory disease (PsA in 5\u201310%, antisynthetase syndrome with deforming arthritis, SLE \"rhupus\", Sj\u00f6gren's), active TB, chronic lung disease in smokers, and healthy relatives. RF-positive with ACPA-negative status widens the differential (CHRONIC list). \"Clinically suspect arthralgia\" predicts RA: onset <1 year, MCP symptoms, morning stiffness \u202160 minutes, worst in the early morning, first-degree relative with RA, inability to make a fist, positive squeeze test. MRI or ultrasound tenosynovitis adds risk. At-risk (ACPA+ arthralgia): explain the risk honestly, stop smoking, treat periodontal disease, monitor closely and start DMARDs as soon as arthritis appears. Preventive drugs are not standard; offer trials. Abatacept for 12 months delayed RA while given; one dose of rituximab delayed onset by about 12 months; methotrexate plus GC improved symptoms and MRI inflammation without preventing RA; hydroxychloroquine and atorvastatin had no effect. Low-positive means >ULN to \u00a33\u00d7 ULN (2 points); high-positive means >3\u00d7 ULN (3 points). Anti-CarP and anti-acetylated protein antibodies may identify some seronegative RA but are research tools.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":114,"s":"A 62-year-old man with hand arthralgia has a positive rheumatoid factor, chronic hepatitis C and no synovitis on examination or ultrasound. How should the RF result be interpreted?","o":["Hepatitis C should be treated with a methotrexate-based DMARD combination before considering antivirals","A positive RF here mandates urgent renal biopsy to exclude cryoglobulinaemic glomerulonephritis","Hydroxychloroquine should be started immediately to prevent progression to erosive arthritis","RF can occur in chronic infection, other autoimmune disease and older adults, and is not diagnostic without a compatible clinical picture","The RF titre should be rechecked only after stopping all antiviral therapy"],"a":3,"e":"RF lacks specificity. It occurs in hepatitis C, cryoglobulinaemia, Sj\u00f6gren's, chronic infections and up to 10-15% of healthy older people. Diagnosis requires clinical synovitis and exclusion of mimics. Causes of a positive RF: hepatitis C (with or without mixed cryoglobulinaemia), Sj\u00f6gren's (very common), SLE, SSc, MCTD, sarcoidosis, endocarditis, TB, syphilis, leprosy, HIV, PBC, chronic liver disease, lymphoma and other B-cell disorders, and healthy older people (up to 10\u201325%). In HCV, arthralgia is common; arthritis is less common and usually non-erosive. HCV-associated arthritis: intermittent, symmetrical smalljoint or monoarticular, non-erosive, ACPA-negative. Look for purpura, neuropathy and low C4 (cryoglobulinaemia). Do not start DMARDs for RF alone. Treat HCV with directacting antivirals. Hydroxychloroquine or low-dose GC can be used for symptoms if needed; avoid hepatotoxic DMARDs until the liver has been assessed.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":115,"s":"A 39-year-old woman has 8 weeks of symmetrical MCP and PIP joint swelling with prolonged morning stiffness. RF and anti-CCP are negative and CRP is normal. What is the best interpretation?","o":["Repeat RF and anti-CCP in three months and treat only if either becomes positive","Diagnose fibromyalgia, since objective markers of inflammation are normal","Arrange nerve conduction studies as the priority investigation","Seronegative RA remains possible; diagnosis rests on clinical synovitis and exclusion of alternatives","Exclude RA entirely because CRP is normal"],"a":3,"e":"Serology and acute-phase reactants support probability and prognosis, but negative tests do not exclude RA. Persistent synovitis warrants early specialist assessment and DMARD treatment. Seronegative symmetrical polyarthritis: seronegative RA, PsA without skin disease (check scalp, umbilicus and natal cleft; ask about family history), viral arthritis (parvovirus B19, hepatitis B prodrome, rubella or vaccine, chikungunya), SLE or other CTD (ANA, ENA), polyarticular gout or CPPD (pseudo-RA), haemochromatosis (2nd and 3rd MCP), sarcoid, hypothyroid arthropathy, RS3PE, paraneoplastic arthritis, checkpoint-inhibitor arthritis, erosive hand OA, enteropathic arthritis. Seronegative patients reach 6/10 on the 2010 criteria only with >10 joints (at least one small) plus raised APR or \u20216 weeks of symptoms, so seronegative RA is under-classified early. Ultrasound or MRI synovitis and tenosynovitis support the diagnosis. Investigations: ANA and ENA, urate and crystal analysis where possible, parvovirus IgM, HBV, HCV and HIV, ferritin and transferrin saturation, TFTs, CK, ultrasound of hands and feet, radiographs of hands, feet and chest. Treat-totarget with methotrexate as in seropositive disease. Seronegative RA has slightly less radiographic progression but similar symptom burden.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":116,"s":"What is the role of the 2010 ACR/EULAR RA classification criteria in clinical practice, and how would you score a woman with 4 weeks of swelling of two MCP joints and one PIP joint, low-positive RF, negative anti-CCP and a raised CRP?","o":["They are classification criteria and do not replace clinical diagnosis; she scores 5 points and does not yet meet the criteria","6 points; she meets the classification criteria","4 points; she does not meet the classification criteria","7 points; she meets the classification criteria","They are diagnostic criteria that confirm RA once met, and she does not meet them"],"a":0,"e":"A score of 6/10 or more classifies RA in a patient with at least one joint with definite synovitis not better explained by another disease. Typical erosive disease can also be classified directly. Scoring here: 1\u20133 small joints = 2; lowpositive RF = 2; abnormal CRP = 1; symptoms under 6 weeks = 0, giving a total of 5. Classification criteria are designed for research cohorts; early treatment can still proceed on clinical grounds. Common misclassification risks: PsA, SLE, viral arthritis, polyarticular CPPD and gout, PMR with peripheral synovitis. Domain Category Points 1 large / 2\u201310 large / 1\u20133 small / 4\u2013 0 / 1 / 2 / Joints 10 small / >10 (\u20211 small) 3 / 5 negative / low-positive (\u00a33\u00d7 ULN) / 0 / 2 / 3 Serology high-positive (>3\u00d7 ULN) normal / abnormal CRP or ESR 0 / 1 APR <6 weeks / \u20216 weeks 0 / 1 Duration DIP, 1st MTP and 1st CMC joints are excluded (OA and gout sites). Direct classification applies with typical erosions in \u20213 separate joints, or in long-standing inactive disease that previously met the criteria.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Crystal Arthropathy"]},{"id":117,"s":"Which plain radiographic finding in a patient with inflammatory arthritis points away from RA and towards calcium pyrophosphate deposition?","o":["Chondrocalcinosis","Periarticular osteopenia with uniform joint-space narrowing","Central 'gull-wing' erosions with osteophytes","Punched-out erosions with overhanging sclerotic edges","Marginal erosions with new bone formation and bony ankylosis"],"a":0,"e":"Chondrocalcinosis suggests calcium pyrophosphate deposition. RA causes periarticular osteopenia, symmetrical uniform joint space narrowing and marginal erosions. Radiographic patterns: | Feature | RA | OA | PsA | Gout | CPPD | |---|---|---|---|---|---| | Distribution | symmetrical MCP, PIP, wrist, MTP | DIP, PIP, 1st CMC | DIP, ray pattern, asymmetric | 1st MTP, asymmetric | wrist (TFCC), knee, 2nd/3rd MCP | | Erosions | marginal (\"bare area\") | central (erosive OA: gull-wing) | marginal with new bone, pencil-incup | punched-out with overhanging edges | subchondral cysts | | Bone density | periarticular osteopenia | preserved | preserved | preserved | preserved | | Other | uniform joint space narrowing | osteophytes, sclerosis | periostitis, ankylosis, acro-osteolysis | tophi, preserved joint space until late | chondrocalcinosis, hook osteophytes (haemochromatosis) | Chondrocalcinosis sites: knee menisci, TFCC of the wrist, symphysis pubis, hip labrum. CPPD can coexist with RA, and RA erosions are less common in patients with chondrocalcinosis.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy"]},{"id":118,"s":"What does a positive power Doppler signal on ultrasound of an MCP joint indicate?","o":["Avascular tissue with no blood flow, consistent with chronic fibrosis","Calcium pyrophosphate crystals","Osteophyte formation at the joint margin","Tendon-sheath calcification consistent with calcific tendinitis","Active synovial inflammation with increased vascularity"],"a":4,"e":"Power Doppler detects increased synovial blood flow in active synovitis. It is more sensitive than clinical examination for subclinical synovitis, though operator-dependent. Doppler signal without inflammatory arthritis: OA with inflammatory flare, crystal arthritis, infection, post-injection or post-trauma, technical artefact (high gain, low PRF), normal nutrient vessels. Grey-scale synovial hypertrophy without Doppler signal can be inactive or fibrotic synovium. OMERACT semi-quantitative grading runs 0\u20133 for greyscale and Doppler. Doppler persisting in clinical remission predicts flare and radiographic progression. Ultrasound is several times more sensitive than X-ray for early erosions and detects tenosynovitis (an early RA marker; ECU tenosynovitis is common). Uses: confirming synovitis when examination is equivocal; distinguishing inflammatory from non-inflammatory pain (see entry on non-inflammatory pain); guiding injections. Routine ultrasound-guided treat-to-target has not improved outcomes over clinical T2T in trials (ARCTIC, TaSER).","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":119,"s":"A 52-year-old woman has 10 weeks of symmetrical small-joint polyarthritis, strongly positive anti-CCP and DAS28-CRP 5.4. What is the most appropriate initial treatment, and why does timing matter (the \"window of opportunity\")?","o":["Methotrexate with short-term glucocorticoid bridging, started as soon as RA is diagnosed, ideally within","Methotrexate; oral glucocorticoids are not routine and, if needed, are used only at low dose for short periods","Methotrexate monotherapy, with biologic therapy prioritised ahead of any glucocorticoid bridging","Sulfasalazine alone, continued indefinitely regardless of response at 3 months","High-dose glucocorticoids including IV methylprednisolone pulses, with IVIG"],"a":0,"e":"3 months of symptom onset A csDMARD should start as soon as RA is diagnosed, with methotrexate as the anchor drug, ideally with short-term glucocorticoids tapered and stopped as quickly as feasible. Biologics are not first-line in DMARD-naive patients. The window of opportunity is the first few months after symptom onset, when prompt DMARD treatment most improves the chance of remission and limits damage; early referral and DMARD initiation, ideally within 3 months, improve long-term outcomes. Very early RA (<12 weeks) achieves DAS28 remission in about 50%, versus about 15% in late-early RA. The biggest gain comes from starting any DMARD early rather than from the specific drug. Up to 75% of untreated patients develop erosions within 2 years. Bridging options: an oral prednisolone taper (e.g. 10\u201320 mg, then taper over 8\u201312 weeks), IM methylprednisolone 80\u2013120 mg, intra-articular injections, or step-down regimens (COBRA 60 fi 7.5 mg over 6 weeks; COBRA-light 30 mg). Stop within about 3 months. Low-dose GC reduces radiographic progression in early RA (COBRA, CAMERA-II, BeSt). Leflunomide or sulfasalazine if methotrexate is contraindicated. Methotrexate monotherapy is preferred over initial triple therapy. Use NSAIDs only for symptoms. NICE and EULAR targets: refer within 6 weeks of symptom onset (even with normal CRP and RF), early arthritis clinic within 3 weeks of referral. Avoid primary-care steroids before assessment because they mask synovitis.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":120,"s":"What is the primary treatment target in RA, how often should disease activity be assessed, and which DAS28 value defines remission?","o":["DAS28 low disease activity only, reassessed annually, with escalation only after radiographic progression","Sustained remission (or low disease activity if remission is unrealistic), assessed every 1\u20133 months while active, with a treatment change if there is no improvement by 3 months or the target is not reached by 6 months; DAS28 remission is below 2.6 Treat-to-target aims for remission (by composite measures such as DAS28, CDAI, SDAI or","Treat-to-target escalation to achieve remission or low disease activity","CDAI remission, assessed every 6 months, with escalation only if swollen joints exceed tender joints","ACR/EULAR Boolean remission alone, reassessed every 12 months regardless of symptoms"],"a":1,"e":"Boolean definitions) or, if not achievable, low disease activity, with regular assessment and escalation. Tight control with frequent composite disease activity measurement and prompt escalation improves outcomes. DAS28: remission below 2.6; low activity 3.2 or less; moderate up to 5.1; high above 5.1. DAS28 remission can coexist with residual swollen joints, so SDAI, CDAI or Boolean definitions are stricter. Things that falsely raise DAS28: fibromyalgia and central sensitisation (high TJC and patient global), OA, depression, high ESR from age, anaemia or paraprotein. Things that falsely lower it: IL-6 or JAK blockade suppressing CRP and ESR (DAS28 overestimates remission on tocilizumab, so prefer CDAI). Score Remission Low Moderate High DAS28 <2.6 \u00a33.2 \u00a35.1 >5.1 \u00a33.3 \u00a311 \u00a326 >26 SDAI \u00a32.8 \u00a310 \u00a322 >22 CDAI Boolean remission: TJC, SJC, CRP (mg/dL) and patient global (0\u201310) each \u00a31; patient global \u00a32 is now accepted. SDAI = TJC28 + SJC28 + PtGA + PhGA + CRP (mg/dL); CDAI omits CRP, so it can be calculated at the bedside. DAS28 ignores the feet. Once the target is sustained, review every 3\u20136 months. Radiographs of hands and feet yearly in the first years. Function is measured with HAQ-DI (0\u20133).","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":121,"s":"A woman with seropositive RA has taken methotrexate 10 mg weekly for 3 months (DAS28-CRP 4.8), or takes 20 mg weekly with persistent nausea and an inadequate response. What should happen before methotrexate is abandoned?","o":["Add an IL-17 or IL-23 inhibitor","Optimise it: increase to 20\u201325 mg weekly, switch to subcutaneous administration (better bioavailability and tolerability), optimise folic acid and reassess within 1\u20133 months","Escalate directly to a biologic DMARD without further optimising methotrexate","Switch to subcutaneous methotrexate","Switch to leflunomide monotherapy instead of further optimising methotrexate"],"a":1,"e":"Methotrexate should be titrated rapidly to an optimal dose (usually 20\u201325 mg weekly, or 0.3 mg/kg) before it can be judged to have failed. Subcutaneous administration improves bioavailability and often reduces gastrointestinal intolerance. Apparent failure that is not true failure: non-adherence (ask about \"methotrexate day\" omissions and intolerance), inadequate dose or duration (<3 months at target dose), non-inflammatory pain, wrong diagnosis (PsA, CPPD, PMR, CTD). Intolerance strategies: split oral dose 12 hours apart, evening dosing, antiemetic, higher folate, SC route. If still not at target after 3\u20136 months on optimised methotrexate, escalate to a b/tsDMARD (next entry). Combination csDMARD therapy (triple therapy) is an alternative strategy where access to biologics is limited (and in NICE pathways), but it adds toxicity.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":122,"s":"A patient with seropositive RA remains active (DAS28-CRP about 4.6) despite 6 months of optimised methotrexate off glucocorticoids. What is the next step, and how does the choice change in a 68-year-old current smoker with a previous MI?","o":["Add a second csDMARD (triple therapy) rather than consider a biologic or JAK inhibitor at this stage","Add a biologic DMARD, or consider a JAK inhibitor after risk assessment; in older patients, smokers or those with CV, malignancy or VTE risk, choose a biologic (such as a TNF inhibitor or abatacept)","Choose a JAK inhibitor as the preferred first advanced therapy, regardless of cardiovascular risk","Continue methotrexate alone for a further 6 months before considering any advanced therapy","Choose rituximab first-line, irrespective of seropositivity status or infection risk"],"a":1,"e":"If the target is not reached with an optimised methotrexate strategy after 3\u20136 months, a bDMARD should be added; a JAK inhibitor may be considered after weighing cardiovascular, malignancy and thromboembolic risk. JAK inhibitors should be used only if no suitable alternative exists in patients aged 65 or over, current or long-term past smokers, or those with cardiovascular, malignancy or VTE risk factors. Access may also depend on local commissioning criteria. Choosing the next drug by comorbidity: - Heart failure (NYHA III\u2013IV) or demyelination: avoid TNFi. - Prior diverticulitis or perforation: avoid IL-6R inhibitors; caution with JAKi. - Recent malignancy or lymphoma: rituximab is preferred. - Recurrent serious infection: abatacept or rituximab (check IgG). - Pregnancy plans: certolizumab or another TNFi. - Cannot take methotrexate: IL-6R inhibitor or JAKi as monotherapy. - ILD: abatacept or rituximab. - Seronegative disease: rituximab works less well. - Obesity: IV or weight-based dosing (tocilizumab IV, infliximab), or a non-TNF drug. The current EULAR position is that methotrexate failure is itself a poor prognostic factor, so there is no longer a separate step of trying a second csDMARD in patients without poor prognostic factors. NICE access criteria in England still specify DAS28 thresholds (>5.1 for severe disease, and 3.2\u20135.1 for moderate disease for some drugs). Biosimilars are the first choice where available. Combine with methotrexate where possible.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":123,"s":"A 58-year-old woman has been in sustained DAS28 remission for 18 months on methotrexate 15 mg weekly and adalimumab 40 mg fortnightly. Glucocorticoids were stopped 2 years ago. She asks about reducing medication. What is the most appropriate advice?","o":["Stop both methotrexate and adalimumab simultaneously now that remission is sustained","Taper methotrexate first while continuing adalimumab fortnightly unchanged","Stop methotrexate now, continuing adalimumab unchanged, rather than tapering the biologic","No tapering should be attempted; lifelong unchanged combination therapy is required","Consider tapering adalimumab by dose reduction or interval extension while continuing methotrexate"],"a":4,"e":"In sustained remission without glucocorticoids, b/tsDMARDs may be tapered, especially when combined with a csDMARD. Stopping all DMARDs commonly leads to flare. Tapering should be gradual with a plan to return to the previous dose if flare occurs. Flare risks during tapering: RF/ACPA positivity, Doppler activity on ultrasound, short remission duration, stopping rather than spacing, and stopping the csDMARD first. Sequence: GC off fi b/tsDMARD interval extension or dose reduction (after \u20216 months of sustained remission) fi csDMARD dose reduction last. Complete DMARD withdrawal is rarely successful (flare in about 50%), but most patients regain control after restarting. Ultrasoundguided tapering does not clearly beat clinical tapering.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":124,"s":"A patient with RA on adalimumab and methotrexate initially responded well but lost response after 18 months. Low trough levels and anti-drug antibodies are found. What is an appropriate next step?","o":["TNF inhibitors","Switch to another TNF inhibitor or to a drug with a different mechanism","Increase the adalimumab dose or frequency beyond the licensed regimen","Add a second biologic with a different mechanism while continuing adalimumab unchanged","Repeat anti-drug antibody testing in 24 months before making any treatment change"],"a":1,"e":"After failure of a first bDMARD, any other bDMARD (from the same or another class) or a JAK inhibitor (after risk assessment) can be used. Immunogenicity-driven secondary failure is a good reason to try another TNF inhibitor. Combining biologics is not recommended. Primary non-response (no improvement by 3\u20136 months) suggests switching mechanism. Secondary loss of response with anti-drug antibodies can respond to a second TNFi (or adding methotrexate). Rituximab works best in seropositive disease. After two mechanisms fail, consider D2T assessment. Never combine two biologics (infection risk without added efficacy).","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":125,"s":"A patient with RA has a history of multiple sclerosis and needs a biologic. Which drug class should generally be avoided?","o":["IL-6 receptor inhibitors","Monoclonal-antibody TNF inhibitor","Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor","Certolizumab pegol","TNF inhibitors"],"a":4,"e":"TNF inhibitors have been associated with new or worsening demyelinating disease and are avoided in established demyelination. Other TNFi neurological effects: optic neuritis, transverse myelitis, Guillain-Barr\u00e9 or CIDP-like neuropathy, PML (rare, more with rituximab). New neurology on a TNFi also needs exclusion of TB meningitis and Listeria. Safer choices with MS or demyelination: rituximab (also an MS therapy), abatacept, IL-6R inhibitors, JAKi (after risk assessment). If demyelination occurs on a TNFi, stop it permanently.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":126,"s":"A 71-year-old man with RA has NYHA class III heart failure and active disease despite methotrexate. Which treatment is usually avoided?","o":["IL-17 inhibitor","Rituximab","A TNF inhibitor","An IL-6 receptor inhibitor","Abatacept"],"a":2,"e":"TNF inhibitor TNF inhibitors can worsen moderate-to-severe (NYHA III-IV) heart failure. A non-TNF biologic is preferred. NYHA I\u2013II: use TNFi cautiously, with echo and monitoring. NYHA III\u2013IV: avoid (worse outcomes in heart-failure trials of infliximab and etanercept). Alternatives: abatacept, rituximab, tocilizumab or sarilumab. Note the lipid rise with IL-6R inhibition. JAKi are generally avoided with established CVD.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":127,"s":"A 66-year-old male ex-smoker with high-titre anti-CCP RA has bibasal crackles. HRCT shows basal reticulation, traction bronchiectasis and honeycombing. Which pattern is most likely?","o":["High-resolution CT chest","Usual interstitial pneumonia","Interstitial lung disease","B lymphocytes and plasma cells","Apremilast"],"a":1,"e":"UIP is the commonest HRCT pattern in RA-ILD, unlike most other connective tissue disease-related ILDs where NSIP predominates. Risk factors include male sex, older age, smoking, high-titre RF/ACPA and the MUC5B promoter variant. Differential of RA-ILD: methotrexate pneumonitis (acute, rare), other drug lung toxicity (leflunomide, TNFi exacerbation, rituximab), infection (TB, PJP, NTM), heart failure, bronchiectasis and obliterative bronchiolitis (airways disease), Caplan's nodules, lung cancer (higher in RA), smoking-related ILD (RB-ILD, DIP), and coexisting IPF (MUC5B). Clinically significant in about 10% (subclinical in up to 30%). UIP is the commonest pattern, then NSIP. UIP, extensive disease, low FVC or DLCO, older age, male sex and high-titre RF/ACPA predict progression and death. Screen at-risk patients with HRCT (PFTs alone miss cases), plus PFTs including DLCO and a 6-minute walk test. Monitor PFTs every 3\u20136 months for 1\u20132 years, then every 6\u201312 months; repeat HRCT at 1\u20132 years.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":128,"s":"A patient with RA-associated interstitial lung disease has active arthritis and needs treatment escalation. Which approach is most appropriate?","o":["Switch to a TNF inhibitor as first-line therapy, since it best controls both joint and lung disease","Add rituximab regardless of ILD pattern or severity, without respiratory input","Use long-term high-dose glucocorticoids as the primary ILD-modifying treatment","Individualised treatment with respiratory input, based on the lung pattern, its progression and the joint disease","Stop methotrexate in all cases of RA-associated ILD regardless of disease stability"],"a":3,"e":"RA-ILD needs multidisciplinary assessment. Options depend on ILD pattern, severity and progression; agents such as rituximab, abatacept or mycophenolate are used on a case-by-case basis, and antifibrotics may be considered for progressive fibrosis. Options: - Immunosuppression for ILD: mycophenolate, rituximab or azathioprine. - Joints: abatacept is often chosen (it may stabilise ILD); JAKi have observational data. - Antifibrotics: nintedanib for progressive pulmonary fibrosis (INBUILD); pirfenidone in UIP (TRAIL1). - Methotrexate can usually continue in stable ILD. Use TNFi cautiously because of reports of ILD exacerbation. - Supportive: stop smoking, vaccinate, pulmonary rehabilitation, oxygen, early transplant referral. - Steroids: short-term only; avoid long-term high doses. Manage in an ILD MDT.","c":"Infection & Immunosuppression","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":129,"s":"A woman with long-standing seropositive RA has recurrent infections, splenomegaly and neutrophils of 0.6 x 10I/L, not drug-related. Which test best distinguishes Felty's syndrome from T-cell large granular lymphocyte leukaemia?","o":["Bone marrow aspirate and trephine biopsy alone","Peripheral blood flow cytometry with T-cell receptor gene rearrangement studies","Serum vitamin B12 and folate levels alone","Troponin I","HLA-DR4 genotyping alone"],"a":1,"e":"Felty's syndrome and T-LGL leukaemia overlap clinically and both associate with HLA-DR4. An expanded clonal CD3+CD8+CD57+ population supports T-LGL leukaemia. Neutropenia in RA: drugs (methotrexate, sulfasalazine, leflunomide, tocilizumab, rituximab late-onset neutropenia, JAKi); Felty's syndrome; T-LGL leukaemia; SLE overlap (\"rhupus\"); B12 or folate deficiency; viral infection; hypersplenism (cirrhosis); myelodysplasia. Felty's triad: seropositive (often nodular, long-standing) RA, splenomegaly and neutropenia (<2.0), plus leg ulcers, recurrent infections, lymphadenopathy, vasculitis and nodular regenerative hyperplasia of the liver (with portal hypertension). It is strongly linked to HLA-DR4. Large granular lymphocytes may be seen on the blood film in both Felty's and T-LGL leukaemia. Felty's: control RA (methotrexate first-line; rituximab for refractory disease). Use G-CSF for severe infection (it can flare arthritis or vasculitis). Splenectomy is a last resort. T- LGL leukaemia: methotrexate, ciclosporin or cyclophosphamide, with haematology input.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":130,"s":"A 72-year-old woman with 25 years of erosive RA is listed for hip replacement under general anaesthesia. She reports occasional occipital headache. What is the most important pre-operative investigation?","o":["Temporal artery ultrasound","DEXA bone densitometry","MRI of the cervical spine with urgent spinal assessment","Plain lateral cervical spine radiograph in a neutral position only, without flexion-extension views","Lateral cervical spine imaging in flexion and extension (or MRI)"],"a":4,"e":"Atlantoaxial subluxation is common in long-standing erosive RA and may be asymptomatic. An anterior atlanto-dental interval over 3 mm is abnormal. Detecting instability before intubation prevents cord injury. Occipital headache in RA: atlanto-axial disease vs cervical spondylosis, GCA (in older people), C2 neuralgia, medication-overuse headache. Cervical RA patterns: anterior atlanto-axial subluxation (commonest; anterior ADI >3 mm abnormal; posterior ADI <14 mm or anterior ADI >9\u201310 mm indicates high myelopathy risk); basilar invagination (cranial settling, with brainstem compression and risk of sudden death); subaxial \"staircase\" subluxation. Symptoms: occipital pain, Lhermitte's sign, hand clumsiness, gait disturbance, bladder dysfunction. Flexion\u2013extension radiographs (performed carefully), MRI for cord compression, CT for bone. Anaesthetic plan: inline stabilisation, fibreoptic intubation, soft collar. Surgery (C1\u2013C2 fusion, occipito-cervical fixation) for myelopathy or severe instability.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":131,"s":"A patient with RA presents with a painful red eye with reduced vision. Ophthalmology confirms necrotising scleritis. What does this most strongly indicate?","o":["Simple dry-eye disease, requiring only lubricant drops","Enthesitis-related arthritis","A benign, self-limiting form of episcleritis requiring no treatment","Hydroxychloroquine-related maculopathy rather than scleral inflammation","Systemic vasculitic disease activity requiring urgent systemic immunosuppression"],"a":4,"e":"Necrotising scleritis in RA reflects systemic vasculitis and carries significant morbidity and mortality without treatment. High-dose glucocorticoids plus a DMARD or biologic are used with urgent ophthalmology involvement. Red eye in RA: Episcleritis: mild discomfort, normal vision, blanches with phenylephrine, self-limiting. - Scleritis: severe boring pain, tender, violaceous, sightthreatening. - Necrotising scleritis or scleromalacia perforans: painless thinning. - Peripheral ulcerative keratitis (corneal melt): an emergency. - Keratoconjunctivitis sicca: secondary Sj\u00f6gren's. - Drug effects: GC cataract or glaucoma, hydroxychloroquine retinopathy. - Infectious keratitis or uveitis in immunosuppressed patients: HSV, VZV, CMV. Other features of rheumatoid vasculitis (long-standing seropositive nodular disease, smokers): nail-fold infarcts, purpura, leg ulcers, digital gangrene, mononeuritis multiplex, scleritis and PUK, mesenteric or renal involvement. Low complement and high-titre RF are typical. Incidence has fallen from about 5% to \u00a31%. High-dose GC (including IV methylprednisolone) plus rituximab or cyclophosphamide for necrotising scleritis, PUK or systemic vasculitis. TNFi (adalimumab or infliximab) is also used for scleritis. Topical lubricants and ophthalmology-led local care.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Spondyloarthritis & AS","Vasculitis","Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":132,"s":"Which approach best fits cardiovascular risk management in a 55-year-old UK patient with RA?","o":["Proactive assessment of cardiovascular and metabolic risk, obesity, mood and smoking","Treat hyperlipidaemia alone, with no additional formal cardiovascular risk calculation","Repeat cardiovascular risk assessment only once, at RA diagnosis, with no further reassessment","Use a national risk tool that includes RA (such as QRISK3), reassess regularly, and control disease activity and traditional risk factors","Avoid statins in RA because of the inflammation-related 'lipid paradox'"],"a":3,"e":"RA confers excess cardiovascular risk. Assessment should be repeated at least every 5 years and after major treatment changes, with good disease control and judicious use of NSAIDs and glucocorticoids. RA roughly doubles CV risk (IHD 1.5\u20132\u00d7; heart failure about 2\u00d7; VTE 1.5\u20132\u00d7). The \"lipid paradox\": active inflammation lowers cholesterol. ACPA is linked to CV death. QRISK3 includes RA, SLE, steroid use and severe mental illness. If the risk tool does not include RA, multiply the calculated risk by 1.5. Lipids should be measured when disease is stable. Statins have the same efficacy as in the general population (TRACE-RA showed a trend to benefit). Disease control (methotrexate, TNFi) is associated with fewer events. Minimise NSAIDs and GC. Check lipids 4\u20138 weeks after starting IL-6R or JAKi (the LDL rise with IL-6 blockade has not translated into excess events).","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":133,"s":"When should annual hydroxychloroquine retinopathy screening begin, and how does this change with risk factors (for example 5.5 mg/kg/day and eGFR 52)?","o":["Begin annual screening immediately, regardless of dose or treatment duration","No screening is required if the patient remains asymptomatic","After 5 years of treatment in patients without risk factors; after 1 year if risk factors are present (dose above 5 mg/kg/day, eGFR below 60 or concomitant tamoxifen)","After 10 years of treatment in all patients, irrespective of dose or renal function","Annual screening from treatment initiation using fundus photography alone, without OCT"],"a":2,"e":"For patients without additional risk factors, annual screening starts after 5 years of therapy. If risk factors are present (dose above 5 mg/kg/day, eGFR below 60 or tamoxifen), annual monitoring starts after 1 year, and the dose should also be reduced to 5 mg/kg/day or less. Screening uses spectral-domain OCT with widefield fundus autofluorescence; routine baseline testing for new starters is no longer recommended in current UK guidance, although the EULAR course still advises baseline imaging within the first year. Other retinal or visual problems in rheumatology patients: GC cataract and central serous chorioretinopathy, uveitis, retinal vasculitis (Beh\u00e7et's, SLE), APS retinal vein occlusion, CMV retinitis (immunosuppression). Patients of Asian heritage often show extramacular (pericentral) toxicity, so widefield imaging is needed. Toxicity is irreversible and can progress after stopping. Early loss is parafoveal (bull's-eye maculopathy is a late sign). Dose by actual body weight, \u00a35 mg/kg/day (e.g. 60 kg gives 300 mg/day, which can be given as alternating 200/400 mg). If definite toxicity is found, stop the drug and discuss alternatives with the ophthalmologist.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":134,"s":"What is the usual perioperative approach to methotrexate and to a subcutaneous TNF inhibitor in a patient with RA having an elective joint replacement?","o":["Stop methotrexate 2 weeks before surgery in all patients regardless of renal function","Continue the biologic unchanged through the perioperative period in all cases","Stop the biologic permanently rather than timing surgery within its dosing cycle","Stop glucocorticoids abruptly before surgery to reduce infection risk","Continue methotrexate in most patients (unless renal function is unstable or there is infection); withhold the biologic so that surgery falls at the end of its dosing interval, restarting once the wound is healing and there is no infection"],"a":4,"e":"Continuing methotrexate does not increase wound complications and reduces flare risk. Biologic timing depends on the drug's dosing interval. Glucocorticoids should not be stopped abruptly perioperatively. Individual surgical and anaesthetic advice still applies. Surgery timing (ACR/AAHKS-based, as in the EULAR course): | Drug | Interval | Operate in | |---|---|---| | Etanercept | weekly | week 2 | | Adalimumab, sarilumab | 2weekly | week 3 | | Golimumab SC | monthly | week 5 | | Infliximab | 4, 6 or 8-weekly | week 5, 7 or 9 | | Tocilizumab | SC weekly / IV 4-weekly | week 2 / week 5 | | Abatacept | SC weekly / IV monthly | week 2 / week 5 | | Rituximab | 6monthly | month 7 | | Ustekinumab | 12-weekly | week 13 | | JAK inhibitors | daily | stop 3 days before; operate on day 4 | Continue csDMARDs (methotrexate, leflunomide, sulfasalazine, hydroxychloroquine). Continue the usual GC dose; routine supraphysiological \"stress dosing\" is not needed for most patients on their usual dose, but anaesthetists may give hydrocortisone cover for major surgery in long-term users (see the GC entry). Restart about 14 days after surgery once healing is satisfactory. Assess the cervical spine before intubation. Do not delay emergency surgery; hold the biologic until healed. Continue all drugs for minor procedures (dental, skin, cataract).","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":135,"s":"A patient with active inflammatory arthritis asks whether he should exercise during a flare. What is the best advice?","o":["Complete rest and immobilisation of all joints until the flare fully resolves","High-impact aerobic exercise is preferred over isometric exercise during a flare","Avoid all exercise until disease activity scores reach remission","Exercise should be deferred until biologic therapy has been escalated","Adapt exercise to symptoms, maintaining movement and rehabilitation while avoiding overload of acutely inflamed joints"],"a":4,"e":"Tailored exercise improves function, cardiovascular health and fatigue in inflammatory arthritis. Core non-drug care: education and a written flare plan; aerobic plus strengthening exercise (safe even in active disease, with isometric work for hot joints); hand exercises (SARAH programme); splints; podiatry and orthoses; occupational therapy and joint protection; work support; CBT for pain, fatigue and mood; smoking cessation; healthy weight; Mediterranean-style diet. Fatigue is one of the most troublesome symptoms and often persists in remission. Treat anaemia, depression and sleep problems, and use graded exercise and CBT-based self-management.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":136,"s":"A patient with RA (or axial SpA) reports worsening pain with high patient-reported scores, but there are no swollen joints, CRP is normal and imaging shows no active inflammation. What should be considered before escalating immunosuppression?","o":["Arrange only a CT scan of the sacroiliac joints to confirm ongoing inflammatory activity","Start opioid analgesia as the first-line treatment for the reported pain","Management principles are the same; biologics require objective evidence of inflammation (raised CRP or MRI inflammation)","Increase the current DMARD dose without further clinical or laboratory reassessment","Non-inflammatory pain (fibromyalgia, central sensitisation, structural damage, secondary OA mechanical pain), mood and sleep disturbance, and other comorbidity; reassess the diagnosis and objective inflammation before switching"],"a":4,"e":"Treatment should match demonstrable inflammatory activity. Disease activity scores (DAS28, BASDAI) can be inflated by non-inflammatory pain, fibromyalgia, mechanical pain and mood. Before switching, confirm the diagnosis, adherence, objective inflammatory activity (swollen joints, CRP, ultrasound or MRI) and the reason for failure. Pain mechanisms in RA: nociceptive (synovitis, damage, secondary OA); neuropathic (carpal tunnel syndrome, cervical myelopathy, vasculitic neuropathy); nociplastic (fibromyalgia in about 20%). Other drivers: depression and anxiety (1 in 3\u20134), obesity, hypothyroidism, vitamin D deficiency, statin myalgia, GC withdrawal, osteonecrosis or insufficiency fracture. Separate pain plan: exercise, CBT and pain management programmes, sleep hygiene, judicious analgesia (avoid long-term opioids and gabapentinoids), duloxetine or amitriptyline for selected patients, and treatment of entrapment neuropathy. Use CDAI or ultrasound to judge inflammation. In axSpA, ASDAS (which includes CRP) is less affected by pain than BASDAI.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Spondyloarthritis & AS","SLE & Lupus","Metabolic Bone Disease","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":137,"s":"A patient with RA has recurrent serious infections and active disease. What should be reviewed before choosing the next treatment?","o":["Serum urate and 24-hour urinary urate excretion","DAS28 and CRP trend over the preceding 3 months alone","HLA-B27 status and family history of spondyloarthritis","Prior infection history, glucocorticoid exposure, vaccination status, comorbidities and the infection profile of each option","Serum immunoglobulin levels only, with no other infection-risk assessment"],"a":3,"e":"Glucocorticoid dose is a major modifiable infection risk. Abatacept and IL-6 or TNF inhibitors differ in serious infection profiles. Serious infection risk is highest in the first 6\u201312 months of therapy; TNFi raises it roughly 1.5\u20132\u00d7. GC dose is the strongest modifiable factor. Abatacept probably has the lowest serious-infection risk; rituximab adds the risk of hypogammaglobulinaemia; JAKi add zoster risk. Taper GC to \u00a35 mg or stop. Vaccinate (influenza, pneumococcal, COVID-19, recombinant zoster). Treat bronchiectasis (sputum clearance, consider prophylactic azithromycin, though note the QT interaction with hydroxychloroquine). Check IgG. Prefer abatacept or rituximab (if IgG is normal). Treat skin and foot portals of entry.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Spondyloarthritis & AS","Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":138,"s":"A 29-year-old man has 7 months of inflammatory back pain, MRI sacroiliitis, negative HLA-B27 and a good response to naproxen. Does he meet the ASAS axial SpA classification criteria, and through which arm?","o":["He meets the clinical arm: HLA-B27 plus two or more SpA features","He does not meet either arm, because MRI sacroiliitis alone is never sufficient","He meets the clinical arm only if CRP is also elevated","He meets the imaging arm: sacroiliitis plus at least one SpA feature","He meets the criteria only if radiographic (not MRI) sacroiliitis is also present"],"a":3,"e":"The criteria apply to back pain of 3 months or more starting before age 45. The imaging arm requires sacroiliitis (active MRI inflammation or definite radiographic change) plus at least one SpA feature; inflammatory back pain and good NSAID response each count. The clinical arm requires HLA-B27 plus at least two features. Mimics of axSpA: mechanical or degenerative back pain; fibromyalgia (a common misdiagnosis in women); osteitis condensans ilii; DISH; sacral insufficiency fracture; septic sacroiliitis (unilateral; S. aureus, TB, Brucella); post-partum or athletic BME; SAPHO; hyperparathyroidism (subchondral resorption at the SIJ); fluorosis; X-linked hypophosphataemia (enthesopathy); ochronosis; Paget's; metastases; hypermobility. ASAS features: IBP, arthritis, heel enthesitis, uveitis, dactylitis, psoriasis, Crohn's or colitis, good NSAID response, family history, HLA-B27, raised CRP. Peripheral SpA criteria require arthritis, enthesitis or dactylitis plus \u20211 of (uveitis, psoriasis, IBD, preceding infection, B27, sacroiliitis) or \u20212 of (arthritis, enthesitis, Rheumatology SCE \u2014 5-option SBA 26 dactylitis, IBP history, family history). Referral strategy: chronic back pain (\u20213 months) starting before 45 plus at least one of IBP, B27, imaging sacroiliitis, peripheral SpA features, uveitis, psoriasis, IBD, family history, NSAID response or raised CRP. Screen patients presenting to ophthalmology, dermatology and gastroenterology. The original ASAS criteria performed below target in the inception cohort (sensitivity 82%, specificity 77%), and misuse as diagnostic criteria led to over-labelling. Classification is not diagnosis.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Psoriatic Arthritis","Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Metabolic Bone Disease","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":139,"s":"Which MRI finding defines active sacroiliitis in axial SpA, and how should a small area of sacroiliac bone marrow oedema be interpreted in a postpartum woman with mechanical back pain and a normal CRP?","o":["Fat metaplasia and ankylosis alone, without any bone marrow oedema","NSAIDs","Any degree of bone marrow oedema, regardless of location or clinical context, confirms active sacroiliitis","Periarticular (subchondral) bone marrow oedema (osteitis) on STIR or fat-suppressed T2 sequences in a typical location defines active sacroiliitis; but BME is non-specific and occurs in healthy people, athletes and postpartum women, so clinical context is essential","Synovitis or capsulitis alone, without bone marrow oedema, is sufficient to define active sacroiliitis"],"a":3,"e":"Bone marrow oedema clearly present and typically located is required. Structural lesions such as erosions and fat metaplasia support the diagnosis but do not alone define active sacroiliitis. Sacroiliac bone marrow oedema is not specific: its distribution, extent, accompanying structural lesions and the clinical picture determine significance, and overdiagnosis is a recognised problem. Non-SpA causes of SIJ BME: post-partum (up to about 60% shortly after delivery), runners and military recruits, mechanical back pain (up to about 20% of healthy people), osteitis condensans ilii (iliac-sided triangular sclerosis in multiparous women; sacrum normal, no erosions), insufficiency fracture (H-shaped sacral pattern), septic sacroiliitis (unilateral, effusion, soft-tissue oedema), degenerative change in older people, gout or CPPD of the SIJ (rare). Mechanical BME typically sits in the anterior iliac side of the lower joint, is small and has no erosions. Erosions and fat metaplasia (backfill, ankylosis) add specificity. Synovitis, capsulitis or enthesitis alone do not qualify. Sequences: STIR or T2 fat-suppressed for activity, T1 for structural lesions. Contrast is rarely needed. Do not request SIJ MRI as a screening test when pre-test probability is low. In post-partum women, consider repeating MRI after 6\u201312 months if the clinical picture is unclear.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Crystal Arthropathy","Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":140,"s":"Which modified New York grading of sacroiliitis is required for radiographic axial SpA (ankylosing spondylitis)?","o":["Bilateral grade 1, or unilateral grade 2","Bilateral grade 2 or more, or unilateral grade 3-4","Unilateral grade 2 alone is sufficient","Bilateral grade 1 or more, regardless of unilateral grade","Any grade 1 change, bilateral or unilateral"],"a":1,"e":"Modified New York criteria require radiographic sacroiliitis (bilateral grade 2 or more, or unilateral grade 3-4) plus at least one clinical criterion: inflammatory back pain for over 3 months, limited lumbar motion, or reduced chest expansion. Radiographic SIJ sclerosis without SpA: OCI, DISH (bridging of the upper ligamentous part only), degenerative change, hyperparathyroidism, Paget's, renal osteodystrophy. Grade Appearance 0 normal 1 suspicious 2 minimal: small erosions or sclerosis, no change in width 3 unequivocal: erosions, sclerosis, widening, narrowing, partial ankylosis 4 complete ankylosis Inter-reader agreement on plain films is poor. Low-dose CT resolves equivocal X-rays (erosions, sclerosis, ankylosis) but shows no inflammation.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Metabolic Bone Disease"]},{"id":141,"s":"A 26-year-old man with mechanical low back pain has a positive HLA-B27 result ordered by his GP. What does this result mean?","o":["A positive result alone confirms axial spondyloarthritis without further assessment","HLA-B27 is found in over 50% of the general white UK population","Enthesitis-related arthritis","About 8% of the white UK population carries HLA-B27 and most never develop spondyloarthritis","A negative HLA-B27 result excludes axial spondyloarthritis"],"a":3,"e":"HLA-B27 is found in roughly 85-95% of white patients with AS but also about 8% of the general population, so its predictive value in unselected back pain is low. It is useful only with clinical and imaging findings. HLA-B27 in context: present in about 85\u201395% of white AS patients but only about 50% in African and Middle Eastern populations. Prevalence in Europe is 6\u20139% (higher in Scandinavians, about 14\u201316%; very high in Haida and other indigenous groups). Only 1\u20135% of carriers develop axSpA (10\u201330% if a first-degree relative is affected). Request it only with a clinical suspicion of SpA (\u20211 SpA feature). A positive result in non-specific mechanical back pain adds little; a negative result lowers but does not exclude probability.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Osteoarthritis & Mechanical"]},{"id":142,"s":"Which ASDAS-CRP values define disease activity states, and how should a fall from 3.6 to 2.4 twelve weeks after starting a TNF inhibitor be interpreted?","o":["ASDAS below 1.3 is inactive disease; a fall of 1.1 or more is a clinically important improvement, so a fall of","ASDAS below 2.1 is inactive disease, and any fall at all is a major improvement","ASDAS has no validated improvement threshold; only the absolute value is informative","A fall of less than 0.5 units still qualifies as a major improvement","ASDAS cannot be calculated without first obtaining a formal BASDAI score"],"a":0,"e":"1.2 supports continuing treatment ASDAS: below 1.3 inactive; 1.3 to below 2.1 low; 2.1 to 3.5 high; above 3.5 very high disease activity. A fall of 1.1 or more is clinically important improvement and 2.0 or more is major improvement. Continuation is supported by an ASDAS improvement of at least 1.1 units after 12\u201316 weeks. ASDAS-CRP combines back pain, morning stiffness duration, patient global, peripheral pain or swelling, and CRP. BASDAI (6 patient questions; \u20214 means active) is the traditional NICE threshold, with a response defined as a \u20212point (or 50%) fall. ASDAS is preferred because it includes CRP and has validated cut-offs. Treat-to-target: ASDAS <2.1, ideally <1.3. Review every 3\u20136 months while active. Repeat imaging is not routine; spinal X-ray progression is assessed no more often than every 2 years.","c":"General Rheumatology","t":["General Rheumatology"]},{"id":143,"s":"What is first-line treatment for active axial SpA, when is a biologic indicated (for example persistent ASDAS 3.3 after naproxen then etoricoxib at full dose for 6 weeks), and which non-drug measures remain important after starting it?","o":["A biologic should be started immediately, without an NSAID trial","Exercise and physiotherapy alone, without any pharmacological therapy, as first-line treatment","A biologic is indicated only after conventional synthetic DMARD failure, as in peripheral RA","Methotrexate as the first disease-modifying treatment, escalating to a biologic only after methotrexate failure","A full-dose NSAID with exercise and education first; after at least two NSAIDs over 4 weeks with ASDAS still 2.1 or more, start a TNF or IL-17 inhibitor (or a JAK inhibitor after risk assessment); exercise, physiotherapy,"],"a":4,"e":"full-dose NSAID with exercise and education first; after at least two NSAIDs over 4 weeks with ASDAS still 2.1 or more, start a TNF or IL-17 inhibitor (or a JAK inhibitor after risk assessment); exercise, physiotherapy, education and smoking cessation remain core throughout NSAIDs are first-line, used continuously if needed for symptom control, after considering gastrointestinal, renal and cardiovascular risk. b/tsDMARDs are indicated when ASDAS is 2.1 or more despite at least two NSAIDs over a total of 4 weeks; a TNF inhibitor or IL-17 inhibitor is the usual first choice. Exercise and physiotherapy remain core treatment regardless of drug therapy, and smoking worsens disease activity and structural progression. 60\u201380% respond to NSAIDs within 48\u201372 hours, and a good response supports the diagnosis. All b/tsDMARDs achieve ASAS40 in about 40\u201350% (vs 15\u201325% with placebo). Response is best in younger patients with short disease duration, raised CRP and MRI inflammation. Longterm TNFi (>4 years) is associated with slower syndesmophyte formation. NSAID safety: coxib or NSAID plus PPI for GI risk; naproxen has the most neutral CV profile; monitor eGFR and BP; avoid after 20 weeks of pregnancy. Choosing the first b/tsDMARD: recurrent uveitis or IBD fi mAb TNFi (or upadacitinib); significant psoriasis fi IL- 17i (or TNFi); pregnancy plans fi certolizumab; age \u202165, smoking, CV or VTE risk fi avoid JAKi; otherwise a biosimilar TNFi on cost. Licensing: bimekizumab and upadacitinib are licensed for both r- and nr-axSpA; tofacitinib for r-axSpA. Non-drug: supervised exercise (land or water, group) beats home exercise; passive modalities have little evidence; stop smoking; manage weight. Surgery: THR for structural hip disease regardless of age; corrective osteotomy at specialist centres. After first b/tsDMARD failure: check the diagnosis, adherence and non-inflammatory pain (fibromyalgia, mechanical pain, fracture, hip OA). After primary TNFi non-response, change mechanism (IL-17i or JAKi); after loss of response or intolerance, a second TNFi is reasonable. Response rates fall with each switch.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Spondyloarthritis & AS","Psoriatic Arthritis","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":144,"s":"What is the role of conventional synthetic DMARDs and of long-term oral glucocorticoids in axial SpA?","o":["Hydroxychloroquine, sulfasalazine and azathioprine","Long-term oral glucocorticoids are recommended to suppress axial inflammation when NSAIDs fail","Neither treats axial disease; sulfasalazine may be considered for peripheral arthritis, and local glucocorticoid injection may help peripheral arthritis or enthesitis","Methotrexate is effective for axial disease but not peripheral arthritis","Sulfasalazine is preferred over NSAIDs for first-line axial symptom control"],"a":2,"e":"csDMARDs do not meaningfully treat axial inflammation; sulfasalazine is the best-evidenced csDMARD for peripheral SpA arthritis. Systemic glucocorticoids have little evidence of benefit in axial disease and cause cumulative harm. Do not delay biologics to try csDMARDs in purely axial disease. Image-guided SIJ steroid injection can help selected patients. A short oral GC course may bridge a peripheral flare. Use paracetamol or tramadol only for residual pain; avoid long-term strong opioids.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":145,"s":"What is the typical presentation of SpA-associated uveitis, how is an acute attack managed, and which biologic is preferred when a patient with axial SpA has three attacks a year?","o":["Monoclonal-antibody TNF inhibitor","Chronic, bilateral, insidious anterior uveitis, managed with long-term oral glucocorticoids alone","Posterior uveitis with retinal vasculitis, managed primarily with intravitreal antibiotics","Acute, unilateral, recurrent (often alternating) anterior uveitis with a painful red eye, photophobia and blurred vision; same-day ophthalmology for topical corticosteroid and a cycloplegic; for recurrent attacks a","Etanercept as the preferred agent for preventing recurrent attacks"],"a":3,"e":"monoclonal anti-TNF antibody such as adalimumab HLA-B27-associated uveitis presents with a painful red eye, photophobia and blurred vision. It needs same-day slitlamp assessment, topical corticosteroids and a cycloplegic, with review of systemic therapy if recurrent. Monoclonal anti-TNF antibodies reduce uveitis recurrence. Etanercept is less effective at preventing uveitis (and may trigger it), and IL-17 inhibitors have a weaker evidence base. Uveitis patterns by disease: | Pattern | Disease | |---|---| | Acute, unilateral, alternating anterior, painful | HLA-B27 (axSpA, ReA, IBD), Beh\u00e7et's (anterior with hypopyon) | | Chronic, bilateral, silent anterior | ANA+ oligoarticular JIA, IBD (sometimes), PsA (more often bilateral and chronic than B27-AAU) | | Granulomatous (mutton-fat KPs), panuveitis | sarcoidosis, Blau, TB, syphilis, VKH | | Posterior with retinal vasculitis or occlusive vasculitis | Beh\u00e7et's, SLE, APS, susceptibility to CMV or toxoplasma | | Tubulointerstitial nephritis and uveitis | TINU (adolescents) | Other red eyes: conjunctivitis (ReA), episcleritis or scleritis (RA, AAV, RP, IBD), keratitis (HSV, PUK), acute angleclosure glaucoma. Complications of recurrent AAU: posterior synechiae (irregular pupil), cataract, glaucoma (including steroidinduced), cystoid macular oedema. A hypopyon in a white eye suggests Beh\u00e7et's. Severe attacks may need periocular or systemic GC. For prevention: adalimumab (licensed for non-infectious uveitis) or infliximab, certolizumab or golimumab; sulfasalazine modestly reduces attacks if a biologic is not indicated. Teach patients to seek same-day care for a painful red eye.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":146,"s":"A patient with axial SpA, RA or psoriatic arthritis also has active Crohn's disease and needs a biologic. Which agents treat both, and which should be avoided?","o":["Monoclonal TNF inhibitors (infliximab, adalimumab) treat both; avoid etanercept (ineffective in IBD) and","IL-23 inhibitors are preferred, since they treat both axial and bowel disease effectively","Etanercept is preferred because it avoids anti-drug antibody formation","IL-17 inhibitors are preferred for their additional benefit on bowel inflammation","Avoid all biologics and manage with sulfasalazine and mesalazine alone"],"a":0,"e":"IL-17 inhibitors (can provoke or worsen IBD) Monoclonal TNF inhibitors treat both the joint disease and inflammatory bowel disease, whereas the soluble receptor etanercept is ineffective in IBD and IL-17 inhibitors can exacerbate it. In PsA, IL-12/23 or IL-23 inhibitors and some JAK inhibitors also treat both peripheral joints and IBD, but IL-23 pathway drugs do not treat axial disease. Gut-and-joint drug map: | Drug | Gut | Axial | Peripheral | |- --|---|---|---| | Infliximab, adalimumab | Crohn's + UC | I | I | | Certolizumab | Crohn's (some regions) | I | I | | Golimumab | UC | I | I | | Upadacitinib | Crohn's + UC | I | I | | Ustekinumab, risankizumab, guselkumab | Crohn's + UC | I | I (PsA) | | Vedolizumab | gut-selective | I (may unmask arthritis) | I | | Etanercept | I | I | I | | IL-17i | worsens | I | I | Use NSAIDs cautiously (short courses or coxibs) in IBD. Sulfasalazine and mesalazine treat mild colitis and peripheral arthritis. Thiopurines and methotrexate help IBD maintenance but not axial disease. Co-manage with gastroenterology.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Spondyloarthritis & AS","Psoriatic Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":147,"s":"A 41-year-old woman with axial SpA has extensive plaque psoriasis (about 15% body surface area) and active spinal disease despite NSAIDs. Which agent is generally preferred?","o":["Triamcinolone hexacetonide","IL-17 pathway inhibition","Warfarin anticoagulation","Monoclonal-antibody TNF inhibitor","An IL-17 inhibitor"],"a":4,"e":"For axial SpA with extensive or high-impact psoriasis, the 2025 BSR guideline prefers an IL-17 inhibitor or a monoclonal TNF inhibitor, ideally with dermatology input. IL-23 inhibitors treat psoriasis but did not show efficacy in axial SpA trials, and etanercept is less effective for skin. The 2025 BSR axSpA guideline prefers an IL-17i or a monoclonal TNFi when psoriasis is extensive or highimpact. IL-17A/F (bimekizumab) gives very high skin clearance (PASI 90 to 100) but more oral candidiasis. Etanercept is less effective for skin, and IL-23 inhibitors do not treat axial disease. Co-manage with dermatology. Consider nail and scalp disease, and genital and flexural sites, as high-impact. Paradoxical psoriasis on a TNFi points towards switching to an IL-17 or IL-23 pathway drug.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Psoriatic Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":148,"s":"A 45-year-old woman with axial SpA has been in sustained remission on a TNF inhibitor for 2 years and asks to reduce treatment. Which is most appropriate?","o":["Stop the TNF inhibitor abruptly, since sustained remission predicts continued control off treatment","Switch to a different TNF inhibitor before considering any dose reduction","Monoclonal-antibody TNF inhibitor","Certolizumab pegol","Consider gradual tapering of the TNF inhibitor rather than stopping it"],"a":4,"e":"In sustained remission, tapering a biologic can be considered as a shared decision. The 2025 BSR axial SpA guideline does not recommend withdrawing targeted therapy in sustained remission, because discontinuation is associated with a high flare rate. In sustained remission (ASDAS <1.3 for \u20216 months), reduce the dose or extend the interval as a shared decision. Complete withdrawal leads to flare in most patients within months, although most regain response on restarting.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":149,"s":"A man with long-standing ankylosing spondylitis falls from standing height and has new neck pain, with no fracture on plain radiographs. What is the next step, and how should his bone health be managed?","o":["Reassurance and early mobilisation, since plain radiographs have excluded a fracture","Urgent CT (and/or MRI) of the whole spine, because fractures follow minor trauma and are missed on plain films; long term, assess and treat fracture risk alongside extension exercise, smoking cessation and falls reduction","Routine physiotherapy manipulation of the neck to assess range of movement","Spinal DXA as the primary tool for fracture-risk assessment in this patient","Treat the chlamydial infection and manage the neck pain with NSAIDs alone"],"a":1,"e":"The ankylosed spine is rigid and osteoporotic. Fractures follow minor trauma, are often missed on plain films, are frequently unstable and may be non-contiguous, so cross-sectional imaging of the whole spine is required. Axial SpA increases vertebral fracture risk, which should be assessed and treated in addition to controlling inflammation. New spinal pain in AS: unstable three-column fracture (cervicothoracic or thoracolumbar junction), Andersson lesion (spondylodiscitis or pseudarthrosis), infective discitis, cauda equina syndrome from dural ectasia with arachnoid diverticula (in long-standing disease), atlantoaxial subluxation, epidural haematoma. Immobilise in the patient's usual posture and never force the neck straight. Spine DXA is falsely normal or high because of syndesmophytes, so use hip DXA (or lateral spine or QCT). CT of the whole spine, plus MRI if there are neurological signs. Urgent spinal surgical opinion. Long term: bisphosphonate where indicated, vitamin D, falls prevention and control of inflammation. DISH carries the same fracture risk.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Metabolic Bone Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":150,"s":"A 62-year-old man with long-standing ankylosing spondylitis has an early diastolic murmur at the left sternal edge and a PR interval of 240 ms. Which pathology best explains both findings?","o":["Aortitis with aortic root dilatation and fibrosis extending into the conduction system","Mitral valve prolapse with a mid-systolic click","Rheumatic mitral stenosis from childhood streptococcal infection","Hereditary haemochromatosis","Osteomalacia"],"a":0,"e":"AS-associated aortitis causes aortic regurgitation, and subaortic fibrosis can extend to the AV node, causing conduction defects from first-degree to complete heart block. Aortic regurgitation with conduction disease: AS or HLA- B27 aortitis, ReA, Takayasu, GCA, syphilitic aortitis, Marfan or Loeys-Dietz (root dilatation without conduction disease), relapsing polychondritis, Cogan's syndrome, IgG4-related aortitis, Beh\u00e7et's. Carditis in HLA-B27 disease features aortic root thickening, AR, subaortic fibrous \"bump\", and AV conduction block (first-degree to complete). Conduction disease can occur without other SpA features (\"B27 lone heart block\"). CV risk is increased overall (inflammation, NSAIDs, smoking). ECG, echo and cardiology review. Pacing for high-grade block. Valve or root surgery as indicated. Control inflammation and CV risk factors.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Vasculitis","Metabolic Bone Disease","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":151,"s":"What does a \"bamboo spine\" on radiography represent?","o":["Osteophyte formation from degenerative disc disease","Flowing anterior ossification sparing the sacroiliac joints (DISH)","An IL-17 inhibitor","Bridging syndesmophytes with vertebral fusion in advanced axial SpA","Disc-space calcification from ochronosis"],"a":3,"e":"Bamboo spine reflects syndesmophytes bridging disc spaces in late radiographic axial SpA. Spinal new bone: syndesmophytes (thin, vertical, marginal; axSpA); non-marginal \"chunky\" syndesmophytes and paravertebral ossification, asymmetric (PsA, ReA); flowing anterior ossification (DISH); osteophytes (horizontal then vertical, degenerative); fluorosis; ochronosis (disc calcification); hypoparathyroidism (ligament calcification); X-linked hypophosphataemia (enthesopathy).","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Osteoarthritis & Mechanical"]},{"id":152,"s":"How does the management of non-radiographic axial SpA compare with that of radiographic axial SpA?","o":["Biologics are not licensed for non-radiographic disease under any circumstances","csDMARDs are preferred over biologics in non-radiographic disease","Management principles are the same; biologics require objective evidence of inflammation (raised CRP or MRI inflammation)","Non-radiographic disease requires a lower ASDAS threshold for biologic eligibility than radiographic disease","Non-radiographic disease is a distinct condition with an entirely different treatment pathway"],"a":2,"e":"Both forms are managed similarly. Biologic eligibility in non-radiographic disease requires objective inflammation. nr-axSpA and r-axSpA have a similar symptom burden and similar treatment response. Women are more often nonradiographic. NICE eligibility for biologics in nr-axSpA requires a raised CRP or MRI inflammation.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":153,"s":"A 71-year-old man with type 2 diabetes has thoracic spinal stiffness. Radiographs show flowing anterior ossification along four contiguous thoracic vertebrae with preserved disc heights and normal sacroiliac joints. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Ochronosis (alkaptonuria)","Spinal tuberculosis (Pott's disease)","Ankylosing spondylitis","Diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis (DISH)","SAPHO syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"DISH shows flowing anterolateral ossification over at least four contiguous vertebrae with preserved disc height and no sacroiliac erosion or fusion. It is associated with diabetes, obesity and metabolic syndrome. DISH: older (>50), obese, diabetic men; mechanical stiffness with little pain; right-sided thoracic flowing ossification over \u20214 contiguous levels; preserved discs; normal SIJ (bridging may occur at the upper ligamentous portion); peripheral enthesophytes; dysphagia or airway problems from cervical osteophytes; normal CRP; B27 not increased. Analgesia, physiotherapy, management of metabolic risk, and surgery for dysphagia or myelopathy. Treat it like AS regarding fracture risk after trauma (whole-spine CT). Heterotopic ossification after hip replacement is more common.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Metabolic Bone Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":154,"s":"A 24-year-old man develops a hot swollen knee and heel pain 3 weeks after urethritis. Urine NAAT is positive for Chlamydia trachomatis. Synovial fluid culture is negative and crystals are absent. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Prolonged broad-spectrum antibiotics for 6 months to eradicate the arthritis","No treatment is needed, since reactive arthritis always resolves within days","Start a biologic immediately without treating the underlying infection","Treat the chlamydial infection (for example doxycycline for 7 days), arrange partner notification, and treat the arthritis with NSAIDs or joint injection","Staphylococcus aureus; aspirate the joint and take blood cultures, then start IV flucloxacillin"],"a":3,"e":"Reactive arthritis requires treatment of the triggering genitourinary infection and contact tracing, but prolonged antibiotics do not alter the arthritis. NSAIDs and intra-articular glucocorticoid are first-line; sulfasalazine is used for chronic disease. Monoarthritis after urethritis: reactive arthritis vs disseminated gonococcal infection (tenosynovitis, pustules, migratory pattern), septic arthritis, gout, HIVassociated arthritis, early PsA or IBD arthritis. Enteric ReA triggers: Salmonella, Shigella, Campylobacter, Yersinia, C. difficile. Post-streptococcal ReA follows S. pyogenes. Onset 1\u20134 weeks after infection. Asymmetric lower-limb oligoarthritis, enthesitis, dactylitis, conjunctivitis or AAU, circinate balanitis, keratoderma blennorrhagicum, oral ulcers, sterile urethritis. B27 is positive in 60\u201380%. Most cases resolve in 6\u201312 months; 15\u201330% become chronic or recurrent. NSAIDs and IA steroid; a short oral GC course for severe polyarthritis. Treat active chlamydia (and partners) and test for other STIs including HIV. Antibiotics do not help postenteric ReA. Sulfasalazine or methotrexate for chronic disease; TNFi for refractory disease.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Psoriatic Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":155,"s":"A 33-year-old woman with ulcerative colitis develops an asymmetrical oligoarthritis of a knee and ankle during a colitis flare. What is the expected course of this arthritis?","o":["Independent of bowel disease activity and often becomes chronic (a Type 2 pattern)","A small-joint polyarthritis unrelated to the colitis, requiring methotrexate","Enthesitis-related arthritis","This pattern usually parallels bowel activity and is self-limiting","Progressive and erosive, requiring early biologic therapy regardless of bowel activity"],"a":3,"e":"Type 1 enteropathic arthritis affects fewer than five large joints and parallels IBD activity. Type 2 is a small-joint polyarthritis independent of gut activity. NSAIDs can worsen IBD and are used cautiously. Type Joints Course Links Type 1 <5 parallels IBD erythema (pauciarticular) activity, selfnodosum, large limiting (<10 joints uveitis weeks) Type 2 \u20215 independent of uveitis (polyarticular) IBD, persistent small joints HLA-B27 Axial SIJ and independent of (50\u201370%) spine IBD Control the bowel disease (type 1 improves with it). Use NSAIDs cautiously (short courses or coxibs). Sulfasalazine for peripheral arthritis; IA steroid; mAb TNFi or upadacitinib for both. Vedolizumab can unmask arthritis. Look for other IBD-associated features: erythema nodosum, pyoderma gangrenosum, AAU, episcleritis, PSC.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":156,"s":"A 42-year-old woman has palmoplantar pustulosis and painful swelling over the sternoclavicular joints. Imaging shows osteitis and hyperostosis of the anterior chest wall. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["SAPHO syndrome","Diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis (DISH)","Osseous sarcoidosis","Polyarteritis nodosa","Takayasu arteritis"],"a":0,"e":"SAPHO (synovitis, acne, pustulosis, hyperostosis, osteitis) characteristically affects the anterior chest wall. Treatment includes NSAIDs, bisphosphonates and, in refractory cases, TNF inhibitors. Anterior chest wall pain and swelling: SAPHO; CRMO or CNO (paediatric equivalent); SpA (sternoclavicular, manubriosternal); septic sternoclavicular arthritis (IV drug use, diabetes); Tietze syndrome or costochondritis; condensing osteitis of the clavicle; Friedrich's disease (avascular necrosis of the medial clavicle); malignancy (lymphoma, metastasis); fibrous dysplasia. Bone scan shows the \"bull's head\" sign at the sternocostoclavicular region. Skin disease (palmoplantar pustulosis, severe acne, hidradenitis) may precede or follow osteitis by years. Cultures may grow Cutibacterium acnes (debated significance). NSAIDs first. Bisphosphonates (IV pamidronate or zoledronate) for osteitis. TNFi for refractory disease. Also methotrexate or sulfasalazine, IL-17 or IL-23 inhibitors (skin), and JAKi (case series). Short courses of doxycycline or azithromycin have mixed results. Paradoxical PPP can occur on TNFi.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":157,"s":"A 45-year-old man has inflammatory arthritis of a knee and two DIP joints. He has no current or past psoriasis, but his mother has psoriasis. He has nail pitting and RF is negative. How does he score on the CASPAR criteria?","o":["3 points; he meets the criteria","5 points; he meets the criteria","2 points; he does not meet the criteria","1 point; he does not meet the criteria","4 points; he meets the criteria, driven by current psoriasis alone"],"a":0,"e":"points; he meets the criteria CASPAR requires inflammatory articular disease plus 3 or more points: current psoriasis 2 (or personal history 1, or family history 1 if no personal history), typical nail dystrophy 1, negative RF 1, dactylitis 1 and juxta-articular new bone formation 1. Here: family history 1 + nails 1 + RF negative 1 = 3. DIP-predominant arthritis: PsA vs nodal or erosive OA (bony nodes, older women, mechanical), gout (tophi, crystals), multicentric reticulohistiocytosis (coral-bead papules), reactive arthritis, and RA (DIPs usually spared). Nail disease mimics: onychomycosis (send clippings), trauma, lichen planus. CASPAR: inflammatory articular disease (joint, spine or entheseal) plus \u20213 points from: - current psoriasis 2, or personal history 1, or family history 1 (if no personal history) - typical nail dystrophy (pitting, onycholysis, hyperkeratosis) 1 - negative RF 1 dactylitis (current or recorded) 1 - juxta-articular new bone on hand or foot Xray 1 Specificity about 99%, sensitivity about 91%. Look for occult psoriasis: scalp, ears, umbilicus, natal cleft, perianal skin and nails. Consider a dermatology opinion. Screen for comorbidities at diagnosis.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Psoriatic Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":158,"s":"A patient with psoriasis has a diffusely swollen second toe, nail pitting and DIP synovitis. What is the diagnosis, and what is dactylitis?","o":["Gout, with tophaceous deposition within the digit","Reactive arthritis, with dactylitis as the sole diagnostic feature","Psoriatic arthritis with dactylitis: diffuse swelling of a whole digit from flexor tenosynovitis, joint synovitis and soft-tissue inflammation","Septic flexor tenosynovitis requiring emergency surgical drainage","3 points; he meets the criteria"],"a":2,"e":"Dactylitis, nail dystrophy and DIP involvement are characteristic of PsA. Dactylitis is a hallmark of PsA and other SpA, and persistent dactylitis is a target for treatment escalation. Causes of a sausage digit: PsA, ReA, other SpA; gout or tophaceous disease; sarcoid dactylitis (lytic phalangeal lesions, lupus pernio); TB dactylitis (spina ventosa, children); sickle cell dactylitis (hand-foot syndrome, infants); syphilis; septic tenosynovitis (Kanavel signs: fusiform swelling, flexed posture, pain on passive extension, sheath tenderness); trauma; thyroid acropachy; secondary hypertrophic osteoarthropathy. \"Hot\" (tender, acute) dactylitis predicts erosion more than \"cold\" (non-tender) dactylitis. Feet are more often affected than hands. Ultrasound shows flexor tenosynovitis with soft-tissue oedema. NSAIDs and local steroid injection to the tendon sheath. csDMARDs have limited efficacy. Persistent dactylitis warrants a bDMARD (TNFi, IL-17i or IL-23i).","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Crystal Arthropathy","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":159,"s":"Hand radiographs in a patient with long-standing psoriatic arthritis show a pencil-in-cup deformity. What causes this appearance?","o":["Subchondral cyst formation without bone resorption","Periarticular osteopenia with marginal erosions","Bone resorption at the distal end with proliferative new bone at the base of the adjacent phalanx","Central erosion with surrounding osteophyte formation (a 'gull-wing' deformity)","Punched-out erosions with overhanging sclerotic margins"],"a":2,"e":"Osteolysis with adjacent new bone formation is typical of PsA. Other features include periostitis, ivory phalanx, ankylosis and relative preservation of bone density. Arthritis mutilans (telescoping \"opera-glass\" digits) in PsA vs RA, multicentric reticulohistiocytosis, neuropathic arthropathy, hyperparathyroidism or renal osteodystrophy, leprosy, and erosive OA (gull-wing deformity, which is central and not pencil-in-cup). Other PsA radiographic features: fluffy periostitis, enthesophytes, acro-osteolysis, DIP ankylosis, \"ivory phalanx\" (sclerosis of the distal phalanx), absence of periarticular osteopenia, ray pattern, asymmetric SIJ disease, chunky paramarginal syndesmophytes.","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","Metabolic Bone Disease"]},{"id":160,"s":"What are the seven criteria for minimal disease activity (MDA) in psoriatic arthritis, and how many must be met?","o":["4 of 7 criteria, with RF negativity mandatory","Tender joints 1 or less, swollen joints 1 or less, PASI 1 or less (or BSA 3% or less), patient pain VAS 15 or less, patient global VAS 20 or less, HAQ 0.5 or less, and tender entheseal points 1 or less; 5 of 7 are required","A DAPSA score of 4 or less, regardless of the seven MDA domains","6 of 7 criteria, excluding the tender entheseal point count","PASI 1 or less alone, without the other six domains"],"a":1,"e":"MDA criteria: tender joints 1 or less, swollen joints 1 or less, PASI 1 or less (or BSA 3% or less), patient pain VAS 15 or less, patient global VAS 20 or less, HAQ 0.5 or less and tender entheseal points 1 or less. RF status forms part of classification, not disease activity. MDA (\u20215 of 7): TJC \u00a31, SJC \u00a31, PASI \u00a31 or BSA \u00a33%, pain VAS \u00a315, PtGA VAS \u00a320, HAQ \u00a30.5, tender entheses \u00a31. VLDA requires all 7 and is the closest to complete remission. MDA captures skin and enthesitis, whereas DAPSA does not. Treat-to-target: assess every 3 months while active; adjust if there is no improvement by 3 months or the target is not met by 6 months (TICOPA showed better outcomes with tight control).","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Psoriatic Arthritis"]},{"id":161,"s":"Which components make up the DAPSA score in psoriatic arthritis?","o":["Tender joints 1 or less, swollen joints 1 or less and PASI 1 or less only (the MDA domains)","66 swollen and 68 tender joint counts, CRP, patient global assessment and patient pain","HAQ score and tender entheseal point count only","68 tender and 66 swollen joint counts plus ESR, without any patient-reported measure","PASI and body-surface area only, without joint counts"],"a":1,"e":"DAPSA sums 66 swollen and 68 tender joints, CRP (mg/dL), patient global and pain scores. Remission is 4 or less, low activity above 4 to 14, moderate above 14 to 28 and high above 28. DAPSA remission \u00a34, low \u00a314, moderate \u00a328, high >28; cDAPSA omits CRP (useful on IL-6 or JAK blockade or with a normal CRP). DAPSA ignores skin, enthesitis and axial disease, so pair it with domain measures.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Psoriatic Arthritis"]},{"id":162,"s":"A woman with newly diagnosed PsA has 7 swollen joints and moderate plaque psoriasis. What is the most appropriate first DMARD, and what is the role of oral glucocorticoids?","o":["Leflunomide; oral glucocorticoids are first-line and used long-term at moderate dose","Methotrexate; oral glucocorticoids are not routine and, if needed, are used only at low dose for short periods","Sulfasalazine; oral glucocorticoids should be avoided even for short-term bridging","A biologic as first-line therapy, bypassing methotrexate","Add an IL-17 or IL-23 inhibitor"],"a":1,"e":"A csDMARD should be started promptly for polyarthritis, with methotrexate preferred when skin involvement is relevant. Hydroxychloroquine is ineffective in PsA. Systemic glucocorticoids have limited benefit, can provoke pustular or erythrodermic psoriasis on withdrawal and cause cumulative harm; intra-articular injection is useful for oligoarticular flares. Other csDMARDs: leflunomide (good evidence, TOPAS) and sulfasalazine (peripheral arthritis only, little skin effect). Ciclosporin is rarely used (nephrotoxicity, hypertension). Hydroxychloroquine is ineffective and may flare psoriasis. Poor-prognosis features that justify early escalation: polyarticular disease, raised CRP, dactylitis, erosions, high HAQ, nail disease. Methotrexate 15\u201325 mg weekly with folate. Consider FIB-4 or elastography if NAFLD or alcohol excess is suspected (common in PsA). If methotrexate is unsuitable (liver disease, pregnancy plans), use leflunomide or sulfasalazine, or proceed directly to a biologic. Enthesitis and axial disease bypass csDMARDs.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Psoriatic Arthritis","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":163,"s":"A patient with PsA has active polyarthritis despite methotrexate 20 mg weekly for 4 months and extensive plaque psoriasis (PASI 14). What is the most appropriate next step?","o":["A TNF inhibitor","Add an IL-17 or IL-23 inhibitor","Switch to leflunomide monotherapy and reassess again in 12 months","Switch to subcutaneous methotrexate","IL-17 inhibitor"],"a":1,"e":"After csDMARD failure in peripheral PsA a bDMARD is started. With relevant skin disease, IL-17A, IL-17A/F, IL-23 or IL12/23 inhibitors may be preferred because of their greater skin efficacy than TNF inhibitors. Biologic choice by domain: | Situation | Preferred | |---|---| | Significant psoriasis | IL-17A (secukinumab, ixekizumab), IL-17A/F (bimekizumab) or IL-23 (guselkumab, risankizumab) | | Axial | TNFi or IL-17i (JAKi) | | Uveitis | mAb TNFi | | IBD | mAb TNFi, IL-23i or ustekinumab, upadacitinib; avoid IL-17i | | Nails | IL-17, IL-23 or TNFi | | Enthesitis or dactylitis | any bDMARD (TNF, IL-17, IL-23) | | Pregnancy plans | certolizumab | Step 3 (bDMARD failure): switch bDMARD, or JAKi (tofacitinib, upadacitinib) after CV, VTE and cancer risk assessment. Abatacept is an option but has little skin effect. Weight loss improves response. Rheumatology SCE \u2014 5-option SBA 36","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Psoriatic Arthritis","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":164,"s":"A 66-year-old woman with PsA has mild oligoarthritis despite methotrexate and declines injections. A biologic or JAK inhibitor is not considered appropriate. Which oral drug is most suitable?","o":["Apremilast","Plasma exchange","Usual interstitial pneumonia","An IL-17 inhibitor","Sulfasalazine, switched from methotrexate"],"a":0,"e":"In mild disease with inadequate response to at least one csDMARD where a biologic or JAK inhibitor is not appropriate, a PDE4 inhibitor may be considered. Intra-articular glucocorticoid also helps oligoarticular flares. Apremilast: modest efficacy, no laboratory monitoring, oral; GI upset and weight loss; mood monitoring. Suited to mild oligoarticular disease without poor prognostic factors, or skin-predominant disease, when a b/tsDMARD is unsuitable. Deucravacitinib (TYK2) is licensed for psoriasis, with PsA trials ongoing. Image-guided or landmark IA steroid for single joints. Consider subcutaneous options if the patient will accept them later, and offer self-injection support.","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":165,"s":"A patient with PsA has persistent Achilles enthesitis confirmed on ultrasound despite two NSAIDs and a local programme of physiotherapy. Peripheral joints are quiet. What is the most appropriate next step?","o":["A second NSAID trial for a further 3 months before considering any escalation","Image-guided glucocorticoid injection directly into the Achilles tendon","Start a biologic DMARD such as a TNF or IL-17 inhibitor csDMARDs have little effect on enthesitis. Persistent, clinically significant enthesitis after NSAIDs warrants a bDMARD. Glucocorticoid injection into or around the","Methotrexate or leflunomide, since csDMARDs are effective for enthesitis","Add an IL-17 or IL-23 inhibitor"],"a":2,"e":"Achilles tendon is avoided because of rupture risk. Heel pain: SpA enthesitis (Achilles insertion, plantar fascia) vs mechanical Achilles tendinopathy (mid-portion, athletic), plantar fasciopathy, retrocalcaneal bursitis, Haglund deformity, calcaneal stress fracture, fluoroquinolone tendinopathy, gout or xanthoma (familial hypercholesterolaemia, with nodular thickened tendons), DISH or enthesophytes, X-linked hypophosphataemia. Ultrasound signs of enthesitis: hypoechogenicity, thickening, Doppler signal at the insertion, erosions and enthesophytes. Fibromyalgia tender points can mimic enthesitis on examination. csDMARDs do not help enthesitis. Use NSAIDs, physiotherapy, heel raises and orthoses, then a bDMARD (TNFi, IL-17i, IL-23i or JAKi). Never inject steroid into the Achilles tendon. Plantar fascia injection, if used, should be given from the medial side, not through the heel pad.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Crystal Arthropathy","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":166,"s":"A patient with mild-looking psoriasis has distressing genital and scalp lesions and active arthritis. How should the skin disease be weighed when choosing treatment?","o":["Only total body surface area, regardless of site or psychological impact","As clinically relevant, because impact and sensitive sites matter as well as body surface area","Dermatology referral is unnecessary since the arthritis is the dominant problem","Mild disease by body-surface-area alone, so standard rather than high-skin-efficacy therapy is appropriate","PASI alone, without considering site-specific impact such as genital or scalp disease"],"a":1,"e":"Psoriasis is clinically relevant when it is extensive or has substantial impact, including scalp, nail, genital and flexural disease. This influences drug choice and joint care with dermatology. Define clinically relevant psoriasis as BSA >10%, PASI >10, DLQI >10, or involvement of special sites (scalp, nails, genital, palmoplantar, flexural, face). Choose agents with high skin efficacy (IL-17 or IL-23). Topicals and phototherapy for mild skin disease. Deucravacitinib, methotrexate, ciclosporin or acitretin are dermatological options; acitretin is teratogenic for 3 years after stopping.","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":167,"s":"Paradoxical psoriasiform eruptions, including palmoplantar pustulosis, are a recognised class effect of which drugs?","o":["IL-17 inhibitors, via an effect on mucosal immunity","Certolizumab pegol","Rituximab, via depletion of skin-resident B cells","TNF inhibitors","Monoclonal-antibody TNF inhibitor"],"a":3,"e":"Paradoxical psoriasis is a recognised class effect of TNF inhibitors. Management includes topical therapy, switching TNF inhibitor or changing to an IL-17 or IL-23 pathway agent. Paradoxical reactions to TNFi: psoriasis (palmoplantar pustulosis, scalp, plaques), hidradenitis, IBD (etanercept), uveitis (etanercept), sarcoid-like granulomas, drug-induced lupus (anti-dsDNA, anti-histone), vasculitis, demyelination, alopecia areata. Main differential: true psoriasis flare, or eczema and fungal infection of the feet. Paradoxical psoriasis is driven by unopposed plasmacytoid dendritic cell type I IFN. It occurs with any TNFi and in any indication (including in patients without prior psoriasis, e.g. Crohn's or RA). It is more common in women and smokers. Mild: topical steroids or vitamin D analogues, continuing the TNFi. Moderate to severe: switch to an IL-17 or IL-23 pathway drug (or ustekinumab, which treats both if IBD is present). Switching TNFi helps in some patients but recurrence is common.","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Metabolic Bone Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":168,"s":"Alongside inflammatory disease control, which comorbidity strategy is particularly important in psoriatic arthritis?","o":["Annual chest radiograph screening for malignancy as the main comorbidity priority","Routine screening for interstitial lung disease in every patient","Proactive assessment of cardiovascular and metabolic risk, obesity, mood and smoking","Screening for osteoporosis as the principal comorbidity concern","Routine screening for hepatitis B and C as the principal comorbidity concern"],"a":2,"e":"PsA is associated with obesity, diabetes, hypertension, fatty liver and depression. Obesity also reduces response to some biologics. At diagnosis and regularly: BP, lipids, HbA1c, BMI and waist circumference, LFTs and FIB-4 (fatty liver), urate, depression screening (PHQ), smoking and alcohol. Multiply CV risk by about 1.5 where not captured. Weight loss of \u20215% improves response. GLP-1 receptor agonists help weight and may reduce disease activity (emerging data).","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy","Metabolic Bone Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":169,"s":"A man has acute pain, redness and swelling of the first MTP joint. Which test confirms gout most specifically, and how should a serum urate of 330 micromol/L measured during the flare be interpreted?","o":["A plain radiograph showing a punched-out erosion with overhanging edge, interpreted as diagnostic without aspiration","A normal serum urate measured during the flare, which reliably excludes gout","Synovial fluid showing needle-shaped, strongly negatively birefringent MSU crystals; urate often falls during a flare, so repeat it at least 2 weeks after the flare settles","Ultrasound double-contour sign alone, without synovial fluid analysis, interpreted as fully diagnostic","Dual-energy CT alone, repeated weekly until urate normalises"],"a":2,"e":"Crystal identification is the reference standard. Ultrasound and dual-energy CT support the diagnosis, but serum urate can be normal during a flare (in about 10\u201340%) and hyperuricaemia alone does not prove gout. Repeat measurement 2 or more weeks after the flare, and crystal analysis, help when the diagnosis is uncertain. Acute podagra: gout vs septic arthritis, cellulitis, CPP arthritis (rarely at the 1st MTP), BCP periarthritis of the 1st MTP (\"hydroxyapatite pseudopodagra\" in young women), PsA or ReA dactylitis, bunion bursitis, trauma or sesamoiditis, stress fracture, Charcot foot, L\u00f6fgren's (ankles). Typical flare: maximal within 6\u201324 hours, often nocturnal, with desquamation; resolves within 1\u20132 weeks. 1st MTP is the first site in about half of patients. Atypical presentations: polyarticular low-grade gout in older women on diuretics and transplant recipients; tophi on Heberden's nodes; \"puffy hand\"; spinal gout (mimics discitis). Aspirate whenever feasible (including asymptomatic previously affected joints between flares). Clinical diagnosis is acceptable in typical recurrent podagra. Always send Gram stain and culture when infection is possible.","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":170,"s":"Synovial fluid from an acutely swollen knee shows rhomboid crystals with weak positive birefringence under compensated polarised light. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Gram stain and bacterial culture","Basic calcium phosphate (hydroxyapatite)","Calcium pyrophosphate crystals","Septic arthritis is a major concern; send cultures and treat urgently if clinical suspicion is high","Chondrocalcinosis"],"a":2,"e":"CPP crystals are rhomboid or rod-shaped with weak positive birefringence. Basic calcium phosphate crystals are not birefringent. Crystals do not exclude coexisting infection, so culture is still required. Acute monoarthritis of the knee in an older person: CPP arthritis, gout, septic arthritis (can coexist), haemarthrosis (anticoagulants, trauma), OA flare, ruptured Baker's cyst, insufficiency fracture or SONK (spontaneous osteonecrosis of the knee), Lyme disease (in endemic areas). Feature MSU CPP needle, 1\u201330 \u00b5m rhomboid or rod, Shape polymorphic, smaller Birefringence strong weak or absent (many invisible) negative (yellow when positive (blue Sign when parallel) parallel to the compensator) intra- and extracellular Location often intracellular Mnemonic: CPP is Positive with Parallel = blue (\"ABC\": Alignment, Blue, Calcium).","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression"]},{"id":171,"s":"A febrile older patient with a history of gout has a hot swollen knee; synovial fluid shows MSU crystals but a WCC of 85,000/mm\u00b3 with 95% neutrophils and he has rigors. What must be excluded, and what is the most appropriate management?","o":["Diagnose gout confidently because MSU crystals were found, and treat with colchicine alone","Inject intra-articular glucocorticoid immediately for symptom relief, pending culture results","Treat as reactive arthritis, given the crystal-positive but systemic presentation","Septic arthritis: send Gram stain and culture and start empirical antibiotics pending results; do not inject steroid or label it gout until infection has been assessed","Reassure the patient that finding MSU crystals excludes septic arthritis by definition"],"a":3,"e":"Crystal arthritis and infection can coexist. Very high synovial WCC, rigors or systemic upset mean infection must be excluded with Gram stain and culture, and empirical antibiotics are justified while results are awaited. A hot joint should not be injected or labelled gout until infection has been assessed. Synovial fluid guide (WCC/mm\u00b3): normal <200; noninflammatory 200\u20132,000 (OA, trauma, AVN); inflammatory 2,000\u201350,000 (RA, SpA, crystals, viral); septic usually >50,000 with >90% neutrophils (but crystal arthritis can exceed 50,000, and septic arthritis can be lower in immunosuppressed patients, prostheses, gonococcal or TB infection). Synovial glucose is low and lactate high in sepsis. Large-joint or polyarticular gout flares can cause fever, high CRP and leucocytosis. Risks for coexisting infection: prostheses, tophaceous skin breakdown, immunosuppression, diabetes, IV drug use. Blood cultures; empirical IV flucloxacillin (or local protocol); orthopaedic review. Treat the flare with colchicine or NSAID only alongside antibiotics until culture is negative at 48 hours.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":172,"s":"When should urate-lowering therapy be discussed and started, for example in a 35-year-old man with his first flare and a urate of 540 micromol/L?","o":["Only after a second flare within 12 months, regardless of urate level or comorbidity","Only once tophi are already clinically visible","ULT should not be started before age 40 regardless of urate level","Discuss and offer ULT to everyone with gout; it is clearly indicated for recurrent flares, tophi, urate arthropathy and stones, and is advised close to diagnosis for patients under 40, those with very high urate or significant comorbidity, so this man should be offered it now","Only after renal stones have already formed"],"a":3,"e":"The BSR gout guideline advises that ULT is discussed and offered to everyone with gout. A single first flare without risk factors does not mandate it. It is clearly indicated for recurrent flares, tophi, urate arthropathy and renal stones, and EULAR advises starting close to diagnosis in young patients (under 40), those with very high urate (above 480 micromol/L) or comorbidities such as renal impairment, hypertension, ischaemic heart disease or heart failure. Education about the chronic nature of gout and the treatment target is central. The 2020 ACR guideline strongly recommends ULT for \u20211 tophus, radiographic damage or \u20212 flares per year. It conditionally recommends ULT after a first flare with CKD stage \u20213, urate >540 \u00b5mol/L (9 mg/dL) or urolithiasis. Asymptomatic hyperuricaemia is not treated. Start once the flare has settled (or during it, with antiinflammatory cover). Nurse-led treat-to-target with education achieved target urate in >90% in a UK trial (Doherty, Nottingham) and reduced flares and tophi.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy"]},{"id":173,"s":"What is the target serum urate on ULT, and how does it differ in tophaceous gout with frequent flares?","o":["Below 420 micromol/L for all patients, with no lower target for tophaceous disease","Below 600 micromol/L, since gout rarely needs stricter control","The same target applies regardless of tophi or flare frequency","Titrate urate-lowering treatment towards a target with monitoring","Below 360 micromol/L for most; below 300 micromol/L for severe or tophaceous gout until tophi resolve"],"a":4,"e":"EULAR and NICE recommend a target below 360 micromol/L (6 mg/dL) for most patients, with a lower target (below 300 micromol/L, 5 mg/dL) for tophi, chronic arthropathy or frequent flares, which accelerates crystal dissolution. The current BSR gout guideline (2017) is stricter and recommends 300 micromol/L or lower for everyone, so UK practice may use the lower target. Once crystal burden has resolved, the usual target can be resumed. Long-term levels below 180 micromol/L are avoided. Check urate monthly during titration, then at least yearly. Tophi shrink at a rate proportional to how far urate is below target. Explain that early flares on ULT signal crystal dissolution, not failure.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy"]},{"id":174,"s":"How should allopurinol be started in a man with gout and normal renal function, how are flares prevented in the early months, and what should happen to allopurinol if a flare occurs once he is established on it?","o":["Start at the maximum dose of 900 mg immediately to reach target faster","Stop allopurinol immediately until the flare fully resolves, then restart at the same dose","Start 100 mg daily and increase by 100 mg every 2\u20134 weeks to target urate; give low-dose colchicine","No flare prophylaxis is required when starting allopurinol","Start at 300 mg daily with no titration, provided renal function is normal"],"a":2,"e":"(0.5\u20131 mg daily) for about 6 months; continue allopurinol unchanged during any flare Starting low and titrating gradually reduces flares and hypersensitivity risk; in CKD a lower starting dose is used (50 so prophylaxis is given for about 6 months (longer with tophi or continued flares), adjusted for renal function; a lowdose NSAID (with PPI) or low-dose prednisolone are alternatives if colchicine is unsuitable. Stopping or changing the dose during a flare causes urate fluctuation and can prolong attacks, so established ULT should be continued. Causes of failure to reach target on allopurinol: nonadherence (the commonest), under-dosing (the maximum is 900 mg), ongoing diuretic use, high alcohol or fructose intake, CKD, very high crystal burden, overproduction states, interacting drugs. Allopurinol hypersensitivity syndrome (DRESS, SJS/TEN; mortality up to 25%) is rare. Risk is highest in the first 8 weeks, with a high starting dose, CKD, diuretics and HLA- B*58:01. Other interactions: azathioprine and mercaptopurine (reduce to 25% or avoid), warfarin, amoxicillin rash. In ALL-HEART, allopurinol did not reduce CV events in IHD. Give a \"pill in the pocket\" supply for early self-treatment of flares. Allopurinol doses above creatinine-clearance-based limits can be reached gradually in CKD with monitoring (titrate by 50 mg steps).","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":175,"s":"A 54-year-old man presents 6 hours into an acute gout flare of the first MTP joint. eGFR is 85 and he takes no interacting drugs. Which colchicine regimen is preferred?","o":["2 mg three times daily for 5 days, then stopped abruptly","1 mg, followed 1 hour later by 0.5 mg on day 1, then low dose until the flare settles","High-dose colchicine, 1 mg every 2 hours until diarrhoea occurs, as in historical regimens","Intravenous colchicine, preferred for rapid relief","500 micrograms twice daily for 2 weeks regardless of response"],"a":1,"e":"mg, followed 1 hour later by 0.5 mg on day 1, then low dose until the flare settles Low-dose colchicine is as effective as older high-dose regimens with far fewer adverse effects, and works best when started within 12-24 hours. NSAIDs, a short oral glucocorticoid course or joint aspiration and injection are alternatives. Options by comorbidity: - Colchicine low-dose regimen; reduce in CKD and older age; avoid with CYP3A4 or P-gp inhibitors. - NSAID at full dose with a PPI (e.g. naproxen 500 mg twice daily); avoid if eGFR <30, heart failure, peptic ulcer or anticoagulation. Prednisolone 30\u201335 mg for 3\u20135 days (non-inferior to NSAIDs). - IA steroid for 1\u20132 accessible joints, after excluding infection. - IM or IV GC or ACTH if the oral route is not possible. - IL-1 blockade (see the next entries). - Combination therapy (e.g. colchicine plus steroid) for severe polyarticular flares. Plus ice, rest and elevation.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":176,"s":"A 75-year-old man with gout and CKD stage 3b is taking clarithromycin for pneumonia and develops a gout flare. What is the most appropriate flare treatment?","o":["High-dose NSAID with a proton pump inhibitor","Standard-dose colchicine, since eGFR is only mildly reduced","Double the clarithromycin dose and add colchicine regardless of the interaction risk","A short course of oral prednisolone","HLA-B*58:01 genotyping"],"a":3,"e":"short course of oral prednisolone Colchicine must be avoided with strong CYP3A4 or P-glycoprotein inhibitors (clarithromycin, ciclosporin, some azoles) because of life-threatening toxicity, especially in CKD. NSAIDs are inadvisable in CKD 3b. Strong CYP3A4 or P-gp inhibitors that make colchicine dangerous: clarithromycin, erythromycin, ketoconazole, itraconazole, ritonavir and cobicistat, ciclosporin, verapamil, diltiazem, grapefruit. Statins add myopathy risk. Signs of colchicine toxicity: diarrhoea, vomiting, then multiorgan failure, myoneuropathy, marrow failure. In CKD 3b with an interacting antibiotic: oral prednisolone (or IA steroid if a single accessible joint). Anakinra is useful in hospitalised patients with CKD.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":177,"s":"A patient with recurrent gout flares has contraindications to colchicine, NSAIDs and glucocorticoids. What can be considered for flare treatment?","o":["An IL-1 inhibitor such as canakinumab or anakinra","A short course of oral prednisolone","A trial of high-dose vitamin C supplementation","Topical NSAID gel alone for a large-joint flare","Intra-articular hyaluronic acid injection"],"a":0,"e":"IL-1 beta, released via the NLRP3 inflammasome, drives gouty inflammation. IL-1 blockade is an option when standard flare treatments are contraindicated, after excluding infection. Canakinumab (anti-IL-1b, single SC dose) is approved for frequent flares when NSAIDs, colchicine and steroids are contraindicated or ineffective. Anakinra (IL-1Ra) is off-label but used for 3\u20135-day courses in hospital and CKD. Exclude infection first.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":178,"s":"A 48-year-old man of Han Chinese ancestry with recurrent gout and CKD stage 3 is due to start allopurinol. Which pre-treatment test is most appropriate?","o":["Joint aspiration and intra-articular glucocorticoid injection","Thiopurine methyltransferase (TPMT) activity or genotype","An IL-1 inhibitor such as canakinumab or anakinra","A short course of oral prednisolone","HLA-B*58:01 genotyping"],"a":4,"e":"HLA-B58:01 is strongly associated with severe allopurinol hypersensitivity (SJS/TEN, DRESS). It is common in Han Chinese, Korean and Thai populations, and testing is advised where available, particularly with CKD. HLA-B57:01 predicts abacavir hypersensitivity. HLA-B58:01 carriage is common in Han Chinese, Korean and Thai populations (and in people of African ancestry in US guidance). HLA-B57:01 predicts abacavir hypersensitivity; HLA-B*15:02 predicts carbamazepine SJS/TEN in South-East Asian populations. If positive, avoid allopurinol and use febuxostat (if there is no major CVD) or a uricosuric. Educate about rash and fever and the need to stop at once.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":179,"s":"A man with tophaceous gout and CKD (eGFR 48) has not reached target despite allopurinol titrated to 900 mg with good adherence. What are the options, and how does a previous MI change them?","o":["1 mg, followed 1 hour later by 0.5 mg on day 1, then low dose until the flare settles","Switch to azathioprine (with or without tacrolimus), stopping mycophenolate at least 6 weeks before conception, and confirm stable disease before conceiving","Rising anti-dsDNA with falling C3 and C4 predicts flare; isolated serological activity warrants closer monitoring (including urinalysis), not treatment escalation","Switch to febuxostat titrated to target with flare prophylaxis, or add a uricosuric; febuxostat should be avoided in major cardiovascular disease unless no other option is appropriate, so a uricosuric or specialist option is","Below 360 micromol/L for most; below 300 micromol/L for severe or tophaceous gout until tophi resolve"],"a":3,"e":"preferred after an MI When target urate is not reached on an appropriate allopurinol dose, options include switching to febuxostat (another xanthine oxidase inhibitor, if there is no major cardiovascular disease) or adding a uricosuric. A cardiovascular mortality signal in the CARES trial led to advice to avoid febuxostat in pre-existing major cardiovascular disease unless no alternative is suitable. Uricosurics such as benzbromarone (available on a named-patient basis in the UK) can be added or substituted. Probenecid is ineffective with reduced renal function. Pegloticase is reserved for refractory tophaceous disease in specialist settings and is not widely available. CARES (US, high dropout) found higher CV and all-cause death than with allopurinol. FAST (Europe) found no excess CV risk. Regulators still advise caution. Febuxostat: Rheumatology SCE \u2014 5-option SBA 42 80 mg daily, rising to 120 mg; no dose change in mild to moderate CKD; contraindicated with azathioprine or mercaptopurine. Benzbromarone works in moderate CKD but carries rare severe hepatotoxicity. Lesinurad has been withdrawn. Uricosurics need high fluid intake (with or without urine alkalinisation) and are avoided with urate stones. Pegloticase (IV every 2 weeks) plus methotrexate more than doubles sustained response (MIRROR); screen for G6PD deficiency and stop if pre-infusion urate rises above 360 \u00b5mol/L (predicts infusion reactions). Emerging options: SEL-212, URAT1 inhibitors (pozdeutinurad, dotinurad), tigulixostat.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":180,"s":"A 59-year-old man with gout takes bendroflumethiazide for hypertension. What is the most appropriate antihypertensive change?","o":["Switch to losartan or a calcium-channel blocker","Continue allopurinol while treating the flare","Switch to a pregnancy-compatible antihypertensive such as labetalol or nifedipine before conception or","Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor","A short course of oral prednisolone"],"a":0,"e":"Thiazide and loop diuretics raise serum urate. Losartan has a modest uricosuric effect. Low-dose aspirin for secondary prevention should not be stopped. Drugs with useful uricosuric effects: losartan (hypertension), fenofibrate (lipids), SGLT2 inhibitors (diabetes, HF, CKD; also lower gout incidence), amlodipine (modest). Continue low-dose aspirin for secondary prevention. Thiazide-like and loop diuretics raise urate; avoid them where possible in gout (but not at the expense of heart failure control).","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":181,"s":"Which lifestyle advice is most appropriate for a patient with chronic gout?","o":["FBC, LFTs and renal function at week 2, then about monthly for the first 3-6 months, then every 3 months, with the interval individualised to toxicity risk","Weight management, moderation of alcohol and sugar-sweetened drinks, and management of cardiovascular and renal risk, recognising that lifestyle alone rarely controls established gout","Use a national risk tool that includes RA (such as QRISK3), reassess regularly, and control disease activity and traditional risk factors","PPI therapy with reflux measures, assessment of dysmotility and aspiration risk, and prokinetics or dilatation where indicated","Glucocorticoids with an immunosuppressant (preferably a monoclonal anti-TNF antibody, or azathioprine); anticoagulation may be added only if bleeding risk is low"],"a":1,"e":"Gout is associated with hypertension, CKD, metabolic syndrome, obesity and cardiovascular disease. Lifestyle change is an adjunct to urate-lowering therapy. Diet alone lowers urate by only about 60 \u00b5mol/L at most, so most patients still need ULT. Avoid blaming patients; genetics outweigh diet. Vitamin C supplements do not help meaningfully. Cherries and low-fat dairy have only observational support. Gradual weight loss (crash diets and fasting trigger flares); limit beer and spirits and avoid binges; reduce sugarsweetened drinks, fructose, offal and shellfish; DASH or Mediterranean diet; hydration; exercise. Screen BP, lipids, HbA1c and renal function. Low-dose colchicine (0.5 mg) is licensed in some countries for CV risk reduction in coronary disease.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":182,"s":"A 78-year-old woman has fever, severe neck pain and stiffness, a raised CRP and calcification around the odontoid peg on CT. How does this finding relate to the CPPD classification criteria?","o":["ANA at a titre of 1:80 or higher on HEp-2 cells (or an equivalent test) at any time, then 10 or more weighted points with at least one clinical criterion","He meets the imaging arm: sacroiliitis plus at least one SpA feature","They are classification criteria and do not replace clinical diagnosis; she scores 5 points and does not yet meet the criteria","It is crowned dens syndrome, which on its own is a sufficient criterion for CPPD classification","In moderate/high-risk populations monoarthritis counts as a major criterion and fever \u202138 \u00b0C and ESR"],"a":3,"e":"After the entry criterion (joint pain, swelling or tenderness), crowned dens syndrome or CPP crystals in synovial fluid are sufficient. Meningitis and GCA are important differentials. Fever with neck pain and stiffness in an older person: crowned dens syndrome vs bacterial meningitis (lumbar puncture if any doubt), GCA or PMR, cervical discitis or epidural abscess, retropharyngeal abscess, acute calcific longus colli tendinitis (C1\u2013C2 prevertebral calcification, painful swallowing), cervical spondylosis flare, atlanto-axial disease (RA). CT shows halo or crown calcification of the transverse ligament or cruciform ligament around the dens. CRP is often very high. It responds dramatically to NSAIDs, colchicine or short-course GC.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":183,"s":"A man aged 44\u201352 (or a woman aged 41\u201342) has early-onset or polyarticular CPPD, sometimes with firm 2nd/3rd MCP swelling, hook-like osteophytes, chondrocalcinosis, diabetes and raised liver enzymes. Which investigations are most appropriate to find an underlying cause?","o":["Serum ACE and a chest radiograph to screen for sarcoidosis alone","Rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies alone","Serum urate and 24-hour urinary urate excretion alone","Calcium and PTH, magnesium, phosphate, ALP, ferritin and transferrin saturation (then HFE genotyping), and TSH; 2nd/3rd MCP arthropathy with hook osteophytes points particularly to haemochromatosis","ANA and complement levels alone"],"a":3,"e":"Early-onset (before about 55\u201360 years) or florid polyarticular CPPD warrants screening for metabolic causes: hyperparathyroidism, haemochromatosis, hypomagnesaemia (including Gitelman syndrome) and hypophosphatasia (low ALP). Familial CPPD (ANKH variants) is another cause. Hereditary haemochromatosis classically causes a 2nd/3rd MCP arthropathy with hook osteophytes and CPPD; raised transferrin saturation and ferritin with HFE C282Y homozygosity confirm it. Venesection prevents organ damage but often does not improve established arthropathy. Other causes of 2nd/3rd MCP arthropathy with osteophytes: idiopathic CPPD (\"pseudo-OA\"), manual labour OA (jackhammer), acromegaly (widened joint spaces), Wilson's disease (young, with neurological or hepatic features and chondrocalcinosis), ochronosis. Other causes of hypomagnesaemia: PPIs, diuretics, short bowel, alcohol, Bartter syndrome. Haemochromatosis arthropathy also affects wrists, hips (rapid OA), ankles and the 4th\u20135th MCPs. Look for diabetes, cirrhosis, skin bronzing, hypogonadism and cardiomyopathy. Transferrin saturation >45% is the best screen; ferritin is also an acute-phase reactant. Treat the metabolic cause: magnesium replacement (may reduce attacks), parathyroidectomy (which can trigger a flare), venesection to ferritin about 50\u2013100 \u00b5g/L. Consider ANKH genetic testing in familial or early-onset disease without a metabolic cause. Pathophysiology: excess extracellular pyrophosphate (ePPi) from chondrocyte ATP via ENPP1 and the ANKH transporter combines with calcium. ALP degrades PPi, with magnesium as a cofactor, which explains the links with hypophosphatasia and hypomagnesaemia. Serum PPi is normal except in hypophosphatasia.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy","Metabolic Bone Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":184,"s":"An 82-year-old woman has acute CPP crystal arthritis of one knee. She has CKD stage 4 and heart failure. Septic arthritis has been excluded. What is the most appropriate treatment?","o":["High-dose oral NSAID with gastroprotection as first-line, since renal impairment is only a relative concern","Joint aspiration and intra-articular glucocorticoid injection","A short course of oral prednisolone","Urate-lowering therapy started immediately to dissolve the crystals","HLA-B*58:01 genotyping"],"a":1,"e":"Aspiration plus intra-articular glucocorticoid is optimal for acute CPP arthritis in a large joint, particularly when NSAIDs or colchicine are hazardous. There is no treatment that dissolves CPP crystals, and urate-lowering drugs have no role. Acute CPP arthritis: ice, rest, aspiration plus IA GC (firstline in a single large joint). Alternatives: oral or IM GC; lowdose colchicine; NSAID with gastroprotection (caution in older people); ACTH. Anakinra for severe, polyarticular or refractory attacks in hospitalised older patients; IL-6 blockade in case reports. No drug dissolves CPP crystals.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":185,"s":"A patient has frequent recurrent attacks of CPP crystal arthritis. What can reduce recurrence?","o":["Allopurinol, titrated to a target serum urate below 360 micromol/L","High-dose NSAID continued indefinitely regardless of renal function","Low-dose colchicine prophylaxis, if tolerated and renal function allows","Intra-articular glucocorticoid injection given prophylactically between attacks","Methotrexate as first-line prophylaxis, given its proven efficacy in a large randomised trial"],"a":2,"e":"Low-dose colchicine or a low-dose NSAID may reduce recurrent attacks. Methotrexate or hydroxychloroquine are occasionally used for chronic inflammatory CPPD. Chronic or recurrent CPP arthritis: low-dose colchicine (0.5\u20131 mg/day) or low-dose NSAID; low-dose GC; hydroxychloroquine. Methotrexate was no better than placebo in a small RCT, so its use is limited. Anakinra or tocilizumab for refractory disease (observational). OA with CPPD is managed as OA, and joint replacement outcomes are similar.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":186,"s":"A 56-year-old man has 9 months of activity-related knee pain and 10 minutes of morning stiffness. Examination shows crepitus without effusion. How should osteoarthritis be diagnosed?","o":["Clinically, without imaging","Only after MRI confirms cartilage loss","Only if synovial fluid analysis excludes inflammatory arthritis","BRAF V600E mutation analysis","Only in patients under 45 years of age"],"a":0,"e":"OA can be diagnosed clinically in people aged 45 or over with activity-related joint pain and either no morning stiffness or stiffness lasting 30 minutes or less. Imaging is reserved for atypical features or suspected alternative diagnoses. Red flags that need imaging or bloods: age <45; stiffness >30 minutes or a warm effusion (inflammatory arthritis, crystals); rapid progression or severe night or rest pain (AVN, SONK or insufficiency fracture, tumour, infection, rapidly destructive OA); constitutional symptoms; locking (mechanical meniscal tear or loose body); hip or spine referred pain to the knee (always examine the hip, especially in children with SUFE). X-ray features: focal joint-space narrowing, osteophytes, sclerosis, cysts. Kellgren\u2013Lawrence grade \u20212 is definite OA. Request weight-bearing knee films with a skyline view. Degenerative meniscal tears on MRI are usually incidental. OA synovial fluid is non-inflammatory (WCC <2,000/\u00b5L). Use WOMAC, KOOS/HOOS or the Oxford hip and knee scores to track outcome.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Paediatric Rheumatology","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":187,"s":"A 63-year-old woman with BMI 34 has knee OA with activity-related pain. Which intervention has the strongest evidence as core treatment?","o":["Graded aerobic and strengthening exercise","Bariatric surgery as first-line treatment, regardless of BMI or a trial of conservative care","Therapeutic exercise with weight loss and education","Intra-articular hyaluronic acid injection","Clinically, without imaging"],"a":2,"e":"Strengthening and aerobic exercise, weight loss where appropriate and education are core for everyone with OA, and improve pain and function regardless of radiographic severity. Exercise has an effect size similar to NSAIDs and lasts while the exercise continues. Weight loss of \u20215\u201310% gives clinically meaningful benefit, with more loss giving more benefit. In STEP 9, semaglutide reduced knee OA pain alongside weight loss. Education: OA is not inevitably progressive, exercise does not \"wear out\" the joint, and pacing helps. Structured programmes include GLA:D and ESCAPEpain. Exercise: quadriceps and hip abductor strengthening plus aerobic fitness; tai chi and aquatic exercise also help. Aids: a walking stick in the opposite hand, footwear and insoles, unloader braces for unicompartmental disease, OT aids. Adjuncts: manual therapy only alongside exercise. TENS, therapeutic ultrasound and laser are not recommended. Severe obesity: bariatric surgery.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":188,"s":"What is the first-line pharmacological option for knee OA and for symptomatic hand OA?","o":["Oral paracetamol taken regularly, as first-line","Oral strong opioids, for short-term flare control","Intra-articular hyaluronic acid injection, as first-line","Oral glucosamine sulphate, as first-line","A topical NSAID"],"a":4,"e":"topical NSAID Topical NSAIDs are first-line for knee and hand OA because of their safety and minimal systemic exposure. Oral NSAIDs are used at the lowest dose for the shortest time with gastroprotection, and oral analgesics generally for limited periods. Paracetamol alone and weak opioids are not routinely recommended for OA. Intra-articular steroids may be considered for painful interphalangeal joints. NSAID choice: naproxen or ibuprofen \u00a31.2 g/day carry lower CV risk; celecoxib carries lower GI risk (PRECISION). Avoid NSAIDs in CKD stage 4\u20135, heart failure and with anticoagulants. Adjuncts: topical capsaicin (knee and hand); duloxetine (widespread pain, depression or central sensitisation); paracetamol only short-term if NSAIDs are contraindicated (minimal benefit). Avoid strong and weak opioids. For hand OA: hand exercises, joint protection and OT, thumb-base splints, IA steroid for inflamed IP or CMC1 joints. Pharmaceutical-grade chondroitin has some support for hand OA pain in some guidance.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":189,"s":"What is the role of intra-articular glucocorticoid injection in knee OA?","o":["No benefit over placebo in any trial","A permanent disease-modifying effect with repeated injections","Routinely recommended every 4 weeks for maintenance thertherapy","It gives short-term relief, particularly for a flare with effusion, once infection is excluded","Triamcinolone hexacetonide"],"a":3,"e":"Intra-articular steroids give relief for weeks as an adjunct to core treatment. Frequent repeated injections are discouraged. Relief usually lasts 2\u20136 weeks (sometimes longer). Ultrasound guidance is needed for the hip. Repeated 3monthly triamcinolone was associated with greater cartilage loss on MRI (McAlindon). Rapidly progressive OA after injection has been reported. Avoid injection within about 3 months before arthroplasty (infection risk). Limit to 3\u20134 injections per joint per year. Extended-release triamcinolone (microspheres) prolongs knee relief with less glucose rise. Hyaluronic acid is not recommended by most guidelines (benefit close to placebo). PRP and stem cells are not recommended outside trials.","c":"Infection & Immunosuppression","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":190,"s":"What is the role of arthroscopic lavage and debridement in knee OA?","o":["A topical NSAID","Clearly superior to physiotherapy for degenerative meniscal tears (contradicted by FIDELITY and ESCAPE)","It is not used for OA pain because it is no better than sham surgery","Recommended first-line for all patients with mechanical symptoms","Effective for diffuse OA pain without mechanical locking"],"a":2,"e":"Trials show arthroscopic lavage or debridement does not help OA pain. Arthroscopy is reserved for true mechanical locking. True mechanical symptoms (locking from a displaced bucket-handle meniscal tear or loose body) are the exception where arthroscopy may help. Arthroscopic partial meniscectomy for degenerative tears offers no benefit over physiotherapy (FIDELITY, ESCAPE trials). Emerging options for refractory knee pain in patients unfit for surgery: genicular nerve radiofrequency ablation and genicular artery embolisation. Unicompartmental replacement and high tibial osteotomy suit younger, active patients with single-compartment disease. Cartilage repair is for focal defects in young patients, not established OA.","c":"Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","t":["Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":191,"s":"What is the role of strong opioids in osteoarthritis?","o":["First-line for moderate OA pain, given proven long-term efficacy","Preferred over NSAIDs because of a better long-term safety profile","Recommended for widespread OA pain with central sensitisation","They are not used routinely, because harms outweigh limited long-term benefit","Safe for long-term use provided renal function is monitored"],"a":3,"e":"Long-term opioids have little benefit for OA pain and substantial harms. Duloxetine may help selected patients with chronic pain and central sensitisation. Use opioids only in exceptional circumstances and shortterm (risks: falls, fractures, dependence, constipation, hypogonadism). Duloxetine, exercise, weight loss, CBT and pain management programmes are preferred for chronic OA pain with sensitisation features.","c":"Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","t":["Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":192,"s":"A 58-year-old woman has painful thumb-base OA limiting her work. Which non-pharmacological measure is particularly recommended?","o":["Intra-articular hyaluronic acid injection","Oral NSAIDs as first-line, before considering any splint","A thumb-base orthosis for long-term symptom relief, with hand exercises","Surgical trapeziectomy as the first-line intervention","A topical NSAID"],"a":2,"e":"thumb-base orthosis for long-term symptom relief, with hand exercises Orthoses for thumb-base OA, education, ergonomic advice and exercise improve function. Surgery is considered when symptoms persist despite conservative care. Radial-sided thumb pain: CMC1 OA (grind test, shoulder sign) vs de Quervain's (Finkelstein), scaphoid fracture or non-union, scaphotrapeziotrapezoid (STT) OA, radial sensory neuritis (Wartenberg), intersection syndrome, gout, and CPPD (wrist). Splints (prefabricated or custom) and education; IA steroid (US-guided) for flares. Surgery is trapeziectomy (with or without ligament reconstruction), with arthrodesis or arthroplasty as alternatives.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":193,"s":"A 61-year-old woman has erosive hand OA with central erosions and gull-wing deformities. She asks about disease-modifying drugs. What is the correct advice?","o":["Conventional and biologic DMARDs are not effective and are not used for hand OA","Methotrexate is first-line and reliably halts erosive progression","Hydroxychloroquine reliably reduces pain and erosive progression (the HERO trial)","TNF inhibitors are effective and licensed for erosive hand OA","A topical NSAID"],"a":0,"e":"Trials of hydroxychloroquine, methotrexate and TNF inhibitors in hand OA, including erosive disease, were negative. PsA (DIP, marginal \"mouse-ear\" erosions, periostitis, nails), multicentric reticulohistiocytosis (coral-bead papules, mutilans), gout (tophi on Heberden's nodes), RA (spares DIPs, marginal erosions). Erosive hand OA presents abruptly, often perimenopausally, with inflamed IP joints. CRP is normal, RF and ACPA are negative, and it can progress to ankylosis. It carries a higher pain and disability burden than nodal OA. HOPE trial: prednisolone 10 mg for 6 weeks reduced pain in inflammatory hand OA. A recent RCT found methotrexate reduced pain in hand OA with synovitis. Hydroxychloroquine (HERO) and TNF blockade were ineffective. Denosumab reduced erosive progression in phase 2 (not licensed for this). Otherwise manage as for nodal hand OA.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":194,"s":"A 71-year-old man has severe hip pain and disability from radiographic OA despite exercise, weight management, analgesia and a walking aid. What is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Referral for consideration of joint replacement after shared decision-making","Continue conservative management indefinitely, since age and comorbidity preclude surgery","Defer referral until radiographic severity reaches Kellgren-Lawrence grade 4","Intra-articular hyaluronic acid injection as an alternative to surgery","Bariatric surgery as a prerequisite before any orthopaedic referral"],"a":0,"e":"Joint replacement is considered when symptoms substantially affect quality of life despite non-surgical management. Age, BMI and comorbidity alone should not be barriers to referral. Refer before prolonged deterioration. Do not exclude patients on age, BMI, smoking or radiographic severity alone; optimise these factors instead. Before referral, exclude a hip source in knee pain (and vice versa) and lumbar spine referral. Optimise weight, diabetes, anaemia and dental health. Avoid IA steroid in the 3 months before surgery. Prehabilitation improves recovery.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":195,"s":"A patient with knee OA asks whether glucosamine will slow progression. What is the best advice?","o":["Strongly recommended, with clear evidence of slowed radiographic progression","Evidence is inconsistent and it is not recommended as a treatment for OA","Effective for rapid symptom relief within days","Proven to reduce the need for future joint replacement","A topical NSAID"],"a":1,"e":"Core treatment remains exercise, weight management and education, with topical NSAIDs as first-line medication. Independent trials (GAIT) show no clinically meaningful benefit from glucosamine or chondroitin for knee or hip OA; industry-sponsored crystalline glucosamine sulphate trials are more positive. Vitamin D supplements did not slow knee OA. Bisphosphonates, hydroxychloroquine, methotrexate and TNF or IL-1 blockers are not recommended routinely.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Metabolic Bone Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":196,"s":"A 42-year-old woman is diagnosed with fibromyalgia. Which treatment has the strongest evidence?","o":["Strong opioids, titrated to effect for persistent pain","Long-term glucocorticoids for widespread pain control","Clinically, without imaging","Growth hormone replacement for associated fatigue","Graded aerobic and strengthening exercise"],"a":4,"e":"Exercise has the strongest evidence. Low-dose amitriptyline, duloxetine and pregabalin have weaker evidence for selected patients; strong opioids, glucocorticoids and growth hormone are not recommended. Exercise is the only strong recommendation in EULAR guidance: graded aerobic and strengthening exercise, started low and progressed slowly, continued long term. CBT and ACT help mood, catastrophising and coping. Meditative movement, mindfulness, hydrotherapy and acupuncture have weak to moderate benefit. Drug Target Notes Amitriptyline 10\u201350 sleep, pain first choice; mg at night anticholinergic effects Duloxetine 60 mg, pain with nausea at the start milnacipran depression or fatigue Pregabalin pain with poor controlled drug in the UK; sleep or anxiety dependence risk sleep, pain US-licensed Cyclobenzaprine (sublingual, TNX-102 (2025) SL) Tramadol rescue serotonin syndrome risk with SNRIs or TCAs Not recommended: strong opioids (opioid-induced hyperalgesia), NSAIDs alone, benzodiazepines, glucocorticoids, sodium oxybate. NICE (chronic primary pain) advises antidepressants only, plus exercise, CBT or ACT, or acupuncture.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":197,"s":"A woman has a year of widespread pain, fatigue, unrefreshing sleep and cognitive symptoms, with no synovitis and normal inflammatory markers, CK and thyroid function. What is the diagnosis, and which findings fulfil the 2016 revised fibromyalgia criteria?","o":["Rheumatoid arthritis, since widespread pain and fatigue are typical presenting features","Polymyalgia rheumatica, given the age and symptom pattern described","Chronic fatigue syndrome, diagnosed once thyroid and inflammatory markers are normal","Myositis, given the degree of fatigue and widespread pain, even with a normal CK","Fibromyalgia, a positive clinical diagnosis: WPI 7 or more with SSS 5 or more (or WPI 4\u20136 with SSS 9 or more), generalised pain in at least 4 of 5 regions, for at least 3 months"],"a":4,"e":"Fibromyalgia is a positive clinical diagnosis. Example: widespread pain index 8, symptom severity score 6, and generalised pain in 4 of 5 regions for 4 months. Required: WPI 7 or more with SSS 5 or more (or WPI 4\u20136 with SSS 9 or more), generalised pain in at least 4 of 5 regions, and symptoms for at least 3 months. A fibromyalgia diagnosis does not exclude other conditions; coexisting disease should be considered, but symptom severity should not be equated with inflammation. Inflammatory: PMR (>50, raised CRP), RA, axSpA (especially in women), SLE, Sj\u00f6gren's, myositis (weakness, raised CK). Endocrine and metabolic: hypothyroidism, osteomalacia or low vitamin D, hyperparathyroidism, adrenal insufficiency, diabetes. Neurological: small-fibre neuropathy, MS, Parkinson's disease, myopathy. Drugs: statins, aromatase inhibitors, opioid-induced hyperalgesia, bisphosphonates, steroid withdrawal. Musculoskeletal: hypermobility spectrum disorder, generalised OA. Other: myeloma or metastases, sleep apnoea, depression, anaemia, HCV, HIV, coeliac disease, ME/CFS (post-exertional malaise). Fatigue is present in >75%, non-restorative sleep, \"fibro fog\", and mood disorder in 30\u201350% (common but not causal). Overlapping syndromes: IBS, migraine, TMD, bladder pain, vulvodynia, POTS. In the 2016 criteria, jaw, chest and abdomen do not count toward generalisation. A Fibromyalgia Severity score (WPI + SSS) \u202112 is consistent with FM. Screening tool: FiRST. The ACR 1990 criteria (\u202111/18 tender points) are obsolete. Tests: FBC, CRP or ESR, U&E, LFTs, bone profile, CK, TSH, vitamin D, ferritin, HbA1c, coeliac serology if GI symptoms. Avoid untargeted ANA and RF (low-titre ANA in about 10% of healthy people). Red flags for another diagnosis: fever, weight loss, abnormal bloods, objective weakness, synovitis, rash, neurological signs. Poor prognosis is predicted by catastrophising, depression, opioid use, litigation and delayed diagnosis.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Connective Tissue Disease","Metabolic Bone Disease","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":198,"s":"A woman can place her palms flat on the floor, hyperextend both elbows beyond 10 degrees and oppose both thumbs to the forearm (Beighton score 5\u20136), with widespread joint pain and fatigue but no skin fragility, family history or features of a heritable connective tissue disorder. Does she meet the adult hypermobility threshold, and how should she be managed?","o":["No; the threshold for adults up to 50 is 6 or more","No; a Beighton score of 5 only counts before puberty","Yes, and she meets full diagnostic criteria for classical Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","No, because skin fragility must also be present to score any hypermobility points","Yes (5 or more for adults up to 50); she has hypermobility spectrum disorder if hEDS criteria are not met, managed with explanation and physiotherapy for strength and proprioception"],"a":4,"e":"Scoring: palms flat 1, elbows 2 (one per side), thumbs 2 = 5. Thresholds are 6 or more before puberty, 5 or more from puberty to 50, and 4 or more over 50. A diagnosis of hypermobile EDS also requires systemic features and exclusion of alternatives. Symptomatic hypermobility that does not meet the 2017 hEDS criteria is classified as a hypermobility spectrum disorder. Management is de-medicalising reassurance, graded exercise, joint stabilisation and painmanagement strategies. Unlike vascular EDS or Marfan syndrome, there is no routine need for vascular surveillance. Other heritable hypermobility conditions: vascular EDS (thin translucent skin, arterial, bowel or uterine rupture), classical EDS (hyperextensible skin, atrophic scars), Marfan, Loeys-Dietz, Stickler, OI, kyphoscoliotic EDS. Acquired increases in range of movement: training (dancers), hypothyroidism, hyperparathyroidism. Beighton (9 points): little-finger MCP extension >90\u00b0 (1 per side), thumb to forearm (1 per side), elbow hyperextension >10\u00b0 (1 per side), knee hyperextension >10\u00b0 (1 per side), palms flat with knees straight (1). Associations of symptomatic hypermobility: chronic widespread pain, fatigue, POTS and dysautonomia, functional GI disorders, anxiety, recurrent subluxation, early OA. Graded strengthening, proprioception and core stability; pacing; CBT and pain management; avoid long-term opioids; treat POTS (fluids, salt, compression). Use orthoses and splints sparingly.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":199,"s":"A 59-year-old woman has lateral hip pain, worse lying on that side and climbing stairs, with tenderness over the greater trochanter. What is the most appropriate first-line management?","o":["Corticosteroid injection alone, without any exercise programme, as first-line","Referral for hip arthroscopy to debride the trochanteric bursa","Strict rest and avoidance of all weight-bearing activity for 6 weeks","Education with a progressive gluteal tendon loading exercise programme","Shockwave therapy as the first-line intervention, before any exercise trial"],"a":3,"e":"Greater trochanteric pain syndrome is usually gluteal tendinopathy. Education plus exercise outperformed corticosteroid injection at one year, while injection gives short-term relief with more recurrence. Lateral hip pain: gluteal tendinopathy or trochanteric bursitis vs hip OA (groin pain, reduced internal rotation), L4\u20135 radiculopathy, meralgia paraesthetica, ITB syndrome, femoral neck stress fracture, AVN, atypical femoral fracture (on bisphosphonates), SpA enthesitis. Pain lying on the affected side, pain on single-leg stance for 30 seconds, crossing the legs and stairs. It mostly affects women aged 40\u201360. LEAP trial: education plus exercise beat steroid injection at 52 weeks. Injection gives short-term relief with more recurrence. Shockwave therapy for refractory cases; gluteal tendon repair for full-thickness tears.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":200,"s":"How do you distinguish frozen shoulder from subacromial pain (for example a woman with diabetes and 4 months of global restriction of active and passive movement, versus a decorator with a painful arc and preserved passive external rotation), and how do treatment options compare?","o":["Frozen shoulder preserves passive range while subacromial pain restricts it equally in all directions","Frozen shoulder causes global loss of active and passive movement, most marked for external rotation with a normal radiograph; subacromial pain preserves passive range (especially external rotation). For persistent frozen shoulder, early structured physiotherapy with injection, manipulation under anaesthesia and arthroscopic capsular release give broadly similar 12-month outcomes","Manipulation under anaesthesia clearly outperforms physiotherapy and capsular release at 12 months","Subacromial pain causes global restriction of passive movement, unlike frozen shoulder","Both conditions require arthroscopic intervention as first-line treatment"],"a":1,"e":"Frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis) causes painful loss of passive movement, especially external rotation, with a normal radiograph; diabetes and thyroid disease are risk factors. In subacromial (rotator cuff-related) pain, passive range is preserved and active elevation is painful. Rotator cuff tears also preserve passive range. Night pain and pain on reaching behind the back occur in both. In the UK FROST trial, none of the three approaches gave a clinically important advantage at 12 months; manipulation was the most cost-effective option, while capsular release carried more serious adverse events. Choice depends on patient preference and local resources. Loss of passive external rotation: frozen shoulder, glenohumeral OA (crepitus; X-ray changes), posterior dislocation (missed after seizure or electric shock), locked fracture-dislocation, inflammatory arthritis, septic arthritis, Milwaukee shoulder, AVN of the humeral head. Painful arc: subacromial pain syndrome, calcific tendinitis, partial cuff tear, ACJ arthritis (high arc 160\u2013180\u00b0). Referred: C5 radiculopathy, Pancoast tumour, diaphragmatic pain, cardiac pain, PMR (bilateral, raised CRP). Frozen shoulder runs through freezing, frozen and thawing phases over 1\u20133 years. Associations: diabetes (up to 20%, often bilateral), thyroid disease, Dupuytren's, prolonged immobilisation, stroke, and checkpoint inhibitors. Early IA steroid injection plus physiotherapy (most effective early); hydrodilatation; MUA or arthroscopic capsular release if refractory. For subacromial pain: exercise-based physiotherapy first, subacromial steroid for short-term relief.","c":"Infection & Immunosuppression","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":201,"s":"A 54-year-old woman has nocturnal paraesthesia in the thumb, index and middle fingers with thenar wasting. Nerve conduction studies confirm severe median nerve compression at the wrist. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Switch to subcutaneous methotrexate","Repeat nerve conduction studies in 6 months before considering any intervention","A further trial of night splinting for 3 more months","Referral for surgical carpal tunnel decompression","A short oral corticosteroid course as an alternative to surgical referral"],"a":3,"e":"Thenar wasting or severe conduction changes indicate axonal loss and warrant decompression. Splinting or injection suits mild to moderate disease. Secondary causes include hypothyroidism, acromegaly, RA, pregnancy and amyloidosis. CTS mimics: C6\u2013C7 radiculopathy (neck pain, reflex change, sensory loss beyond the hand), pronator syndrome (thenar eminence sensation affected, no nocturnal symptoms), AIN syndrome (purely motor; cannot make the \"OK\" sign), thoracic outlet syndrome, polyneuropathy, MND (wasting without sensory loss), syringomyelia. Thenar eminence sensation is spared in CTS because the palmar cutaneous branch passes superficial to the retinaculum. Associations: pregnancy, diabetes, hypothyroidism, RA (flexor tenosynovitis), acromegaly, amyloid (ATTR, AL, dialysis b2-microglobulin), obesity, gout, wrist fracture. Phalen's, Tinel's and Durkan's tests; relief from flicking the hand. Mild to moderate disease: night splint and steroid injection (effective short to medium term). Thenar wasting, axonal loss on NCS or failure of conservative care: surgical release. Treat the underlying cause. In bilateral CTS in older men, consider screening for ATTR (echo, cardiac biomarkers, IIITc-DPD scintigraphy if suspected).","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":202,"s":"A 40-year-old man has 6 months of low back pain. Inflammatory markers and examination are normal, there are no red flags, and he has marked fear-avoidance and deconditioning. What is most appropriate?","o":["Explain the pain mechanism, support graded activity and rehabilitation, and address psychosocial factors while remaining alert to red flags","Urgent MRI of the lumbar spine to exclude structural pathology","Strict bed rest until symptoms fully resolve","Long-term opioid therapy titrated to pain scores","Urgent referral for epidural steroid injection as first-line treatment"],"a":0,"e":"Chronic non-specific back pain is best managed with education, graded activity and psychological approaches. Imaging without red flags rarely helps and can reinforce fear. STarT Back risk stratification. Explain the pain mechanism (pain does not equal damage); keep active and return to work; group exercise or graded activity; CBT-informed combined physical and psychological programmes for high-risk patients; NSAIDs short-term. Avoid opioids, gabapentinoids and benzodiazepines for chronic low back pain. No imaging without red flags.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":203,"s":"A woman 6\u20138 weeks after a wrist or distal radius fracture has burning pain out of proportion to the injury, allodynia, swelling, colour and sweating asymmetry and reduced finger movement; radiographs show only the healing fracture. What is the diagnosis and approach?","o":["Reflex sympathetic dystrophy from nerve entrapment at the fracture site, treated with surgical decompression","A somatic symptom disorder, best managed with prolonged immobilisation to prevent further injury","Complex regional pain syndrome (type 1), diagnosed clinically using the Budapest criteria; manage with early explanation and graded rehabilitation (physiotherapy or OT, desensitisation, graded motor imagery) with pain","Osteomyelitis, requiring urgent surgical debridement and IV antibiotics","Explain the pain mechanism, support graded activity and rehabilitation, and address psychosocial factors while remaining alert to red flags"],"a":2,"e":"management and psychological support CRPS is a clinical diagnosis. Type 1 has no definable nerve injury. Outcomes are best with early, graded rehabilitation, desensitisation, mirror or graded motor imagery, and psychological support, in a multidisciplinary approach. Prolonged immobilisation worsens it. Infection (cellulitis, osteomyelitis), DVT, fracture non-union, inflammatory arthritis, gout, peripheral nerve injury or entrapment, Raynaud's or vascular insufficiency, factitious disorder (tight tourniquet), disuse. Budapest criteria: continuing disproportionate pain; symptoms in \u20213 of 4 categories and signs in \u20212 of 4 categories (sensory: allodynia, hyperalgesia; vasomotor: temperature asymmetry >1 \u00b0C, colour; sudomotor or oedema; motor or trophic: reduced ROM, weakness, tremor, dystonia, hair, nail and skin changes); and no better explanation. CRPS I accounts for about 90% (CRPS II involves a major nerve injury). F:M is 3\u20134:1, peaking at 50\u2013 70 years. It follows 2\u20135% of distal radius fractures. \"Warm\" CRPS is early and inflammatory; \"cold\" CRPS carries a worse prognosis and often includes dystonia. The threestage model is no longer valid. About 70\u201380% improve within a year. Drugs: neuropathic agents; bisphosphonates (IV neridronate, pamidronate, zoledronate) in early CRPS with bone changes; short oral GC for early warm CRPS; topical lidocaine or capsaicin; IV ketamine in specialist centres. Avoid long-term opioids. NSAIDs are of limited value. Interventional: sympathetic blocks (limited evidence); spinal cord or dorsal root ganglion stimulation for refractory cases. Amputation is exceptional. Prevention: avoid tight casts, mobilise early. Vitamin C 500 mg for 50 days after distal radius fracture has mixed evidence. Supportive findings: spotty osteopenia on X-ray; periarticular uptake in the delayed phase of a bone scan.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":204,"s":"A 48-year-old woman has 9 months of subacromial pain with an intact rotator cuff, only partly helped by 3 months of exercise and one steroid injection. She asks about arthroscopic subacromial decompression. What is the most accurate advice?","o":["Early arthroscopic decompression gives a clear, lasting benefit over rehabilitation","Repeated steroid injections, continued indefinitely, are the mainstay of treatment","An MRI arthrogram is required before any further conservative treatment","Decompression has not outperformed placebo arthroscopy; continue progressive rehabilitation","Surgery should be offered now, since conservative treatment has already been tried for long enough"],"a":3,"e":"Placebo-controlled surgical trials found that arthroscopic subacromial decompression was no better than investigational arthroscopy alone, and neither gave a clinically important benefit over no treatment. Older practice of routine decompression for impingement has largely been abandoned. A progressive loading programme remains the mainstay. CSAW and FIMPACT trials showed arthroscopic subacromial decompression was no better than placebo or diagnostic arthroscopy. Progressive loading programme (scapular and cuff strengthening) for 12 weeks or more, with or without subacromial steroid or US-guided barbotage if calcific deposits are present. Refer to surgery for full-thickness tears, calcific deposits refractory to barbotage, or ACJ OA.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":205,"s":"Two weeks after a fall, a 56-year-old man cannot actively elevate the arm above 60 degrees and has weak external rotation, with near-full passive range. Ultrasound shows a full-thickness cuff tear with minimal retraction. What is the best management?","o":["Physiotherapy alone for at least 6 months before any imaging","Reassurance, since near-full passive range excludes a significant tear","Subacromial steroid injection, deferring any surgical opinion","Prompt shoulder surgical referral for consideration of early repair","Observation for 3 months to allow the tear to heal spontaneously"],"a":3,"e":"An acute traumatic full-thickness cuff tear with loss of active elevation in a fit patient should be referred early. Repair outcomes are better before tendon retraction and muscle atrophy develop. Delaying with injection or prolonged conservative care risks an irreparable tear. Weak active elevation with full passive range: cuff tear, suprascapular nerve palsy (spinoglenoid ganglion, traction), axillary nerve injury after dislocation (regimental badge sensory loss), neuralgic amyotrophy, C5 radiculopathy, pain inhibition (calcific tendinitis), deltoid avulsion. Drop-arm sign and external rotation lag sign. Acute tears often occur in people >40 after dislocation or a fall. Chronic degenerative tears in people >60 are often asymptomatic. Acute traumatic tear in a fit patient: ultrasound or MRI and surgical repair ideally within 3\u20136 weeks. Degenerative tears: physiotherapy first. Massive irreparable tears: reverse shoulder arthroplasty or tendon transfer.","c":"Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","t":["Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":206,"s":"An 82-year-old woman has a swollen, destroyed shoulder with a large blood-stained, non-inflammatory effusion and superior migration of the humeral head. Which crystals are most likely responsible?","o":["Monosodium urate crystals","No crystals; this is a purely degenerative process","Basic calcium phosphate (hydroxyapatite)","Gram stain and bacterial culture","Calcium pyrophosphate crystals"],"a":2,"e":"Milwaukee shoulder is a destructive arthropathy of elderly women with rotator cuff loss and large, often bloodstained effusions with low cell counts. Basic calcium phosphate crystals are implicated. They are not seen on routine polarised microscopy, although alizarin red staining can help. Destructive shoulder arthropathy in older people: Milwaukee shoulder (BCP), rapidly progressive OA, septic arthritis (low threshold to aspirate), neuropathic (Charcot) shoulder from syringomyelia (with loss of pain and temperature sensation in a cape distribution), AVN, CPPD destructive arthropathy, RA, amyloid (\"shoulder pad\" sign), haemophilic or anticoagulant haemarthrosis, metastasis. Analgesia, physiotherapy, repeated aspiration with steroid injection, tidal irrigation, reverse total shoulder arthroplasty for severe cases. Always send fluid for culture.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":207,"s":"A carpenter has 6 weeks of lateral elbow pain on gripping, with pain on resisted wrist extension. He requests a steroid injection to return to work quickly. What is the most appropriate advice?","o":["Immediate steroid injection, since this gives the best long-term outcome","Surgical release of the common extensor origin as first-line treatment","Education and progressive loading; injection helps briefly but worsens 1-year outcome","Complete rest from gripping activities for 6 months, avoiding any loading exercise","Education with a progressive gluteal tendon loading exercise programme"],"a":2,"e":"Lateral elbow tendinopathy is usually self-limiting. Randomised data show that corticosteroid injection improves pain at 4 to 6 weeks but leads to poorer recovery and higher recurrence at 1 year than placebo. Older texts recommended early injection; current practice favours education and progressive loading. Bilateral or multisite enthesitis should prompt consideration of spondyloarthritis. Lateral elbow pain: tendinopathy vs radial tunnel syndrome (pain 3\u20135 cm distal to the epicondyle, middle finger test, no weakness; coexists in about 5%), PIN syndrome (finger drop without sensory loss), radiocapitellar OA, plica, posterolateral instability, C6 radiculopathy, SpA enthesitis. Load management, forearm strap, isometric then eccentric loading. Most resolve within 1\u20132 years. Shockwave therapy has mixed evidence. Surgery is rarely needed. PRP evidence is mixed.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":208,"s":"A 45-year-old plumber has a hot, red, tender swelling over the olecranon, with surrounding cellulitis and a temperature of 38.2\u00b0C. A small abrasion is present over the bursa. What is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Intra-articular (not bursal) aspiration with empirical IV antibiotics","Oral NSAIDs alone, without aspiration, since the abrasion is unlikely to be significant","Immediate corticosteroid injection into the bursa for rapid symptom relief","Oral antibiotics without aspiration, reserving aspiration for cases that fail to improve","Aspirate for Gram stain, culture and crystals; start antistaphylococcal antibiotic"],"a":4,"e":"Septic olecranon bursitis is common after skin breaches in manual workers and is usually due to Staphylococcus aureus. Fluid should be sent for culture and polarised microscopy, since gout is the main alternative. Corticosteroid must not be injected when infection is possible. Olecranon swelling: septic bursitis (S. aureus; also streptococci), gout (tophi, crystals), RA nodules or bursitis, traumatic or haemorrhagic bursitis, CPPD, TB or atypical mycobacteria (chronic), uraemic bursitis (dialysis). Fluid WCC in septic bursitis is often lower than in septic arthritis (>2,000\u20135,000/\u00b5L is suspicious). Gram stain sensitivity is moderate. Aspirate (avoiding a direct track through cellulitic skin), then oral antibiotics if mild and IV if systemically unwell or immunosuppressed. Repeat aspiration as needed. Surgical bursectomy for refractory or recurrent septic bursitis. Nonseptic, non-crystal bursitis: compression and NSAIDs; consider steroid only once infection is excluded.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":209,"s":"A 40-year-old cyclist has paraesthesia of the little finger and the ulnar half of the ring finger, with weakness of finger abduction. Which clinical finding best localises the ulnar nerve lesion to the elbow rather than to Guyon's canal at the wrist?","o":["The A1 pulley at the metacarpal head","Weakness of thumb opposition and abductor pollicis brevis","Preserved sensation over the dorsal ulnar hand, regardless of lesion site","Sensory loss over the dorsal ulnar aspect of the hand","Median nerve at the carpal tunnel"],"a":3,"e":"The dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch arises proximal to the wrist and does not pass through Guyon's canal. Loss of dorsal ulnar hand sensation therefore indicates a lesion proximal to the wrist, most often cubital tunnel syndrome. Intrinsic weakness, Froment's sign and palmar sensory loss occur with lesions at either site. C8\u2013T1 radiculopathy (medial forearm sensory loss via the medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve), thoracic outlet syndrome, Pancoast tumour (Horner's syndrome), MND, HNPP. Cubital tunnel (the second commonest entrapment): symptoms worse with elbow flexion; intrinsic wasting, Froment's and Wartenberg's signs, clawing (often less with a proximal lesion because FDP to the ring and little fingers is also weak, the \"ulnar paradox\"). Guyon's canal causes: cycling (\"handlebar palsy\"), ganglion, hook of hamate fracture, ulnar artery thrombosis (\"hypothenar hammer\"). Guyon's canal lesions spare the dorsal ulnar hand. Avoid prolonged elbow flexion (night extension splint, elbow pad). Surgery (in situ decompression or transposition) for weakness or refractory symptoms.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":210,"s":"A 32-year-old woman, 3 months after the birth of her first child, has radial wrist pain when lifting the baby. Ulnar deviation of the wrist with the thumb flexed into the palm reproduces the pain. Which tendons are affected?","o":["Flexor digitorum superficialis and profundus","Warfarin anticoagulation","Flexor carpi radialis and palmaris longus","Extensor carpi ulnaris and extensor digiti minimi","Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis"],"a":4,"e":"De Quervain's tenosynovitis affects the first extensor compartment (APL and EPB). It is common in new parents and is reproduced by Finkelstein-type manoeuvres. First-line treatment is activity modification, a thumb spica splint and corticosteroid injection into the tendon sheath. Radial wrist pain in a new parent: de Quervain's vs CMC1 OA (grind test), scaphoid fracture, intersection syndrome (pain 4\u20136 cm proximal to the wrist with crepitus, involving the 2nd compartment where APL and EPB cross ECRL and ECRB), Wartenberg's syndrome (superficial radial nerve; dorsoradial paraesthesia, Tinel's sign), inflammatory tenosynovitis (RA), post-partum CTS. Thumb spica splint and steroid injection (very effective, often first-line). Surgical release of the first compartment if refractory (look for a separate EPB subsheath).","c":"Paediatric Rheumatology","t":["Paediatric Rheumatology"]},{"id":211,"s":"A 58-year-old man with diabetes has painful clicking and intermittent locking of the ring finger in flexion. A tender nodule is palpable at the base of the finger in the palm. At which structure does the tendon catch?","o":["The flexor digitorum superficialis at the PIP joint","Diabetic cheiroarthropathy (limited joint mobility)","The extensor tendon at the MCP joint","The A1 pulley at the metacarpal head","Development of MALT lymphoma"],"a":3,"e":"Trigger finger is stenosing tenosynovitis of the flexor tendon at the A1 pulley. Steroid injection into the tendon sheath is effective first-line treatment, although response is lower in diabetes. Percutaneous or open A1 pulley release is used if injection fails. Associations: diabetes (often multiple digits, poorer injection response), RA (flexor tenosynovitis), hypothyroidism, amyloid, gout, Dupuytren's. Paediatric trigger thumb is a different entity (Notta's node). Steroid injection (about 60\u201390% success, lower in diabetes); repeat once if needed. Percutaneous or open A1 pulley release. In RA, avoid A1 release because it worsens ulnar drift; synovectomy is preferred.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy","Paediatric Rheumatology"]},{"id":212,"s":"A 66-year-old man has Dupuytren's disease of both palms. He has a 35-degree fixed flexion deformity at the right ring finger MCP joint and cannot lay the right palm flat on a table. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Serial splinting in extension to gradually correct the contracture","Intralesional collagenase injection, now first-line in the UK","Refer to a hand surgeon to discuss needle fasciotomy or limited fasciectomy","Observation only, since Dupuytren's contracture rarely progresses","Low-dose radiotherapy as the first-line treatment for this degree of contracture"],"a":2,"e":"A positive tabletop test, an MCP contracture of about 30 degrees or any PIP contracture with functional impairment are usual triggers for referral. Options include percutaneous needle fasciotomy and limited fasciectomy, with dermofasciectomy for recurrent disease. Splinting alone does not correct established contracture. Palmar thickening and contracture: diabetic cheiroarthropathy (prayer sign, waxy skin), palmar fasciitis and polyarthritis (paraneoplastic, rapid onset, ovarian cancer), SSc sclerodactyly, trigger finger, camptodactyly, flexor tendon adhesion, ulnar nerve claw hand. Risk factors: men of northern European ancestry, family history (autosomal dominant with variable penetrance), diabetes, alcohol, smoking, epilepsy drugs (phenobarbital), manual work. Dupuytren's diathesis (Garrod's knuckle pads, Ledderhose plantar fibromatosis, Peyronie's disease) predicts aggressive, recurrent disease. Ring and little fingers are most affected. Observe while function is preserved. Intervene with a positive tabletop test, MCP contracture of about 30\u00b0 or any functionally limiting PIP contracture. Options: needle fasciotomy (quicker recovery, higher recurrence), limited fasciectomy, dermofasciectomy for recurrence. Collagenase is no longer available in Europe. Low-dose radiotherapy can be used for early progressive disease (NICE IPG).","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":213,"s":"An obese man has burning pain and numbness over the anterolateral thigh, worse with a tight belt. Power, knee reflex and straight leg raise are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["L3 radiculopathy","Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve entrapment","Femoral neuropathy","Femoroacetabular impingement (cam type)","Small-fibre neuropathy"],"a":1,"e":"The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (L2-L3) is purely sensory and can be compressed near the anterior superior iliac spine. Risk factors include obesity, pregnancy, tight belts and diabetes. Normal motor findings and reflexes distinguish it from L3 radiculopathy and femoral neuropathy. L2\u2013L3 radiculopathy (weak hip flexion and adduction), femoral neuropathy (weak quadriceps, absent knee jerk; consider retroperitoneal haematoma on anticoagulation, needing urgent CT), diabetic amyotrophy (proximal pain then weakness and weight loss), hip disease, pelvic tumour. Meralgia paraesthetica (LFCN, L2\u2013L3) is purely sensory: anterolateral thigh burning, worse standing or walking. Causes: obesity, tight belts or trousers, pregnancy, diabetes, prone surgery, iliac crest bone graft, seat-belt trauma. It is often self-limiting. Reassurance, weight loss, loose clothing, neuropathic agents, US-guided nerve block (diagnostic and therapeutic), and rarely surgical decompression or neurectomy.","c":"Pregnancy & Reproductive","t":["Pregnancy & Reproductive"]},{"id":214,"s":"A 50-year-old woman who wears narrow high-heeled shoes has burning forefoot pain radiating into the third and fourth toes. Squeezing the forefoot while pressing the third web space produces a painful click. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Interdigital (Morton's) neuroma","Tarsal tunnel syndrome","SAPHO syndrome","Small-fibre neuropathy","Greater trochanteric pain syndrome"],"a":0,"e":"Morton's neuroma is perineural fibrosis of the common digital nerve, usually in the third web space. Mulder's click is characteristic. Management includes wider footwear, a metatarsal pad, steroid injection and, if refractory, excision. Forefoot pain: Morton's neuroma vs MTP synovitis (RA, often early and with a positive squeeze test), intermetatarsal bursitis, metatarsal stress fracture, Freiberg's infraction (AVN of the 2nd metatarsal head; adolescent girls), plantar plate tear (2nd toe deviation), metatarsalgia from fat pad atrophy or claw toes, tarsal tunnel syndrome, peripheral neuropathy, gout. Wide toe box, metatarsal pad and US-guided steroid injection. Alcohol sclerosis or radiofrequency ablation has variable evidence. Neurectomy if refractory (risk of stump neuroma). Ultrasound shows a hypoechoic mass.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":215,"s":"A 44-year-old runner has burning paraesthesia over the sole of the foot, worse after prolonged standing. Tapping behind and below the medial malleolus reproduces the symptoms. Which nerve is most likely compressed?","o":["Posterior tibial nerve under the flexor retinaculum","Tibialis posterior tendon","Sural nerve at the lateral ankle","Median nerve at the carpal tunnel","Superficial peroneal nerve"],"a":0,"e":"Tarsal tunnel syndrome is compression of the posterior tibial nerve or its plantar branches beneath the flexor retinaculum. Causes include ganglia, tenosynovitis and varicosities. Nerve conduction studies and MRI or ultrasound help confirm the cause. Burning sole pain: tarsal tunnel syndrome vs plantar fasciopathy, S1 radiculopathy, length-dependent polyneuropathy (bilateral; check glucose, B12, alcohol, paraprotein, thyroid), small-fibre neuropathy (Sj\u00f6gren's, sarcoid, amyloid), Baxter's nerve entrapment (first branch of the lateral plantar nerve; medial heel pain), calcaneal stress fracture, erythromelalgia. Arch-support orthoses; treat tenosynovitis (RA, SpA); NSAIDs and neuropathic agents; injection; ultrasound or MRI for a mass; surgical release (variable results).","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":216,"s":"A 46-year-old teacher has 4 months of plantar heel pain that is worst with the first steps in the morning. She is tender at the medial calcaneal tubercle. There are no features of spondyloarthritis. What is the most appropriate first-line management?","o":["Immediate corticosteroid injection through the plantar skin as first-line treatment","Urgent MRI to exclude a calcaneal stress fracture before any treatment","Complete non-weight-bearing rest in a cast for 6 weeks","Referral for surgical fasciotomy as first-line treatment","Plantar fascia and calf stretching, supportive footwear and load modification"],"a":4,"e":"Most plantar heel pain settles within a year with conservative measures. Steroid injection gives short-term benefit but risks fat-pad atrophy and fascial rupture, and should never be given through the plantar skin. Imaging is reserved for atypical or refractory cases. Plantar heel pain: plantar fasciopathy vs SpA enthesitis (bilateral, enthesitis elsewhere, raised CRP), calcaneal stress fracture (squeeze test positive), fat pad atrophy (central heel, older people), Baxter's nerve entrapment, tarsal tunnel syndrome, S1 radiculopathy, calcaneal tumour or infection, Sever's disease in children (calcaneal apophysitis). Plantar fascia-specific and calf stretching, heel cups or insoles, night splints, weight loss, load modification. Shockwave therapy for refractory cases. Steroid injection gives short-term benefit (risks fat pad atrophy and rupture; give it via a medial approach). Most cases resolve within 12 months. Surgery is rarely needed.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Paediatric Rheumatology","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":217,"s":"A 68-year-old man taking prednisolone 10 mg daily for polymyalgia rheumatica is prescribed levofloxacin for pneumonia. Which complication is particularly increased by this combination?","o":["Atypical femoral fracture","Peptic ulceration with gastrointestinal bleeding","Low-dose aspirin (75-100 mg daily)","Achilles tendinopathy and tendon rupture","QT prolongation and ventricular arrhythmia"],"a":3,"e":"Fluoroquinolones increase the risk of tendinopathy and tendon rupture, especially of the Achilles tendon, and the risk is greatest in older people and with concomitant corticosteroids. Their use is restricted, and patients should be told to stop the drug at the first sign of tendon pain. Other drug causes of tendinopathy: statins (rare), aromatase inhibitors (tenosynovitis, trigger finger), local steroid injection, anabolic steroids, isotretinoin. Patient factors: age >60, renal failure, transplant recipients, RA, diabetes, gout. Avoid fluoroquinolones unless there is no alternative, particularly with concomitant GC (MHRA advice). If tendon pain develops: stop the drug, rest the tendon and get ultrasound. A ruptured Achilles needs orthopaedic review (Simmonds' test).","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":218,"s":"A 64-year-old woman with knee osteoarthritis develops sudden pain and swelling of the right calf after gardening. A crescent of bruising appears below the medial malleolus. What is the most appropriate next investigation?","o":["Compression ultrasound of the leg","High-resolution CT chest","CT of the chest","MRI of the lumbar spine","D-dimer alone, without imaging"],"a":0,"e":"A ruptured popliteal (Baker's) cyst mimics DVT, and the two can coexist. Ultrasound distinguishes them. The 'crescent sign' of bruising around the malleolus suggests cyst rupture, but DVT must still be excluded before treatment. Acute calf pain: DVT, ruptured Baker's cyst (\"pseudothrombophlebitis\"), gastrocnemius (medial head) tear (\"tennis leg\"), plantaris rupture, compartment syndrome, cellulitis, superficial thrombophlebitis, haematoma on anticoagulation, popliteal artery aneurysm thrombosis (pulses), myositis. Compression ultrasound. Treat the underlying knee synovitis (aspiration and IA steroid). Analgesia and elevation for cyst rupture. Anticoagulate if DVT is confirmed (both can coexist).","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":219,"s":"A 24-year-old footballer has 6 months of deep groin pain on sitting and during kicking. Pain is reproduced by passive hip flexion, adduction and internal rotation. Radiographs show a non-spherical femoral head-neck junction. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Transient osteoporosis of the hip","Rotator cuff-related shoulder pain","Adhesive capsulitis","Greater trochanteric pain syndrome","Femoroacetabular impingement (cam type)"],"a":4,"e":"A positive FADIR test with a cam deformity indicates femoroacetabular impingement, which can cause labral and chondral damage. Management starts with activity modification and physiotherapy; arthroscopic surgery is considered for persistent symptoms. SUFE presents in younger adolescents. Young adult hip or groin pain: FAI (cam or pincer) with labral tear vs adductor-related groin pain, inguinal-related pain (sportsman's hernia), osteitis pubis, femoral neck stress fracture (urgent MRI; tension-side fractures need surgery), AVN, SUFE (adolescents), DDH, iliopsoas tendinopathy or bursitis (\"snapping hip\"), axSpA, sacroiliitis, referred lumbar pain. FADIR (flexion, adduction, internal rotation) is sensitive but not specific. X-ray shows a cam deformity (non-spherical head\u2013neck junction, raised alpha angle) or pincer deformity (crossover sign, acetabular over-coverage). MR arthrogram assesses the labrum and cartilage. Activity modification and physiotherapy (core and hip strengthening). Intra-articular injection can be diagnostic. Hip arthroscopy (FASHIoN and UK FAIT trials showed modest benefit over physiotherapy) for persistent symptoms without significant OA.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Metabolic Bone Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":220,"s":"A patient with low back pain develops bilateral sciatica and new perineal or saddle sensory loss, with or without urinary retention. What is the most appropriate action, and which other back pain features are red flags?","o":["Emergency lumbar MRI immediately, at any time of day, with same-day spinal surgical referral; other red flags are fever or immunosuppression (infection), weight loss or a cancer history (malignancy), significant trauma or osteoporosis (fracture), and progressive neurological deficit","Outpatient MRI within 2 weeks, with routine neurosurgical referral","Reassurance if a bladder scan shows no retention","Urgent lumbar X-ray as the first investigation of choice","Observation overnight with a repeat examination in the morning"],"a":0,"e":"Suspected cauda equina syndrome is a surgical emergency, and delay worsens bladder, bowel and sexual outcomes. Outcomes are best when decompression occurs before retention (incomplete CES, CES-I); established retention (CES-R) carries a worse prognosis. Emergency MRI is indicated regardless of time of day, and a normal bladder scan does not exclude the diagnosis. CES mimics and causes: central disc herniation (the commonest cause, at L4/5 or L5/S1), spinal stenosis with acute decompensation, epidural abscess or haematoma (anticoagulation, post-procedure), metastatic cord or cauda equina compression, AS with dural ectasia (arachnoid diverticula), conus medullaris lesion, GBS, transverse myelitis (SLE, Sj\u00f6gren's, NMOSD), functional neurological disorder. Non-spinal back pain: AAA, renal colic or pyelonephritis, pancreatitis, gynaecological disease or endometriosis, hip OA, herpes zoster. CES features: bilateral leg symptoms, saddle or perineal anaesthesia, new bladder dysfunction (reduced sensation, poor stream, retention), bowel incontinence, sexual dysfunction, reduced anal tone. A history of cancer is the most specific red flag; others have low specificity individually. The national pathway (GIRFT) aims for MRI within about 4 hours. Do a post-void bladder scan, but do not wait for retention. Red-flag bloods: FBC, CRP or ESR, protein electrophoresis and free light chains, calcium, ALP, PSA. Spondylodiscitis: blood cultures and biopsy before antibiotics if stable, and treat for at least 6 weeks. Metastatic spinal cord compression: dexamethasone and urgent oncology.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Metabolic Bone Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":221,"s":"A 74-year-old man has bilateral buttock and thigh aching after walking 200 m. It is relieved by sitting or leaning forward on a shopping trolley but not by standing still. Foot pulses are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Peripheral arterial disease (vascular claudication)","Bilateral hip osteoarthritis","Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve entrapment","Osteoporotic insufficiency fracture of the sacrum","Lumbar spinal stenosis (neurogenic claudication)"],"a":4,"e":"Neurogenic claudication is relieved by lumbar flexion (sitting, stooping) rather than by simply stopping, and pulses are preserved. Vascular claudication eases on standing still. MRI confirms canal stenosis; decompression is considered for disabling or progressive symptoms. Hip OA (groin pain, reduced internal rotation), trochanteric pain syndrome, peripheral neuropathy, venous claudication, spinal AVM, ATTR amyloid (lumbar stenosis is an early \"red flag\" feature). Feature Neurogenic Vascular Relief sitting or flexion standing still (minutes) (seconds to minutes) Posture worse with irrelevant extension or standing Cycling, uphill tolerated provokes walking symptoms Pulses and normal reduced ABPI Sensory common uncommon symptoms Flexion-based physiotherapy, walking or cycling programme, weight loss, analgesia. Epidural steroids give little benefit in stenosis. Decompression (laminectomy) beats continued conservative care for severe, refractory symptoms. Adding fusion is usually unnecessary without instability (Swedish Spinal Stenosis Study).","c":"Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","t":["Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":222,"s":"A 29-year-old man has 3 weeks of L5 sciatica with no neurological deficit or red flags. His GP started pregabalin and diazepam. What is the most appropriate advice?","o":["Continue both and add amitriptyline for neuropathic pain","Increase the pregabalin dose and add a short course of oral corticosteroids","Continue diazepam long-term for muscle spasm, in addition to pregabalin","Explain the pain mechanism, support graded activity and rehabilitation, and address psychosocial factors while remaining alert to red flags","Stop both; use an NSAID if needed, keep active, consider epidural steroid if severe"],"a":4,"e":"Gabapentinoids, other antiepileptics, oral corticosteroids and benzodiazepines are not recommended for sciatica because of lack of benefit and harms. Older texts suggested diazepam for spasm; this is no longer advised. Most disc-related sciatica improves within 3 months. Sciatica has a lifetime incidence of 13\u201340% and peaks at 45\u201364. Most cases improve over 6\u201312 weeks; large sequestered fragments often regress. 10\u201320% still have symptoms at 1 year. SLR at 30\u201370\u00b0 has about 90% sensitivity; crossed SLR has about 90% specificity for disc herniation. The femoral stretch test assesses L2\u2013L4. Stay active and do physiotherapy. Short-course NSAIDs. Image-guided epidural steroid for acute severe sciatica (short-term relief only; does not reduce surgery). Pregabalin was no better than placebo (PRECISE). Use amitriptyline or duloxetine only selectively, with stopping rules. Microdiscectomy for CES, progressive motor deficit, or disabling pain beyond 6\u201312 weeks with matching MRI: it gives faster relief, but 1\u20132-year outcomes are similar.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":223,"s":"A man has back pain radiating to the dorsum of the foot, weak great toe extension and ankle dorsiflexion, dorsal foot sensory loss and normal reflexes. Which nerve root is affected?","o":["S1","L4","L5","Median nerve at the carpal tunnel","Tibialis posterior tendon"],"a":2,"e":"L5 radiculopathy, usually from an L4/5 disc, causes weakness of EHL, ankle dorsiflexion and hip abduction, with dorsal foot and great toe sensory loss; the major reflexes are usually preserved. A reduced ankle reflex with weak plantarflexion and lateral foot sensory loss indicates S1. Foot drop: L5 radiculopathy (inversion and hip abduction weak, back pain) vs common peroneal neuropathy (inversion spared), sciatic neuropathy (piriformis or deep gluteal syndrome), lumbosacral plexopathy (tumour, haematoma, diabetic amyotrophy), mononeuritis multiplex (vasculitis), MND, HNPP, parasagittal cortical lesion (UMN signs). Root Weakness Sensory Reflex L3 knee extension, hip anterior thigh knee adduction to medial knee L4 knee extension, ankle medial shin knee dorsiflexion and inversion L5 EHL, ankle (medial dorsum of dorsiflexion, hip foot, great hamstring) abduction, inversion toe S1 plantarflexion, lateral foot, ankle eversion, hip sole extension","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis"]},{"id":224,"s":"A 15-year-old fast bowler has 2 months of low back pain that is worse on extension and on standing on one leg while extending the spine. Neurology is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Disc herniation (flexion-related pain)","Scheuermann's disease","Spondylolysis (pars interarticularis stress fracture)","Facet joint syndrome","Osteoid osteoma"],"a":2,"e":"Pars stress fractures, usually at L5, occur in adolescents who repeatedly hyperextend, such as fast bowlers and gymnasts. MRI (or SPECT/CT) detects early stress reaction. Management is activity modification and rehabilitation; bracing is sometimes used. Adolescent extension-related pain: spondylolysis vs facet irritation, Scheuermann's, disc herniation (flexion-related), osteoid osteoma (night pain relieved by NSAIDs), ERA or juvenile SpA, discitis (young children who refuse to walk), tumour (Ewing's, leukaemia, ABC). Pars stress fractures (usually L5) occur in extension sports, with prevalence about 6%. Progression risk is higher in girls, dysplastic slips, high-grade slips and during the growth spurt. Meyerding grades I\u2013V. The \"one-legged hyperextension (stork) test\" is positive. Rest from sport for 6\u201312 weeks; core and hamstring physiotherapy; a brace for early (acute) lysis to promote healing; fusion for high-grade (III\u2013IV), progressive or neurologically symptomatic slips.","c":"Paediatric Rheumatology","t":["Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":225,"s":"A 16-year-old boy has a rigid thoracic kyphosis that does not correct on extension. Radiographs show wedging of three adjacent vertebrae with Schmorl's nodes. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Enthesitis-related arthritis","Hyperparathyroidism","Scheuermann's disease","Congenital kyphosis","Postural kyphosis"],"a":2,"e":"Scheuermann's kyphosis is a structural adolescent kyphosis defined by wedging in at least three adjacent vertebrae. Postural kyphosis corrects on extension. Most cases are managed with extension-focused physiotherapy; bracing or surgery is reserved for severe deformity. Postural kyphosis (corrects on extension), congenital kyphosis, AS (older, sacroiliitis), osteoporotic wedge fractures, infection (TB gibbus), neuromuscular disease. Scheuermann's: wedging \u20215\u00b0 of \u20213 adjacent vertebrae, endplate irregularity, Schmorl's nodes, rigid thoracic kyphosis >45\u00b0. Extension-focused physiotherapy; a brace while still growing; surgery for severe deformity (>70\u201375\u00b0) or intractable pain.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Metabolic Bone Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":226,"s":"A 61-year-old woman has chronic low back pain without sciatica. Non-surgical treatment has failed, and clinical assessment suggests facet joint origin. Her pain is moderate to severe. Which intervention is appropriate to consider?","o":["Lumbar fusion surgery as first-line treatment","Spinal cord stimulation, without a prior diagnostic block","Discography to confirm the diagnosis before any intervention","Facet joint steroid injection alone, repeated routinely every 3 months","Radiofrequency denervation after a positive medial branch block"],"a":4,"e":"Radiofrequency denervation can be considered for chronic low back pain thought to arise from the facet joints, but only after a positive diagnostic medial branch block. Facet steroid injections have not shown benefit over placebo, and spinal injections are not recommended for non-specific low back pain. Facet RF denervation (NICE NG59) is used only after a positive diagnostic medial branch block and failed nonsurgical care; benefit in RCTs is mixed (MINT). Other interventions: epidural steroid for acute severe sciatica only; basivertebral nerve ablation for Modic-related chronic LBP (emerging); spinal cord stimulation for persistent spinal pain after surgery with neuropathic leg pain. Do not offer facet steroid injections, discography, fusion or disc replacement for non-specific LBP outside research.","c":"General Rheumatology","t":["General Rheumatology"]},{"id":227,"s":"A 44-year-old woman has 18 months of chronic primary pain without an identifiable underlying condition. She is taking codeine, pregabalin and naproxen with little benefit. Which management is most appropriate?","o":["Increase the opioid dose and add a benzodiazepine for sleep","Exercise and psychological therapy, considering an antidepressant, and tapering analgesics","Continue all three drugs unchanged while adding gabapentin","Start a biologic DMARD, since conventional analgesics have failed","Refer for spinal fusion surgery given the chronicity of symptoms"],"a":1,"e":"For chronic primary pain, current guidance supports exercise programmes, psychological therapies and acupuncture, and allows consideration of selected antidepressants. Opioids, gabapentinoids, NSAIDs, paracetamol and benzodiazepines should not be started, and existing ones should be reviewed and tapered through shared decisionmaking. NICE NG193 chronic primary pain options: supervised group exercise, CBT or ACT, acupuncture (within a course), and antidepressants (amitriptyline, citalopram, duloxetine, fluoxetine, paroxetine, sertraline). Do not start gabapentinoids, opioids, NSAIDs, paracetamol, benzodiazepines, local anaesthetic or steroid injections, or ketamine. Taper existing opioids and gabapentinoids gradually (about 10% every 2\u20134 weeks) with shared goals; watch for withdrawal and mood change. Consider a pain management programme. Screen for depression and suicide risk.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":228,"s":"A 72-year-old woman has a painful acute L1 osteoporotic compression fracture. What does early management involve, and when is vertebral augmentation appropriate?","o":["Analgesia and mobilisation; augmentation only for severe persistent pain at a recent fracture","Long-term daily calcitonin for pain control","Immediate vertebroplasty for all acute compression fractures regardless of severity","Strict bed rest for 6 weeks to allow fracture healing","High-dose oral glucocorticoids to reduce inflammation at the fracture site"],"a":0,"e":"Older texts recommended calcitonin for fracture pain, but long-term calcitonin is no longer used because of an increased malignancy risk. Vertebral augmentation is limited to severe persistent pain at a recent fracture confirmed on MRI. Osteoporosis treatment and falls assessment should follow. Acute vertebral fracture: osteoporosis vs metastasis or myeloma (posterior element involvement, convex posterior border and soft-tissue mass on MRI, pedicle destruction, above T5), infection, trauma. Low-energy fracture in a younger patient or a man warrants a secondary osteoporosis screen. Many vertebral fractures are silent (height loss >4 cm, kyphosis). Vertebral fracture assessment on DXA or a lateral spine film detects them. Analgesia (paracetamol, short NSAIDs or opioids with laxatives), early mobilisation, physiotherapy, a short spinal brace in selected patients. NICE TA279: augmentation (vertebroplasty or kyphoplasty) only for severe ongoing pain after a recent fracture confirmed on MRI despite optimal analgesia; sham-controlled trials were mixed. Start osteoporosis treatment (often an anabolic agent if there are multiple vertebral fractures) and a falls assessment.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":229,"s":"What DXA T-score defines osteoporosis in a postmenopausal woman?","o":["-2.5 or lower","Avacopan","Enthesitis-related arthritis","-1.0 to -2.5","3 points; he meets the criteria"],"a":0,"e":"A T-score of -2.5 or lower at the lumbar spine, femoral neck or total hip defines osteoporosis; -1.0 to -2.5 is osteopenia. T-scores apply to postmenopausal women and men aged 50 or over. Low BMD is not always osteoporosis: osteomalacia (raised ALP, low phosphate or calcium, Looser zones), myeloma, hyperparathyroidism, renal osteodystrophy, OI, hypophosphatasia, mastocytosis, Gaucher's. Severe or established osteoporosis means T \u00a3-2.5 plus a fragility fracture. A hip or vertebral fragility fracture establishes osteoporosis clinically regardless of BMD. Fractures of the skull, face, fingers and toes are not fragility sites. Core secondary screen: FBC, ESR or CRP, adjusted calcium, phosphate, ALP, albumin, eGFR, LFTs, 25(OH)D, PTH, TSH, SPEP and FLC, coeliac serology (tTG); testosterone, SHBG, LH and FSH in men. Targeted tests: 24-hour urine calcium, cortisol, tryptase, PSA, HbA1c.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Metabolic Bone Disease"]},{"id":230,"s":"What does FRAX estimate?","o":["The lifetime risk of any fracture","The 10-year probability of a major osteoporotic fracture and of hip fracture","The current T-score adjusted for age alone","The probability of fracture within 5 years only","BMD alone, without clinical risk factors"],"a":1,"e":"FRAX combines age, sex, BMI, prior fracture, parental hip fracture, smoking, glucocorticoids, RA, secondary osteoporosis and alcohol, with or without femoral neck BMD. Standard FRAX underestimates risk with high-dose glucocorticoids and multiple fractures. FRAX inputs: age, sex, BMI, prior fracture, parental hip fracture, current smoking, GC (\u20215 mg for \u20213 months), RA, secondary osteoporosis, alcohol \u20213 units/day, \u00b1 femoral neck BMD. It underestimates risk with high-dose GC, recent, multiple or vertebral fractures, falls, diabetes and spine\u2013hip discordance. UK practice (NOGG) uses age-dependent FRAX thresholds with high and very high risk categories. The US uses fixed thresholds (MOF \u202120% or hip \u20213%). QFracture is an alternative UK tool that includes falls and more comorbidities.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":231,"s":"A woman on prednisolone has, or is about to start, long-term glucocorticoids: for example a 68-year-old on 7.5 mg daily with a wrist fragility fracture and femoral neck T-score -1.8, or a 74-year-old starting 40 mg daily for GCA. How should bone protection be decided?","o":["Only the 74-year-old needs bone protection; the 68-year-old can wait for a DXA result","Both need bone protection now: prior fragility fracture, age 70 or over, or 7.5 mg/day or more for 3 months or more confer high fracture risk despite an osteopenic T-score, so treatment (such as oral alendronate","Neither needs bone protection until an actual fracture occurs","A DXA scan must be obtained before any bone-protective treatment starts","Only the 68-year-old needs treatment, since her T-score is already abnormal"],"a":1,"e":"with calcium and vitamin D) should start with the glucocorticoid without waiting for DXA Glucocorticoids increase fracture risk at higher BMD than in postmenopausal osteoporosis, and fracture risk rises within weeks of starting them. Fracture risk depends on the whole clinical picture. People aged 70 or over, those with a prior fragility fracture, or those on 7.5 mg/day or more for 3 months or more should start bone protection without waiting for DXA. GIOP is the commonest secondary cause. The main mechanism is osteoblast and osteocyte apoptosis (reduced formation), with an early resorption rise, reduced calcium absorption, hypogonadism, and myopathy with falls. Risk rises within 3\u20136 months even at 2.5\u20137.5 mg/day. 30\u201350% of long-term users fracture. Assess within 6 months of starting \u20212.5 mg/day for \u20213 months in adults \u202140 (FRAX + BMD); in under-40s only with a prior fracture or other risks. Adjust FRAX for >7.5 mg/day: MOF \u00d71.15, hip \u00d71.2. Moderate risk: oral bisphosphonate first-line; zoledronate if oral is unsuitable. High or very high risk: teriparatide is superior to alendronate for vertebral fractures in GIOP. Denosumab is effective but needs a bisphosphonate follow-on. Ongoing: reassess every 12\u201318 months; consider stopping bone therapy once GC stops and risk falls. Women of childbearing potential: use bisphosphonates cautiously (long skeletal retention); prefer teriparatide, calcium and vitamin D where possible.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":232,"s":"What is first-line pharmacological treatment for most postmenopausal women with osteoporosis, and which counselling point is essential for oral alendronate?","o":["Raloxifene, with no particular administration instructions","Denosumab by subcutaneous injection every 6 months, with no fasting requirement","Teriparatide by daily subcutaneous injection, continued indefinitely","An oral bisphosphonate (alendronate or risedronate) with calcium and vitamin D repletion; take it once weekly on an empty stomach with plain water, stay upright for at least 30 minutes and delay food for 30 minutes","Calcium and vitamin D supplementation alone, without any prescription drug"],"a":3,"e":"Oral bisphosphonates are first-line unless contraindicated or not tolerated, with IV zoledronate as an alternative. Correct administration avoids poor absorption and oesophagitis, and calcium and other medicines should be separated from the dose. Dental review is important. Contraindications to oral bisphosphonates: eGFR <30\u201335, oesophageal stricture or achalasia, inability to stay upright for 30 minutes, hypocalcaemia, pregnancy. Alternatives: IV zoledronate (needs eGFR \u202135 and hydration) or denosumab (no renal clearance). Fracture reduction: alendronate and risedronate reduce vertebral fractures by about 40\u201370% and hip fractures by about 40%. Zoledronate (5 mg IV yearly) reduces vertebral fractures by about 70%, reduced mortality after hip fracture, and causes an acute-phase reaction (mainly with the first dose). Denosumab reduces vertebral fractures by about 68% and hip by about 40%. Raloxifene reduces vertebral fractures only, carries VTE risk and reduces breast cancer. HRT is first-line for women <60 or within 10 years of menopause with symptoms. Non-drug measures: progressive resistance and impact exercise, balance training (tai chi, Otago), falls prevention (home hazards, vision, sedative review, hip protectors in care homes), stop smoking, protein 1\u20131.2 g/kg/day.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Pregnancy & Reproductive"]},{"id":233,"s":"A 72-year-old with two recent vertebral fractures and a spine T-score of -3.6 needs treatment. What is the best initial approach, and what are the key contraindications of romosozumab and teriparatide?","o":["An antiresorptive alone, since anabolic agents are reserved for bisphosphonate failure","Romosozumab is safe regardless of cardiovascular history","Teriparatide is contraindicated in all patients over 70 years of age","An antiresorptive first, followed by an anabolic agent, giving an equivalent response","An anabolic agent first (teriparatide, or romosozumab if there is no prior MI or stroke), followed by antiresorptive therapy; romosozumab is contraindicated after myocardial infarction or stroke, and teriparatide after skeletal radiotherapy, in"],"a":4,"e":"Paget's disease or unexplained raised ALP, with open epiphyses, bone malignancy or metastases, hypercalcaemia or severe renal impairment Very high fracture risk (recent or multiple vertebral fractures, very low T-score) favours anabolic therapy first, followed by an antiresorptive to consolidate gains. An imbalance in serious cardiovascular events was seen in a pivotal trial of romosozumab, which is given monthly for 12 months only and must be followed by antiresorptive therapy. Because of an osteosarcoma signal in rats, teriparatide is contraindicated in the situations above; treatment is limited to 24 months, and it is effective in glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis. The EULAR course notes the rat osteosarcoma signal has not been borne out in human surveillance, but the label contraindications still apply. Very high risk includes recent fracture (within 12\u201324 months, especially vertebral or hip), multiple fractures, fracture on treatment, T \u00a3-3.0 (\u00a3-3.5 in some guidance), very high FRAX and high-dose GC. VERO: teriparatide beat risedronate for vertebral fractures. ARCH: romosozumab followed by alendronate beat alendronate alone. Abaloparatide (PTHrP analogue, 80 \u00b5g) is an alternative. Sequencing: anabolic then antiresorptive is best. Bisphosphonate then anabolic is acceptable but gives a blunted response. Going from denosumab to teriparatide causes transient hip bone loss, so avoid it or overlap the two. Always follow an anabolic immediately with an antiresorptive.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":234,"s":"A 70-year-old woman has received denosumab 60 mg every 6 months for 5 years. She wants to stop because of planned dental work. What is the most important advice?","o":["Stop immediately with no follow-on treatment, since denosumab has no rebound effect","Do not stop without follow-on therapy; plan a bisphosphonate (often zoledronate) about 6 months after the last dose","Switch directly to teriparatide, with no bisphosphonate follow-on","Continue denosumab unchanged through the dental procedure, with no other action needed","Delay the next dose indefinitely until dental work is fully complete"],"a":1,"e":"Stopping or delaying denosumab causes rapid rebound bone turnover and a risk of multiple vertebral fractures. A bisphosphonate is given as follow-on, with turnover markers guiding further dosing. Rebound after stopping denosumab: turnover overshoots above baseline 3\u201318 months after the last dose, with a risk of multiple vertebral fractures, especially after longer use and in people with prior vertebral fractures. Denosumab has no drug holiday. Give zoledronate about 6 months after the last denosumab dose, monitoring CTX at 3 and 6 months and repeating zoledronate if CTX rises. Or continue denosumab long term (studied to 10 years). Dental work: routine dentistry is fine. For extractions, there is no need to stop; coordinate timing (dental work about 3\u20134 months after a dose, next dose after healing) and use good oral hygiene. Biosimilars are now available.","c":"General Rheumatology","t":["General Rheumatology"]},{"id":235,"s":"A woman on alendronate for 8 years has 2 months of dull thigh pain. Which rare complications are linked to long-term antiresorptives, and what should be done?","o":["Statin-induced myopathy; check creatine kinase and stop the statin","Reassurance only, since thigh pain is a common benign effect of bisphosphonates","Metastatic bone disease; arrange urgent oncology referral","Atypical femoral fracture and osteonecrosis of the jaw; get full-length radiographs of both femora (MRI or isotope imaging if normal and suspicion persists) and stop the bisphosphonate if an AFF or stress reaction is found","Continue alendronate unchanged and review again in 12 months"],"a":3,"e":"Prodromal thigh or groin pain in long-term bisphosphonate users may precede an atypical femoral fracture, which is often bilateral. Osteonecrosis of the jaw and AFF are rare complications of long-term antiresorptive therapy with bisphosphonates or denosumab. Good dental hygiene and dental review before treatment reduce ONJ risk, and drug holidays can be considered in lower-risk long-term bisphosphonate users. Thigh pain on bisphosphonates: AFF or stress reaction vs hip OA, trochanteric pain, L3 radiculopathy, osteomalacia (Looser zone on the medial femoral neck), hypophosphatasia (bisphosphonates worsen it), metastasis, Paget's. AFF: a transverse subtrochanteric or shaft fracture from low energy, with lateral cortical \"beaking\". Risk rises after 3\u20135 years and in Asian patients and those on GC. ONJ: exposed necrotic jaw bone for >8 weeks without prior radiation; about 1 in 10,000 to 1 in 100,000 patient-years in osteoporosis, far higher at oncology doses. Risk factors: extraction or implants, poor hygiene, GC, smoking, diabetes, long duration, anti-angiogenic drugs. Other bisphosphonate effects: hypocalcaemia, AF (zoledronate; inconsistent), uveitis or scleritis, oesophagitis. Stop the antiresorptive, give teriparatide, and arrange orthopaedic review (prophylactic intramedullary nailing for incomplete fractures with pain). Image the contralateral femur. Check vitamin D and ALP. Overall, fractures prevented far outnumber AFFs.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":236,"s":"A woman has taken alendronate for 5 years. When should treatment continue (for example after a prior hip fracture with a hip T-score of -2.6), and when is a drug holiday appropriate (no fractures, no steroids, femoral neck T-score -2.1)?","o":["Stop permanently in everyone after 5 years, regardless of fracture history","Switch to denosumab in everyone reaching 5 years, regardless of risk","Continue only if a DXA shows further bone loss during a holiday","A drug holiday is never appropriate once treatment has started","Continue (oral up to 10 years) with a previous hip or vertebral fracture, age 70 or over, a fracture on treatment, or prednisolone 7.5 mg/day or more; otherwise take a drug holiday of about 18 months to 3 years, then reassess"],"a":4,"e":"After 5 years of oral bisphosphonate (3 years of zoledronic acid), treatment should be reviewed. Continuation is generally advised for people aged 70 or over, those with a previous hip or vertebral fracture, those who fractured on treatment, or those on at least 7.5 mg prednisolone daily. Otherwise, a holiday with reassessment is reasonable. The EULAR course criteria differ slightly: continue at age \u202175 or a hip T-score \u00a3-2.5 (up to 10 years oral or 6 years IV). FLEX and HORIZON extension trials showed fewer vertebral fractures with continuation in high-risk patients. Reassess BMD and FRAX after about 2 years of holiday, and earlier after a new fracture. Denosumab has no holiday.","c":"Drug Monitoring & Safety","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":237,"s":"Before IV zoledronic acid in a housebound 79-year-old with 25(OH)D 18 nmol/L, or before denosumab in an 81-year-old with eGFR 22, what is most important?","o":["Check renal function only, with no attention to vitamin D or calcium","Proceed directly to treatment, since both drugs are safe regardless of baseline vitamin D or calcium","Give denosumab first in both cases, regardless of renal function","Replete vitamin D (and correct calcium) first, then give the drug and recheck calcium afterwards; in advanced CKD consider CKD-mineral bone disorder before choosing an antiresorptive","No specific precautions are needed beyond standard consent"],"a":3,"e":"Vitamin D deficiency increases the risk of symptomatic hypocalcaemia after IV bisphosphonates or denosumab, so deficiency should be corrected with a loading regimen first; oral bisphosphonates can start alongside replacement. Denosumab can cause severe symptomatic hypocalcaemia, particularly with eGFR below 30 or on dialysis, so hypocalcaemia and vitamin D deficiency should be corrected first and calcium monitored after dosing. In advanced CKD, CKD-mineral bone disorder (including adynamic bone) should also be considered. Loading dose: cholecalciferol about 300,000 IU over 6\u201310 weeks (e.g. 50,000 IU weekly for 6 weeks), then 800\u20132000 IU daily. Recheck calcium at 1 month (this can unmask primary hyperparathyroidism). In CKD 4\u20135: check PTH and ALP; a bone biopsy helps if turnover is uncertain. Antiresorptives suit high-turnover disease; teriparatide suits adynamic bone. Avoid or reduce bisphosphonates when eGFR is <30\u201335.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":238,"s":"A 45-year-old woman with coeliac disease has proximal weakness and diffuse bone pain. Calcium is lownormal, phosphate low, alkaline phosphatase and PTH raised. Radiographs show Looser zones in the pubic rami. What is the diagnosis?","o":["68Ga-DOTATATE PET/CT","3 points; he meets the criteria","Paget disease of bone","Osteomalacia due to vitamin D deficiency","Primary hyperparathyroidism"],"a":3,"e":"Vitamin D deficiency reduces calcium absorption, causing secondary hyperparathyroidism, phosphaturia and defective mineralisation. Looser zones are characteristic. Treatment is vitamin D loading with calcium and management of malabsorption. DIFFERENTIALS & OTHER CAUSES Pattern Ca Causes of osteomalacia: - low/N Vitamin D Calcium-limited: nutritional deficiency deficiency, malabsorption (coeliac, gastric bypass, FGF23-mediated N pancreatic disease), liver (XLH, TIO) disease, CKD, enzymeinducing antiepileptics, vitamin Fanconi N D-dependent rickets (1A, 1B, N/high Hypophosphatasia 2). - Phosphate-limited, FGF23-mediated: XLH, ADHR, Clinical features: bone pain and tenderness (ribs, pelvis, tibiae), proximal myopathy with TIO, fibrous dysplasia, IV ferric a waddling gait (normal CK), and pseudofractures. Looser zones appear at the pubic carboxymaltose. - Phosphaterami, medial femoral neck, ribs and axillary border of the scapula. A bone scan shows limited, renal tubular: Fanconi symmetrical hot spots that mimic metastases. It is often mistaken for fibromyalgia, PMR syndrome (tenofovir, adefovir, or myositis. myeloma light chains, ifosfamide, Wilson's disease); hereditary hypophosphataemic rickets with hypercalciuria; phosphate binders and antacids. - Mineralisation inhibitors: aluminium, fluoride, etidronate. - Low ALP: hypophosphatasia. PO4 ALP PTH 25(OH)D 1,25(OH)2D FGF23 low/N high high low variable N N/mild low high N low or high high inappropriately N N/high low/N low high N N N/high low/N low N N N Vitamin D loading, calcium if intake is low, treat malabsorption (higher doses; activated vitamin D in liver or kidney disease). ALP rises transiently, then normalises over months.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Metabolic Bone Disease","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":239,"s":"A 24-year-old man with short stature, lifelong bowed legs and an affected mother has low phosphate with renal phosphate wasting, normal calcium and raised FGF23. What is the diagnosis and targeted therapy?","o":["Autosomal recessive hypophosphataemic rickets; calcitriol alone","X-linked hypophosphataemia; burosumab","Tumour-induced osteomalacia; tumour resection","A short course of oral prednisolone","IL-1 blockade (canakinumab or anakinra)"],"a":1,"e":"PHEX mutations cause excess FGF23, renal phosphate wasting and low or inappropriately normal calcitriol. Burosumab neutralises FGF23; conventional therapy is oral phosphate with active vitamin D. XLH is the commonest heritable rickets (X-linked dominant PHEX). Children have bowed legs, short stature and dental abscesses. Adults have pseudofractures, enthesopathy (mimicking SpA or DISH), early OA, hearing loss and spinal stenosis. Burosumab (anti-FGF23) is licensed for children and symptomatic adults and is superior to conventional therapy for healing rickets. Conventional therapy is oral phosphate plus calcitriol; monitor for nephrocalcinosis (renal ultrasound), hyperparathyroidism and urine calcium.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":240,"s":"A woman in her late 40s or early 50s has recurrent metatarsal stress fractures, early loss of adult teeth (and sometimes chondrocalcinosis) and has been referred for alendronate. ALP is persistently low. What is the diagnosis, which drug should be avoided, and what is the next step?","o":["Osteomalacia; replace vitamin D before considering any other treatment","Osteoporosis; start alendronate as planned","Paget's disease; start zoledronic acid","Hypophosphatasia; avoid bisphosphonates; check pyridoxal-5'-phosphate and ALPL genetics","Osteogenesis imperfecta; refer for genetic counselling only"],"a":3,"e":"A persistently low ALP with stress fractures and premature tooth loss suggests hypophosphatasia (ALPL mutations). A raised pyridoxal-5'-phosphate supports the diagnosis. Bisphosphonates resemble pyrophosphate, an ALP substrate; they and other antiresorptives can worsen the mineralisation defect and have been linked to atypical femoral fractures in this condition. Enzyme replacement (asfotase alfa) is used in severe disease. Other causes of low ALP: zinc or magnesium deficiency, hypothyroidism, Wilson's disease, pernicious anaemia, coeliac disease, milk-alkali syndrome, massive transfusion, cardiac surgery or bypass, recent bisphosphonate or denosumab therapy, glucocorticoids, cachexia, clofibrate. Always confirm low values on repeat testing. Raised urinary phosphoethanolamine. Associations: chondrocalcinosis and CPPD (pyrophosphate accumulation), calcific periarthritis, AFF-like fractures, muscle weakness, pain. Avoid antiresorptives. Teriparatide has been used off-label. Physiotherapy, orthopaedic and dental care.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":241,"s":"How is Paget's disease monitored and treated: for example a 74-year-old with femoral pain and bowing, ALP 310 U/L and increased uptake throughout the femur, versus a 71-year-old with incidental asymptomatic pelvic disease and mildly raised ALP?","o":["ALP should be normalised in all patients regardless of symptoms, using repeated zoledronic acid","Calcium levels are the primary marker of disease activity, not ALP","Raised ALP with normal calcium and phosphate is the activity marker; a single zoledronic acid infusion treats pagetic bone pain, but asymptomatic disease is monitored rather than treated to normalise ALP","Osteosarcoma arising in pagetic bone","Both patients require treatment, since any ALP elevation mandates zoledronic acid"],"a":2,"e":"ALP reflects increased bone turnover; hypercalcaemia is unusual unless the patient is immobilised. In Paget's disease, a bisphosphonate (zoledronic acid preferred) treats bone pain. Intensive treatment aimed at normalising ALP does not prevent complications better than symptom-directed treatment, so it is not supported in asymptomatic patients. Treatment is still considered before elective surgery on pagetic bone. Teriparatide is contraindicated because of osteosarcoma risk. Sclerotic metastases (prostate, breast), lymphoma, fibrous dysplasia, hyperparathyroidism (brown tumours), mastocytosis, osteopetrosis, hyperostosis frontalis interna, fluorosis. Other causes of raised ALP: liver disease (check GGT), osteomalacia, fracture healing, hyperparathyroidism, metastases, adolescence, pregnancy. 70\u201390% are asymptomatic. It affects older people of British or northern European ancestry (Lancashire), M:F 3:2, and incidence is falling. Genetics: SQSTM1 (p62) in 20\u201350% of familial cases, RANK, OPTN, VCP (multisystem proteinopathy). Sites: pelvis, spine, femur, skull, tibia. X-ray signs: \"blade of grass\" lytic front, osteoporosis circumscripta, cotton-wool skull, picture-frame and ivory vertebrae, thickened iliopectineal line. Complications: fractures (banana-type), secondary OA (the commonest cause of pain), deafness (30\u201350% with skull disease), basilar invagination, spinal stenosis, high-output cardiac failure, hypercalcaemia on immobilisation, osteosarcoma (<0.5%). Zoledronate 5 mg IV once (most durable effect). Alternatives: risedronate 30 mg daily for 2 months, alendronate 40 mg daily for 6 months, pamidronate. Replete vitamin D first and give calcium and vitamin D for about 10 days afterwards. Monitor ALP (or P1NP) at 3\u20136 months, then yearly. Retreat for recurrent pain with a rising ALP. Calcitonin is no longer recommended. Supportive care: analgesia, heel raises, hearing aids, arthroplasty (more blood loss). Prophylactic zoledronate in SQSTM1 carriers reduced new lesions (ZiPP) but is not routine.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Metabolic Bone Disease","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":242,"s":"A 48-year-old man has bone pain, proximal weakness and rib fractures. Phosphate is low with renal phosphate wasting, calcium normal and FGF23 raised; there is no family history. Which investigation is most likely to find the cause?","o":["Thyroid function tests","MRI of both hips","Anti-Jo-1","Anti-SRP","68Ga-DOTATATE PET/CT"],"a":4,"e":"Adult-onset FGF23-mediated hypophosphataemia suggests tumour-induced osteomalacia. These small mesenchymal tumours express somatostatin receptors, so 68Ga-DOTATATE PET/CT is the most sensitive localisation test, followed by anatomical imaging. Complete resection is curative. TIO tumours are small benign phosphaturic mesenchymal tumours (in bone, soft tissue, sinuses or skin) that secrete FGF23, causing a low TmP/GFR and low or inappropriately normal 1,25(OH)2D. It is often misdiagnosed as fibromyalgia or SpA. Localise with Ga-68 DOTATATE PET/CT, then MRI, with venous sampling for FGF23 if needed. Before surgery, or if the tumour is unresectable, give oral phosphate plus calcitriol or burosumab. Other acquired causes: IV ferric carboxymaltose (recheck phosphate), fibrous dysplasia, epidermal naevus syndrome.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":243,"s":"A 78-year-old man with long-standing Paget's disease of the femur develops increasing, constant pain with a tender soft-tissue swelling over the femur. ALP is rising despite recent zoledronic acid. What must be excluded first?","o":["A simple insufficiency fracture through pagetic bone","Osteosarcoma arising in pagetic bone","A further Paget's flare requiring repeat zoledronic acid alone","The A1 pulley at the metacarpal head","Complex regional pain syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"Sarcomatous change occurs in under 1% of patients with Paget's disease but carries a poor prognosis. New severe pain, swelling or a pathological fracture with rising ALP after treatment needs urgent MRI and biopsy. New severe constant pain, swelling, a lytic lesion or a pathological fracture in pagetic bone, with ALP rising despite treatment: urgent MRI, CT staging and biopsy at a sarcoma centre. Giant cell tumour is a rarer complication (skull and facial bones; may respond to dexamethasone).","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":244,"s":"A 58-year-old man presents with two thoracic vertebral fragility fractures. DXA shows a lumbar spine Z-score of -2.9. Which set of investigations is most important to identify secondary causes?","o":["Testosterone, myeloma screen, coeliac serology, calcium/PTH and vitamin D","Full pre-biologic screening (hepatitis B/C, HIV, latent TB) alone","Thyroid function tests and a fasting glucose alone","Genetic testing for osteogenesis imperfecta alone","A repeat DXA scan in 2 years before any further investigation"],"a":0,"e":"Up to half of men with osteoporosis have a secondary cause, including hypogonadism, myeloma, glucocorticoids, alcohol, coeliac disease and hyperparathyroidism. Myeloma must be excluded in any patient with vertebral fractures and a low Z-score. Treatment should not wait for a further DXA. Secondary causes in men (about 50% have one): hypogonadism (including ADT), alcohol, GC, myeloma or MGUS, coeliac disease, hyperparathyroidism, hyperthyroidism, Cushing's, mastocytosis (urticaria pigmentosa, raised tryptase), hypercalciuria, HIV, anticonvulsants, COPD, homocystinuria, haemochromatosis, OI (blue sclerae). Bisphosphonates, denosumab and teriparatide or abaloparatide are effective in men. Treat hypogonadism (testosterone if indicated). Men have higher post-fracture mortality.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":245,"s":"A 45-year-old woman has asymptomatic hypercalcaemia (adjusted calcium 2.75 mmol/L) with a PTH in the upper part of the reference range. Her 25(OH)D is replete. The calcium:creatinine clearance ratio is 0.006. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Takayasu arteritis","Primary hyperparathyroidism","Tertiary hyperparathyroidism due to chronic kidney disease","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia","Antisynthetase syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"A calcium:creatinine clearance ratio below 0.01 strongly suggests FHH (usually a CASR variant), and parathyroidectomy does not help. Values above 0.02 favour primary hyperparathyroidism, and intermediate values warrant genetic testing. Vitamin D should be replete before the test is interpreted. Hypercalcaemia with non-suppressed PTH: primary hyperparathyroidism, FHH (CASR, GNA11, AP2S1), lithium, thiazides (unmasking), tertiary hyperparathyroidism (CKD), MEN1 or 2A. Hypercalcaemia with suppressed PTH: malignancy (PTHrP, lytic metastases, myeloma), granulomatous disease (raised 1,25D; sarcoid, TB, lymphoma), vitamin D toxicity, milk-alkali syndrome, thyrotoxicosis, immobilisation (Paget's), Addison's, vitamin A, theophylline. Calcium:creatinine clearance ratio = (urine Ca \u00d7 serum Cr) / (serum Ca \u00d7 urine Cr). Below 0.01 suggests FHH; above 0.02 suggests PHPT. FHH: reassurance, no surgery; family screening; genetic confirmation. PHPT surgical criteria: calcium >0.25 mmol/L above ULN, osteoporosis or fragility fracture, eGFR <60, stones or hypercalciuria, age <50.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Metabolic Bone Disease"]},{"id":246,"s":"A woman in the third trimester of pregnancy develops progressive hip pain. MRI shows diffuse marrow oedema of the femoral head and neck without a subchondral fracture line. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Transient osteoporosis of the hip","Greater trochanteric pain syndrome","Osteonecrosis of the femoral head","Spinal tuberculosis (Pott's disease)","Femoroacetabular impingement (cam type)"],"a":0,"e":"Transient osteoporosis of the hip affects women in late pregnancy and middle-aged men. Diffuse marrow oedema without the focal subchondral line of osteonecrosis is typical. It is self-limiting over months; management is analgesia and protected weight-bearing to prevent fracture. Diffuse marrow oedema of the hip: transient osteoporosis or bone marrow oedema syndrome (reversible) vs osteonecrosis (focal, double-line sign), subchondral insufficiency fracture, stress fracture, septic arthritis or osteomyelitis, early inflammatory arthritis, pigmented villonodular synovitis, tumour. Pregnancy- and lactationassociated osteoporosis presents with vertebral fractures post-partum. Analgesia and protected weight-bearing (crutches) until resolution (6\u201312 months). Bisphosphonates (or iloprost) may hasten recovery outside pregnancy. Avoid bisphosphonates in pregnancy. Early MRI to exclude fracture.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":247,"s":"A 30-year-old woman with SLE who received high-dose prednisolone last year has 6 weeks of groin pain on weight-bearing. Hip radiographs are normal. What is the most sensitive investigation for the likely diagnosis?","o":["Plain radiographs of both hips","Kidney biopsy","68Ga-DOTATATE PET/CT","High-resolution CT chest","MRI of both hips"],"a":4,"e":"Glucocorticoid-associated osteonecrosis of the femoral head is often bilateral and is common in SLE. MRI detects it before radiographic change, when joint-preserving measures such as core decompression are most likely to help. Both hips should be imaged. Groin pain in SLE: AVN vs septic arthritis (immunosuppressed), insufficiency fracture (GIOP), lupus arthritis, iliopsoas haematoma or abscess, hernia, referred lumbar pain. Risk in SLE is about 5\u201310%, linked to GC dose (especially >20 mg/day early, and pulses), aPL, Cushingoid habitus, vasculitis and Raynaud's. Femoral head disease is bilateral in about 50%. Other sites: femoral condyles, humeral head, talus. ARCO staging (I\u2013IV). Before collapse: protected weightbearing, reduce GC, core decompression with or without graft or cells. Bisphosphonates and anticoagulation (if thrombophilic) have mixed evidence. After collapse or with secondary OA: arthroplasty. Large weight-bearing lesions usually collapse within 2\u20133 years.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Connective Tissue Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":248,"s":"A 7-year-old girl has precocious puberty, large irregular caf\u00e9-au-lait patches and expansile 'ground-glass' lesions in several long bones. What is the genetic basis, and which metabolic complication can occur?","o":["An autosomal dominant NF1 mutation; lesions may cause scoliosis","A postzygotic activating GNAS mutation; lesions may secrete FGF23","A PHEX mutation; lesions may cause renal phosphate wasting","A RANK mutation; lesions may cause osteopetrosis","An ALPL mutation; lesions may cause fractures with a low ALP"],"a":1,"e":"postzygotic activating GNAS mutation; lesions may secrete FGF23 McCune-Albright syndrome combines polyostotic fibrous dysplasia, 'coast of Maine' caf\u00e9-au-lait macules and endocrine hyperfunction. The GNAS mutation is somatic and mosaic, and a germline mutation would be lethal. Growth hormone excess worsens craniofacial fibrous dysplasia, and phosphate wasting should be checked. FD/MAS is caused by a post-zygotic GNAS activating mutation (mosaic). Fibrous dysplasia is monostotic in about 70%, at craniofacial sites, the proximal femur (\"shepherd's crook\"), ribs and tibia, with \"ground-glass\" lesions. Caf\u00e9au-lait macules have \"coast of Maine\" borders and often respect the midline. Endocrine features: peripheral precocious puberty (commonest), hyperthyroidism, GH excess, neonatal Cushing's, FGF23 phosphate wasting. Analgesia; IV bisphosphonates for pain (they do not heal lesions; oral forms are ineffective); denosumab is investigational (rebound hypercalcaemia). Treat endocrinopathies, especially GH excess. Correct phosphate and vitamin D. Intramedullary rodding for deformity. Malignant transformation is <1% (risk rises with GH excess or radiation). Compare neurofibromatosis type 1, where caf\u00e9-au-lait macules have smooth \"coast of California\" borders.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":249,"s":"A 52-year-old man with pulmonary sarcoidosis has an adjusted calcium of 3.0 mmol/L, suppressed PTH, a low-normal 25(OH)D and a raised 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. What is the mechanism and the most appropriate treatment?","o":["Renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase upregulation from PTH excess; parathyroidectomy","IL-17 pathway inhibition","Osteoclast-mediated bone resorption from PTHrP; bisphosphonates","Macrophage 1-alpha-hydroxylase; glucocorticoids, fluids, stop vitamin D","Direct vitamin D receptor activation; stop glucocorticoids"],"a":3,"e":"Granuloma macrophages convert 25(OH)D to calcitriol without feedback control. Hypercalcaemia and hypercalciuria respond to glucocorticoids; hydroxychloroquine is an alternative. Vitamin D and calcium supplements should be avoided, and renal function and nephrocalcinosis monitored. 1,25(OH)2D-mediated hypercalcaemia: sarcoidosis, TB, fungal infection, lymphoma, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, berylliosis, silicone or paraffin granulomas, Crohn's disease, CYP24A1 mutations (loss of 24hydroxylase; idiopathic infantile hypercalcaemia, stones). Hydration, stop calcium and vitamin D supplements, sun avoidance, prednisolone 20\u201340 mg (calcium normalises within days to weeks). Hydroxychloroquine or ketoconazole are steroid-sparing options. Monitor renal function, urine calcium and nephrocalcinosis.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Metabolic Bone Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":250,"s":"What are the entry criterion and minimum score for the 2019 EULAR/ACR SLE classification criteria?","o":["A malar rash plus photosensitivity, with no minimum point threshold","Any positive autoantibody, with no entry criterion required","Four of eleven clinical and immunological criteria, with no weighting","ANA at a titre of 1:80 or higher on HEp-2 cells (or an equivalent test) at any time, then 10 or more weighted points with at least one clinical criterion","A SLEDAI score of 4 or more, with no serological requirement"],"a":3,"e":"Criteria count only if not better explained by another condition, need not occur simultaneously, and only the highestweighted item in each domain counts. Renal and neuropsychiatric domains carry high weights; biopsy-proven class III or IV lupus nephritis alone scores 10. Classification criteria do not replace clinical diagnosis. Domain Items (points) fever >38.3 \u00b0C (2) Constitutional leucopenia <4 (3); thrombocytopenia Haematological <100 (4); AIHA (4) delirium (2); psychosis (3); seizure (5) Neuropsychiatric non-scarring alopecia (2); oral ulcers Mucocutaneous (2); SCLE or DLE (4); ACLE (6) effusion (5); acute pericarditis (6) Serosal joint involvement (6) Musculoskeletal proteinuria >0.5 g/24 h (4); class II or V Renal (8); class III or IV (10) any (2) aPL low C3 or C4 (3); both low (4) Complement SLE-specific Ab anti-dsDNA or anti-Sm (6) Sensitivity about 96%, specificity about 93%. SLICC criteria (\u20214 with \u20211 clinical and \u20211 immunological, or biopsy nephritis plus ANA or dsDNA) remain valid. About 10\u201320% of patients with clinician-diagnosed early lupus do not meet the criteria. Treat them anyway (HCQ).","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":251,"s":"A 32-year-old woman with arthralgia has an ANA of 1:160 but no other clinical features of connective tissue disease. What is the best interpretation?","o":["A titre of 1:160 confirms SLE even without other clinical features","ANA should be repeated monthly to monitor for evolving disease","A negative ANA would be expected in early SLE, making this diagnosis more likely","The titre alone determines whether immunosuppression should start","ANA positivity alone does not establish SLE or another connective tissue disease"],"a":4,"e":"ANA is sensitive but non-specific and occurs in healthy people and other conditions. A negative ANA makes SLE much less likely. ANA positivity without SLE: healthy people (about 13% at 1:80, 3\u20135% at \u20211:160), older age, relatives, autoimmune thyroid or liver disease, other CTDs, infection (EBV, HCV, HIV, TB, endocarditis), malignancy, drugs. A DFS70 (dense fine speckled) pattern alone argues against systemic autoimmune disease. ANA sensitivity is 95\u201398% on HEp-2 IIF. Rare ANA-negative SLE occurs with anti-Ro on substrates lacking Ro60 or heavy proteinuria; if suspicion is high, test anti-Ro or ENA directly. Do not repeat ANA to monitor activity. Do not order ANA without clinical suspicion. Investigate further (ENA, dsDNA, complement, urinalysis, FBC) only with compatible features.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":252,"s":"Which antibody is most specific for SLE?","o":["Anti-Jo-1","Anti-Sm","MRI of both hips","Anti-histone","Anti-NXP2"],"a":1,"e":"Anti-Sm is highly specific but insensitive (about 10-30%). Anti-dsDNA is also highly specific and tracks activity, particularly nephritis. Antibody Frequency Associations 60\u201370% nephritis; tracks activity; dsDNA Crithidia or Farr assays most specific 10\u201330% about 99% specific; does Sm not track activity U1-RNP 25\u201340% Raynaud's, myositis, MCTD; possibly less nephritis Ro (Ro60, 30\u201340% SCLE, photosensitivity, Ro52) Sj\u00f6gren's, neonatal lupus and CHB, cytopenias 10\u201315% with Ro; neonatal lupus La 10\u201320% psychosis, depression, Ribosomal P lupus hepatitis 50\u201370% non-specific Histone (>95% in DIL) C1q 30\u201360% active proliferative LN; negative result has high NPV 60\u201380% nephritis; may be positive Nucleosome when dsDNA is negative 30\u201340% thrombosis, obstetric aPL events, livedo, low platelets, valves","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":253,"s":"Which serological pattern predicts an impending renal flare in quiescent SLE, and how should a modest isolated rise in anti-dsDNA without clinical change be managed?","o":["Rising anti-dsDNA alone, without any complement change, is more predictive than the combined pattern","Rising anti-dsDNA with falling C3 and C4 predicts flare; isolated serological activity warrants closer monitoring (including urinalysis), not treatment escalation","Any isolated serological rise should prompt immediate treatment escalation","Falling complement alone, without any change in anti-dsDNA, is the more specific predictor","Serological activity should be ignored entirely unless accompanied by a rise in CRP"],"a":1,"e":"Rising anti-dsDNA with falling complement reflects immune-complex activity, particularly nephritis. Serological activity supports closer monitoring, but treatment escalation should follow clinical or organ activity rather than isolated serology. A markedly raised CRP in SLE should prompt a search for infection or serositis. About 10\u201315% of patients are serologically active but clinically quiescent; continue HCQ. Persistently low C4 with normal C3 may reflect inherited partial C4 deficiency (low copy number). A CH50 near zero suggests congenital early classical-pathway deficiency. Cell-bound C4d panels may help diagnose ANA-positive patients with an uncertain picture. Shorten the review interval (urinalysis, UPCR, FBC, C3/C4, dsDNA every 4\u20138 weeks). Check HCQ adherence (wholeblood levels <200 ng/mL suggest non-adherence).","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":254,"s":"Who should take hydroxychloroquine in SLE, and what is the correct dose for a 60 kg woman?","o":["Only patients with renal impairment should take hydroxychloroquine","Only patients with confirmed nephritis should take hydroxychloroquine","At a fixed dose of 400 mg daily, regardless of body weight","All patients unless contraindicated, at no more than 5 mg/kg actual body weight per day, so 300 mg daily for 60 kg","Only during pregnancy; it can be stopped once the baby is born"],"a":3,"e":"Hydroxychloroquine reduces flares, damage accrual and thrombosis and improves survival. It is continued in pregnancy and breastfeeding. At 5 mg/kg actual body weight, 60 kg gives 300 mg/day, which can be achieved with alternating doses; 400 mg daily would be about 6.7 mg/kg/day and increase retinal toxicity risk. If HCQ is not tolerated: chloroquine (more retinotoxic) or mepacrine (skin; yellow skin discoloration; no retinal toxicity). Rare HCQ toxicities: cardiomyopathy and conduction disease, QT prolongation, neuromyopathy (curvilinear bodies), hypoglycaemia, pigmentation, psoriasis flare. HCQ benefits: flares fall by about 50%; it reduces nephritis progression, thrombosis, seizures, CV events, CHB recurrence and pre-eclampsia, and improves lipids and glucose. Retinal toxicity is about 1% at 5 years and 10\u201320% at 20 years. Reduce the dose if eGFR is <30. Whole-blood levels >750\u20131150 ng/mL are linked to fewer flares, but do not exceed 5 mg/kg.","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":255,"s":"What is the target for maintenance glucocorticoid dosing in SLE?","o":["5 mg/day prednisolone equivalent or less, withdrawn when possible","20 mg/day or less, reduced slowly over several years","10 mg/day or less, maintained indefinitely once remission is reached","7.5 mg/day or less, which carries no excess long-term risk","15 mg/day or less, only tapered after 5 years of stability"],"a":0,"e":"mg/day prednisolone equivalent or less, withdrawn when possible Glucocorticoids are useful for rapid control, including IV methylprednisolone pulses in organ-threatening disease, but maintenance should be minimised. Early addition of immunosuppressants or biologics facilitates tapering. GC strategy: IV methylprednisolone 250\u20131000 mg for 1\u20133 days allows a lower oral start (\u00a30.5 mg/kg/day, maximum about 40 mg) and a faster taper. Reach \u00a35 mg/day within about 6 months, then try to withdraw. Damage accrues even at 5\u20137.5 mg/day and rises sharply above 7.5 mg/day. Start a steroid-sparing agent early rather than waiting for steroid dependence.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":256,"s":"A patient with SLE has persistent arthritis, rash or cutaneous lupus (without major organ involvement) despite hydroxychloroquine, with or without azathioprine, and cannot reduce prednisolone below 10 mg daily. What are the steroid-sparing options?","o":["High-dose glucocorticoids alone, continued indefinitely at the current dose","Switching hydroxychloroquine for chloroquine, with no other change","Cyclophosphamide as the next steroid-sparing agent of choice","Stopping hydroxychloroquine and relying on prednisolone alone","Methotrexate (or azathioprine or mycophenolate) first; then add belimumab or anifrolumab if disease remains active or glucocorticoids cannot be tapered to 5 mg/day or less"],"a":4,"e":"For non-organ-threatening disease not controlled by hydroxychloroquine, methotrexate, azathioprine or mycophenolate are options, chosen by organ phenotype, toxicity and pregnancy plans. Belimumab or anifrolumab are added when there is inadequate response or glucocorticoids cannot be tapered to 5 mg/day or less. The 2026 BSR guideline suggests anifrolumab may be more appropriate than belimumab in non-renal SLE, and names it the preferred first-line biologic for refractory cutaneous lupus; belimumab requires autoantibody positivity with high activity. Rituximab is an option when other choices are less suitable, particularly in organ-threatening disease. Smoking cessation and photoprotection are essential, and smoking reduces hydroxychloroquine efficacy. Things that look like refractory lupus but are not: fibromyalgia (in 20\u201330%), OA, damage (Jaccoud's), steroid myopathy, hypothyroidism, non-adherence, drug rash, rosacea or seborrhoeic dermatitis (malar rash mimics), dermatomyositis (knuckle involvement, heliotrope, raised CK), tinea or psoriasis (SCLE mimics). Severity Treatment Mild (skin, HCQ, topicals, NSAIDs, short low-dose GC; joints, methotrexate if persistent mucosa) HCQ + methotrexate, azathioprine or MMF Moderate with a GC bridge; add belimumab or anifrolumab pulse GC + MMF or cyclophosphamide (\u00b1 Severe a third agent in LN); rituximab if refractory; IVIG or PLEX as rescue Erosive rhupus: treat like RA (methotrexate, abatacept, rituximab). Ultrasound helps separate true synovitis from fibromyalgia.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Psoriatic Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":257,"s":"What are the mechanisms of anifrolumab and belimumab, and which adverse effect is increased with anifrolumab?","o":["Anifrolumab depletes CD20-positive B cells; belimumab blocks IL-6","Anifrolumab inhibits TNF-alpha; belimumab inhibits IL-17","Anifrolumab and belimumab both block the same BLyS/BAFF pathway","Anifrolumab blocks IL-6 receptor signalling; belimumab blocks type I interferon","Anifrolumab blocks the type I interferon receptor (IFNAR1) and increases herpes zoster; belimumab inhibits soluble BLyS/BAFF"],"a":4,"e":"Anifrolumab reduced disease activity and glucocorticoid use in moderate-to-severe non-renal SLE. Belimumab reduces B-cell survival; it reduces flares and glucocorticoid use in autoantibody-positive SLE and improves renal outcomes when added to standard lupus nephritis treatment. Anifrolumab: 300 mg IV every 4 weeks (a weekly SC autoinjector is licensed in the US after TULIP-SC). In TULIP- 2, BICLA response was 48% vs 32%. It works especially for skin and joints and is effective regardless of IFN signature (more so if IFN-high). Zoster is 6\u20137% vs 1\u20132%; there are also more URTIs and bronchitis. Not established in severe LN (IRIS ongoing) or NPSLE. Belimumab: IV 10 mg/kg (weeks 0, 2, 4, then every 4 weeks) or SC 200 mg weekly; licensed from age 5 (IV). Works best in serologically active disease. Watch for a depression and suicidality signal. In BEAT-LUPUS, belimumab after rituximab reduced severe flares (it counters the postrituximab BAFF rebound). Give recombinant zoster vaccine before anifrolumab. Ask about mood on belimumab. Neither has been studied in severe active NPSLE.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":258,"s":"What is the role of rituximab in SLE?","o":["It is first-line therapy for all new lupus nephritis, ahead of mycophenolate","It is used off-label for refractory organ-threatening disease, such as haematological, neuropsychiatric or renal involvement","It is only used for mild, non-organ-threatening skin and joint disease","It is contraindicated in SLE because of the EXPLORER and LUNAR trial results","It is reserved for pregnancy, as the only immunosuppressant considered safe"],"a":1,"e":"Randomised trials missed their primary endpoints, but real-world evidence supports rituximab in refractory disease. Immunoglobulins and HBV status should be checked beforehand. EXPLORER (non-renal) and LUNAR (LN) missed their primary endpoints, but observational data and NHS commissioning support use. Typical dose: 1 g \u00d7 2, two weeks apart, with methylprednisolone, often with cyclophosphamide or MMF. Serum sickness is more common in SLE. Indications: refractory LN, AIHA, ITP, TTP, NPSLE or myelitis, rhupus, CAPS. Before treatment: HBV, immunoglobulins, vaccines \u20212 weeks earlier. Obinutuzumab (type II anti-CD20) is licensed for LN.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Infection & Immunosuppression","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":259,"s":"When is a kidney biopsy indicated in SLE: for example new proteinuria, haematuria and rising creatinine, or a UPCR of 65 mg/mmol on two occasions with a bland sediment and normal creatinine in a patient who feels well?","o":["Biopsy only once creatinine has already doubled","Biopsy only if proteinuria exceeds 3 g/day; otherwise manage empirically","Biopsy is never required if the patient feels clinically well","Biopsy (if safe) for persistent proteinuria of 0.5 g/day or more (UPCR about 50 mg/mmol or more), an active glomerular sediment or an unexplained fall in eGFR, so both need a biopsy","Biopsy only in the first patient; the second needs no biopsy since creatinine is normal"],"a":3,"e":"Biopsy defines the histological class, activity and chronicity and guides treatment, subject to bleeding risk. It is advised for persistent proteinuria of 0.5 g/day or more, especially with glomerular haematuria or casts. Clinically silent nephritis is well recognised, so regular urinalysis, protein quantification, renal function and blood pressure checks are essential. LN mimics: hypertensive or diabetic nephropathy; drug toxicity (NSAIDs, PPI interstitial nephritis, CNI); aPL nephropathy or TMA, TTP, aHUS; pre-eclampsia; renal vein thrombosis; post-infectious, IgA or ANCA GN; anti-GBM; MGRS; lupus podocytopathy (MCD or FSGS-like, nephrotic with class I/II). LN affects 40\u201350% of adults and 50\u201370% of children, usually within 5 years. Risk is higher with Black, Hispanic or Asian ancestry, male sex, young age, anti-dsDNA, low complement and anti-C1q. 10\u201330% of proliferative LN progresses to kidney failure within 10\u201315 years. Screen: urinalysis, UPCR, BP and eGFR every 6\u201312 months if stable, and every 3 months if high-risk or active. Spot UPCR is preferred to 24-hour collection. Consider repeat biopsy for refractory disease, suspected class change, or before stopping maintenance (residual activity predicts relapse).","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":260,"s":"Renal biopsy in lupus nephritis shows endocapillary proliferation with subendothelial deposits involving at least 50% of glomeruli. What is the ISN/RPS class?","o":["Mycophenolate mofetil","Class III","Class IV","TNF inhibitors","Anti-SRP"],"a":2,"e":"Class III is focal (under 50% of glomeruli) and class IV diffuse (50% or more) proliferative nephritis. Class V is membranous; class VI is advanced sclerosis (90% or more of glomeruli). Class Histology Usual approach I minimal mesangial treat extrarenal disease only usually none; if proteinuria >3 II mesangial g, consider podocytopathy proliferative (GC \u00b1 CNI) focal (<50% of III induction then maintenance glomeruli), subendothelial deposits diffuse (\u202150%) as III; most aggressive IV V membranous immunosuppress if nephrotic (subepithelial) or >1 g/day despite RAAS blockade; III/IV + V is treated as III/IV \u202190% globally VI no immunosuppression for the kidney; prepare for RRT sclerosed and transplant The 2018 revision dropped the A/C and S/G sub-labels in favour of a modified NIH activity index (0\u201324) and chronicity index (0\u201312). A chronicity index \u20214 predicts poor renal survival. Always report podocytopathy and TMA.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":261,"s":"What is the initial therapy for class IV lupus nephritis (proteinuria 3.2 g/day, creatinine 98 \u00b5mol/L), and what is the low-dose (Euro-Lupus) cyclophosphamide regimen?","o":["Oral prednisolone alone, with no steroid-sparing agent, at a fixed low dose","IV methylprednisolone then a planned oral taper, with hydroxychloroquine and mycophenolate combined with belimumab, a calcineurin inhibitor or obinutuzumab; Euro-Lupus is 500 mg IV every 2 weeks for 6 doses (total 3 g), followed by maintenance","Rituximab monotherapy, without any concomitant immunosuppressant","Mycophenolate alone, at the lowest possible dose from the outset","High-dose glucocorticoids alone, without any steroid-sparing agent"],"a":1,"e":"Current EULAR (2025) and BSR (2026) recommendations favour combination therapy for class III/IV nephritis: mycophenolate plus belimumab, plus a calcineurin inhibitor (voclosporin or tacrolimus) or plus obinutuzumab, on a background of hydroxychloroquine and glucocorticoids (IV pulses then a taper to 5 mg/day or less by about 4\u20136 months). Mycophenolate alone or Euro-Lupus cyclophosphamide (with or without belimumab) are alternatives with lower efficacy. The low-dose regimen has similar efficacy to high-dose cyclophosphamide with less gonadal and infection toxicity, and is followed by maintenance with azathioprine or mycophenolate. Targets are a 25% fall in uPCR by 3 months, 50% by 6 months and uPCR below 70 mg/mmol by 12 months. Add-on Trial Best for BLISS-LN proteinuria <3 g, Belimumab (43% vs frequent extrarenal 32%) flares; less effective with cyclophosphamide AURORA (CR heavy proteinuria, mixed Voclosporin or 41% vs 23%) class V; avoid if eGFR tacrolimus \u00a345 fewer flares, steroid Obinutuzumab REGENCY (CR about sparing; watch IgG 46% vs 33% at week 76) MMF 2\u20133 g/day (preferred in Black and Hispanic patients and women of childbearing age). High-dose NIH cyclophosphamide is reserved for crescentic or rapidly progressive GN. The EULAR course defines complete response as UPCR <50 mg/mmol with stable eGFR on \u00a35 mg/day prednisolone by 12 months. Proteinuria may take 18\u201324 months to settle. Not on track: check adherence (MPA and HCQ levels), switch induction agent, add a third agent or anti-CD20, consider re-biopsy. Pure class V: RAAS blockade; if nephrotic or >1 g/day, MMF plus CNI. Consider anticoagulation in nephrotic class V with albumin <20\u201325 g/L.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":262,"s":"After successful induction for lupus nephritis, what is the recommended maintenance strategy?","o":["Stop all immunosuppression once remission is reached at 6 months","Switch to cyclophosphamide pulses every 3 months for maintenance","Continue induction-dose glucocorticoids unchanged for the full 3 years","Continue mycophenolate-based therapy (with belimumab, a calcineurin inhibitor or obinutuzumab where used) for at least 3 years, with hydroxychloroquine and glucocorticoid withdrawal","Mycophenolate mofetil"],"a":3,"e":"Maintenance should continue for at least 3 years. The 2026 BSR guideline prefers continuing the induction combination (mycophenolate with obinutuzumab, belimumab or a calcineurin inhibitor), using mycophenolate alone if combinations are unsuitable, and azathioprine if mycophenolate is not tolerated or pregnancy is planned. Full glucocorticoid withdrawal should be attempted. Renal function, proteinuria, sediment, complement and anti-dsDNA are monitored. Use mycophenolate at the lowest effective dose (about 1\u20132 g/day), and continue belimumab, voclosporin or obinutuzumab if used in induction. Continue for \u20213\u20135 years after response, then withdraw slowly. Switch to azathioprine for pregnancy. Continue HCQ indefinitely. Monitor every 2\u20134 weeks during induction and every 3 months once stable (BP, eGFR, UPCR, sediment, FBC, albumin, C3/C4, dsDNA, IgG on anti-CD20). Persistent proteinuria >0.7\u20130.8 g/day at 12 months predicts poor renal outcome.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":263,"s":"Which measures complement immunosuppression in lupus nephritis?","o":["High-dose NSAIDs for symptomatic relief of arthralgia","Long-term prophylactic anticoagulation in all patients, regardless of APS status","Avoiding all antihypertensives until proteinuria resolves","Permissive hypertension, since blood pressure control does not affect renal outcomes","Blood pressure control with ACE inhibitor or ARB for proteinuria, lipid management, and an SGLT2 inhibitor where appropriate"],"a":4,"e":"Renoprotection reduces proteinuria and progression but does not replace treatment of active proliferative nephritis. EULAR 2025 suggests adding an SGLT2 inhibitor if proteinuria persists after 12 months of treatment. Potassium and renal function need monitoring. Renoprotection checklist: HCQ (reduce if eGFR <30); ACE inhibitor or ARB with BP <130/80 (systolic <120 if tolerated); SGLT2 inhibitor once stable with eGFR \u202120 (evidence from CKD trials, not LN-specific); statin; avoid NSAIDs; sodium restriction; PJP prophylaxis with high-dose GC; bone protection; vaccination; fertility preservation if using cyclophosphamide. In kidney failure, transplantation is preferred (recurrence <5\u201310%), with anticoagulation if APS is present.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Infection & Immunosuppression","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":264,"s":"A woman with SLE has isolated severe thrombocytopenia (12 x 10I/L) with mucosal bleeding. What is the most appropriate initial treatment?","o":["Platelet transfusion alone, without any immunosuppression","High-dose glucocorticoids including IV methylprednisolone pulses, with IVIG","Oral prednisolone alone at a low dose, reassessed in 2 weeks","Watchful waiting, since ITP in SLE is usually self-limiting","Anticoagulation first, given the risk of associated antiphospholipid syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"Acute severe immune thrombocytopenia is treated with high-dose glucocorticoids and IVIG when bleeding or a rapid rise is needed. Maintenance agents include MMF, azathioprine or ciclosporin; rituximab, thrombopoietin receptor agonists or splenectomy are later options. Thrombocytopenia in SLE: immune (ITP-like), TTP or TMA (schistocytes, high LDH, low haptoglobin, ADAMTS13 <10%), aPL-associated or catastrophic APS, HLH or MAS, drugs (azathioprine, MMF, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, co-trimoxazole), hypersplenism, marrow infiltration, Evans syndrome, pseudothrombocytopenia (EDTA clumping). Maintenance options: MMF, azathioprine, ciclosporin, rituximab, TPO receptor agonists (eltrombopag, romiplostim), splenectomy. Aspirin can still be used in aPL thrombocytopenia once platelets are >50.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":265,"s":"A patient with active generalised SLE develops seizures and psychosis. Infection, metabolic causes and drugs have been excluded. What is the most appropriate treatment?","o":["Oral prednisolone alone, without any steroid-sparing agent","Antipsychotic medication alone, without immunosuppression","High-dose glucocorticoids (including pulses) with cyclophosphamide","Plasma exchange alone, without glucocorticoids or cyclophosphamide","Anticoagulation alone, regardless of antiphospholipid antibody status"],"a":2,"e":"Inflammatory neuropsychiatric lupus is treated with high-dose glucocorticoids and cyclophosphamide (or rituximab if refractory), plus symptomatic therapy. Thrombotic events related to aPL are managed with anticoagulation. NP events in SLE are attributable to SLE in only about a third. Mimics: infection (LP, cultures; PML on rituximab), steroid psychosis (first 1\u20132 weeks at >40 mg/day), metabolic disturbance (uraemia, hyponatraemia, hypoglycaemia), PRES (hypertension, CNIs), TTP, aPL thrombosis, drugs, primary psychiatric disease, NMOSD (AQP4-IgG), MOG antibody disease, MS. The ACR nomenclature lists 19 syndromes (12 CNS, 7 PNS). Attribution weighs timing (within 6 months of diagnosis), event type, confounders and supportive features (activity, MRI, CSF, anti-ribosomal P, aPL). Mechanisms: thrombotic or ischaemic (focal) vs inflammatory or antibody-mediated (diffuse: anti-NMDA NR2, anti-ribosomal P). Investigations: MRI with contrast, DWI, FLAIR and SWI; CSF (mainly to exclude infection); aPL, anti-ribosomal P, AQP4 and MOG; EEG; NCS; neuropsychological tests. Transverse myelitis is an emergency: pulse GC and cyclophosphamide within hours, plus PLEX if poor response; treat as NMOSD if AQP4-positive. Seizures: antiepileptics; long-term treatment may be unnecessary after a single provoked seizure with normal MRI and EEG. Cognition: treat vascular risk, mood and sleep; do not escalate immunosuppression. Chorea: usually aPL-related; antithrombotics. Maintenance: MMF or azathioprine. Refractory disease: rituximab, IVIG, PLEX.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":266,"s":"A patient with SLE on immunosuppression develops fever and pleuritic chest pain. What must be considered before attributing this to a lupus flare?","o":["Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia","Infection (including opportunistic infection) and pulmonary embolism","A simple viral upper respiratory tract infection, requiring no further work-up","Malignancy alone, without considering infection or thromboembolism","A flare of serositis alone, once a chest radiograph is normal"],"a":1,"e":"Infection is a leading cause of death in SLE and can mimic or coexist with a flare. Thromboembolism must also be considered, especially with aPL. Feature Infection Flare rigors, high low-grade Fever neutrophilia, leucopenia, lymphopenia WCC left shift >50 mg/L CRP normal or mild rise (except serositis or arthritis) >0.5 Procalcitonin normal rising dsDNA, falling Serology unchanged C3/C4 If in doubt, treat both: cultures and antibiotics, with senior review of lupus therapy. Consider opportunistic organisms (PJP, CMV, TB, fungi, Nocardia, Listeria, zoster). Do a CTPA for pleuritic pain with aPL. Risk factors for serious infection: activity, GC (even 5\u201310 mg), cyclophosphamide or B-cell depletion, lymphopenia, hypogammaglobulinaemia, LN, low albumin, hyposplenism. HCQ is protective.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":267,"s":"A patient with SLE on mycophenolate and prednisolone develops fever with a neutrophil count of 0.3 x 10I/L. What should happen?","o":["Oral antibiotics only, since she is not currently febrile with chills","Increase mycophenolate to treat presumed disease activity","Urgent assessment and treatment for neutropenic sepsis, with review of drug toxicity Rheumatology SCE \u2014 5-option SBA 77","Observation with a repeat FBC in 48 hours, since she is not acutely unwell","Stop prednisolone only, continuing mycophenolate unchanged"],"a":2,"e":"Urgent assessment and treatment for neutropenic sepsis, with review of drug toxicity Fever with neutropenia is a medical emergency requiring prompt cultures and empirical antibiotics. Neutropenic sepsis pathway: cultures and broad-spectrum antibiotics within 1 hour, withhold myelotoxic drugs (MMF, azathioprine, cyclophosphamide, co-trimoxazole), and consider G-CSF. Isolated asymptomatic lupus leucopenia or lymphopenia does not need immunosuppression. Check TPMT and NUDT15 before azathioprine.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":268,"s":"A patient with SLE develops fever, confusion, thrombocytopenia and worsening renal function. What is the key immediate principle?","o":["Treat only for the single most likely diagnosis, to avoid unnecessary immunosuppression","Assume a lupus flare alone, since fever is common in active SLE","Wait for ADAMTS13 results before starting any treatment","Treat only for catastrophic APS, since thrombocytopenia is the dominant feature","Assess urgently and in parallel for infection, thrombotic microangiopathy, catastrophic APS macrophage activation syndrome and severe lupus activity"],"a":4,"e":"Fever, confusion, thrombocytopenia and renal decline in SLE can reflect several life-threatening processes at once. A blood film for schistocytes, ADAMTS13, ferritin, fibrinogen, cultures and aPL testing help distinguish infection, thrombotic microangiopathy, catastrophic APS, MAS and severe lupus activity. Process Key tests schistocytes, ADAMTS13 <10%, high LDH, TTP low haptoglobin \u20213 organs within a week, microthrombi, Catastrophic aPL (often LA, triple-positive) APS ferritin >5,000\u201310,000, falling ESR, HLH or MAS fibrinogen and platelets, raised triglycerides, sCD25, haemophagocytosis on marrow cultures, procalcitonin, prolonged PT and Sepsis or DIC APTT, low fibrinogen dsDNA, C3/C4, active sediment Lupus flare Treat in parallel: plasma exchange for TTP (with caplacizumab where indicated); anticoagulation plus GC plus PLEX and/or IVIG for CAPS; GC plus anakinra or ciclosporin for MAS; antibiotics.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":269,"s":"A patient with SLE reports severe fatigue. Disease activity indices are low. What is the most appropriate approach?","o":["Repeat disease activity indices weekly until a cause is found","Increase immunosuppression empirically, since fatigue likely reflects subclinical activity","Refer for a sleep study as the first and only step","Assess and address sleep, mood, anaemia, thyroid disease, vitamin D, pain and deconditioning, as well as disease activity","Reassure that fatigue in SLE always resolves once serology normalises"],"a":3,"e":"Fatigue in SLE is multifactorial. Exercise, sleep and psychological approaches are useful. Fatigue occurs in 80\u201390% and correlates poorly with activity. Screen and treat depression and anxiety (25\u201340%), sleep disorders, fibromyalgia, anaemia, hypothyroidism, vitamin D deficiency and deconditioning. Graded aerobic exercise and psychological interventions are most effective. Anifrolumab and belimumab modestly improve fatigue.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Metabolic Bone Disease","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":270,"s":"A 29-year-old woman with SLE and photosensitive cutaneous disease is clinically stable on hydroxychloroquine. Which is the most appropriate long-term preventive care strategy?","o":["Recommend sun protection only during a cutaneous flare and omit routine cardiovascular assessment","Stop hydroxychloroquine when symptoms settle and avoid vaccination while taking immunomodulatory therapy","Rely exclusively on a standard cardiovascular risk calculator without reviewing disease-related risk factors","Advise regular broad-spectrum sun protection, review vaccination and bone health, and assess both traditional and SLE-related cardiovascular risk factors","Replace cardiovascular risk-factor treatment with long-term oral glucocorticoids"],"a":3,"e":"Photoprotection and regular assessment of comorbidities, cardiovascular risk factors and vaccination are important components of SLE care. Continue hydroxychloroquine unless contraindicated and minimise chronic glucocorticoid exposure. Individualise screening and immunisation according to disease, treatment and national policy. Sources: 2025 ACR SLE treatment guideline; EULAR 2023 SLE management recommendations.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":271,"s":"A 62-year-old man taking hydralazine develops arthralgia, serositis and a positive ANA. Anti-dsDNA is negative and complement is normal. Which antibody is most typical?","o":["Anti-MDA5","Anti-histone","Anti-Sm","Anti-NXP2","Anti-Jo-1"],"a":1,"e":"Drug-induced lupus (hydralazine, procainamide, isoniazid, minocycline) typically has anti-histone antibodies, rarely involves the kidney or CNS, and resolves after withdrawal. TNF inhibitor-induced lupus more often features antidsDNA. Type Drugs Serology Features procainamide, anti-histone arthralgia, Classic >95%; myalgia, fever, DIL hydralazine (slow serositis; dsDNA and acetylators), low kidney and isoniazid, complement CNS rare minocycline (prare ANCA, hepatitis), quinidine, methyldopa, chlorpromazine, sulfasalazine, terbinafine Antiinfliximab, rash, arthritis, dsDNA TNFetanercept, about 90%, serositis induced adalimumab low (ATIL) complement about 50% Drug- PPIs, thiazides, anti-Ro annular CCBs, ACE induced photosensitive inhibitors, SCLE rash terbinafine, taxanes, TNFi, ICIs IFN-a, ICIs, JAK Other variable can unmask and other true SLE targeted agents Stop the drug; symptoms settle within weeks, though ANA can persist for months to years. Use NSAIDs, short lowdose GC or HCQ if symptoms persist. If features persist beyond about 6 months, reconsider true SLE. Do not rechallenge. Hydralazine can also cause ANCA vasculitis (MPO, often with anti-histone and anti-elastase).","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":272,"s":"A 49-year-old woman develops annular, polycyclic, photosensitive plaques on her upper back and arms 6 weeks after starting terbinafine. Anti-Ro is positive. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Tinea corporis, confirmed by fungal scraping","Levamisole-adulterated cocaine","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia","Drug-induced subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus","Polymorphic light eruption"],"a":3,"e":"SCLE causes non-scarring annular or papulosquamous lesions and is strongly associated with anti-Ro. Triggers include terbinafine, PPIs, thiazides, calcium-channel blockers and checkpoint inhibitors. Annular photosensitive lesions: SCLE vs tinea corporis (scrape for fungus), granuloma annulare, erythema annulare centrifugum, psoriasis, polymorphic light eruption, dermatomyositis, Sj\u00f6gren's annular erythema (Asian patients), neonatal lupus. Stop the trigger (lesions resolve over weeks to months), photoprotection, topical steroids, HCQ. Look for systemic SLE (about 50% of SCLE meets criteria, usually mild). In women of childbearing age, anti-Ro has implications for future pregnancy (CHB).","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Paediatric Rheumatology"]},{"id":273,"s":"Which set of conditions defines remission in SLE according to the DORIS definition?","o":["SLEDAI of 4 or less, with prednisolone 7.5 mg/day or less","Complete normalisation of complement and anti-dsDNA, regardless of clinical findings","Clinical SLEDAI 0 with glucocorticoids stopped entirely, since any dose excludes remission","Clinical SLEDAI 0, physician global assessment below 0.5, prednisolone 5 mg/day or less, with stable antimalarials, immunosuppressants and biologics allowed","Physician global assessment below 0.5 alone, without reference to glucocorticoid dose"],"a":3,"e":"Serology is ignored in clinical SLEDAI. Lupus low disease activity state (SLEDAI 4 or less, prednisolone 7.5 mg/day or less) is an alternative target. LLDAS: SLEDAI-2K \u00a34 with no major-organ activity, no haemolysis or GI activity, no new features, PGA \u00a31, prednisolone \u00a37.5 mg/day, with standard immunosuppressants and biologics tolerated. Spending \u202150% of follow-up in LLDAS halves damage, flares and mortality. Reaching a target on high-dose GC does not count. Treat-to-target: assess activity at every visit and damage yearly. Aim for remission or LLDAS within 6\u201312 months with prednisolone \u00a35 mg. Escalate if the target is not met by 3\u20136 months, after checking adherence (up to 50% are nonadherent) and excluding non-lupus causes. Flare predictors: young onset, prior LN, CNS disease or vasculitis, persistently active serology, stopping HCQ, rapid GC withdrawal, pregnancy, UV, infection.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":274,"s":"Which laboratory tests form the antiphospholipid antibody criteria, and why must positive results be repeated?","o":["Anti-dsDNA and anti-Sm, repeated at least 12 weeks apart","ANA and complement levels, repeated at least 6 weeks apart","Rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP, with no need for repeat testing","Lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin and anti-beta2-glycoprotein I antibodies, repeated at least 12 weeks apart, because transient antibodies are common","A single lupus anticoagulant test only, without repetition"],"a":3,"e":"Positivity must be persistent on two occasions at least 12 weeks apart, with moderate to high titres for solid-phase assays. Transient aPL occur with infection, drugs and other conditions, and low-titre results have little clinical significance. Triple positivity identifies the highest-risk profile. Causes of transient or non-pathogenic aPL: infections (syphilis, HIV, HCV, EBV, parvovirus, COVID-19, leprosy), drugs (chlorpromazine, procainamide, hydralazine, phenytoin, quinidine), malignancy (lymphoma), older age, other autoimmune diseases. Other prothrombotic states to consider: factor V Leiden, prothrombin G20210A, protein C, protein S or antithrombin deficiency, PNH (flow cytometry), myeloproliferative neoplasm (JAK2), HIT, malignancy, nephrotic syndrome, Beh\u00e7et's. LA is the strongest single predictor of thrombosis. Testing is unreliable during acute thrombosis and on anticoagulants (see next entry). Do a dRVVT plus an LA-sensitive APTT (two tests).","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":275,"s":"A patient with a recent pulmonary embolism started apixaban before antiphospholipid testing. Lupus anticoagulant is reported as positive. How should this be interpreted?","o":["A true positive result, confirming APS regardless of anticoagulation","DOACs can cause false-positive lupus anticoagulant results; repeat off anticoagulation or with DOACremoval techniques, and confirm persistence","A result that cannot be interpreted and should simply be ignored","An indication to switch immediately to warfarin without retesting","Confirmation of high-risk APS, requiring lifelong warfarin without further testing"],"a":1,"e":"DOACs, heparin and VKAs interfere with clotting-based LA assays, whereas solid-phase assays for anticardiolipin and anti-beta2-glycoprotein I are unaffected. Options: retest LA \u20212 days after stopping a DOAC (if clinically safe); use DOAC-removal agents (DOAC-Stop) before LA testing; switch to LMWH (with anti-Xaneutralising reagents); rely on solid-phase assays and antib2GPI, which are unaffected; or use the Taipan or Ecarin ratio (insensitive to VKAs). Confirm persistence at 12 weeks. Pre-test counselling matters, because finding highrisk APS changes the choice of anticoagulant (a DOAC is not appropriate).","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus"]},{"id":276,"s":"Which obstetric complications meet the clinical criteria for APS?","o":["Two unexplained miscarriages before 10 weeks, with no further criteria needed","Any history of infertility or a single miscarriage at any gestation","A single episode of pre-eclampsia at any gestation, regardless of severity","Delivery before 37 weeks for any indication","Three or more unexplained consecutive miscarriages before 10 weeks, unexplained fetal death at or beyond 10 weeks, or delivery before 34 weeks for severe pre-eclampsia or placental insufficiency"],"a":4,"e":"These are the revised Sapporo (Sydney) clinical criteria. Maternal anatomical and hormonal causes and parental chromosomal abnormalities should be excluded for recurrent early loss. The 2023 ACR/EULAR APS classification criteria weight obstetric items differently: recurrent early losses score lowest, while severe pre-eclampsia or placental insufficiency before 34 weeks score highest. Recurrent miscarriage work-up: parental karyotype (balanced translocation), uterine anomalies (3D ultrasound), thyroid disease, diabetes, PCOS, hyperprolactinaemia, inherited thrombophilia (limited relevance), cervical insufficiency (second-trimester loss), infection, maternal age. Sydney clinical obstetric criteria: - \u20213 unexplained consecutive miscarriages before 10 weeks - \u20211 unexplained fetal death at or beyond 10 weeks - \u20211 delivery before 34 weeks for severe pre-eclampsia, eclampsia or placental insufficiency The 2023 criteria give the most weight to severe early preeclampsia or placental insufficiency before 34 weeks.","c":"Pregnancy & Reproductive","t":["Pregnancy & Reproductive","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":277,"s":"A 30-year-old woman has triple-positive antiphospholipid antibodies but no history of thrombosis or pregnancy complications. What is the recommended prophylaxis?","o":["Full-dose therapeutic anticoagulation with warfarin","No prophylaxis is needed in asymptomatic carriers","Prophylactic-dose LMWH indefinitely, without aspirin","Clopidogrel, in preference to aspirin","Low-dose aspirin (75-100 mg daily)"],"a":4,"e":"Asymptomatic carriers with a high-risk aPL profile are offered low-dose aspirin, along with management of other thrombotic risk factors. Hydroxychloroquine is added in SLE. Low-dose aspirin for a high-risk aPL profile (and consider it for low-risk profiles in SLE), plus HCQ in SLE. Manage CV risk factors. Avoid oestrogen-containing contraception and HRT. Use thromboprophylaxis (LMWH) in high-risk situations such as surgery, immobilisation, the post-partum period and long-haul travel.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":278,"s":"A woman with persistent antiphospholipid antibodies has a first unprovoked DVT. What is the recommended long-term anticoagulation, and which anticoagulant should be avoided if she is triple-positive?","o":["Warfarin anticoagulation","Switch to a DOAC after an initial period of warfarin, once stable","No long-term anticoagulation is needed after a first provoked event","Long-term warfarin with target INR 2\u20133; avoid DOACs such as rivaroxaban","Antiplatelet therapy alone, without any anticoagulant"],"a":3,"e":"A first unprovoked venous event in APS warrants long-term vitamin K antagonist therapy with INR 2\u20133. Rivaroxaban was associated with more thrombotic events than warfarin in triple-positive APS (TRAPS trial), so DOACs are avoided in high-risk APS. They may be considered only in selected lower-risk (single- or double-positive) venous APS when a VKA is not feasible or a stable INR cannot be achieved. Other trials: ASTRO-APS (apixaban, more strokes) and Ordi-Rimola (rivaroxaban, more arterial events). Point-ofcare INR may be inaccurate when LA is strongly positive; check against laboratory INR. Recurrence despite INR 2\u20133: check adherence and time in therapeutic range, then increase the target to 3\u20134, add lowdose aspirin, or switch to therapeutic LMWH. Add HCQ (and possibly a statin) as adjuncts. Provoked VTE with a low-risk profile can be considered for 3\u20136 months of treatment only after specialist review.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Crystal Arthropathy"]},{"id":279,"s":"A young adult (with or without SLE) has an ischaemic stroke with persistently positive lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin and anti-beta2-glycoprotein I antibodies. What is the most appropriate long-term treatment?","o":["Low-dose aspirin alone, without anticoagulation","A DOAC such as rivaroxaban, given its ease of use","Prophylactic-dose LMWH alone, continued indefinitely","Long-term warfarin; DOACs are avoided after arterial events in APS","No long-term anticoagulation, since this was a single isolated event"],"a":3,"e":"Arterial thrombosis with persistent aPL is managed with a vitamin K antagonist: INR 2\u20133 or 3\u20134 (balancing bleeding and recurrence), or INR 2\u20133 plus low-dose aspirin. This applies whether lupus activity is high or low. Young stroke differential: cardioembolism (PFO with paradoxical embolism, AF, Libman\u2013Sacks or infective endocarditis), arterial dissection, vasculitis (primary CNS, Takayasu, Beh\u00e7et's, SLE), RCVS, CADASIL, Fabry disease, sickle cell disease, moyamoya, DADA2 (lacunar strokes, livedo), drugs (cocaine, amphetamines), inherited thrombophilia (venous events more than arterial), Sneddon's syndrome, homocystinuria. Investigate with MRI and MRA, echo (TOE for vegetations), Holter and a thrombophilia screen. Manage BP, lipids and smoking. Continue HCQ in SLE.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":280,"s":"A woman with known APS develops renal failure, livedo, confusion and thrombocytopenia over 5 days, with microthrombi on skin biopsy. What is the first-line treatment?","o":["Anticoagulation plus high-dose glucocorticoids plus plasma exchange and/or IVIG","Anticoagulation alone, without any immunosuppression","High-dose glucocorticoids alone, without anticoagulation","Plasma exchange alone, without anticoagulation or glucocorticoids","IV methylprednisolone and IVIG alone, without anticoagulation"],"a":0,"e":"Catastrophic APS (multi-organ small-vessel thrombosis within a week) has high mortality. Triple therapy is first-line and triggers such as infection are treated; rituximab or eculizumab are used for refractory disease. TTP (ADAMTS13 <10%), aHUS (complement), HIT (heparin exposure; PF4 antibodies), DIC (sepsis), HELLP, malignant hypertension, scleroderma renal crisis, sepsis. CAPS: thrombosis in \u20213 organs within a week, histological small-vessel occlusion, and aPL. Mortality is about 30\u2013 50%. Common organs: kidney, lung (ARDS, DAH), brain, heart, skin, adrenals (haemorrhage), liver. About half have a trigger: infection, surgery, stopping anticoagulation, SLE flare, pregnancy or the puerperium. Triple therapy: therapeutic heparin, high-dose GC (pulses) and PLEX and/or IVIG. Treat the trigger. For refractory disease: rituximab or eculizumab (complement C5 inhibition). Intensive care.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":281,"s":"A 32-year-old woman with three consecutive unexplained miscarriages before 10 weeks has persistent medium-high titre anticardiolipin antibodies. She is newly pregnant. What is the most appropriate treatment?","o":["No treatment, since she has not had a prior thrombosis","Switch to therapeutic-dose LMWH with low-dose aspirin before 6 weeks' gestation, continuing through pregnancy and postpartum","High-dose aspirin alone, without any heparin","Low-dose aspirin plus prophylactic-dose LMWH","Warfarin throughout pregnancy, switching to LMWH only near delivery"],"a":3,"e":"Obstetric APS without prior thrombosis is treated with low-dose aspirin and prophylactic LMWH, continuing LMWH for 6 weeks postpartum. Live birth rates in treated obstetric APS are about 70\u201380%. HCQ added to standard therapy is under study (HYPATIA) and is often used for refractory cases. Start aspirin before conception (or from a positive test) and LMWH from a positive pregnancy test or the fetal heart. Monitor with uterine artery Doppler at 20\u201324 weeks and serial growth scans. Refractory obstetric APS: add HCQ, consider increasing LMWH to intermediate or therapeutic dose, add low-dose prednisolone in the first trimester, or IVIG (expert centre). Women with aPL-positivity but no events: aspirin, plus postpartum LMWH for 6 weeks if highrisk or other risk factors.","c":"Pregnancy & Reproductive","t":["Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":282,"s":"How should thrombotic APS be managed in pregnancy, including in a woman on warfarin who finds she is 5 weeks pregnant?","o":["Low-dose aspirin plus prophylactic-dose LMWH","Stop anticoagulation entirely for the first trimester to avoid teratogenicity","Continue warfarin unchanged throughout pregnancy","Switch to therapeutic-dose LMWH with low-dose aspirin before 6 weeks' gestation, continuing through pregnancy and postpartum","Switch to a DOAC for the remainder of pregnancy"],"a":3,"e":"Thrombotic APS needs therapeutic anticoagulation throughout pregnancy and postpartum. Warfarin is teratogenic (embryopathy risk is greatest between 6 and 12 weeks) and DOACs are not used in pregnancy. Warfarin is compatible with breastfeeding postpartum. Warfarin embryopathy: nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses (chondrodysplasia punctata). Later exposure carries fetal intracranial haemorrhage risk. Peri-delivery: plan neuraxial anaesthesia timing (stop prophylactic LMWH 12 hours before, therapeutic 24 hours before). Restart LMWH after delivery and bridge back to warfarin. Use a monitored anti-Xa level in extremes of weight or renal impairment. Women on warfarin should be counselled before conception and do early pregnancy tests.","c":"Pregnancy & Reproductive","t":["Pregnancy & Reproductive","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":283,"s":"What is the role of hydroxychloroquine in thrombosis in SLE?","o":["It fully replaces anticoagulation in confirmed thrombotic APS","It has no effect on thrombotic risk and is used only for skin and joint disease","It is used only once thrombosis has already occurred, not as background therapy","It may reduce thrombotic risk as background therapy but does not replace anticoagulation in confirmed thrombotic APS","It should be stopped once a patient starts anticoagulation"],"a":3,"e":"Hydroxychloroquine is part of background SLE therapy and may add benefit in aPL-positive patients. HCQ reduces thrombosis in aPL-positive SLE (observational data). Mechanisms include reduced aPL binding to phospholipid bilayers, reduced platelet activation and improved lipids. Continue HCQ in all SLE patients. It is adjunctive only: confirmed thrombotic APS still needs a VKA. HCQ is also used in refractory obstetric APS. Statins are an adjunct in recurrent thrombosis.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":284,"s":"A patient meets the systemic sclerosis classification criteria. What is the minimum total score required?","o":["9","6","Right-heart catheterisation","3 points; he meets the criteria","12"],"a":0,"e":"Skin thickening of the fingers of both hands extending proximal to the MCP joints scores 9 and is sufficient alone. Otherwise weighted items (puffy fingers, sclerodactyly, fingertip lesions, telangiectasia, abnormal nailfold capillaries, PAH or ILD, Raynaud's, SSc-specific antibodies) are summed. Item Points 9 Bilateral finger skin thickening proximal to (sufficient) the MCPs Puffy fingers / sclerodactyly distal to the 2 / 4 MCPs Digital ulcers / pitting scars 2 / 3 2 Telangiectasia 2 Abnormal nailfold capillaries PAH and/or ILD 2 Raynaud's 3 ACA, Scl-70 or RNA pol III 3 Sensitivity about 91%, specificity about 92%. The criteria do not apply if skin thickening spares the fingers or a mimic explains the features better.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease"]},{"id":285,"s":"A woman has a year of Raynaud's and puffy fingers, a centromere-pattern ANA and giant capillaries with haemorrhages on nailfold capillaroscopy. How should this be interpreted, and how does the scleroderma capillaroscopy pattern evolve?","o":["Primary Raynaud's phenomenon requiring only reassurance","Established diffuse systemic sclerosis requiring immediate immunosuppression","Very early systemic sclerosis (VEDOSS) requiring monitoring for organ involvement; the pattern evolves from early (giant capillaries, microhaemorrhages) through active to late (capillary loss, avascular areas, ramified capillaries)","Mixed connective tissue disease, defined by this capillaroscopy pattern alone","A normal finding in healthy young women, requiring no follow-up"],"a":2,"e":"Raynaud's with puffy fingers, SSc-specific antibodies and a scleroderma capillaroscopy pattern indicates very early SSc, so baseline and serial screening for ILD, PAH and other organ involvement is needed. Primary Raynaud's has normal capillaroscopy and negative autoantibodies. The capillaroscopic stage correlates with digital ulcer risk. Secondary Raynaud's: SSc, SLE, MCTD, Sj\u00f6gren's, myositis (especially antisynthetase), UCTD; vibration white finger (hand-arm vibration syndrome); hypothenar hammer syndrome; thoracic outlet syndrome (unilateral); atherosclerosis or Buerger's disease (smokers); cryoglobulinaemia, cold agglutinins, paraprotein; drugs (bblockers, ergots, sympathomimetics, chemotherapy such as bleomycin and cisplatin, interferon); hypothyroidism. Feature Primary Secondary (SSc) Raynaud's teens to 20s usually after 30 Onset mild, severe, painful, may Severity symmetrical be asymmetric Ulcers, pitting, no yes gangrene Capillaroscopy normal scleroderma pattern positive, SSc- ANA negative or low titre specific Abnormal capillaroscopy plus an SSc antibody gives a >60\u201380% chance of progression to SSc. Refer anyone with Raynaud's plus puffy fingers, a positive ANA or abnormal capillaroscopy. A handheld dermatoscope works for clinic capillaroscopy.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":286,"s":"What does the modified Rodnan skin score measure?","o":["Digital ulcer count and severity, scored 0 to 3","Internal organ involvement, scored across 12 domains","Nailfold capillary density, scored at 8 sites","Pulmonary function decline, scored over 17 body sites","Skin thickness at 17 body sites scored 0-3 (maximum 51)"],"a":4,"e":"The mRSS is the main skin outcome in diffuse SSc trials and correlates with disease severity and mortality. The mRSS scores 17 sites from 0 to 3 (total 0\u201351). Skin progresses over the first 1\u20133 years in dcSSc, then often softens. Early rapid progression predicts organ involvement and mortality.","c":"General Rheumatology","t":["General Rheumatology"]},{"id":287,"s":"Which clinical phenotypes are associated with anti-centromere, anti-Scl-70 and anti-RNA polymerase III antibodies?","o":["Anti-centromere: limited cutaneous disease, calcinosis and late PAH. Anti-Scl-70: diffuse disease and","Anti-centromere: diffuse disease with early renal crisis. Anti-Scl-70: limited disease with late PAH","Anti-RNA polymerase III: limited disease with a low cancer risk","All three antibodies carry an equally high risk of scleroderma renal crisis","These antibodies are non-specific and carry no prognostic value in systemic sclerosis"],"a":0,"e":"ILD. Anti-RNA polymerase III: rapidly progressive diffuse skin disease, renal crisis, GAVE and cancer around disease onset Anti-PM/Scl is associated with myositis overlap. Patients with anti-RNA polymerase III need close blood pressure and renal monitoring, and age-appropriate cancer screening. Antibody Frequency Subset Associations 20\u201340% late PAH, calcinosis, ACA limited digital ischaemia; ILD uncommon; better survival Scl-70 15\u201340% early ILD, digital ulcers, diffuse > limited cardiac disease; worse survival 5\u201325% rapid skin progression, RNA pol diffuse renal crisis, cancer \u00b12\u2013 III 3 years from onset, GAVE, contractures U3-RNP 4\u201310% PAH, cardiac and diffuse muscle disease, GI; poor prognosis Th/To 2\u20135% ILD, PAH limited PM/Scl 2\u20133% myositis, ILD, overlap calcinosis, mechanic's hands; good prognosis U1-RNP 5\u201310% arthritis, myositis, PAH MCTD 2\u20134% myositis, arthritis Ku overlap Antibodies are present in 90\u201395% and are largely mutually exclusive. With anti-RNA pol III: home BP monitoring, and extra cancer vigilance (breast and lung) within 2\u20133 years of onset.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease"]},{"id":288,"s":"A 44-year-old woman with limited cutaneous SSc has frequent painful Raynaud's attacks despite keeping warm. What is the first-line drug?","o":["A dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker such as nifedipine","A beta-blocker such as propranolol","Gastric antral vascular ectasia (GAVE)","An ACE inhibitor such as ramipril","A selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor such as fluoxetine"],"a":0,"e":"dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker such as nifedipine CCBs are first-line, with PDE5 inhibitors as an alternative or addition. IV iloprost is used for severe attacks. Betablockers may worsen vasospasm. General: whole-body warmth, stop smoking, avoid vasoconstrictors (b-blockers, sympathomimetics, decongestants, triptans). First line: a dihydropyridine CCB (nifedipine MR, amlodipine) and/or a PDE5 inhibitor (sildenafil, tadalafil). Alternatives: losartan, fluoxetine, topical nitrates. Severe or refractory: IV iloprost. Refractory: botulinum toxin to the palm, digital sympathectomy, fat grafting.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease"]},{"id":289,"s":"A patient with SSc has a painful digital ulcer despite nifedipine; another has five new ulcers this year despite nifedipine and sildenafil, which heal with iloprost. What is used for healing, and what reduces new ulcers?","o":["Wound care, assessment for infection, and a PDE5 inhibitor and/or IV iloprost for healing; bosentan reduces the number of new digital ulcers but does not speed healing","Topical nitrates applied directly to the ulcer bed","Oral antibiotics alone, without any vascular treatment, for all ulcers","Observation only, since digital ulcers in SSc typically heal spontaneously","Surgical debridement as first-line, before any medical therapy"],"a":0,"e":"PDE5 inhibitors and IV prostanoids help ulcer healing. Infected ulcers need antibiotics, and critical ischaemia needs urgent vascular assessment. Bosentan requires monthly LFTs and effective contraception. Digital ulcer types and mimics: ischaemic fingertip ulcers vs ulcers over calcinosis or extensor surfaces (traction, mechanical), infection or osteomyelitis (probe to bone; Xray or MRI), macrovascular occlusion (ulnar artery occlusion is common in SSc; also emboli, thoracic outlet syndrome), vasculitis, cholesterol emboli, cryoglobulinaemia, Buerger's disease. Critical ischaemia: admit for IV iloprost, analgesia and consider 24\u201372 hours of anticoagulation plus aspirin. Arrange urgent vascular and hand surgery review. Do duplex or angiography for macrovascular disease.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":290,"s":"Which patients are at greatest risk of scleroderma renal crisis, how is renal crisis treated (for example BP 186/104, rising creatinine and microangiopathic haemolysis in early diffuse SSc on glucocorticoids), and what is the role of prophylactic ACE inhibition?","o":["Highest risk: longstanding limited cutaneous disease with anti-centromere antibody; avoid ACE inhibitors, which worsen outcomes","ACE inhibitors are given prophylactically to all anti-RNA polymerase III-positive patients","Highest risk: early (under 4 years) rapidly progressive diffuse disease, anti-RNA polymerase III, tendon friction rubs and prednisolone above about 15 mg/day. Treat with an ACE inhibitor such as captopril titrated to","Treat with an ARB rather than an ACE inhibitor, since ARBs act faster","Stop the ACE inhibitor immediately if creatinine rises after starting it"],"a":2,"e":"blood pressure, continued even on dialysis. ACE inhibitors are not given prophylactically ACE inhibitors are the mainstay of renal crisis and are continued even if dialysis is needed, as recovery can occur over months. CCBs can be added for blood pressure control. Prophylactic ACE inhibition has not prevented renal crisis and may be associated with worse outcomes. Creatinine may rise initially during treatment, which should not prompt withdrawal. AKI in SSc: renal crisis vs ANCA (MPO) GN (consider Dpenicillamine history), drug nephrotoxicity (NSAIDs, CNIs), TTP, aHUS, hypovolaemia (GI loss), pre-eclampsia, obstructive uropathy. Renal crisis is a TMA emergency: new accelerated hypertension (often >150/85, or a systolic rise \u202130 mmHg) with AKI, schistocytes, thrombocytopenia, headache, visual disturbance, seizures, heart failure and pericardial effusion. Normotensive SRC occurs in about 10% and carries a worse prognosis. Incidence is 2\u20135% overall and 10\u201315% in early diffuse disease. Ciclosporin also raises risk. Captopril 6.25\u201312.5 mg, increased every 4\u20138 hours, aiming for a fall of 10\u201320 mmHg per day without hypotension. Then add a CCB, then an a-blocker, then IV prostacyclin. 20\u201350% need dialysis; recovery can continue for up to 2 years, so delay transplant listing. Eculizumab for refractory TMA. One-year mortality is 10\u201320%. Prevention: home BP monitoring, avoid high-dose GC, and keep prednisolone \u00a310\u201315 mg with BP checks.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":291,"s":"A woman with newly diagnosed anti-Scl-70-positive diffuse SSc needs baseline assessment; another patient has ILD involving 25% of the lungs with FVC 68% and declining. What is the key baseline investigation, and what is first-line treatment?","o":["Echocardiography alone, without any pulmonary function testing","Cyclophosphamide as first-line, ahead of mycophenolate, for all SSc-ILD","High-resolution CT only once symptoms are already present","HRCT chest with full PFTs (FVC and DLCO) in every patient at baseline; mycophenolate is first-line for significant or progressive SSc-ILD","An annual chest radiograph alone, without HRCT or PFTs"],"a":3,"e":"HRCT is advised at baseline in all patients with SSc, with PFTs including FVC and DLCO and regular follow-up PFTs, especially in early disease. MMF, cyclophosphamide or rituximab are used for SSc-ILD, with MMF commonly first. Nintedanib is added for progressive fibrosis, and tocilizumab may be considered in early ILD with raised acute-phase reactants. High-dose glucocorticoids risk renal crisis. Breathlessness in SSc: ILD, PAH, cardiac disease (diastolic dysfunction, myocarditis), aspiration from reflux, anaemia (GAVE), chest wall restriction from truncal skin, myositis (respiratory muscles), drug pneumonitis (methotrexate, cyclophosphamide), infection, lung cancer (higher in SSc- ILD). ILD is present on HRCT in 50\u201365%, and 25\u201330% develop clinically progressive disease. It is the leading cause of death (30\u201335%). The usual pattern is NSIP. Risk factors: dcSSc, Scl-70, Th/To, U3-RNP, Ro52, male sex, African ancestry, early disease, raised CRP. Extent >20% on HRCT (Goh staging) predicts progression. Significant progression means FVC falls \u202110%, or 5\u20139% with DLCO \u202115%. Trial evidence: SLS-II (MMF \u00bb cyclophosphamide, better tolerated); SLS-I (cyclophosphamide); RECITAL and DESIRES (rituximab); focuSSced (tocilizumab preserved FVC); SENSCIS (nintedanib slowed FVC decline by 40\u2013 45%, including on MMF). Selected rapid progressors: AHSCT. End stage: oxygen, pulmonary rehabilitation, lung transplant (needs reflux control). Monitoring: PFTs every 3\u20136 months in early disease, at least yearly otherwise; treat reflux.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":292,"s":"A 40-year-old man has early diffuse cutaneous SSc with rapidly progressive skin thickening (mRSS 28) and no significant organ involvement. Which treatment is most appropriate for the skin disease?","o":["High-dose glucocorticoids alone, continued long-term","Cyclophosphamide alone, reserved specifically for skin disease","Rituximab as first-line, ahead of methotrexate or mycophenolate","Mycophenolate mofetil (or methotrexate or rituximab)","An ACE inhibitor, since it also helps skin fibrosis"],"a":3,"e":"Methotrexate, MMF or rituximab are used for skin fibrosis. Autologous stem cell transplantation is considered in selected rapidly progressive patients at experienced centres. Early dcSSc skin: methotrexate or MMF (preferred if ILD is present), or rituximab. Tocilizumab when CRP is raised (benefit mainly in lung preservation). GC only at \u00a310\u201315 mg. Symptomatic care: emollients and antihistamines for itch, laser for telangiectasia, surgical removal of calcinosis (drug evidence for sodium thiosulfate, diltiazem or bisphosphonates is weak), physiotherapy for contractures, mouth exercises for microstomia. Emerging: CAR-T, anifrolumab.","c":"Drug Monitoring & Safety","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":293,"s":"What is the role of autologous haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in systemic sclerosis?","o":["It is now first-line therapy for all diffuse SSc, regardless of organ risk","It carries no excess mortality compared with conventional immunosuppression","It is avoided in any patient with lung involvement","It is considered for selected patients with rapidly progressive diffuse disease at risk of organ failure, at experienced centres","It is used routinely for limited cutaneous disease with mild skin involvement"],"a":3,"e":"Trials show survival benefit in selected patients, balanced against substantial treatment-related mortality. Careful cardiopulmonary screening is required. ASTIS, SCOT and ASSIST trials showed event-free and overall survival benefit despite 5\u201310% treatment-related mortality. It suits selected patients under 65 with rapidly progressive dcSSc and without severe cardiac disease or advanced organ failure (cardiac MRI and right heart catheterisation screening).","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":294,"s":"A woman with limited SSc has progressive breathlessness with FVC 92%, DLCO 41% (FVC/DLCO 2.2) and a raised tricuspid regurgitant velocity. How is PAH screened for and confirmed?","o":["Spirometry alone, repeated every 5 years","Serum troponin alone, checked only if symptomatic","Observation only, since FVC is well preserved at 92%","Screen at least annually (PAH is often asymptomatic early and a normal echo does not exclude it) by combining symptoms, DLCO, NT-proBNP, echocardiography and algorithms such as DETECT; confirm by right heart catheterisation","Right-heart catheterisation"],"a":3,"e":"Isolated low DLCO with a high FVC/DLCO ratio suggests PAH. Diagnosis needs right heart catheterisation: mean PAP above 20 mmHg, PAWP 15 mmHg or less, and PVR above 2 Wood units. PH groups in SSc (often coexisting): 1, PAH including pulmonary veno-occlusive disease (PVOD worsens on vasodilators: suspect it with septal lines, ground glass, lymphadenopathy or a very low DLCO); 2, left heart disease (diastolic dysfunction); 3, ILD with hypoxia; 4, CTEPH (V/Q scan). PAH affects 8\u201312% of patients. Risk factors: limited cutaneous disease, ACA, U3-RNP, Th/To, long duration, many telangiectasias, FVC/DLCO >1.6, raised NT-proBNP. DETECT applies to disease >3 years with DLCO <60%. Screening detects milder disease and improves survival. Echo, PFTs and NT-proBNP yearly; RHC if suspected; refer to a PH centre.","c":"General Rheumatology","t":["General Rheumatology"]},{"id":295,"s":"A patient with SSc has newly diagnosed PAH (WHO functional class III) on right heart catheterisation, with low to intermediate risk. Which approach is most appropriate?","o":["A single PDE5 inhibitor alone, reserving combination therapy for later escalation","High-dose glucocorticoids as the primary treatment","A TNF inhibitor","Observation with a repeat right heart catheterisation in 6 months before starting treatment","Initial combination therapy with a PDE5 inhibitor and an endothelin receptor antagonist"],"a":4,"e":"Initial combination therapy is standard. IV prostacyclin is added for high-risk patients. Vasoreactivity-guided CCB therapy does not apply to connective tissue disease-associated PAH. Initial dual therapy: an ERA (ambrisentan, macitentan) plus a PDE5 inhibitor (tadalafil, sildenafil), or riociguat in place of the PDE5 inhibitor. High risk: add parenteral prostacyclin (epoprostenol, treprostinil) or selexipag. Sotatercept (activin signalling inhibitor) is licensed as add-on therapy. Supportive care: diuretics, oxygen, rehabilitation, transplant referral. Anticoagulation is not routine (GAVE bleeding risk). Pregnancy is strongly advised against (ERAs are teratogenic). Three-year survival is 50\u201360% in older series and is improving.","c":"Pregnancy & Reproductive","t":["Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":296,"s":"A woman with SSc has diarrhoea, bloating and weight loss. A glucose hydrogen breath test is positive. What is the most appropriate treatment?","o":["A single continuous course of broad-spectrum antibiotics for life","Total parenteral nutrition as first-line, before any antibiotic trial","Pancreatic enzyme replacement alone, without antibiotics","Oral vancomycin as first-line for small intestinal bacterial overgrowth","Rotating or intermittent courses of antibiotics for small intestinal bacterial overgrowth"],"a":4,"e":"Small bowel dysmotility predisposes to bacterial overgrowth and malabsorption. Prokinetics help dysmotility, and nutritional screening with dietetic input is important. Rotate antibiotics (rifaximin, doxycycline, metronidazole, co-amoxiclav). Prokinetics (prucalopride, low-dose erythromycin, domperidone). Octreotide for pseudoobstruction. Correct B12 (often low from SIBO), vitamin D, iron and fat-soluble vitamins. Screen nutrition with MUST, BMI and albumin; parenteral nutrition for intestinal failure. Malnutrition predicts mortality. Pneumatosis intestinalis is often benign but needs surgical assessment if there is peritonism.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease"]},{"id":297,"s":"A patient with SSc has severe reflux and dysphagia. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["PPI therapy with reflux measures, assessment of dysmotility and aspiration risk, and prokinetics or dilatation where indicated","Immediate oesophageal dilatation without a trial of medical therapy","Observation only, since dysphagia in SSc rarely needs active management","High-dose immunosuppression, since dysmotility reflects active inflammation","A PDE5 inhibitor, since it also improves oesophageal motility"],"a":0,"e":"Oesophageal dysmotility with reflux is near-universal in SSc. Aspiration can contribute to lung disease. High-dose PPI (often twice daily) with H2 blocker add-on at night; raise the head of the bed; small meals; prokinetics. Endoscopy for dysphagia, alarm features or Barrett's surveillance; dilatation for strictures. Treat reflux aggressively in ILD (aspiration) and before lung transplant. GI involvement occurs in about 90% (mouth to anus).","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":298,"s":"A 63-year-old woman with limited cutaneous SSc for 18 years has anaemia and melaena. Endoscopy shows linear red stripes in the gastric antrum. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Gastric antral vascular ectasia (GAVE)","Multiple myeloma","Oesophageal variceal bleeding from portal hypertension","Peptic ulcer disease from chronic NSAID use","Right-heart catheterisation"],"a":0,"e":"GAVE (\"watermelon stomach\") causes chronic GI bleeding in SSc and is treated endoscopically (argon plasma coagulation) with iron replacement. Iron-deficiency anaemia in SSc: GAVE, oesophagitis, telangiectasia bleeding anywhere in the GI tract, malabsorption (SIBO, coeliac overlap), NSAIDs, anticoagulants. Other causes of anaemia: renal crisis (MAHA), anaemia of chronic disease, B12 deficiency. Iron (often IV), endoscopic argon plasma coagulation (repeated) or band ligation. Transfusion if severe. GAVE is associated with anti-RNA pol III and early diffuse disease.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":299,"s":"What is the leading cause of death in systemic sclerosis?","o":["Scleroderma renal crisis, now that it is uncommon with ACE inhibitor prophylaxis","Pulmonary complications: interstitial lung disease and pulmonary arterial hypertension","Right-heart catheterisation","Gastrointestinal bleeding from GAVE","Cardiac arrhythmia from myocardial fibrosis alone"],"a":1,"e":"Since ACE inhibitors transformed renal crisis outcomes, ILD and PAH are the main causes of SSc-related death. SSc-related deaths are about 55% of the total. Causes: ILD (commonest), PAH, cardiac disease; renal crisis is now uncommon. Non-SSc deaths: cancer, infection, atherosclerotic CVD. Poor prognostic factors: older onset, male sex, diffuse disease with high or rapidly rising mRSS, friction rubs, FVC <70%, PAH, cardiac involvement, renal crisis, raised CRP, anaemia.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":300,"s":"Which measures help hand function in systemic sclerosis?","o":["Intra-articular steroid injection into every affected joint monthly","Hand physiotherapy and occupational therapy, stretching, skin care and joint protection","Systemic immunosuppression alone, without any rehabilitation input","Surgical release of contractures as the first-line measure","Botulinum toxin injection as the first-line measure for hand stiffness"],"a":1,"e":"Rehabilitation helps maintain range of movement and function. Hand OT and physiotherapy, splints, paraffin wax baths, stretching, skin care (emollients), and protecting the digits from cold and trauma. Mouth-stretching exercises and dental hygiene for microstomia. Treat calcinosis and contractures surgically where needed. Psychological support (body image, depression).","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease"]},{"id":301,"s":"What are the cardinal features of Sj\u00f6gren's disease?","o":["Dry eyes and dry mouth due to lymphocytic infiltration of exocrine glands","Symmetrical peripheral synovitis with morning stiffness","Progressive proximal muscle weakness with a raised CK","Raynaud's phenomenon with digital ulceration","Recurrent oral and genital ulceration"],"a":0,"e":"Sj\u00f6gren's can also affect joints, skin, lungs, kidneys, nerves and blood, and carries an increased lymphoma risk. Sicca causes: - Drugs (the commonest cause): anticholinergics, TCAs, SSRIs, antihistamines, diuretics, bblockers, opioids, antipsychotics, ICIs. - Age and lifestyle: ageing, menopause, mouth breathing, smoking, anxiety, dehydration. - Infection: HCV, HIV (DILS), HTLV-1, EBV. - Infiltrative: IgG4-RD, sarcoid, amyloid, lymphoma, haemochromatosis. - Other: head and neck radiation, GVHD, diabetes, fibromyalgia, blepharitis or meibomian gland dysfunction, contact lenses, prior LASIK. Oral: red, fissured, depapillated tongue; \"cracker sign\"; cervical (gum-line) caries; candidiasis and angular cheilitis. Eyes: grittiness, mucus strands, punctate erosions, filamentary keratitis. Also nasal crusting, dry cough (xerotrachea), xerosis, vaginal dryness. Fatigue (70\u201380%) is the most disabling symptom.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain"]},{"id":302,"s":"A 52-year-old woman has dry eyes and mouth. Anti-Ro/SSA is positive and Schirmer's test is 4 mm in 5 minutes in both eyes. Lip biopsy has not been performed. How does she score on the 2016 Sj\u00f6gren's classification criteria?","o":["3; she does not meet the classification criteria","5; she meets the classification criteria on serology alone","6; she meets the classification criteria even without any positive test","4; she is classified as having Sj\u00f6gren's","3 points; he meets the criteria"],"a":3,"e":"Weighted items: labial gland focus score 1 or more (3), anti-Ro/SSA (3), ocular staining score 5 or more (1), Schirmer 5 mm/5 min or less (1), unstimulated salivary flow 0.1 mL/min or less (1). A score of 4 or more classifies, provided inclusion criteria are met and exclusions are absent. Anti-La alone does not score. Entry requires ocular or oral dryness symptoms, or suspected SjD from an ESSDAI domain. Exclusions: head and neck radiation, active HCV (PCR-positive), AIDS, sarcoidosis, amyloidosis, GVHD, IgG4-RD. Either a positive biopsy or anti-Ro is needed, because the minor items total only 3. Stop anticholinergic drugs before testing. Investigations: Schirmer I, ocular staining score, tear breakup time, unstimulated sialometry, salivary ultrasound, labial biopsy if seronegative. Also anti-Ro and anti-La, RF, immunoglobulins, C3/C4, cryoglobulins, SPEP and FLC, FBC, urinalysis, U&E and bicarbonate.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":303,"s":"What does the ESSDAI measure in Sj\u00f6gren's disease?","o":["Systemic disease activity across 12 organ domains","Patient-reported dryness, fatigue and pain alone","Glandular involvement only, without other organ domains","Response to rituximab specifically, as a treatment-response index","Diagnostic classification, not disease activity"],"a":0,"e":"The ESSDAI guides systemic treatment. The patient-reported ESSPRI captures dryness, fatigue and pain. ESSDAI domains: constitutional, lymphadenopathy, glandular, articular, cutaneous, pulmonary, renal, muscular, PNS, CNS, haematological and biological. It is the primary outcome in trials.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":304,"s":"Which medication class commonly worsens dryness in Sj\u00f6gren's disease?","o":["ACE inhibitors and ARBs","Topical beta-blockers for glaucoma","Anticholinergic drugs, sedating antihistamines and tricyclic antidepressants","TNF inhibitors","SGLT2 inhibitors for diabetes"],"a":2,"e":"Medication review is an important part of managing sicca symptoms. Review and switch drying drugs: anticholinergic bladder drugs (try mirabegron), sedating antihistamines (use nonsedating), TCAs (alternative analgesics), diuretics, opioids, antipsychotics. Also avoid caffeine, alcohol and smoking.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":305,"s":"What is the stepwise management of dry eyes in Sj\u00f6gren's disease, including severe dry eye with corneal damage despite frequent tears?","o":["Oral pilocarpine alone, without any topical treatment","Preservative-free artificial tears and gels with lid hygiene first; for refractory disease, topical ciclosporin","Systemic immunosuppression as first-line, before any topical treatment","Punctal plugs as the first step, before any lubricant trial","Rituximab as first-line for routine dry eye without corneal damage"],"a":1,"e":"(or lifitegrast) with ophthalmology input, plus autologous serum drops, punctal plugs or short topical steroid courses Ocular lubricants are foundational; use preservative-free drops if more than 4 times daily, and gels or ointments at night. Refractory disease is managed under ophthalmology care with topical immunomodulatory drops, autologous serum drops or punctal plugs. Short courses of topical steroid may be used with care. Avoid dry environments and screen glare, and use wraparound glasses. Scleral lenses for severe disease. Oral pilocarpine also helps the eyes. Watch for corneal ulceration or melt.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":306,"s":"How is xerostomia managed according to residual gland function, and which dental complication is particularly important?","o":["Pilocarpine for all patients, regardless of residual gland function","Tailor to gland function: non-pharmacological stimulation for mild dysfunction; pilocarpine or cevimeline when residual function remains; saliva substitutes when there is none. Rapidly progressive dental caries is the key complication","Dental extraction as the first-line response to caries risk","Saliva substitutes first-line, even with substantial residual gland function","No specific dental surveillance is needed beyond routine population screening"],"a":1,"e":"Muscarinic agonists stimulate residual function; sweating and urinary frequency are common adverse effects. Immunosuppressants do not improve dryness. Regular dental review, fluoride and treatment of oral candidiasis are essential. General measures: frequent sips of water, sugar-free gum or xylitol lozenges, a humidifier. Dental: high-fluoride toothpaste (5000 ppm), fluoride varnish, review every 3\u20136 months, treat candida. Substitutes: gels, sprays, oxygenated glycerol triester. Pilocarpine 5 mg 3\u20134 times daily (contraindicated in asthma, narrow-angle glaucoma and uncontrolled cardiac disease).","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":307,"s":"What is the role of systemic treatment in Sj\u00f6gren's disease with fatigue and arthralgia but no organ involvement, and with mild inflammatory arthritis?","o":["Immunosuppression started empirically for fatigue alone, without assessing reversible causes","High-dose glucocorticoids as first-line for mild inflammatory arthritis","Fatigue alone does not warrant immunosuppression (assess reversible causes and use symptomdirected care); for arthritis, hydroxychloroquine with short-term glucocorticoids if needed, and methotrexate for persistent arthritis","Biologic therapy as first-line for arthralgia without synovitis","Methotrexate started immediately for fatigue, before any other assessment"],"a":2,"e":"Fatigue, pain and dryness respond poorly to immunosuppression; exercise, sleep and treatment of comorbidity are central. The BSR guideline allows a 6\u201312 month trial of hydroxychloroquine for significant fatigue and systemic symptoms, but not routine immunosuppressants. Hydroxychloroquine is used for musculoskeletal and cutaneous features. Fatigue contributors: hypothyroidism (15\u201330% have autoimmune thyroiditis), anaemia, coeliac disease, depression, poor sleep (nocturia, dry mouth), fibromyalgia, PBC, RTA with hypokalaemia. Arthritis in SjD is symmetrical, non-erosive and intermittent (Jaccoud-type is possible). Erosions or anti-CCP suggest RA overlap.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":308,"s":"A woman with Sj\u00f6gren's has severe refractory systemic disease, such as cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis with palpable purpura, neuropathy and glomerulonephritis (HCV-negative, type II cryoglobulins). What is the most appropriate treatment?","o":["B-cell targeted therapy with rituximab plus glucocorticoids, adding plasma exchange for lifethreatening disease","Watchful waiting, since cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis in Sj\u00f6gren's is usually self-limiting","Topical treatment alone, since systemic involvement is not present","Oral prednisolone alone, without rituximab or plasma exchange","Anticoagulation as the primary treatment, given the vasculitic presentation"],"a":0,"e":"Rituximab is used for severe refractory systemic disease, particularly cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis, which is a severe fatigue (TEARS and TRACTISS trials). By manifestation: | Manifestation | Treatment | |---|---| | Cutaneous vasculitis or purpura | GC; rituximab if cryoglobulinaemic | | ILD | GC + MMF or azathioprine; rituximab; nintedanib if progressive | | Cryoglobulinaemic GN, vasculitic neuropathy | pulse GC + rituximab or cyclophosphamide; PLEX | | Sensory ganglionopathy | often refractory: IVIG, rituximab | | Small-fibre neuropathy | neuropathic agents; IVIG in selected cases | | Cytopenias | GC, rituximab, IVIG | | CNS or myelitis | pulse GC + cyclophosphamide or rituximab | Belimumab is used off-label in some cases.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":309,"s":"A 58-year-old woman with Sj\u00f6gren's has persistent unilateral parotid enlargement for 6 months, palpable purpura, low C4 and mixed cryoglobulins. What is the most important concern?","o":["Risk-stratified malignancy screening","The A1 pulley at the metacarpal head","B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma","Sarcoidosis with Heerfordt syndrome","Development of MALT lymphoma"],"a":4,"e":"Lymphoma risk (about 5-10% lifetime, mostly MALT) is higher with persistent gland enlargement, purpura, low C4, cryoglobulinaemia, monoclonal gammopathy and lymphopenia. Ultrasound and biopsy of the gland are warranted. Parotid enlargement: - Bilateral: SjD, IgG4-RD (Mikulicz), sarcoid (Heerfordt's: parotitis, uveitis, facial palsy, fever), HIV (DILS, lymphoepithelial cysts), HCV, diabetes, alcohol, bulimia, malnutrition, amyloid, mumps, drugs (iodine). - Unilateral or firm: MALT lymphoma, pleomorphic adenoma, Warthin's tumour, parotid carcinoma, bacterial parotitis or stones. Lymphoma predictors: persistent parotid enlargement, palpable purpura, low C4 (and C3), cryoglobulins, monoclonal component, raised b2-microglobulin and FLC, CD4 lymphopenia, neutropenia, lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, high ESSDAI, young onset, male sex, focus score \u20213, germinal centres. High-risk patients: review every 3\u20136 months with parotid ultrasound, and biopsy any persistent mass (core or open). MALT lymphoma: rituximab-based regimens (\u00b1 chemotherapy) with haematology. Gastric MALT may need H. pylori eradication.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":310,"s":"A 39-year-old woman with Sj\u00f6gren's has weakness, serum potassium 2.6 mmol/L, a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis and urine pH 6.5. What is the most likely cause?","o":["Hypokalaemic periodic paralysis from primary aldosteronism","Small-fibre neuropathy","Levamisole-adulterated cocaine","Distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis due to tubulointerstitial nephritis","Diuretic-induced hypokalaemia with metabolic alkalosis"],"a":3,"e":"Tubulointerstitial nephritis typically causes distal RTA, with inability to acidify urine, hypokalaemia and nephrocalcinosis. Glomerulonephritis is less common and often related to cryoglobulinaemia. Normal anion gap acidosis with hypokalaemia: distal (type 1) RTA (urine pH >5.5; SjD, SLE, PBC, amphotericin, lithium, toluene); proximal (type 2) RTA (bicarbonate wasting, Fanconi syndrome: myeloma, tenofovir, ifosfamide, Wilson's); diarrhoea (urine anion gap negative); ureteric diversion; acetazolamide. TIN with distal RTA can cause hypokalaemic periodic paralysis, nephrocalcinosis, stones and osteomalacia. Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and Fanconi syndrome also occur. TIN occurs early; GN (MPGN from cryoglobulins) occurs later and signals lymphoma risk. Potassium citrate or bicarbonate. GC (\u00b1 MMF, rituximab) for active TIN with declining eGFR. Renal biopsy if eGFR is falling or there is proteinuria or haematuria.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Metabolic Bone Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":311,"s":"A woman with Sj\u00f6gren's has burning feet with normal nerve conduction studies. Which diagnosis is most likely?","o":["Adhesive capsulitis","Diabetic peripheral neuropathy","Inclusion-body myositis","Small-fibre neuropathy","Tarsal tunnel syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"Small-fibre neuropathy is a recognised extraglandular feature and is confirmed by skin biopsy for intraepidermal nerve fibre density. Sensory ganglionopathy also occurs. Burning feet: small-fibre neuropathy (SjD, sarcoid, diabetes or prediabetes, alcohol, HIV, amyloid, Fabry disease, B12 deficiency, coeliac disease, chemotherapy) vs large-fibre length-dependent neuropathy (abnormal NCS), tarsal tunnel syndrome, erythromelalgia (myeloproliferative), sensory ganglionopathy (non-length-dependent, ataxic). Sensory ganglionopathy is characteristic of SjD: asymmetric sensory ataxia with pseudoathetosis and areflexia, from dorsal root ganglion injury. Exclude paraneoplastic anti-Hu ganglionopathy (small cell lung cancer) and cisplatin. Other neurology: trigeminal neuropathy, mononeuritis multiplex (cryoglobulins), autonomic neuropathy, myelitis or NMOSD (AQP4-IgG), white matter lesions. Small-fibre neuropathy: confirm with skin biopsy (reduced intraepidermal nerve fibre density) or QSART; treat with neuropathic analgesia, with IVIG in selected immune cases. Ganglionopathy responds poorly (IVIG, rituximab).","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":312,"s":"What are the typical clinical features of inflammatory myopathy, and which rash is characteristic of dermatomyositis?","o":["Symmetrical small-joint synovitis with morning stiffness over an hour","Asymmetric distal weakness with sparing of facial muscles","A malar rash sparing the nasolabial folds, with photosensitivity","Subacute, symmetrical proximal weakness with raised muscle enzymes; Gottron's papules and a heliotrope rash are characteristic of dermatomyositis","Painless proximal weakness without any skin involvement, typical of all subtypes"],"a":3,"e":"Extramuscular involvement includes ILD, dysphagia, myocarditis and arthritis. Skin disease may precede or dominate muscle involvement (clinically amyopathic dermatomyositis). DM rash mimics: SLE (spares the knuckles), psoriasis (scalp), seborrhoeic dermatitis, rosacea, contact dermatitis, drug eruption (hydroxyurea DM-like eruption), photosensitivity reactions, flagellate erythema from bleomycin or shiitake mushrooms. Pathognomonic DM signs: Gottron's papules and sign, heliotrope rash. Characteristic: V-sign, shawl and holster signs, facial erythema, flagellate erythema, itchy scalp, inverse Gottron's (palmar), periungual erythema with ragged cuticles. Late: poikiloderma, calcinosis. Skin can precede weakness by 3\u20136 months. Prominent myalgia suggests another diagnosis. Use CDASI to measure skin activity. Photoprotection matters (UV triggers skin and muscle flares).","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease"]},{"id":313,"s":"A patient with suspected inflammatory myositis has a normal CK. Does this exclude myositis?","o":["A normal CK does not exclude myositis; review weakness, other enzymes, MRI, EMG, antibodies and biopsy","Yes; a normal CK makes myositis very unlikely regardless of other features","Yes, provided EMG is also normal","No, but only if the patient also has a rash","Yes, in patients over 60 years of age"],"a":0,"e":"normal CK does not exclude myositis; review weakness, other enzymes, MRI, EMG, antibodies and biopsy Clinically amyopathic dermatomyositis (especially anti-MDA5) and longstanding disease may have normal CK. CK is normal in about 20% of DM. Aldolase can be raised with a normal CK in perimysial or fascial disease. AST and ALT are usually muscle-derived, so check GGT before blaming methotrexate. EMG shows a myopathic pattern with fibrillations; a normal EMG does not exclude IIM. CK is reliable for activity in IMNM, partly in ASyS, and not in DM or IBM.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":314,"s":"A patient with suspected inflammatory myositis needs a muscle biopsy. Which investigation best guides biopsy site selection?","o":["MRI of the sacroiliac joints using inflammatory sequences","MRI of the thighs with STIR (fat-suppressed) sequences","Cross-sectional vascular imaging such as PET-CT, CTA or MRA","Plain radiographs of the affected limb","CT of the chest, abdomen and pelvis"],"a":1,"e":"STIR MRI shows active muscle oedema and avoids sampling end-stage or unaffected muscle. If EMG is used, the contralateral muscle should be biopsied to avoid needle artefact. Target an affected but not end-stage muscle (deltoid, biceps, vastus lateralis), avoiding recently needled muscles. A biopsy is not always needed: a typical clinical picture plus an MSA (DM rash with anti-TIF1g; anti-HMGCR with typical IMNM) may suffice. Biopsy when antibodies are negative or discordant, when IBM, dystrophy or metabolic myopathy is possible, or when the response is poor. Sarcoplasmic MxA is highly specific for DM (ENMC criteria).","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":315,"s":"At diagnosis of inflammatory myositis, what should be assessed?","o":["Genetic testing for muscular dystrophy in every patient","Muscle strength, CK, lung function and HRCT, swallowing, cardiac involvement and malignancy risk","HLA-B27 status alone, without any organ-specific assessment","A single CK measurement alone, without functional or organ assessment","Joint ultrasound alone, without any assessment of lung or cardiac involvement"],"a":1,"e":"Extramuscular disease drives prognosis, and antibody profile guides risk stratification. At diagnosis: MMT-8, CK, PFTs with DLCO plus HRCT (for all), swallowing assessment (SLT), troponin I, ECG and echo (CMR if suspected), an extended myositis antibody panel, cancer screening stratified by risk, bone health, CV risk and vaccination. Severe features: MRC <3 proximally, dysphagia or aspiration, falling FVC or nocturnal hypoventilation, rapid progression, rhabdomyolysis, cardiac involvement.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Connective Tissue Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":316,"s":"A 45-year-old woman has Gottron's papules, periungual erythema, painful palmar papules, digital ulcers and rapidly worsening breathlessness. CK is normal and HRCT shows rapidly progressive ground-glass change and consolidation. Which antibody is most likely?","o":["Anti-histone","Anti-Jo-1","Anti-SRP","Anti-TIF1-gamma","Anti-MDA5"],"a":4,"e":"Anti-MDA5 dermatomyositis is often clinically amyopathic with cutaneous ulceration and carries a high risk of rapidly progressive ILD with high mortality. Early intensive combination immunosuppression is used. RP-ILD in a CTD: anti-MDA5, ASyS with acute exacerbation, SLE pneumonitis or DAH, PJP, CMV, COVID-19, drug pneumonitis, acute exacerbation of fibrotic ILD. Anti-MDA5 skin clues: palmar papules, painful digital ulcers or skin necrosis, alopecia, oral ulcers, arthritis, little weakness. Ferritin and KL-6 are raised. Pathogenesis involves intense type I IFN, macrophage activation and lung vasculopathy. Early mortality from RP-ILD is 30\u201360%. An emergency: pulse GC plus two more agents immediately (the Japanese triple regimen of high-dose GC, tacrolimus and IV cyclophosphamide; or rituximab, MMF, IVIG or tofacitinib). Add PLEX if refractory. Monitor for CMV and PJP (give prophylaxis). Track ferritin and KL-6. Refer early for lung transplant.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":317,"s":"A 49-year-old woman has proximal weakness, non-erosive arthritis, cracked hyperkeratotic skin on the radial borders of her fingers, Raynaud's phenomenon and basal crackles. Which antibody is most likely?","o":["Anti-MDA5","Anti-SRP","Anti-topoisomerase I (Scl-70)","Anti-Jo-1","High-resolution CT chest"],"a":3,"e":"Antisynthetase syndrome (anti-Jo-1, PL-7, PL-12 and others) features myositis, ILD, mechanic's hands, Raynaud's, arthritis and fever. ILD determines prognosis and may dominate. Antisynthetase antibodies: Jo-1 (commonest; myositis plus ILD), PL-7 and PL-12 (lung-dominant, little myositis), EJ, OJ, KS, Zo, Ha. Arthritis occurs in up to 90%, is symmetric and non-erosive, and can sublux (\"floppy thumb\" in about 19% of Jo-1). ACPA, RF and Ro52 can be positive. ILD is usually NSIP or OP. The model is lung-first (smoking or inhaled triggers in HLA-DRB1*03:01 carriers). Test the extended myositis panel in any \"seronegative RA\" with ILD, mechanic's hands or raised CK. Treatment is driven by the lung: GC plus MMF, azathioprine or tacrolimus; methotrexate for arthritis without significant ILD; rituximab or IV cyclophosphamide for severe or progressive ILD (RECITAL showed similar efficacy, with fewer adverse events for rituximab).","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":318,"s":"A 67-year-old man presents with a heliotrope rash, shawl sign and proximal weakness. Anti-TIF1-gamma is positive. What is the most important next step?","o":["Topical steroid treatment for the skin alone, without systemic assessment","Enhanced malignancy screening, including CT of the chest, abdomen and pelvis, with age-and sexappropriate tests","Genetic counselling only, since this is a familial condition","Reassurance, since TIF1-gamma is not associated with malignancy in adults","Immediate high-dose immunosuppression alone, without any cancer screening"],"a":1,"e":"Anti-TIF1-gamma and anti-NXP2 in adult dermatomyositis confer high cancer risk. Screening is repeated over the first 3 years, when risk is greatest. Cancer risk by group: | Group | Cancer risk | |---|---| | Anti- TIF1g (adult DM) | very high (20\u201350%) | | Anti-NXP2 (adult) | moderate to high | | Seronegative DM or IMNM | high | | SAE, Mi-2 | intermediate | | HMGCR | uncertain or modest | | SRP, ASyS, overlap, MDA5 (Western) | low | | IBM, juvenile | not raised | About a quarter of adults with IIM develop cancer within 3 years either side of onset (up to 30% in DM). High-risk features: age >40, male sex, dysphagia, skin necrosis or ulceration, rapid onset, refractoriness, TIF1g, NXP2, seronegative disease. Lower risk: ILD, Raynaud's, arthritis, antisynthetase antibodies. Tumours can express mutated TIF1 genes, making the myositis paraneoplastic. IMACS screening: a basic panel for standard risk; basic plus enhanced (CT neck, chest, abdomen and pelvis; tumour markers; mammography; pelvic and TV ultrasound; FOB) for moderate risk; enhanced panel annually for 3 years for high risk, with FDG-PET if negative. Nasoendoscopy for South-East Asian patients (nasopharyngeal carcinoma). Re-screen at flare or refractoriness. Treat the cancer first. IVIG spares GC without oncological concern.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Paediatric Rheumatology","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":319,"s":"In juvenile dermatomyositis, which antibody is most strongly associated with calcinosis?","o":["Anti-MDA5","Anti-Jo-1","Anti-Sm","Anti-NXP2","Anti-histone"],"a":3,"e":"Anti-NXP2 is associated with calcinosis and more severe muscle disease. Early, effective disease control reduces calcinosis risk. JDM accounts for up to 4 per million children per year. Antibodies include TIF1g (the commonest in JDM, but not cancer-associated in children), NXP2 (calcinosis, severe weakness, GI vasculopathy), MDA5 (ILD, arthritis, ulcers) and Mi-2. Calcinosis occurs in 20\u201340% overall, more with delayed treatment. Aggressive early control: pulse methylprednisolone with methotrexate (first line), then IVIG, MMF, rituximab or JAK inhibitors. Calcinosis has no validated treatment (options: intensify immunosuppression, sodium thiosulfate, bisphosphonates, diltiazem, surgery). Photoprotection.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":320,"s":"A 70-year-old man on atorvastatin for 3 years has progressive proximal weakness. CK is 8,500 U/L and weakness persists 3 months after stopping the statin. Biopsy shows necrotic fibres with minimal inflammation. What is the most likely diagnosis and management?","o":["Inclusion-body myositis","Anti-HMGCR immune-mediated necrotising myopathy; immunosuppression such as glucocorticoids with IVIG and/or methotrexate","Hypothyroid myopathy; thyroid replacement alone","Toxic statin myopathy; reassurance since it will resolve within weeks of stopping","Relapsing polychondritis"],"a":1,"e":"Anti-HMGCR myopathy persists after statin withdrawal and needs immunosuppression; IVIG is particularly effective. Statins should be avoided thereafter. Statin-related muscle disease: toxic self-limited myopathy (improves within weeks of stopping; HMGCR negative) vs anti-HMGCR IMNM (persists or progresses after stopping; HMGCR positive; HLA-DRB1*11:01). Also hypothyroidism (which raises statin myopathy risk), interacting drugs (fibrates, macrolides, ciclosporin), and an unmasked metabolic myopathy. Stop statins and never restart. Use PCSK9 inhibitors or ezetimibe for lipids. GC plus IVIG at induction, plus methotrexate or another DMARD. IVIG can be used early or even first-line. CK tracks activity and guides tapering. Younger anti-HMGCR patients recover more slowly and may mimic a dystrophy. Zilucoplan failed in phase 2; FcRn blockers are under study.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":321,"s":"A 38-year-old woman has rapidly progressive severe proximal weakness, dysphagia, CK 15,000 U/L and an arrhythmia. Biopsy shows necrotising myopathy with minimal inflammation. Which antibody is most likely?","o":["Anti-Jo-1","68Ga-DOTATATE PET/CT","Thyroid function tests","Anti-SRP","Anti-MDA5"],"a":3,"e":"Anti-SRP is associated with severe necrotising myopathy, dysphagia and cardiac involvement, often needing early combination therapy. Anti-SRP (about 5% of IIM): acute aggressive proximal weakness, very high CK, early atrophy, dysphagia, cardiac involvement and sometimes ILD; no rash or arthritis. It is often steroid-refractory. IMNM antibodies are directly pathogenic (complement-mediated), and titres track CK and strength. Aggressive early therapy: GC plus IVIG plus early rituximab, then MMF or tacrolimus; PLEX in case reports. Cardiac monitoring.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":322,"s":"A 72-year-old man has 3 years of slowly progressive finger flexor and quadriceps weakness, falls, dysphagia, a mildly raised CK and positive anti-cN1A antibodies. What is the likely diagnosis and how does it respond to treatment?","o":["Antisynthetase syndrome, which responds well to early immunosuppression","Anti-HMGCR necrotising myopathy, which improves rapidly with IVIG","This is likely inclusion body myositis, which responds poorly to immunosuppression; management is supportive","Polymyositis, which typically responds well to glucocorticoids alone","A statin-induced toxic myopathy that will resolve within weeks"],"a":2,"e":"IBM typically affects people over 50 with asymmetric deep finger flexor and quadriceps weakness. Biopsy shows rimmed vacuoles. Exercise, falls prevention and swallowing management are key. Distal or asymmetric weakness: ALS (fasciculations, UMN signs), myotonic dystrophy, distal myopathies, FSHD (scapular winging, facial weakness), multifocal motor neuropathy, CIDP, sporadic late-onset nemaline myopathy (paraprotein). IBM: over 45, male; insidious asymmetric weakness of the deep finger flexors and quadriceps; falls in 40\u201370%; dysphagia (cricopharyngeal) in 40\u201360%; CK <15\u00d7 ULN. Diagnosis is often delayed by 3\u20135 years. Anti-cN1A is present in 30\u201350% but also occurs in Sj\u00f6gren's and SLE. It is associated with T-LGL leukaemia and Sj\u00f6gren's. Strength falls by 3.5\u201317% per year, but life expectancy is near normal. There is no approved disease-modifying drug. Use exercise, falls prevention, orthoses (knee brace, AFO), OT, SLT, and IVIG, cricopharyngeal dilatation or myotomy for dysphagia. Avoid long-term GC (they lower CK but do not help strength). Failed trials: bimagrumab, arimoclomol, ulviprubart (anti-KLRG1). A sirolimus trial is ongoing.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":323,"s":"What is the first-line treatment for active dermatomyositis or polymyositis, and how does it change with severe weakness and dysphagia with aspiration risk?","o":["NSAIDs and physiotherapy alone, reserving glucocorticoids for severe cases","Methotrexate alone, without any glucocorticoid, even in severe disease","Topical steroids alone, since the disease is primarily cutaneous","IV immunoglobulin alone, without any glucocorticoid, as first-line","High-dose glucocorticoids with an early steroid-sparing agent (methotrexate, azathioprine or mycophenolate); for severe disease, IV methylprednisolone pulses then high-dose oral glucocorticoids with IVIG"],"a":4,"e":"and an early steroid-sparing agent IVIG, rituximab or calcineurin inhibitors are used for refractory or severe disease, and cyclophosphamide for severe ILD. IVIG has proven efficacy in dermatomyositis and is useful for dysphagia. Speech and language therapy swallowing assessment is essential, and respiratory muscle weakness needs monitoring. Prednisolone 0.5\u20131 mg/kg/day (40\u201360 mg), tapering after about 6 weeks according to response, to 10\u201320 mg by 6 months and ideally \u00a35 mg. Continue a DMARD for \u20212 years after remission. Methotrexate is not contraindicated in ILD, but MMF or tacrolimus is preferred when ILD dominates. IVIG (2 g/kg every 4 weeks) carries a thromboembolism risk, so hydrate and infuse slowly. Supportive: PJP prophylaxis with high-dose GC plus a second agent (especially in ILD or anti-MDA5); specialist exercise programme (safe even in active disease); bone health; vaccination. Skin: photoprotection, topical steroids or tacrolimus, HCQ (drug rash is more common in DM), methotrexate, MMF, IVIG, JAK inhibitors. Refractory disease: rituximab (RIM trial missed its primary endpoint, but most patients improved), abatacept, cyclophosphamide, JAKi or brepocitinib, CAR-T trials. Failure to improve at 4\u20136 months: consider wrong diagnosis (dystrophy, Pompe), IBM, steroid myopathy (normal CK, no MRI oedema), damage (fat on MRI), hidden cancer, non-adherence.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":324,"s":"A patient with active polymyositis has a raised troponin T without chest pain or ECG change. Which test best distinguishes cardiac from skeletal muscle origin?","o":["Serum myoglobin","Troponin I","Serum IGF-1","CK-MB alone, without troponin","Repeat troponin T in 24 hours without further testing"],"a":1,"e":"Troponin T and CK-MB can be released from regenerating skeletal muscle, whereas troponin I is cardiac-specific. A raised troponin I warrants assessment for myocarditis. A raised troponin I warrants ECG, echo and CMR (T2 oedema, LGE), with Holter monitoring. Treat myocarditis with intensified immunosuppression (pulse GC, IVIG) and cardiology input. Screen all adults with troponin I, ECG and echo; IIM also raises atherosclerotic risk.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease"]},{"id":325,"s":"What is the significance of myositis-associated antibodies (such as anti-PM/Scl, anti-Ku and anti-U1-RNP) compared with myositis-specific antibodies?","o":["They are more specific than myositis-specific antibodies for predicting malignancy","They indicate a worse prognosis than myositis-specific antibodies in all cases","They indicate overlap connective tissue disease features rather than a specific myositis subtype","They are mutually exclusive with myositis-specific antibodies and never coexist","They define distinct myositis subtypes, just as myositis-specific antibodies do"],"a":2,"e":"Myositis-specific antibodies (Jo-1, MDA5, SRP, HMGCR, TIF1-gamma, Mi-2, NXP2) define clinical phenotypes, whereas associated antibodies suggest overlap syndromes. MAA associations: anti-PM/Scl (SSc overlap, ILD, calcinosis, mechanic's hands; good muscle response); anti-Ku (SSc or SLE overlap, arthritis); anti-U1-RNP (MCTD); anti-Ro52 (worse ILD when combined with Jo-1 or MDA5). Overlap myositis is treated by the dominant organ: IVIG and GC for muscle, MMF or rituximab for ILD, methotrexate or biologics for erosive arthritis, CCBs and PDE5 inhibitors for Raynaud's.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":326,"s":"A 73-year-old woman presents with a new temporal headache, jaw claudication and scalp tenderness. CRP is 82 mg/L and she has no visual symptoms. GCA is strongly suspected. What is the most appropriate immediate action?","o":["Start prednisolone 15 mg daily and reassess in four weeks","Start oral prednisolone 40\u201360 mg daily immediately and arrange urgent fast-track specialist assessment","Wait for temporal artery ultrasound or biopsy confirmation before treating","Withhold treatment until ophthalmology has excluded every other headache cause","Give intravenous methylprednisolone to every suspected case irrespective of visual or ischaemic symptoms"],"a":1,"e":"Strongly suspected cranial GCA requires immediate glucocorticoid treatment without waiting for imaging or biopsy. In patients without threatened vision or other critical ischaemia, initial oral prednisolone 40\u201360 mg/day is commonly recommended, with urgent specialist review. Visual ischaemic symptoms require same-day emergency assessment and may warrant intravenous methylprednisolone. Sources: BSR GCA guideline and EULAR large-vessel vasculitis guidance.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":327,"s":"A patient with suspected GCA was started on glucocorticoids yesterday. What should happen to diagnostic testing, and when and how should temporal artery biopsy be done?","o":["Stop glucocorticoids until biopsy is performed, to preserve test sensitivity","Delay biopsy for at least 4 weeks to allow inflammation to settle","Proceed urgently with ultrasound and/or biopsy (never withhold treatment to preserve test sensitivity) biopsy ideally within 2 weeks of starting glucocorticoids, with a specimen of at least 1 cm","A negative biopsy at this stage reliably excludes GCA","Biopsy is no longer useful once glucocorticoids have started, so it should not be done"],"a":2,"e":"Sensitivity declines with time on steroids but remains useful in the first days. Confirming the diagnosis matters because treatment is prolonged and toxic. Skip lesions cause false negatives, so an adequate length is needed, and a negative biopsy does not exclude GCA, particularly large-vessel disease. Biopsy sensitivity is 60\u201380% (it shrinks to about 0.7 cm after fixation; still useful up to 4 weeks). Contralateral biopsy adds little. Steroids reduce ultrasound yield after 1\u20132 weeks and PET yield after a few days. High probability plus positive imaging needs no further test. Low probability plus negative imaging makes GCA unlikely. Use biopsy when imaging is unavailable, equivocal, or negative despite high suspicion.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":328,"s":"In a fast-track GCA clinic, what is the first-line imaging test for suspected cranial GCA?","o":["Ultrasound of the temporal and axillary arteries","MRI of the brain with contrast","CT angiography of the aorta","Temporal artery biopsy, before any imaging","FDG-PET/CT as the routine first test"],"a":0,"e":"A non-compressible halo sign supports GCA, with accuracy comparable to biopsy in experienced hands. FDG-PET, CT or MR angiography assess large-vessel involvement. Halo sign mimics: atherosclerosis (calcified plaques, older people), AAV involving the temporal artery, amyloid, and operator error (excess compression or gain). FDG-PET/CT is best for large-vessel and aortic disease and PUO; modern scanners also detect cranial arteries. Highresolution cranial MRI shows mural enhancement. CTA or MRA images the aorta and its branches.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":329,"s":"A 72-year-old woman has arm claudication, constitutional upset and a raised CRP, but no headache or jaw claudication and normal temporal arteries. Could this be GCA?","o":["No, since normal temporal arteries exclude GCA","No, because jaw claudication is required for the diagnosis","No, limb claudication always indicates peripheral arterial disease instead","No, since CRP elevation without cranial symptoms suggests an alternative diagnosis","GCA can present with large-vessel (extracranial) disease without cranial symptoms"],"a":4,"e":"Large-vessel GCA causes limb claudication, bruits, pulse or blood pressure asymmetry and aortitis. Imaging of the aorta and branches (ultrasound, PET-CT, CT or MR angiography) is used. Aortitis and large-vessel vasculitis: GCA, Takayasu (<40), IgG4-related periaortitis, Beh\u00e7et's, Cogan's syndrome, relapsing polychondritis, SpA (aortic root), sarcoid, Erdheim\u2013Chester (\"coated aorta\"), infection (syphilis, TB, Salmonella, Staphylococcus: mycotic aneurysm), isolated aortitis (found at surgery), radiation. Atherosclerosis can mimic it on imaging. Baseline aortic imaging (CT, MRA or PET) in all patients with GCA, and surveillance every 1\u20132 years with largevessel involvement or risk factors. Control BP and stop smoking.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":330,"s":"How should glucocorticoids be tapered once GCA is controlled, and what is added for a patient who relapses twice below 15 mg daily?","o":["Taper to 7.5 mg/day within 1 month, stopping by 6 months regardless of relapse","Add etanercept, a TNF inhibitor, for relapsing disease","Increase prednisolone indefinitely rather than adding any steroid-sparing agent","Stop glucocorticoids abruptly once CRP normalises","Taper to 15\u201320 mg/day within 2\u20133 months, aiming to stop within 12\u201318 months; for relapsing disease (or high toxicity risk) add tocilizumab with a glucocorticoid taper, with upadacitinib or methotrexate as alternatives"],"a":4,"e":"Initial prednisolone 40\u201360 mg/day is tapered to 15\u201320 mg/day within 2\u20133 months. The 2025 EULAR recommendations aim to stop glucocorticoids within 12\u201318 months (the 2018 version targeted 5 mg/day or less after 1 year). Tocilizumab increases sustained remission and reduces cumulative glucocorticoid dose. The 2025 EULAR recommendations reserve adjunctive therapy (tocilizumab, or the JAK inhibitor upadacitinib) for relapsing or refractory disease or high glucocorticoid-toxicity risk, with methotrexate as an alternative. TNF inhibitors are ineffective in GCA. Relapses occur in 40\u201360%, usually below 10\u201315 mg or within 2 years, most often as PMR or headache. A rising CRP alone is not a relapse. Tocilizumab and upadacitinib suppress CRP and ESR, so judge activity clinically and by imaging. With tocilizumab or upadacitinib, a 26-week taper to zero is standard. Tocilizumab is usually given for 1 year (sometimes 2), and relapse after stopping is common. Minor relapse: return to the last effective dose (or add 5\u201310 mg), then taper again; add or switch a steroid-sparing agent. Major relapse (ischaemic symptoms or progressive aortitis): 40\u201360 mg (pulses if visual), plus tocilizumab or upadacitinib. Remember JAKi cautions in older patients with CV or VTE risk. GC toxicity affects about 85% of those on long courses.","c":"Drug Monitoring & Safety","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":331,"s":"A 74-year-old woman with newly diagnosed GCA has no history of cardiovascular or cerebrovascular disease. Should low-dose aspirin be started?","o":["Yes, in all patients, given the inflammatory and thrombotic risk of GCA","Yes, but only for the first month of treatment","Not routinely; antiplatelet therapy is reserved for other established indications","No; aspirin is specifically contraindicated with glucocorticoids in GCA","They are not used routinely, because harms outweigh limited long-term benefit"],"a":2,"e":"Evidence of benefit is limited and bleeding risk is increased on glucocorticoids. Gastroprotection and bone protection should be considered. Use aspirin only for usual CV indications, adding a PPI if combined with GC. Some clinicians use short-term aspirin for critical cranial ischaemia. Other measures: bone protection from day 1, BP, glucose, eye and mood checks, PJP prophylaxis with high doses plus other immunosuppression, vaccination, a steroid card. Follow up at weeks 0, 1\u20132, 3 and 6, then months 3, 6, 9 and 12.","c":"Infection & Immunosuppression","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":332,"s":"A patient being treated for PMR develops jaw claudication. What is the most appropriate response?","o":["Continue the current prednisolone dose and review again in 4 weeks","Urgent assessment for GCA, with high-dose glucocorticoids if it is suspected","Add a steroid-sparing agent without reassessing for GCA","Arrange dental review before any other action, since jaw claudication suggests dental disease","Begin high-dose glucocorticoids urgently and arrange rapid assessment"],"a":1,"e":"Patients with PMR should be asked about headache, scalp tenderness, jaw or tongue claudication and visual symptoms at every review. At every PMR review, ask about headache, scalp tenderness, jaw or tongue claudication, visual symptoms and limb claudication. Poor response or relapse of PMR should prompt imaging for large-vessel GCA (ultrasound of axillary arteries, PET-CT).","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":333,"s":"What initial glucocorticoid dose is recommended for PMR (new bilateral shoulder and hip girdle pain, 90 minutes of stiffness, ESR 58, no GCA features), and how should it be tapered after a complete response?","o":["Prednisolone 60 mg daily, tapering by 10 mg weekly regardless of response","Prednisolone 5 mg daily, increasing only if there is no response","Methotrexate as first-line, with glucocorticoids reserved for non-responders","Prednisolone 15 mg daily (up to 25 mg if needed); reduce to 10 mg/day within about 2 months, then by about 1 mg every 4 weeks, aiming to stop within about a year if remission is maintained","Prednisolone 40 mg daily, stopping abruptly once symptoms resolve"],"a":3,"e":"The 2025 EULAR recommendations advise 15\u201325 mg/day initially, and the 2026 BSR guideline describes a typical starting dose of 15 mg, increased to a maximum of 25 mg if response is inadequate. Very low (\u00a37.5 mg) or very high (>30 mg) starting doses are avoided. A brisk response is expected within 1\u20132 weeks; EULAR advises that a steroid trial should not be used as a diagnostic test. EULAR advises tapering to 10 mg/day within the first 2 months and aiming to stop within a year, while the 2026 BSR guideline describes a standard taper over 9\u201312 months, with faster options for those at high toxicity risk under closer review. Expect \u202170% global improvement within 1 week and a normal CRP within 3\u20134 weeks. Use the lower end of the dose range with comorbidity or diabetes and the higher end with high relapse risk. A single IM methylprednisolone injection (e.g. 120 mg every 3 weeks, tapering) is an alternative. NSAIDs have little role. The EULAR course notes typical duration is 1\u20132 years, with about a third needing longer low-dose treatment.","c":"Drug Monitoring & Safety","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":334,"s":"Under the 2026 BSR PMR guideline, what bone-protection principle applies to people starting glucocorticoids?","o":["Osteosarcoma arising in pagetic bone","Offer calcium and vitamin D alone, without a bisphosphonate","Offer a bisphosphonate unless contraindicated","Offer a bisphosphonate only after a fragility fracture has occurred","Offer bone protection only to patients already classified as high-risk on FRAX"],"a":2,"e":"The 2026 BSR guideline recommends bone protection for all people with PMR starting glucocorticoids, with bone health review (and DXA) at diagnosis. A bisphosphonate is the usual choice unless contraindicated, guided by individual fracture risk and local pathways. Bone protection: calcium, vitamin D and a bisphosphonate (per BSR, for all starting GC unless contraindicated). DXA at diagnosis where available. Monitor BP, glucose, weight, mood and cataracts. Give a steroid card and sick-day rules.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":335,"s":"Which features are required to classify polymyalgia rheumatica, and what does shoulder ultrasound typically show?","o":["Age 40 or over, unilateral shoulder pain and normal inflammatory markers","Age 50 or over, bilateral shoulder aching and raised CRP and/or ESR, plus sufficient additional points ultrasound typically shows subdeltoid bursitis, biceps tenosynovitis and glenohumeral synovitis","Any age, bilateral hip pain alone, regardless of CRP or ESR","Age 50 or over with unilateral shoulder pain only, regardless of CRP","Age 50 or over with bilateral shoulder pain but normal CRP and ESR"],"a":1,"e":"Additional points come from morning stiffness over 45 minutes, hip pain or limited movement, absence of RF/ACPA, absence of other joint involvement and characteristic ultrasound findings. Ultrasound findings add to classification and help distinguish PMR from mimics. The 2025 EULAR recommendations stress that classification criteria should not be used to diagnose individual patients. 2012 EULAR/ACR scoring: morning stiffness >45 minutes, 2 points; hip pain or limited range, 1; absence of RF and ACPA, 2; no other joint pain, 1; ultrasound (\u20211 shoulder plus \u20211 hip abnormal), 1; both shoulders abnormal, 1. The threshold is \u20214 without ultrasound (sensitivity 68%, specificity 78%) or \u20215 with ultrasound. Distal features occur in 25\u201350% (peripheral synovitis, CTS, RS3PE-like oedema). PET shows interspinous bursae, ischial tuberosities and shoulders. Work-up: FBC, U&E, LFTs, bone profile, CK, TSH, glucose, SPEP and FLC, RF and ACPA, vitamin D, urinalysis, chest radiograph. A normal CRP should prompt reconsideration.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Metabolic Bone Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":336,"s":"A patient with PMR relapses during taper, sometimes repeatedly (for example a 66-year-old woman with peripheral arthritis and ESR 70 at diagnosis who has relapsed twice). How should relapse be managed, and what steroid-sparing treatment is appropriate?","o":["Stop glucocorticoids immediately and start methotrexate alone","Increase glucocorticoids indefinitely without adding any steroid-sparing agent","Return to the last effective dose and taper again more slowly; for repeated relapse or high relapse risk add methotrexate 7.5\u201310 mg weekly, with an IL-6 receptor inhibitor (sarilumab or tocilizumab) for refractory disease","Switch to a TNF inhibitor for steroid-sparing effect","Adult-onset Still's disease; start an IL-1 inhibitor (such as anakinra) or an IL-6 inhibitor, ideally early after diagnosis"],"a":2,"e":"Relapses are common, especially in the first year. Return to the last effective dose and taper over 4\u20138 weeks back to the dose at which relapse occurred. The 2026 BSR guideline advises discussing a relapse with rheumatology and referring after a second relapse or 18 months of glucocorticoids. Repeated relapse should prompt steroid-sparing therapy and reassessment of the diagnosis. Methotrexate is considered early in those at high relapse risk, with relapse or with glucocorticoid toxicity. For people who cannot taper glucocorticoids, both the 2026 BSR and 2025 EULAR recommendations support an IL-6 receptor antagonist; EULAR prefers sarilumab for relapsing or refractory PMR (NHS access depends on commissioning). TNF inhibitors are not effective. Relapse affects 30\u201350%. High-risk features: female sex, ESR >40, peripheral arthritis. SAPHYR: sarilumab gave higher sustained remission with a 14-week taper than placebo with a 52-week taper. SEMAPHORE and PMR- SPARE: tocilizumab improved GC-free remission, including in early PMR. JAK inhibitors are under study. Treat symptoms, not an isolated CRP rise. Look for largevessel GCA in relapsing PMR.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":337,"s":"A patient labelled as PMR has an atypical course: partial response to prednisolone 15 mg with wrist and MCP synovitis; very high inflammatory markers with weight loss and a poor steroid response; or true proximal weakness with CK 2,400 U/L. What should be done?","o":["Continue the same prednisolone dose without further investigation","Increase prednisolone to 30 mg daily without reconsidering the diagnosis","Assume large-vessel GCA alone, without considering other mimics","Arrange malignancy screening alone, without considering infection or other mimics","Reconsider the diagnosis and investigate mimics: late-onset RA, infection (including endocarditis) malignancy, large-vessel GCA, and, when there is true weakness with a raised CK, inflammatory myopathy,"],"a":4,"e":"hypothyroidism and drug-induced myopathy An incomplete response, age under 60, prominent peripheral arthritis or systemic features should prompt reconsideration of the diagnosis. PMR causes pain and stiffness, not true weakness, and CK is normal; weakness with a raised CK points to myopathy. PMR mimics include malignancy, infection (including endocarditis), late-onset RA, CPPD, myopathy and endocrine disease. Category Mimics elderly-onset RA (especially Inflammatory seronegative), RS3PE, SpA, CPPD (pseudo-PMR), myositis, vasculitis (LV- GCA, AAV), ICI-PMR myeloma, lymphoma, paraneoplastic Malignancy solid tumours septic arthritis, endocarditis, spinal Infection infection, TB hypothyroidism, hyperparathyroidism, Endocrine and osteomalacia, statin myopathy metabolic bilateral cuff disease, adhesive capsulitis, Mechanical cervical spondylosis, OA, fibromyalgia (normal CRP) Parkinson's disease (rigidity) Neurological Red flags: age <60, chronic onset, no shoulder involvement, normal or extremely high markers, prominent systemic features or weight loss, erosive peripheral arthritis, neurological signs, poor response to 15\u201325 mg within 1\u20132 weeks. Tests: RF and ACPA, ultrasound of the hands; SPEP and FLC, CT or PET-CT for malignancy and LV-GCA; blood cultures and echo; CK, TSH and myositis antibodies; vitamin D, calcium and PTH. Refer to specialist care if atypical, under 60, steroid-dependent or refractory.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Vasculitis","Connective Tissue Disease","Metabolic Bone Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Fibromyalgia & Chronic Pain","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":338,"s":"A young woman has left arm claudication, a 30 mmHg inter-arm systolic difference, a subclavian bruit, raised CRP and MRA showing subclavian wall thickening and stenosis. What distinguishes Takayasu arteritis from GCA, and what treatment is recommended alongside glucocorticoids?","o":["Takayasu affects the aorta and major branches in younger patients, usually women under 40 (age at onset is the main distinction from GCA); add a glucocorticoid-sparing agent such as methotrexate, azathioprine or mycophenolate from the outset","Takayasu spares the pulmonary arteries entirely, unlike GCA; no steroid-sparing agent is needed","Takayasu occurs only in patients over 60, unlike GCA; glucocorticoids alone are sufficient","GCA and Takayasu are distinguished by ANCA status, and both are treated with glucocorticoids alone","Takayasu is distinguished from GCA by a positive temporal artery biopsy; treat with glucocorticoids alone"],"a":0,"e":"Both are granulomatous large-vessel vasculitides. Takayasu arteritis needs combined therapy; tocilizumab or TNF inhibitors are used for refractory disease. Elective revascularisation is performed in remission unless ischaemia is critical. Subclavian or aortic stenosis in a young person: Takayasu vs fibromuscular dysplasia, thoracic outlet syndrome (positional, cervical rib), atherosclerosis (risk factors), radiation arteritis, IgG4-related disease, Beh\u00e7et's, Cogan's, sarcoid, infection (TB, syphilis), congenital coarctation, mid-aortic syndrome (NF1, Williams syndrome), Marfan or Loeys-Dietz (aneurysmal). Numano types I\u2013V describe distribution (from arch branches to the whole aorta). Pulmonary artery involvement and coronary ostial lesions occur. Hypertension is common and often missed because of subclavian stenosis. Induction: prednisolone 40\u201360 mg/day, tapered, plus methotrexate, azathioprine, MMF or leflunomide. Refractory: tocilizumab, TNFi (infliximab, adalimumab); JAKi are emerging. CV protection: aspirin or statin per risk; strict BP control (measure on an unaffected limb). Revascularisation: stent or bypass in inactive disease (restenosis is higher when active); bypass lasts longer than stenting. Monitoring: MRA, CTA or PET (markers can be normal when active).","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":339,"s":"A 29-year-old man of Turkish origin has recurrent oral and genital ulcers and erythema nodosum-like lesions. What is required for classification of Beh\u00e7et's disease under the International Study Group criteria?","o":["Recurrent genital ulceration alone, without any additional feature required","Recurrent oral ulceration at least 3 times in 12 months, plus two of: genital ulcers, eye lesions, skin lesions or positive pathergy test","A positive pathergy test alone is sufficient for classification","HLA-B*51 positivity alone, without any clinical feature","Recurrent oral ulceration alone, with no additional criterion needed"],"a":1,"e":"Recurrent oral ulceration is mandatory in this classification. Beh\u00e7et's is a variable-vessel vasculitis affecting eyes, vessels, nervous system and gut. ICBD criteria (\u20214 points, more sensitive): oral ulcers 2, genital ulcers 2, ocular lesions 2; skin, neurological, vascular and pathergy 1 each. Oral ulcers are not mandatory under ICBD. Pathergy is positive in up to 60% in Turkey, the Middle East and Japan, but rarely in northern Europeans. HLA-B*51 is present in 50\u201360% (vs about 15% of controls) and supports but does not establish the diagnosis. There is no diagnostic test, so the diagnosis is clinical. Check FBC (cyclic neutropenia), B12, folate, ferritin, coeliac serology, HSV swabs, syphilis serology and HLA-B*51. Ophthalmology screening and a vascular assessment if symptomatic.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis"]},{"id":340,"s":"A patient with Beh\u00e7et's has recurrent oral and genital ulcers without major organ involvement. What is the first-line treatment, and what is used if oral ulcers persist despite topical treatment and colchicine?","o":["Colchicine with topical treatments first; for refractory ulcers, apremilast (randomised trial evidence) or a","High-dose systemic glucocorticoids as first-line, continued long-term","Topical treatment alone, with no role for colchicine","Rituximab as first-line, ahead of colchicine or topical treatment","Methotrexate as first-line, with glucocorticoids avoided entirely"],"a":0,"e":"TNF inhibitor Colchicine prevents recurrent mucocutaneous lesions, especially erythema nodosum and genital ulcers. Apremilast reduced oral ulcers in a randomised trial (RELIEF), and the 2025 EULAR recommendations advise apremilast or a TNF inhibitor when mucocutaneous disease is refractory to, or intolerant of, colchicine. Chronic systemic glucocorticoids should be avoided. Topicals: steroid mouthwash and creams, sucralfate, local anaesthetics. Further escalation: azathioprine, interferon-a, TNFi; thalidomide as a last resort (teratogenic, neuropathy). Arthritis: colchicine first, then IA steroid, then azathioprine, IFN-a or TNFi. Other points: stopping smoking can paradoxically worsen oral ulcers. Pregnancy often improves Beh\u00e7et's, and colchicine can continue. Emerging: IL-17 or IL-23 blockade (secukinumab, ustekinumab) for mucocutaneous disease; tocilizumab for refractory eye or neurological disease; JAKi.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":341,"s":"How should Beh\u00e7et's posterior uveitis or retinal vasculitis be treated, and how does management change when it is sight-threatening?","o":["Topical steroids alone are sufficient for posterior uveitis, since it rarely threatens sight","Observation alone, since Beh\u00e7et's uveitis often resolves spontaneously","All Beh\u00e7et's uveitis needs systemic immunosuppression and glucocorticoids should never be used alone: azathioprine with glucocorticoids, or ciclosporin, interferon-alpha or a monoclonal anti-TNF agent; for sight- threatening disease, high-dose glucocorticoids with a monoclonal anti-TNF antibody (preferably infliximab) combined with another immunosuppressant","Glucocorticoids alone, without any steroid-sparing agent, are sufficient even for sight-threatening disease","IL-1 blockade alone, without glucocorticoids, for sight-threatening disease"],"a":2,"e":"Sight-threatening posterior segment disease needs urgent ophthalmology-led treatment. The 2025 EULAR update increasingly favours monoclonal anti-TNF antibodies (especially infliximab) for eye involvement, both for induction and maintenance. Azathioprine, ciclosporin or interferon-alpha remain options for less severe posterior disease. Retinal vasculitis: Beh\u00e7et's (occlusive, veins and arteries, hypopyon), sarcoid (periphlebitis, \"candle-wax drippings\"), TB (Eales disease), syphilis, toxoplasmosis, CMV or ARN (herpes viruses; exclude before immunosuppression), SLE or APS, GPA, PAN, Susac syndrome (BRAO, deafness, corpus callosum lesions), MS (intermediate uveitis, periphlebitis), intraocular lymphoma masquerade. Isolated anterior uveitis can be treated topically, with systemic azathioprine in young men with poor-prognosis features. Avoid ciclosporin in neuro-Beh\u00e7et's (it is neurotoxic and can induce neuro-BD). Arrange regular ophthalmology review. Adalimumab is licensed for noninfectious posterior uveitis (VISUAL I/II).","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":342,"s":"A 31-year-old man with Beh\u00e7et's disease develops an acute femoral DVT. Pulmonary artery aneurysm has been excluded. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Anticoagulation alone, without any immunosuppression","Thrombolysis as first-line, given the acute presentation","No specific treatment is needed beyond compression stockings","Glucocorticoids with an immunosuppressant (preferably a monoclonal anti-TNF antibody, or azathioprine); anticoagulation may be added only if bleeding risk is low","High-dose anticoagulation alone, since pulmonary artery aneurysm has been excluded"],"a":3,"e":"Thrombosis in Beh\u00e7et's is driven by vessel wall inflammation. The 2025 EULAR recommendations advise glucocorticoids with immunosuppressives, preferably anti-TNF antibodies, continued as maintenance. Anticoagulants may be added only if bleeding risk is low and pulmonary artery aneurysms have been excluded, because rupture can be fatal. Thrombosis in Beh\u00e7et's is inflammatory, with an adherent thrombus, so immunosuppression prevents recurrence and anticoagulation alone adds little. Venous disease is the commonest vascular form (superficial thrombophlebitis, DVT, Budd\u2013Chiari, IVC thrombosis, CVST). Pulmonary artery aneurysms present with haemoptysis and are lethal. Arterial or pulmonary artery aneurysm: high-dose GC plus cyclophosphamide, infliximab if refractory. Embolisation is preferred to open surgery. Operate urgently on symptomatic peripheral aneurysms under immunosuppression (anastomotic aneurysm risk). Parenchymal neuro-BD: high-dose GC plus azathioprine or TNFi (preferred for severe disease). GI disease: 5-ASA or azathioprine; TNFi or thalidomide for severe disease; surgery for perforation or bleeding. CVST: GC with short anticoagulation (after excluding PAA). Mortality comes mainly from PAA, Budd\u2013Chiari, CNS and major vessel disease in young men. vasculitis","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":343,"s":"How does the Chapel Hill consensus nomenclature primarily classify vasculitis?","o":["By the presence or absence of ANCA positivity alone","By the specific autoantibody profile associated with each disease","By the predominant size of vessel involved, with additional aetiological categories","By age at onset alone, with no reference to vessel size","By the organs involved alone, regardless of vessel calibre"],"a":2,"e":"Large-vessel (Takayasu, GCA), medium-vessel (PAN, Kawasaki), small-vessel (ANCA-associated and immunecomplex) and variable-vessel (Beh\u00e7et's, Cogan's) categories are recognised. Category Diseases GCA (>50), Takayasu (<50) Large PAN (no GN, ANCA-negative), Kawasaki Medium Small: ANCA MPA, GPA, EGPA Small: anti-GBM, cryoglobulinaemic, IgA vasculitis, HUV immune complex Beh\u00e7et's, Cogan's Variable Single-organ cutaneous leucocytoclastic angiitis, PACNS, isolated aortitis lupus, rheumatoid, sarcoid, RP vasculitis Systemic disease HCV cryoglobulinaemia, HBV-PAN, Probable syphilitic aortitis, drug-associated cause (hydralazine, PTU, levamisole), cancer Chapel Hill is a nomenclature, not diagnostic criteria. Large and medium vessels do not involve capillaries or venules.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Paediatric Rheumatology","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":344,"s":"A patient with suspected small-vessel vasculitis needs ANCA testing. Which approach is most appropriate?","o":["A clinical diagnosis alone, without any laboratory testing","Indirect immunofluorescence alone, without antigen-specific immunoassays","ANCA testing in anyone with unexplained systemic symptoms, regardless of clinical features","Antigen-specific immunoassays for PR3-ANCA and MPO-ANCA as the primary test","A single ANCA test is sufficient regardless of clinical suspicion"],"a":3,"e":"High-quality PR3 and MPO immunoassays are the preferred screening tests. Test only with compatible features (pulmonary\u2013renal syndrome, RPGN, mononeuritis, ENT destruction, scleritis, palpable purpura). Use high-quality PR3 and MPO immunoassays; IIF is optional. Request anti-GBM in parallel for RPGN or DAH.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis"]},{"id":345,"s":"Which ANCA specificity is most strongly associated with GPA, and which features best distinguish GPA from MPA?","o":["MPO-ANCA (p-ANCA) in GPA, with granulomatous disease more typical of MPA","ANCA status does not help distinguish GPA from MPA at all","PR3-ANCA (c-ANCA) in GPA; GPA more often has granulomatous ENT or lung disease with PR3-ANCA whereas MPA usually has MPO-ANCA without granulomas","GPA and MPA are distinguished by complement levels, not ANCA specificity","PR3-ANCA occurs equally often in GPA and MPA, so it does not help distinguish them"],"a":2,"e":"MPO-ANCA (p-ANCA) predominates in MPA, renal-limited vasculitis and ANCA-positive EGPA. These are patterns rather than absolute rules, and both cause pauci-immune necrotising glomerulonephritis and alveolar haemorrhage. Destructive midline nasal disease: GPA vs cocaine-induced midline destructive lesion (often PR3 or anti-elastase positive), NK/T-cell lymphoma, invasive fungal sinusitis, TB, leprosy, leishmaniasis, syphilis, sarcoidosis, IgG4-RD, relapsing polychondritis (cartilage only, no mucosal destruction). GPA: PR3 in 65\u201375%, MPO in 10\u201320%, ANCA-negative in about 10% (more often limited ENT disease). MPA: MPO in 60\u201380%, PR3 in about 10%, negative in 10\u201320%. EGPA: ANCA-positive in 30\u201340% (MPO).","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":346,"s":"How should a low-positive MPO-ANCA without vasculitic features, or a rise in ANCA in a patient with vasculitis in remission (asymptomatic, normal urinalysis), be interpreted?","o":["A rising ANCA titre alone should always trigger pre-emptive treatment escalation","ANCA alone neither diagnoses vasculitis nor justifies escalation; assess the clinical phenotype and alternative causes, and in remission monitor more closely with clinical, urine and renal assessment","A low-positive result always indicates smouldering active vasculitis","Only indirect immunofluorescence should be used to confirm the result","Any positive ANCA mandates urgent immunosuppression regardless of symptoms"],"a":1,"e":"ANCA can be positive in infection (including endocarditis), drug exposure and other inflammatory disease; testing is most useful when a compatible syndrome exists. A rise or reappearance of ANCA modestly increases relapse risk (especially in PR3 and renal disease) but is an imperfect predictor. ANCA positivity without AAV: infective endocarditis (often PR3, with low complement), TB, HIV, hepatitis C, IBD and PSC (atypical p-ANCA), autoimmune hepatitis, drugs (hydralazine, PTU, minocycline, levamisole, allopurinol), anti-GBM disease, SLE, RA, cystic fibrosis. In remission with rising ANCA: review every 1\u20133 months with urinalysis, creatinine and symptoms. Consider early retreatment only when the clinical relapse risk is high (PR3, prior relapses), as a shared decision.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":347,"s":"What is induction therapy for organ- or life-threatening GPA or MPA (for example PR3-GPA with RPGN, creatinine 240, haemoptysis and sinus disease), and which trial supports rituximab?","o":["Oral glucocorticoids alone at standard dose, without rituximab or cyclophosphamide","Methotrexate with glucocorticoids, reserving rituximab for relapse only","Glucocorticoids (IV pulses then the reduced-dose PEXIVAS taper) with rituximab or cyclophosphamide rituximab was non-inferior to cyclophosphamide and superior in relapsing disease (RAVE)","Rituximab maintenance alone, without any induction glucocorticoids","Azathioprine with glucocorticoids as first-line induction"],"a":2,"e":"Organ- or life-threatening GPA or MPA needs urgent induction with rituximab or IV cyclophosphamide. The 2025 BSR recommendations prefer rituximab for relapsing disease, and it is a first-line option particularly in PR3-ANCA disease and when fertility preservation matters. Oral prednisolone follows the reduced-dose (PEXIVAS) taper to 5 mg/day by 4\u20135 months, which is as effective as standard dosing with fewer infections. Avacopan can be considered to spare glucocorticoids. Pulmonary\u2013renal syndrome: AAV, anti-GBM disease (or double-positive), SLE, cryoglobulinaemia, IgA vasculitis, TTP, catastrophic APS, infection (Legionella, leptospirosis, hantavirus, endocarditis), pulmonary oedema with AKI, PE with renal infarct. Rituximab: 375 mg/m\u00b2 weekly \u00d7 4, or 1 g \u00d7 2 two weeks apart (RAVE, RITUXVAS). Cyclophosphamide: IV 15 mg/kg (adjusted for age and renal function) at weeks 0, 2 and 4, then every 3 weeks for 3\u20136 months (CYCLOPS). Some centres combine rituximab with low-dose IV cyclophosphamide for severe GN. Adjuncts: - PJP prophylaxis: co-trimoxazole for \u20216 months after the last rituximab or cyclophosphamide dose. - Screening: HBV, HCV, HIV, TB, Strongyloides; baseline IgG. - Vaccines: before rituximab. - Protection: bone, PPI, VTE prophylaxis (high risk when active), ACE inhibitor or ARB for proteinuria. - Fertility: preservation before cyclophosphamide. - Bladder cancer: surveillance for haematuria once disease is inactive.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":348,"s":"A 49-year-old woman has new GPA limited to sinus disease and nasal crusting, with no renal or other organthreatening involvement. Which induction regimen is most appropriate?","o":["Cyclophosphamide with glucocorticoids as first-line, reserved for all new GPA","High-dose glucocorticoids alone, without any steroid-sparing agent","Methotrexate alone, without rituximab as even an alternative","A rituximab maintenance regimen alone, without an induction phase","Rituximab with glucocorticoids, with methotrexate or mycophenolate as alternatives"],"a":4,"e":"Cyclophosphamide is reserved for organ-or life-threatening disease. For non-organ-threatening disease, EULAR recommends glucocorticoids with rituximab, and the 2025 BSR recommendations allow methotrexate or mycophenolate, with a lower glucocorticoid regimen (LoVAS-style) considered. Non-organ-threatening disease: GC plus rituximab is preferred. Methotrexate (if eGFR >50) or MMF are alternatives with higher relapse rates. Co-trimoxazole can be an adjunct in limited ENT disease (it reduces relapse and S. aureus carriage). Nasal douching and ENT care.","c":"Drug Monitoring & Safety","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":349,"s":"A 70-year-old woman with MPA has a creatinine rising to 420 micromol/L due to active crescentic glomerulonephritis. Which adjunct may be considered?","o":["Apremilast","Disseminated gonococcal infection","Avacopan","An IL-17 inhibitor","Plasma exchange"],"a":4,"e":"Plasma exchange may be considered when creatinine exceeds 300 micromol/L due to active glomerulonephritis. It did not reduce death or end-stage kidney disease overall in a large trial and is not used routinely, including for alveolar haemorrhage alone. PEXIVAS showed no overall benefit of PLEX for death or ESKD. A later meta-analysis suggested a reduced 12-month ESKD risk with high creatinine. PLEX remains mandatory for anti-GBM double positivity. For DAH with hypoxaemia it is an individual decision.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":350,"s":"A patient with newly diagnosed severe MPA also has poorly controlled diabetes and osteoporosis. Which agent may be used with rituximab or cyclophosphamide to substantially reduce glucocorticoid exposure?","o":["Avacopan","Plasma exchange","Methotrexate","-2.5 or lower","Felty syndrome"],"a":0,"e":"Avacopan, an oral C5a receptor antagonist, was non-inferior for remission at 26 weeks and superior for sustained remission at 52 weeks compared with a prednisolone taper. LFTs need monitoring. ADVOCATE: avacopan (30 mg twice daily for 52 weeks) was non-inferior for remission at 26 weeks and superior for sustained remission at 52 weeks, with better eGFR recovery (especially at low eGFR) and lower GC toxicity. Monitor LFTs (hepatotoxicity, rare vanishing bile duct syndrome). It is a CYP3A4 substrate, so watch for interactions.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":351,"s":"After rituximab-based induction, a patient with GPA is in remission. Which maintenance strategy is most appropriate?","o":["Rituximab maintenance for 24-48 months after remission induction","Methotrexate maintenance alone, continued indefinitely without a defined endpoint","No maintenance therapy is needed once remission is achieved","Cyclophosphamide maintenance, continued for 24-48 months","Rituximab with glucocorticoids continued indefinitely at induction dose"],"a":0,"e":"The 2025 BSR recommendations prefer rituximab (500-1000 mg every 4-6 months) for maintenance for 24-48 months, with azathioprine or methotrexate as alternatives and mycophenolate if these are unsuitable. Glucocorticoids may be withdrawn within 6-12 months. MAINRITSAN: rituximab was superior to azathioprine. MAINRITSAN-3 showed fewer relapses with extended rituximab. Relapse risk is higher with PR3, ENT or lung disease, prior relapse, persistent ANCA and S. aureus carriage. Rituximab 500 mg every 6 months (or 1 g every 4\u20136 months) for 24\u201348 months, longer in high-risk patients. Check IgG before each dose (IVIG if low with infections). Major relapse: treat as new disease, preferring rituximab. Minor relapse: increase GC and optimise maintenance.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":352,"s":"A patient with GPA develops increasing stridor due to subglottic stenosis. What is the best immediate approach?","o":["Reassurance, since subglottic stenosis rarely progresses to airway compromise","Increase systemic immunosuppression alone, without any ENT or respiratory input","Urgent airway assessment with ENT and respiratory input, alongside disease-directed treatment","Immediate tracheostomy, without any attempt at local or systemic treatment","Await outpatient ENT review, since this is rarely urgent"],"a":2,"e":"Subglottic stenosis can threaten the airway and may not track systemic markers; it often needs local procedures as well as immunosuppression. Stridor or hoarseness in a rheumatology patient: GPA subglottic stenosis; relapsing polychondritis (laryngotracheal chondritis or tracheomalacia); RA cricoarytenoid arthritis; amyloid; post-intubation stenosis; angioedema (ACE inhibitors, HUV, C1-inhibitor deficiency); vocal cord palsy (aortic aneurysm, mediastinal mass); SLE; Beh\u00e7et's. Flow\u2013volume loop, CT of the airway, laryngoscopy. Intralesional steroid injection with dilatation (laser or balloon); tracheostomy for critical stenosis. Stenosis can progress despite systemic remission.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis"]},{"id":353,"s":"What proportion of EGPA patients are ANCA-positive, what is a leading cause of death, and what treats relapsing non-organ-threatening EGPA that relapses below 10 mg prednisolone?","o":["About 30\u201340% are ANCA-positive (usually MPO); cardiac involvement is a leading cause of death mepolizumab (or benralizumab) treats relapsing, steroid-dependent disease","Over 90% are ANCA-positive (usually PR3); renal failure is the leading cause of death","EGPA never involves the heart, unlike GPA and MPA","Oral corticosteroids alone are used for relapsing disease, with no role for biologics","ANCA status does not correlate with any clinical phenotype in EGPA"],"a":0,"e":"ANCA positivity is associated with glomerulonephritis, neuropathy and purpura; ANCA-negative disease more often features cardiac and lung involvement. ECG, troponin, echocardiography and cardiac MRI are performed at diagnosis. Rheumatology SCE \u2014 5-option SBA 110 Anti-IL-5/IL-5R therapy reduces relapses and glucocorticoid exposure and is recommended by the 2025 BSR recommendations for non-life- or organ-threatening EGPA and for maintenance. Severe EGPA is treated with glucocorticoids plus cyclophosphamide, or rituximab if cyclophosphamide is unsuitable. Hypereosinophilia: EGPA vs hypereosinophilic syndrome (FIP1L1-PDGFRA, tryptase, B12, T-cell clonality), parasites (Strongyloides serology before steroids), ABPA, drug reaction (DRESS), chronic eosinophilic pneumonia, eosinophilic fasciitis, lymphoma (Hodgkin, T-cell), adrenal insufficiency, mastocytosis, IgG4-RD. Mepolizumab 300 mg SC every 4 weeks (MIRRA). Benralizumab 30 mg SC every 4 weeks was non-inferior to mepolizumab in MANDARA, with more patients stopping GC. REOVAS: rituximab was not superior to conventional therapy in EGPA but is commonly used. Maintenance after severe disease: azathioprine, methotrexate, MMF or rituximab, with or without anti-IL-5. Asthma and sinus care: inhaled therapy and ENT management of polyps. Cardiac EGPA needs aggressive immunosuppression plus heart failure therapy.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":354,"s":"A 32-year-old woman has retiform purpura with necrosis of both earlobes, arthralgia, neutropenia and very high titre MPO-ANCA, with PR3-ANCA also positive. What is the most likely cause?","o":["Levamisole-adulterated cocaine","Polyarteritis nodosa","Cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis","Felty syndrome","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis"],"a":0,"e":"Levamisole-contaminated cocaine causes cutaneous vasculopathy, neutropenia and dual ANCA positivity, often with anti-elastase and antiphospholipid antibodies. Management centres on stopping cocaine. Retiform purpura or necrosis: levamisole, cryoglobulinaemia (type I), APS or CAPS, calciphylaxis, cholesterol emboli, warfarin or heparin necrosis, purpura fulminans (protein C deficiency, meningococcaemia), ecthyma gangrenosum, vasculitis (PAN, AAV). Levamisole-cocaine causes retiform purpura (ears, cheeks), neutropenia or agranulocytosis, very high MPO with PR3 (dual positivity), anti-elastase, aPL, low complement, and occasionally GN. Cocaine cessation (with addiction services) and wound care. GC or immunosuppression only for organ-threatening disease. Recurrence follows re-exposure.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":355,"s":"A man has testicular pain, mononeuritis multiplex, livedo, hypertension and renal and mesenteric microaneurysms on angiography, ANCA-negative and HBsAg-positive. What distinguishes PAN from AAV, and how is HBV-PAN treated?","o":["PAN causes glomerulonephritis identical to MPA; HBV-PAN is treated with prolonged immunosuppression alone","PAN affects medium-sized arteries, causing microaneurysms and organ infarction without glomerulonephritis; HBV-PAN is treated with antivirals plus short-course glucocorticoids and plasma exchange","PAN affects small vessels and capillaries, causing pulmonary haemorrhage","HBV-PAN is treated with long-term glucocorticoids and cyclophosphamide, deferring antivirals","PAN and AAV are distinguished only by ANCA status, with identical vessel involvement"],"a":1,"e":"HBV-associated PAN is immune-complex mediated. Prolonged immunosuppression promotes viral replication, so antivirals (entecavir or tenofovir) are combined with brief glucocorticoids and plasma exchange. PAN causes neuropathy, skin lesions, renal artery aneurysms and mesenteric ischaemia; glomerulonephritis and lung haemorrhage point to small-vessel vasculitis. Other microaneurysm causes: segmental arterial mediolysis, fibromuscular dysplasia, endocarditis (mycotic), vEDS, DADA2 (young, lacunar strokes, livedo), cocaine or amphetamines, atrial myxoma emboli, hairy cell leukaemia-associated PAN. Feature PAN MPA small (\u00b1 Vessels medium arteries medium) MPO in 70\u2013 ANCA negative 90% absent (infarcts, GN RPGN renovascular hypertension) DAH, ILD Lung spared Microaneurysms yes rare HBV link yes Rheumatology SCE \u2014 5-option SBA 111 no uncommon (20\u201330%) Relapse common Idiopathic PAN: non-severe disease, GC \u00b1 methotrexate or azathioprine; severe disease (FFS \u20211: neurological, renal, GI, cardiac), GC plus cyclophosphamide then maintenance. Cutaneous PAN (often post-streptococcal): NSAIDs, colchicine, dapsone, short GC. DADA2: TNF inhibitors. Screen all PAN patients for HBV.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":356,"s":"A patient with suspected medium-vessel vasculitis has abdominal pain out of proportion to examination and a rising lactate. What is the immediate priority?","o":["Urgent colonoscopy as the first investigation, before any imaging","Immediate high-dose glucocorticoids alone, without surgical input","Urgent surgical and medical assessment for mesenteric ischaemia, alongside vasculitis treatment","Observation with serial examination over 24 hours","Oral antibiotics alone, pending stool culture results"],"a":2,"e":"Bowel ischaemia is life-threatening and requires urgent imaging and surgical input. Urgent CT angiography, surgical review and pulse GC. Bowel ischaemia, perforation, gallbladder or appendix infarction and pancreatitis can all occur in PAN. GI involvement scores in the FFS. Consider cyclophosphamide.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":357,"s":"A 57-year-old woman has palpable purpura, arthralgia, peripheral neuropathy and low C4. Type II cryoglobulins are present and hepatitis C RNA is positive. Disease is mild without organ threat. What is the first-line treatment?","o":["Rituximab monotherapy, deferring antiviral treatment","Direct-acting antiviral therapy for hepatitis C","High-dose glucocorticoids alone, without any antiviral or immunosuppressive agent","Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor","Interferon-based antiviral therapy as first-line"],"a":1,"e":"Mild to moderate HCV-associated cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis often responds to viral eradication. Severe disease may need rituximab, with glucocorticoids and/or plasma exchange, alongside antivirals. HCV accounts for 70\u201390% of mixed cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis. Cryoglobulins are found in 40\u201360% of chronic HCV, but only 5\u201315% become symptomatic. Meltzer triad (purpura, arthralgia, weakness) is present in only 25\u201330% at diagnosis. Organ involvement: skin 70\u201390% (orthostatic purpura, ochre pigmentation, malleolar ulcers); joints 40\u2013 80%; painful axonal sensory neuropathy 20\u201360%; MPGN 20\u201335% (a major adverse prognostic factor); lymphoma 5\u2013 10%. Mechanism: the HCV E2 protein binds CD81 on B cells, with t(14;18) and BAFF promoting clonal RFproducing B cells. Severity Treatment DAAs (sofosbuvir/velpatasvir for 12 Mild to moderate weeks, or glecaprevir/pibrentasvir for 8 weeks; SVR >95%) Severe (GN, rituximab \u00b1 moderate GC, then or with ulcers, motor DAAs neuropathy) Life-threatening PLEX + IV methylprednisolone + rituximab; DAAs as soon as feasible Vasculitis persists or relapses after SVR in 10\u201320% (autonomous clone, lymphoma), so give rituximab. Screen for HBV before DAAs (reactivation risk). Rituximab can complex with cryoglobulins (serum sickness), so with a high cryocrit use PLEX first or a lower dose. Interferon regimens are obsolete. Low-grade splenic marginal zone lymphoma may regress after HCV eradication.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":358,"s":"A 52-year-old man has palpable purpura over the legs, abdominal pain and arthralgia after an upper respiratory infection. Skin biopsy shows leukocytoclastic vasculitis with IgA deposits. What is the most important follow-up investigation?","o":["Serial urinalysis for blood and protein, blood pressure and renal function","Skin biopsy repeated at 3 months to confirm resolution","Abdominal CT to look for underlying malignancy","Anti-topoisomerase I (Scl-70)","Faecal occult blood testing alone"],"a":0,"e":"Adults with IgA vasculitis more often develop significant nephritis than children. Renal biopsy is needed for significant proteinuria or impaired function. Palpable purpura: IgA vasculitis, cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis, AAV, drug hypersensitivity vasculitis (antibiotics, NSAIDs, allopurinol), infection (meningococcaemia, gonococcaemia, endocarditis, hepatitis B or C), SLE or RA vasculitis, HUV, paraneoplastic vasculitis (haematological malignancy), Schamberg's disease (non-palpable), scurvy (perifollicular). Take a deep punch biopsy within 24\u201348 hours for H&E and direct IF (IgA). IgA vasculitis features purpura on the legs and buttocks, arthritis, abdominal pain (intussusception in children) and IgA nephritis. Adults have a worse renal prognosis and more often an underlying cause (malignancy, infection, IBD). Supportive care for mild disease. GC for GI or severe disease. Nephritis is managed like IgA nephropathy (RAAS blockade, GC; MMF or cyclophosphamide for crescentic disease). Monitor urine for at least 6 months (children) and longer in adults.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":359,"s":"A 45-year-old woman has urticarial lesions lasting more than 24 hours that leave bruising, with arthralgia, low C3, C4 and C1q, and anti-C1q antibodies. Which diagnosis best fits, and what associations should be sought?","o":["Chronic spontaneous urticaria, with no further investigation needed","Schnitzler syndrome, requiring a search for monoclonal IgM","Adult-onset Still's disease, requiring ferritin and a search for an evanescent rash","CAPS, requiring NLRP3 genetic testing","Hypocomplementaemic urticarial vasculitis; associations include SLE and obstructive lung disease"],"a":4,"e":"Individual urticarial vasculitis lesions persist beyond 24 hours, may burn or hurt and leave pigmentation. The hypocomplementaemic form overlaps with SLE and can cause COPD-like lung disease, angioedema, glomerulonephritis and uveitis. Urticaria with systemic features: urticarial vasculitis (normocomplementaemic or hypocomplementaemic), Schnitzler syndrome (monoclonal IgM, fever, bone pain), CAPS (neutrophilic urticaria from childhood), adult-onset Still's (evanescent rash), SLE, serum sickness, chronic spontaneous urticaria (lesions <24 hours, no bruising, antihistamine-responsive). Skin biopsy (leucocytoclastic vasculitis). Mild disease: antihistamines, dapsone, colchicine, HCQ. Systemic disease: GC plus MMF or azathioprine; rituximab, omalizumab, anakinra or canakinumab for refractory disease. Screen for SLE, HCV, malignancy and COPD (HUV causes early emphysema; avoid smoking).","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":360,"s":"A patient with systemic vasculitis develops acute foot drop from mononeuritis multiplex. What is the most appropriate approach?","o":["Physiotherapy and an ankle-foot orthosis alone, without any change to immunosuppression","Urgent assessment and treatment of active vasculitic neuropathy, because prompt therapy may prevent permanent deficit","Observation with serial nerve conduction studies over several months","Oral glucocorticoids alone, without reassessment of systemic therapy","Referral for elective nerve decompression surgery"],"a":1,"e":"New motor neuropathy indicates organ-threatening disease. Nerve conduction studies and, where needed, nerve biopsy support the diagnosis. Mononeuritis multiplex is a stepwise, asymmetric painful neuropathy in multiple named nerves (foot drop, wrist drop). Causes: PAN (60\u201370%), EGPA (45\u201375%), MPA, GPA, cryoglobulinaemia, RA vasculitis, SLE, Sj\u00f6gren's, sarcoid, diabetes, HIV, HNPP, leprosy, lymphoma (neurolymphomatosis), amyloid. NCS show an axonal multifocal pattern. Combined sural nerve and muscle biopsy (sural plus peroneus brevis) increases yield. Induction as for severe vasculitis. Neuropathic pain treatment, AFO or splints and physiotherapy. Recovery is slow (months).","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":361,"s":"A previously well man has a hot swollen knee and fever. Which organism most commonly causes native joint septic arthritis in adults, and what is the initial management if he has no prosthesis, MRSA risk or penicillin allergy?","o":["Neisseria gonorrhoeae; start IV ceftriaxone empirically in all adults","Coagulase-negative staphylococci; start oral flucloxacillin alone","Mycobacterium tuberculosis; await culture before any antibiotic","Staphylococcus aureus; aspirate the joint and take blood cultures, then start IV flucloxacillin","Streptococcus pyogenes; start IV benzylpenicillin alone"],"a":3,"e":"Empirical flucloxacillin is used for adults without risk factors; clindamycin if penicillin-allergic, vancomycin if MRSA risk, and a third-generation cephalosporin for older or frail patients or suspected gonococcal or Gram-negative infection. Gonococcus is considered in young sexually active adults, and Gram-negative organisms in the elderly, immunosuppressed and people who inject drugs. Kingella is a cause in young children. Acute hot joint: septic arthritis; crystal arthritis (gout, CPP; can coexist); reactive arthritis; haemarthrosis (trauma, anticoagulants, haemophilia, PVNS/TGCT); inflammatory arthritis flare; Lyme disease; viral arthritis; acute rheumatic fever; cellulitis or bursitis mimicking a joint; osteomyelitis near the joint; Charcot joint; SONK or insufficiency fracture. Fever is present in about 60%. Blood cultures are positive in about 50%. CRP, ESR and WCC support but cannot rule out infection. Procalcitonin and synovial lactate may help. Inoculate synovial fluid into blood culture bottles. Screen for the source (skin, urine, endocarditis); do gonococcal NAAT if relevant. Use ultrasound for hip effusion and guided aspiration, and MRI for osteomyelitis or abscess. Hold biologics and JAKi. Early physiotherapy once controlled.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":362,"s":"A hot swollen native knee has a synovial fluid white cell count of 80,000/mm\u00b3 with 90% neutrophils. Which interpretation is safest?","o":["Gram stain and bacterial culture","Septic arthritis is a major concern; send cultures and treat urgently if clinical suspicion is high","Staphylococcus aureus; aspirate the joint and take blood cultures, then start IV flucloxacillin","A count below 50,000/mm\u00b3 reliably excludes septic arthritis","This cell count is diagnostic of gout and excludes infection"],"a":1,"e":"High cell counts with neutrophil predominance raise concern for infection, but no cut-off is perfectly diagnostic and crystal arthritis can overlap. WCC guide: non-inflammatory 200\u20132,000; inflammatory 2,000\u201350,000; septic usually >50,000 with >90% neutrophils (but lower in early, partially treated, gonococcal, TB or immunosuppressed cases). Crystal arthritis can exceed 50,000. Crystals do not exclude infection. Synovial glucose is low and lactate high in sepsis.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression"]},{"id":363,"s":"A patient with suspected septic arthritis is hypotensive and tachycardic. What is correct regarding aspiration and antibiotics?","o":["Aspirate and await results before starting any antibiotic, since cultures must not be compromised","Treat the hypotension alone first, deferring cultures and antibiotics until stable","Give oral antibiotics only, since IV access may be difficult in shock","Delay antibiotics until surgical review confirms the diagnosis","Take blood and joint cultures rapidly if feasible, but do not delay antibiotics and resuscitation"],"a":4,"e":"In sepsis, resuscitation and antibiotics take priority. In stable patients, aspiration before antibiotics improves culture yield. Sepsis-6 within the hour: cultures, IV antibiotics, fluids, lactate, oxygen, urine output. Image-guided aspiration as soon as feasible, without delaying antibiotics. Urgent orthopaedic washout. Consider an MRSA-active agent in healthcare-associated cases.","c":"Infection & Immunosuppression","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression"]},{"id":364,"s":"What is the role of joint washout in septic arthritis, and how long should antibiotics continue in uncomplicated S. aureus native-knee infection that improves rapidly after washout?","o":["Washout is never needed if blood cultures are positive","Washout may be needed for large joints, purulent fluid or failure to improve (guided by orthopaedic assessment); about 2 weeks of targeted therapy is non-inferior to 4 weeks after drainage","Intra-articular antibiotics should be given at the time of washout","A full 6 months of antibiotics is required regardless of surgical drainage","Washout is only indicated for prosthetic joint infection, never native joints"],"a":1,"e":"Daily needle aspiration, arthroscopic washout or open debridement are used depending on joint, response and organism; the hip usually needs surgery. Older guidance advised about 2 weeks IV followed by 4 weeks oral, without trial evidence. Randomised data now support shorter targeted courses after surgical drainage in uncomplicated native-joint infection, with early switch to oral therapy (OVIVA showed oral is non-inferior to IV in bone and joint infection). Bacteraemia, osteomyelitis, prostheses and resistant organisms need longer treatment. Intra-articular antibiotics cause chemical synovitis and are not used. Monitor clinical response, CRP and repeat aspiration. Physiotherapy to restore range. Recheck the diagnosis if there is no improvement (TB, fungi, crystals).","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression"]},{"id":365,"s":"A patient develops a painful, warm, swollen knee after total knee replacement, either 9 months later (clinically stable) or 2 weeks after surgery with wound erythema and fever but a well-fixed implant. What is the first action, and which strategy suits early infection?","o":["Start empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics immediately in both scenarios, before any aspiration","Assume aseptic loosening in both scenarios, since the implant is stable","Treat as gout without aspiration, given the recent surgery","Proceed directly to two-stage revision in both scenarios","Urgent orthopaedic referral for aspiration, ideally before any antibiotics; early postoperative (or acute haematogenous) infection with a stable implant and short symptom duration suits DAIR (debridement, antibiotics and implant retention)"],"a":4,"e":"Antibiotics before aspiration reduce culture yield and can compromise decisions on debridement, implant retention or revision, so they should, where possible, be withheld until aspirate and intraoperative samples have been taken. Chronic or late infections usually need one- or two-stage revision in a specialist MDT. Painful prosthesis: PJI vs aseptic loosening (X-ray cannot separate them), instability, periprosthetic fracture, metallosis or ALVAL (metal-on-metal), crystal arthritis in the replaced joint, referred hip or spine pain, CRPS, haemarthrosis. DAIR (with exchange of mobile parts) suits infection with symptoms <3\u20134 weeks, a stable implant and a susceptible organism. One-stage revision suits a known susceptible organism with good soft tissues. Two-stage revision is for difficult or unknown organisms or poor soft tissue. Suppressive antibiotics when surgery is impossible. Prevention: optimise diabetes, skin, weight and smoking; decolonise S. aureus; perioperative prophylaxis; DMARD timing; avoid IA steroid within 3 months before surgery.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":366,"s":"A 23-year-old sexually active woman has migratory polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis of the wrist and a few painless pustules on her hands. What is the most likely diagnosis and treatment?","o":["Reactive arthritis following a recent gastrointestinal infection","Secondary syphilis, requiring only a single dose of penicillin","Still's disease, requiring high-dose glucocorticoids","Brucellosis, requiring prolonged combination antibiotics","Disseminated gonococcal infection; IV ceftriaxone, with testing for other STIs and partner notification"],"a":4,"e":"The arthritis-dermatitis-tenosynovitis triad is characteristic. Blood, synovial fluid and mucosal site cultures with NAAT should be taken. Arthritis with dermatitis and tenosynovitis: DGI vs meningococcaemia, ReA, secondary syphilis, infective endocarditis, HBV prodrome, Lyme disease, Still's disease, Sweet's syndrome, sarcoid. Two patterns: the triad of migratory polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis and painless pustules; or purulent mono- or oligoarthritis without skin lesions. Risk factors: women (asymptomatic mucosal infection), menstruation or pregnancy, MSM, terminal complement deficiency (C5\u2013 C9), eculizumab, HIV. Mucosal NAAT and culture are positive in about 80%; synovial culture in about 50%. Ceftriaxone 1 g IV daily, switching after improvement for \u20217 days in total. Get culture sensitivities (resistance is rising). Test for chlamydia, HIV and syphilis, and treat partners. Recurrent neisserial infection warrants a CH50 check.","c":"Pregnancy & Reproductive","t":["Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":367,"s":"How is Lyme disease treated: erythema migrans (a 12 cm expanding lesion with central clearing 2 weeks after positive)?","o":["Erythema migrans should not be treated until serology confirms the diagnosis","Erythema migrans requires IV ceftriaxone as first-line, before any oral option","Lyme arthritis should be treated with NSAIDs alone, without antibiotics","A single dose of doxycycline is sufficient for both erythema migrans and Lyme arthritis","Erythema migrans: treat clinically with doxycycline for 21 days without serology. Lyme arthritis: doxycycline for 28 days"],"a":4,"e":"Erythema migrans is diagnostic of Lyme disease and should be treated without serology, which is often negative early in infection; amoxicillin is an alternative. A small reaction appearing within 48 hours of a bite is usually a hypersensitivity reaction, not erythema migrans. Arthritis persisting after antibiotics may be post-infectious and is managed with anti-inflammatory treatment. Lyme arthritis is a large-joint mono- or oligoarthritis (often a big knee effusion with little pain). Synovial WCC is 10,000\u2013 25,000. IgG is almost always positive. Synovial PCR is positive in most untreated cases. About 10% of US patients have antibiotic-refractory arthritis (HLA-DR-linked). Manifestation Treatment doxycycline 10\u201321 days (amoxicillin, EM cefuroxime) Facial palsy, doxycycline or IV ceftriaxone 14\u201321 meningitis, days radiculitis IV ceftriaxone if high-degree block, Carditis otherwise oral doxycycline doxycycline 28 days (amoxicillin in Arthritis young children and pregnancy) second 28-day oral course or IV Persistent arthritis ceftriaxone 2\u20134 weeks; then NSAIDs, IA steroid, DMARDs, synovectomy Prevention: tick avoidance and checks. Single-dose doxycycline within 72 hours of a high-risk bite in highincidence areas.","c":"Paediatric Rheumatology","t":["Pregnancy & Reproductive","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":368,"s":"A 64-year-old man with diabetes has severe progressive thoracolumbar back pain, fever and raised CRP. MRI shows discitis with adjacent endplate oedema. Blood cultures are negative and he is haemodynamically stable. What is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Image-guided biopsy for culture (including mycobacteria) before antibiotics","NSAIDs","Start empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics immediately, without a biopsy","Repeat blood cultures only, with no imaging-guided biopsy","Treat empirically for spondyloarthritis with NSAIDs and physiotherapy"],"a":0,"e":"A microbiological diagnosis guides prolonged antibiotic therapy. TB, Brucella and fungi should be considered. Urgent surgery is needed for cord compression or instability. Spondylodiscitis organisms: S. aureus, streptococci, Enterobacterales, TB (Pott's), Brucella (lumbar, older men), Salmonella, fungi (Candida in IVDU). Mimics: Modic type 1 change, erosive osteochondrosis, Andersson lesion (AS), SAPHO, metastasis, destructive spondyloarthropathy of dialysis, CPPD or gout of the spine. Blood cultures (positive in 50\u201370% of pyogenic cases); CTguided biopsy (repeat if negative) with TB PCR, fungal and Brucella cultures. Echo for S. aureus or streptococci. Six weeks of targeted therapy (longer for undrained abscess or implants). Surgery for neurological deficit, instability or a large abscess. MRI lags clinical improvement, so follow clinically and with CRP.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":369,"s":"A 30-year-old man from Pakistan has thoracic back pain, night sweats and weight loss. MRI shows destruction of two adjacent vertebral bodies with a large paraspinal abscess and relative disc sparing. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Spinal tuberculosis (Pott's disease)","Metastatic spinal disease from an undiagnosed primary tumour","Discitis from Staphylococcus aureus","Brucellosis; combination antibiotics for at least 6 weeks","Transient osteoporosis of the hip"],"a":0,"e":"Spinal TB typically affects the thoracic spine with anterior vertebral destruction, subligamentous spread, early disc preservation and large cold abscesses. Pott's disease: thoracic predominance, anterior vertebral destruction, subligamentous spread, skip lesions, early disc preservation, large calcified cold (psoas) abscesses, gibbus deformity. 10\u201340% of spinal infections in endemic areas. CT-guided biopsy (AFB, GeneXpert, culture, histology). Standard anti-TB therapy for 9\u201312 months. Surgery for cord compression, instability or deformity. Screen for HIV. Notify public health.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":370,"s":"A patient with RA has fever, a new murmur, anaemia, positive blood cultures, purpura and haematuria. Which condition can mimic systemic vasculitis here?","o":["Infective endocarditis","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia","Multiple myeloma","Development of MALT lymphoma","Systemic lupus erythematosus with lupus nephritis"],"a":0,"e":"Endocarditis causes immune-complex disease, glomerulonephritis and positive autoantibodies including ANCA and RF. Infection must be excluded before immunosuppression. Endocarditis can cause immune-complex GN, low complement, positive RF, PR3-ANCA (or MPO), splinters, Janeway lesions, Osler nodes and Roth spots, emboli, arthralgia, back pain and discitis, and septic arthritis. Three sets of blood cultures before antibiotics, echo (TOE if TTE is negative), modified Duke criteria. Never start immunosuppression for \"vasculitis\" until endocarditis is excluded. Culture-negative endocarditis: consider Bartonella, Coxiella (Q fever), T. whipplei and HACEK organisms; consider Libman\u2013Sacks endocarditis in SLE or APS.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":371,"s":"In a patient with an organ-threatening rheumatic presentation and high clinical suspicion, confirmatory tests are still pending. Which principle is safest?","o":["Wait for all confirmatory tests before starting any treatment","Treat based on the single most likely diagnosis only","Delay treatment until a tissue diagnosis is obtained","Treat urgently while obtaining investigations in parallel","Treat only after excluding every alternative diagnosis"],"a":3,"e":"Vision-threatening GCA, pulmonary haemorrhage, rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, septic arthritis and airway compromise all need time-critical treatment. Time-critical presentations: - GCA with visual symptoms: IV methylprednisolone. - DAH or pulmonary\u2013renal syndrome: pulse GC + rituximab or cyclophosphamide \u00b1 PLEX; test anti-GBM. - RPGN: pulse GC, biopsy within days. - Septic arthritis: aspiration then antibiotics. - Airway threat (RP, GPA): ENT and ICU. - Scleroderma renal crisis: captopril. - CAPS: triple therapy. - MAS: GC + anakinra. - Cauda equina syndrome: MRI and decompression. - Fractured ankylosed spine: CT and immobilise. - Neutropenic sepsis: antibiotics within 1 hour. - Adrenal crisis: hydrocortisone 100 mg. - Methotrexate toxicity: folinic acid.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":372,"s":"A patient with inflammatory arthritis taking a JAK inhibitor develops sudden pleuritic chest pain and breathlessness. Which diagnosis must be considered first?","o":["Pulmonary embolism, alongside other acute cardiopulmonary causes","Chondrocalcinosis","An interstitial lung disease flare, given the patient's inflammatory arthritis","Apremilast","Pleuritic pain from the underlying inflammatory arthritis alone"],"a":0,"e":"JAK inhibitors are associated with venous thromboembolism, particularly in those with additional risk factors. CTPA, D-dimer (often raised in inflammation), leg ultrasound. Anticoagulate and stop or switch the JAKi (switch to a biologic; avoid JAKi re-exposure after VTE). Other JAKi emergencies: zoster (including disseminated), serious infection, MACE. Assess VTE risk before starting: prior VTE, cancer, immobility, obesity, oestrogen therapy.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":373,"s":"A primary school teacher develops sudden symmetrical small-joint polyarthritis two weeks after children in her class had 'slapped cheeks'. RF is weakly positive. What is the most likely diagnosis and course?","o":["Early rheumatoid arthritis; start methotrexate without delay","Parvovirus B19 arthropathy; self-limiting, confirm with IgM","Reactive arthritis following a recent infection, requiring NSAIDs for months","Hepatitis B prodrome, requiring antiviral therapy","Rubella arthritis, requiring long-term DMARD therapy"],"a":1,"e":"Adults with parvovirus B19 often present with an acute symmetrical small-joint polyarthritis mimicking RA, sometimes with transient RF or ANA positivity. Most cases settle within weeks, and NSAIDs suffice. Serology avoids unnecessary DMARD therapy. Infection in pregnancy carries a risk of fetal anaemia and hydrops. Acute symmetrical small-joint polyarthritis in an adult: parvovirus B19, rubella or recent vaccination, HBV prodrome (urticaria, raised ALT), HCV, HIV seroconversion, EBV or CMV, chikungunya or other alphaviruses after travel, early RA, SLE (parvovirus can mimic it: rash, cytopenias, positive ANA, low complement), Still's, sarcoid, ReA, PSRA or ARF. Check parvovirus IgM (positive for up to about 3 months) or PCR. Use NSAIDs and reassure (most settle within weeks, sometimes months). Check rubella immunity and inform obstetric services if pregnant or in contact with pregnant women (risk of fetal hydrops and loss, especially before 20 weeks). Aplastic crisis can occur in haemolytic anaemias.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Infection & Immunosuppression","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":374,"s":"A 38-year-old man with untreated HIV infection has bilateral painless parotid swelling, dry eyes and mouth, and a circulating CD8+ lymphocytosis. Anti-Ro and anti-La are negative. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Diffuse infiltrative lymphocytosis syndrome (DILS)","Primary Sj\u00f6gren's disease, with anti-Ro positivity expected","Sarcoidosis with Heerfordt syndrome","Lymphoma infiltrating both parotid glands","Mumps parotitis, requiring supportive care alone"],"a":0,"e":"DILS is a CD8+ T-cell infiltrative disorder in HIV that mimics Sj\u00f6gren's disease. Unlike primary Sj\u00f6gren's, it is usually autoantibody negative, involves more prominent parotid swelling and may infiltrate other organs. It improves with effective antiretroviral therapy. DILS: bilateral parotid enlargement, sicca, CD8+ lymphocytosis (>1,000/\u00b5L), lymphocytic interstitial pneumonitis, nephritis, hepatitis, neuropathy, aseptic meningitis; anti-Ro and anti-La negative. It is less common in the ART era. Antiretroviral therapy; GC for organ-threatening infiltration. Exclude lymphoma. Other HIV rheumatology: HIVassociated arthritis, ReA, severe PsA, painful articular syndrome, myopathy (including zidovudine), osteonecrosis, IRIS-triggered autoimmunity.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":375,"s":"A 42-year-old woman returns from R\u00e9union with fever, a maculopapular rash and severe, disabling symmetrical polyarthralgia of the hands, wrists and ankles. Four months later she still has persistent hand arthritis. Which infection is most likely?","o":["Disseminated gonococcal infection","Complex regional pain syndrome","Development of MALT lymphoma","Chikungunya virus","Enthesitis-related arthritis"],"a":3,"e":"Chikungunya, an Aedes-transmitted alphavirus, causes intense acute polyarthralgia. A substantial minority develop chronic inflammatory arthritis that can mimic seronegative RA. Chronic disease is managed with NSAIDs, short courses of steroids and sometimes methotrexate. Dengue arthralgia is not usually prolonged. Chikungunya: abrupt fever, severe symmetric polyarthralgia of the hands, wrists and ankles, periarticular oedema and rash. Chronic arthritis develops in 25\u201340% and can mimic RA or SpA. RT-PCR in the first week, IgM after 5\u201310 days. Analgesia and NSAIDs (only after excluding dengue, because of bleeding risk), short GC for subacute disease, methotrexate for chronic inflammatory arthritis. Other alphaviruses: Ross River (Australia), Sindbis, Mayaro, O'nyong-nyong.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Spondyloarthritis & AS","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":376,"s":"A 54-year-old man has a 6-year history of migratory, seronegative large-joint arthritis that worsened after starting a TNF inhibitor. He now has diarrhoea, weight loss, low-grade fever and lymphadenopathy. What is the most appropriate diagnostic test?","o":["Serum ACE level and a chest radiograph for sarcoidosis","Colonoscopy with random biopsies for inflammatory bowel disease","Duodenal biopsy for PAS staining and T. whipplei PCR","Stool culture and Clostridioides difficile toxin testing","Increase the TNF inhibitor dose, since the diarrhoea suggests inadequate disease control"],"a":2,"e":"Whipple's disease often starts with years of migratory arthritis before gastrointestinal symptoms. Immunosuppression, particularly TNF inhibition, can unmask or accelerate it. Diagnosis is by PAS staining and T. whipplei PCR of duodenal tissue. Treatment is ceftriaxone followed by prolonged co-trimoxazole, or doxycycline with hydroxychloroquine. Whipple's disease mostly affects middle-aged white men. Prodromal migratory non-destructive arthritis can precede GI symptoms by years. Other features: weight loss, diarrhoea, fever, lymphadenopathy, hyperpigmentation, culture-negative endocarditis, uveitis, and CNS disease (oculomasticatory myorhythmia is pathognomonic). Red flag: worsening after TNFi or other immunosuppression. Duodenal biopsy (PAS-positive foamy macrophages) with T. whipplei PCR. Saliva or stool PCR alone is not diagnostic (healthy carriers). Do a CSF PCR in all cases. Treat with ceftriaxone for 2 weeks then co-trimoxazole for 12 months, or doxycycline plus HCQ for 12 months (with lifelong doxycycline in some). Watch for IRIS early in treatment.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":377,"s":"How is acute rheumatic fever classified under the revised Jones criteria in a high-incidence community (for example ankle monoarthritis, fever 38.2 \u00b0C, ESR 35 and a raised ASO), and how is recurrence prevented?","o":["Only the original single-tier Jones criteria apply, regardless of population risk","Fever and arthralgia alone, without any other criteria, are sufficient for diagnosis","Monoarthritis never counts towards the Jones criteria, in any population","ASO titre alone, without any clinical criteria, confirms the diagnosis","In moderate/high-risk populations monoarthritis counts as a major criterion and fever \u202138 \u00b0C and ESR"],"a":4,"e":"\u202130 as minor criteria, so this is ARF; prevent recurrence with IM benzathine benzylpenicillin every 3\u20134 weeks The revised Jones criteria lowered the thresholds for moderate/high-risk populations: monoarthritis or polyarthralgia can count as major criteria, and fever \u202138 \u00b0C and ESR \u202130 count as minor criteria. Older texts describe only the single criteria set. Echocardiography is recommended for all suspected cases to detect subclinical carditis, and septic arthritis should still be excluded in monoarthritis. Secondary prophylaxis is the most effective way to prevent recurrent ARF, which drives progression of valve disease. Duration depends on severity, typically at least 10 years after the last episode or into adulthood (age 21), and longer (to 40 or lifelong) with moderate or severe carditis. Oral penicillin or erythromycin are less effective alternatives. Diagnosis requires evidence of preceding GAS infection plus 2 major, or 1 major and 2 minor criteria (recurrence: 2 major, 1 major and 2 minor, or 3 minor). Major criteria: carditis (clinical or echo), arthritis, chorea (Sydenham's; may occur alone), erythema marginatum, subcutaneous nodules. Low-risk thresholds: polyarthritis only, fever \u202138.5 \u00b0C, ESR \u202160 or CRP \u20213 mg/dL. Eradicate GAS (penicillin). High-dose aspirin or NSAIDs for arthritis (a dramatic response is typical; little response suggests another diagnosis). GC for severe carditis, plus heart failure care. Treat chorea with haloperidol, valproate or carbamazepine.","c":"Infection & Immunosuppression","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":378,"s":"A man with active pulmonary TB develops a sterile symmetrical polyarthritis that settles with antituberculous therapy. What is this condition?","o":["Rheumatoid arthritis triggered by TB infection","Poncet's disease (reactive tuberculous arthritis)","Look for an underlying malignancy","Septic arthritis requiring synovial biopsy and culture before treatment","Add an IL-17 or IL-23 inhibitor"],"a":1,"e":"Poncet's disease is a sterile reactive polyarthritis associated with active TB elsewhere, and it resolves with TB treatment. Direct tuberculous arthritis is usually a chronic monoarthritis of a large weight-bearing joint with positive synovial biopsy or culture. Pyrazinamide can cause hyperuricaemia and arthralgia. Arthritis in TB: Poncet's (sterile reactive polyarthritis), direct TB arthritis (chronic monoarthritis), TB dactylitis, spinal TB, drug-induced (pyrazinamide hyperuricaemia and gout; isoniazid drug-induced lupus; rifampicin and fluoroquinolone tendinopathy), and BCG-induced ReA after intravesical therapy. Standard anti-TB therapy; NSAIDs for symptoms. Poncet's resolves with treatment. Exclude septic TB arthritis with synovial biopsy if monoarticular.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Psoriatic Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":379,"s":"A 40-year-old shepherd who drinks unpasteurised goat's milk has undulant fevers, night sweats and unilateral buttock pain. MRI shows unilateral sacroiliitis with an adjacent effusion. What is the most likely diagnosis and treatment?","o":["Ankylosing spondylitis; start a TNF inhibitor","Spinal tuberculosis (Pott's disease)","Brucellosis; combination antibiotics for at least 6 weeks","Septic sacroiliitis from Staphylococcus aureus; IV flucloxacillin","Transient osteoporosis of the hip"],"a":2,"e":"Brucellosis commonly causes sacroiliitis, spondylitis and peripheral arthritis. Combination antibiotic therapy is needed to prevent relapse, and spondylitis requires longer courses. The laboratory must be told, because Brucella is a significant hazard to laboratory staff. Brucellosis is acquired from unpasteurised dairy, animal contact (farmers, vets, abattoirs) or laboratories, mainly in the Mediterranean, Middle East, Central Asia and Latin America (B. melitensis). Features: undulant fever, sweats, hepatosplenomegaly and cytopenias. Osteoarticular disease occurs in about a third: unilateral sacroiliitis (young), lumbar spondylitis (older; \"Pedro Pons\" anterior epiphysitis), large-joint arthritis, bursitis. Prolonged blood cultures (biohazard: warn the laboratory), PCR, serology (SAT \u20211:160; cross-reacts with Yersinia). Doxycycline plus rifampicin for 6 weeks, or doxycycline plus an aminoglycoside for the first 1\u20132 weeks. Spondylitis needs \u20213 months. Special regimens apply in pregnancy and children.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Metabolic Bone Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Paediatric Rheumatology","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":380,"s":"A 72-year-old man on warfarin for atrial fibrillation has an INR of 2.6 and a painful knee effusion from CPPD. Which approach is most appropriate?","o":["Low-dose colchicine prophylaxis during initiation and titration","Immediate factor VIII replacement with rest, ice and analgesia","Urgent management for suspected septic arthritis while cultures are pending","Proceed without stopping warfarin if the INR is in range","Avoid aspiration altogether while anticoagulated"],"a":3,"e":"The risk of significant bleeding after joint aspiration or injection in therapeutically anticoagulated patients is very low. Interrupting anticoagulation risks thrombosis. The INR should be checked and be within the target range (generally up to about 3). Use a fine needle and apply pressure afterwards. Use a fine needle, apply firm pressure for 5 minutes, and consider ultrasound guidance. DOACs: no need to stop for most joint procedures (time the procedure at the trough if worried). Antiplatelets: continue. Haemophilia: aspirate only with factor cover. For an INR above about 3\u20133.5, defer elective procedures or seek haematology advice.","c":"Infection & Immunosuppression","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":381,"s":"After a knee steroid injection, which feature most strongly suggests iatrogenic septic arthritis rather than a post-injection flare?","o":["Mild pain persisting beyond a week without fever","A low-grade temperature on the day of injection only","Macrophage activation syndrome","Worsening beyond 48 hours with fever and systemic upset","Erythema at the injection site within the first hour"],"a":3,"e":"A post-injection flare (a crystal-like reaction to the steroid ester) starts within hours and settles within 48-72 hours. Iatrogenic infection is rare and usually develops days later with worsening pain and systemic features. Urgent aspiration for culture is needed if sepsis is suspected. Worsening after injection: post-injection crystal flare (onset within hours, settles in 48\u201372 hours, afebrile); iatrogenic septic arthritis (days later, worsening, fever); underlying crystal or inflammatory flare; haemarthrosis; steroidinduced rapid chondrolysis or \"rapidly progressive OA\" (weeks to months); missed fracture or AVN. Ice and analgesia for a flare. Urgent aspiration for culture if sepsis is suspected. Use aseptic no-touch technique and avoid injecting through infected or broken skin.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":382,"s":"A 58-year-old man with insulin-treated type 2 diabetes is to have a subacromial injection of methylprednisolone 40 mg. What advice is most appropriate?","o":["Avoid the injection entirely, since diabetes is an absolute contraindication","No specific precautions are needed, since this is a local injection only","Reduce the insulin dose by half before the injection, as a precaution","Monitor glucose closely for several days; adjust insulin if needed","Delay the injection until HbA1c is rechecked in 3 months"],"a":3,"e":"Intra-articular and soft-tissue corticosteroids are absorbed systemically and commonly raise blood glucose for up to several days in people with diabetes. Patients should monitor glucose and have a plan to adjust treatment. Larger doses and multiple sites increase the effect. Advise checking capillary glucose 4 times daily for 2\u20135 days and give a correction-dose plan. Expect larger rises with higher doses, multiple sites and long-acting preparations. Extended-release triamcinolone (microsphere) causes less glucose rise. Consider a lower dose or an alternative (e.g. physiotherapy) if control is poor. Warn about facial flushing and transient BP rise.","c":"Drug Monitoring & Safety","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":383,"s":"After a twisting injury, a 27-year-old skier has a tense knee effusion. Aspiration yields blood with fat globules floating on the surface. What does this finding indicate?","o":["Triamcinolone hexacetonide","An intra-articular fracture (lipohaemarthrosis)","ACL rupture, without any bony injury","A bleeding disorder such as haemophilia","Simple synovitis from a meniscal tear, without fracture"],"a":1,"e":"Fat globules in a haemarthrosis indicate marrow fat released through an intra-articular fracture, such as a tibial plateau fracture. A horizontal-beam lateral radiograph may show a fat-fluid level, and CT defines the fracture. Acute haemarthrosis without fat suggests ACL rupture, patellar dislocation or a bleeding disorder. Acute traumatic haemarthrosis: ACL rupture (the commonest in adults), patellar dislocation, osteochondral fracture, peripheral meniscal tear, intra-articular fracture (fat globules, lipohaemarthrosis). Atraumatic haemarthrosis: anticoagulation, haemophilia or von Willebrand disease, PVNS/TGCT, Charcot joint, CPPD or Milwaukee shoulder, haemangioma, scurvy, Ehlers\u2013Danlos. Horizontal-beam lateral radiograph (fat\u2013fluid level), CT for the fracture, MRI for ligaments. Orthopaedic referral.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":384,"s":"A 69-year-old woman with severe knee osteoarthritis is on the waiting list for total knee replacement, expected in 6 weeks. She asks for an intra-articular corticosteroid injection for pain. What is the most appropriate advice?","o":["Proceed with the injection now, since timing before surgery makes no difference to infection risk","Avoid steroid injection within about 3 months of arthroplasty","Injection is absolutely contraindicated at any time before joint replacement","Arrange urgent aspiration first to exclude infection before any injection","Proceed with the injection, since exercise alone has already failed"],"a":1,"e":"Large cohort studies show that intra-articular corticosteroid in the months before hip or knee replacement increases the risk of periprosthetic infection, and most surgeons avoid injections within 3 months of surgery. Analgesia and physiotherapy should be optimised instead. More generally, the same joint should not be injected more often than about every 3 months. Optimise analgesia (topical or oral NSAIDs with gastroprotection), physiotherapy and prehabilitation, weight loss, a walking aid. Genicular nerve block or RFA may bridge selected patients. If an injection is unavoidable for severe symptoms, discuss timing with the surgeon (most defer surgery by \u20213 months).","c":"Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","t":["Osteoarthritis & Mechanical","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":385,"s":"A 4-year-old girl has oligoarticular JIA affecting one knee. ANA is positive and her eyes are white and asymptomatic. What is the most appropriate eye management?","o":["No screening is needed since the eyes are currently asymptomatic","Regular slit-lamp screening by ophthalmology at frequent intervals (for example every 3 months)","Screening only if uveitis symptoms develop","Annual screening only, regardless of ANA status or age of onset","Screening is only required once methotrexate is started"],"a":1,"e":"Young-onset ANA-positive oligoarticular JIA carries the highest risk of chronic anterior uveitis, which is asymptomatic and can cause cataract, glaucoma and band keratopathy. Uveitis occurs in 10\u201320% overall (up to 30% in early-onset ANA+ oligoarthritis): chronic, non-granulomatous, often bilateral, white and painless. About 90% develops within 4 years of onset and up to 10% precedes arthritis. Complications: synechiae, band keratopathy, cataract, glaucoma, macular oedema, amblyopia. Screening intervals: | Risk | Group | Frequency | |---|---|--- | | High | oligo, RF-neg poly, psoriatic or undifferentiated; ANA+; onset <7; duration \u00a34 years | every 3 months | | Medium | same subtypes but ANA-, older onset or longer duration | every 6 months | | Low | systemic, RF+ poly, ERA | yearly (urgent review for red eye) | Treatment: topical GC plus mydriatics. If steroid-dependent (>2 drops/day beyond 3 months) or poor prognostic signs, add methotrexate (SC). If uncontrolled, add adalimumab (SYCAMORE roughly halved treatment failure). Then infliximab, tocilizumab, abatacept, JAKi. Avoid etanercept (ineffective and associated with new uveitis). Stopping adalimumab after 1 year of control led to frequent recurrence (ADJUST), so withdraw cautiously.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":386,"s":"A 13-year-old boy has heel pain with Achilles enthesitis, asymmetrical lower-limb oligoarthritis and an episode of painful red eye. HLA-B27 is positive. Which JIA category is most likely?","o":["Scheuermann's disease","Juvenile psoriatic arthritis","Enthesitis-related arthritis","Systemic JIA","Oligoarticular JIA, extended course"],"a":2,"e":"ERA typically affects boys over 6 with enthesitis, lower-limb arthritis, HLA-B27 and symptomatic acute anterior uveitis. Some develop axial disease later. Adolescent heel pain with arthritis: ERA vs Sever's disease (calcaneal apophysitis), Osgood\u2013Schlatter or Sinding- Larsen\u2013Johansson (knee), reactive arthritis, IBD arthropathy, stress fracture, osteoid osteoma, CRMO, tarsal coalition. Features: lower-limb large joints, tarsitis, heel enthesitis, sacroiliitis in adolescence (juvenile SpA). Uveitis is acute, symptomatic, unilateral and recurrent. NSAIDs first. TNFi or secukinumab for sacroiliitis or enthesitis. Methotrexate does not treat axial disease. Physiotherapy.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Psoriatic Arthritis","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":387,"s":"A 7-year-old with polyarticular JIA needs methotrexate. What is the usual dose and route?","o":["20-25 mg/m\u00b2 weekly, by mouth only","A fixed dose of 7.5 mg weekly regardless of body size","2.5 mg three times weekly, by mouth","Daily low-dose oral methotrexate rather than weekly dosing","10-15 mg/m\u00b2 weekly, with subcutaneous administration preferred at higher doses"],"a":4,"e":"Folic acid reduces adverse effects. Anticipatory nausea is a common practical problem in children. Use methotrexate for polyarthritis from the start, or for oligoarthritis that persists or relapses after injections, extended oligoarthritis, uveitis or poor prognostic features. Manage nausea with SC dosing, antiemetics, distraction and taste-masking, and consider \"methotrexate intolerance severity score\" assessment. Leflunomide is an alternative. Escalate to a biologic after about 3 months of inadequate response, or early in high-risk disease (RF/CCP+, hip, cervical spine or wrist involvement, erosions).","c":"Paediatric Rheumatology","t":["Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":388,"s":"A 6-year-old with oligoarticular JIA has an active knee effusion. Which intra-articular corticosteroid is preferred?","o":["Methylprednisolone acetate","Triamcinolone hexacetonide","Hydrocortisone acetate","An IL-17 inhibitor","Betamethasone, given its shorter duration of action"],"a":1,"e":"Triamcinolone hexacetonide has the longest duration of effect. Sedation or anaesthesia may be needed in young children, and extra-articular leakage can cause subcutaneous atrophy. US-guided injection where possible, under sedation, nitrous oxide or local anaesthesia. Remission lasts months in most knees. Risks: subcutaneous atrophy, periarticular calcification, transient Cushingoid features if multiple joints are injected. For oligoarthritis, IA steroid comes first, then methotrexate if persistent or recurrent, then a biologic if refractory.","c":"Paediatric Rheumatology","t":["Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":389,"s":"A boy with systemic JIA develops persistent fever, hepatosplenomegaly and confusion, with ferritin rising to 12,000 \u00b5g/L while platelets, ESR and fibrinogen fall. What is the diagnosis, and what is first-line treatment?","o":["Sepsis with disseminated intravascular coagulation; broad-spectrum antibiotics alone","Macrophage activation syndrome; high-dose glucocorticoids with IL-1 blockade (anakinra), adding ciclosporin or other agents if refractory","Simple viral hepatitis with reactive hepatosplenomegaly","Catastrophic antiphospholipid syndrome; anticoagulation and plasma exchange","Lymphoma; urgent oncology referral and biopsy"],"a":1,"e":"Rising ferritin with falling platelets, ESR and fibrinogen, and raised transaminases, triglycerides and D-dimer, characterise MAS, a form of secondary HLH. Infection must be sought in parallel. MAS is life-threatening and requires urgent intensive treatment, often with critical care input. Sepsis or DIC, primary HLH (perforin, UNC13D and similar), EBV-HLH, malignancy-associated HLH (lymphoma, leukaemia), Kawasaki, TTP, drug reaction (DRESS), MIS-C, SLE-associated MAS. 2016 classification: a febrile patient with known or suspected Still's and ferritin >684 ng/mL plus any 2 of platelets \u00a3181, AST >48, triglycerides >156 mg/dL (1.76 mmol/L) and fibrinogen \u00a3360 mg/dL. Supportive: soluble CD25, CXCL9, haemophagocytosis on marrow. Ferritin:ESR ratio >21 favours MAS over active Still's. Overt MAS occurs in about 10% of Still's (subclinical in 30\u201340%), at onset, during flares or with infection (EBV). IV methylprednisolone (30 mg/kg/day, maximum 1 g, \u00d7 3) plus high-dose anakinra, with ciclosporin as an adjunct. Emapalumab (anti-IFN-g) is FDA-approved for MAS in Still's when GC fail (monitor for CMV). Rescue: ruxolitinib or etoposide. Supportive: blood products, treat triggers, ICU.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":390,"s":"A 3-year-old boy has 6 days of fever, bilateral non-purulent conjunctivitis, red cracked lips, a polymorphous rash, cervical lymphadenopathy and oedema of the hands. What is the most appropriate treatment?","o":["Oral antibiotics for presumed streptococcal infection","IVIG 2 g/kg with aspirin, ideally within 10 days of fever onset","Oral prednisolone alone, without IVIG","Supportive care only, since the illness is usually self-limiting","IL-1 blockade (canakinumab or anakinra)"],"a":1,"e":"Kawasaki disease is treated to reduce coronary aneurysm risk; corticosteroids are added in high-risk patients. Echocardiography is needed at diagnosis and follow-up. Prolonged fever with rash and conjunctivitis in a child: Kawasaki vs MIS-C (post-COVID; GI symptoms, shock, myocarditis, older children), scarlet fever, staphylococcal or streptococcal toxic shock, measles, adenovirus, EBV, Stevens\u2013Johnson syndrome, DRESS, systemic JIA, leptospirosis, Rocky Mountain spotted fever. Fever \u20215 days plus \u20214 of 5: bilateral non-exudative conjunctivitis; oral changes (red cracked lips, strawberry tongue); polymorphous rash (often perineal); extremity changes (oedema, then periungual peeling at 2\u20133 weeks); cervical node \u20211.5 cm. Incomplete Kawasaki (infants): use laboratory features (CRP, albumin \u00a33 g/dL, anaemia, ALT, platelets \u2021450 after day 7, WCC \u202115, sterile pyuria) plus echo. Coronary aneurysms occur in 15\u201325% untreated and 4\u20135% treated. It is the leading cause of acquired heart disease in children in high-income countries. IVIG 2 g/kg plus aspirin (moderate to high dose until afebrile, then 3\u20135 mg/kg for 6\u20138 weeks). Primary adjunctive GC for high risk (Kobayashi score, infants, aneurysms at diagnosis). IVIG resistance (10\u201320%): second IVIG, GC, infliximab, ciclosporin or anakinra. Giant aneurysms (\u20218 mm): anticoagulation plus aspirin. Echo at diagnosis, 1\u20132 weeks and 4\u20136 weeks. Defer live vaccines for 11 months after IVIG.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":391,"s":"An 11-year-old girl has 4 months of pain in the distal femur and clavicle, with normal blood counts and mildly raised CRP. MRI shows multifocal bone marrow oedema. Biopsy excludes infection and malignancy. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","o":["High-dose bisphosphonate therapy as first-line","BRAF V600E mutation analysis","Broad-spectrum antibiotics for presumed multifocal osteomyelitis","Look for an underlying malignancy","NSAIDs"],"a":4,"e":"Chronic non-bacterial osteomyelitis is autoinflammatory. Bisphosphonates or TNF inhibitors are used for refractory or vertebral disease. Multifocal bone pain in a child: CNO or CRMO vs bacterial osteomyelitis (usually unifocal, fever), leukaemia (cytopenias, LDH, night pain), lymphoma, Langerhans cell histiocytosis, Ewing's sarcoma or neuroblastoma metastases, hypophosphatasia, scurvy (restricted diets, autism), SAPHO (adolescents), Majeed syndrome (LPIN2, with dyserythropoietic anaemia), DIRA (IL1RN). CNO sites: metaphyses of long bones, clavicle, vertebrae and pelvis, symmetric and recurrent. Associated with psoriasis, PPP, acne and IBD. Whole-body MRI maps silent lesions. NSAIDs first. For refractory disease or vertebral involvement (collapse risk): bisphosphonates (pamidronate) or TNFi, with methotrexate or sulfasalazine for some. Monitor with whole-body MRI. musculoskeletal problems","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":392,"s":"A tall 19-year-old man has an aortic root Z-score of 3.1 and bilateral ectopia lentis, with no family history and genetic testing pending. Can Marfan syndrome be diagnosed under the revised Ghent nosology?","o":["Aortic root Z \u20212 plus ectopia lentis is diagnostic","Autosomal dominant COL5A1 or COL5A2 variants","Diagnosis requires a positive FBN1 genetic test regardless of clinical features","Lofgren syndrome","Aortic root Z-score alone, without any other feature, is sufficient"],"a":0,"e":"The revised Ghent nosology replaced the older 1996 criteria (which older texts still describe). It gives cardinal weight to aortic root dilatation and ectopia lentis, and their combination is diagnostic once Loeys-Dietz, Shprintzen-Goldberg and vascular EDS features are excluded. The systemic score is used when one cardinal feature is missing. Marfanoid habitus: Loeys\u2013Dietz, vEDS, Shprintzen\u2013 Goldberg syndrome (craniosynostosis, intellectual disability), homocystinuria, familial thoracic aortic aneurysm syndromes (ACTA2, MYH11), congenital contractural arachnodactyly (Beals; FBN2, crumpled ears), Stickler, MEN2B, Klinefelter syndrome. Revised Ghent nosology (without family history): 1. Aortic root Z \u20212 plus ectopia lentis. 2. Aortic root Z \u20212 plus a causal FBN1 variant. 3. Aortic root Z \u20212 plus systemic score \u20217. 4. Ectopia lentis plus an FBN1 variant known to cause aortic disease. Systemic score items include wrist and thumb signs (3), pectus carinatum (2) or excavatum (1), hindfoot deformity, pneumothorax, dural ectasia, protrusio acetabuli, reduced upper-to-lower segment ratio with increased arm span, scoliosis or kyphosis, reduced elbow extension, facial features, skin striae, myopia >3 dioptres, and MVP. b-blockers or ARBs (losartan); annual echo (with MRI or CT of the whole aorta); prophylactic aortic root surgery (typically at about 5.0 cm, or lower with risk factors); avoid contact and isometric sports and fluoroquinolones; ophthalmology review; pregnancy counselling (dissection risk, especially if root >4.0\u20134.5 cm); screen first-degree relatives.","c":"Pregnancy & Reproductive","t":["Pregnancy & Reproductive","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":393,"s":"A 14-year-old tall boy with long limbs and mild learning difficulty has inferonasal dislocation of both lenses. He recently had an unprovoked DVT. Which diagnosis is most likely?","o":["Homocystinuria (cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency)","Greater trochanteric pain syndrome","Marfan syndrome, given the tall stature and lens dislocation","Rotator cuff-related shoulder pain","Loeys-Dietz syndrome"],"a":0,"e":"Homocystinuria produces a marfanoid habitus with downward (inferonasal) lens dislocation, intellectual disability and thrombosis. In Marfan syndrome, lenses usually dislocate upward and intellect is normal. Plasma total homocysteine is diagnostic, and many patients respond to pyridoxine. Homocystinuria vs Marfan: downward (inferonasal) vs upward (superotemporal) lens dislocation; intellectual disability vs normal intellect; arterial and venous thrombosis; osteoporosis; malar flush. Inheritance is autosomal recessive (CBS). It is included in UK newborn screening. Plasma total homocysteine. Pyridoxine trial (about half respond). Methionine-restricted diet, betaine, B12 and folate. Thromboprophylaxis around surgery and pregnancy. Avoid nitrous oxide anaesthesia (it raises homocysteine).","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":394,"s":"A 22-year-old woman has joint hypermobility, a bifid uvula, widely spaced eyes and tortuous arteries on CT angiography. Her father died of aortic dissection at an aortic diameter of 4.2 cm. Which diagnosis is most likely?","o":["Antisynthetase syndrome","Felty syndrome","Adhesive capsulitis","Loeys-Dietz syndrome","Takayasu arteritis"],"a":3,"e":"Loeys-Dietz syndrome is caused by variants in TGF-beta pathway genes (TGFBR1/2, SMAD3, TGFB2/3). It features hypertelorism, bifid uvula or cleft palate, and widespread arterial tortuosity and aneurysms. Dissection occurs at smaller aortic diameters than in Marfan syndrome, so surveillance and surgical thresholds are lower. Loeys\u2013Dietz features: hypertelorism, bifid uvula or cleft palate, arterial tortuosity and aneurysms throughout the body, craniosynostosis, club foot, translucent skin, early dissection, and allergic or GI eosinophilic disease. Image head to pelvis. Aortic surgery thresholds are lower (about 4.0\u20134.5 cm). ARB or b-blocker; annual MRA from head to pelvis; early surgery; specialist pregnancy care; genetic counselling (AD).","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Pregnancy & Reproductive"]},{"id":395,"s":"A 28-year-old woman with genetically confirmed vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (COL3A1) attends clinic. Which management principle is most appropriate?","o":["Routine catheter angiography for surveillance imaging","Elective prophylactic aortic surgery at routine intervals","Non-invasive imaging, avoid elective arterial procedures, consider celiprolol","Encourage isometric strength training to build vascular resilience","Aggressive surgical management of any arterial aneurysm found"],"a":2,"e":"Vascular EDS carries a risk of arterial, bowel and uterine rupture, and invasive procedures are hazardous because of tissue fragility. Celiprolol reduced arterial events in a randomised trial. Pregnancy is high risk and needs specialist planning. vEDS features: thin translucent skin with visible veins, extensive bruising, characteristic face (thin lips and nose, prominent eyes), acrogeria, hypermobility of small joints only. It causes arterial dissection or rupture, bowel perforation (sigmoid), uterine rupture in pregnancy, pneumothorax, and carotid\u2013cavernous fistula. Median survival is about 50. Celiprolol (BBEST trial). Non-invasive imaging (avoid catheter angiography). An emergency card. MDT care. Avoid isometric exertion and contact sports. Plan pregnancy in a specialist centre (delivery planning; consider caesarean). Surgery is hazardous (tissue friability), so manage conservatively where possible. Genetic counselling.","c":"Pregnancy & Reproductive","t":["Pregnancy & Reproductive","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":396,"s":"A 30-year-old man has hyperextensible, velvety skin, widened atrophic 'cigarette-paper' scars over his shins and generalised joint hypermobility. His mother is similarly affected. Which genetic basis is most likely?","o":["Autosomal dominant COL5A1 or COL5A2 variants","Disseminated gonococcal infection","3 points; he meets the criteria","Prosthetic joint infection","Autosomal recessive TNXB variants"],"a":0,"e":"Classical EDS is autosomal dominant and is usually caused by COL5A1/COL5A2 variants. Some tables wrongly list it as recessive. Classical-like EDS (TNXB) is recessive, and hypermobile EDS has no identified gene and is diagnosed clinically using the 2017 criteria. Classical EDS: marked skin hyperextensibility, wide atrophic \"cigarette-paper\" scars, easy bruising, molluscoid pseudotumours and subcutaneous spheroids, generalised hypermobility, hernias, prolapse. Major criteria are skin hyperextensibility with atrophic scarring plus GJH. Diagnosis needs genetic confirmation. Wound care with careful layered suturing and longer suture retention, protective padding for shins, physiotherapy, vitamin C (some advocate it for bruising), and screening echo for aortic root dilatation (occasionally present).","c":"General Rheumatology","t":["General Rheumatology"]},{"id":397,"s":"A 25-year-old woman has had seven low-trauma fractures since childhood. She has blue-grey sclerae and early conductive hearing loss, and her height is normal. Her father has a similar history. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Non-accidental injury","Idiopathic juvenile osteoporosis","Hypophosphatasia","Osteogenesis imperfecta type I (COL1A1 null allele)","Osteogenesis imperfecta type III"],"a":3,"e":"OI type I is the mildest and most common form. It is autosomal dominant, with blue sclerae, hearing loss beginning in early adulthood and near-normal stature. Bisphosphonates reduce fracture rates in children, and adults need audiology review and fracture-risk management. Recurrent childhood fractures: OI vs non-accidental injury (a key safeguarding differential; metaphyseal corner fractures, posterior rib fractures, fractures of different ages), idiopathic juvenile osteoporosis, hypophosphatasia, rickets, Menkes disease (copper), immobility, GC, leukaemia. Sillence types: | Type | Severity | Features | |---|---|---| | I | mild, commonest | blue sclerae, hearing loss (20s\u201330s), near-normal stature | | II | perinatal lethal | multiple fractures in utero | | III | progressively deforming | short stature, dentinogenesis imperfecta, grey sclerae | | IV | moderate | normal or grey sclerae, variable | | V | IFITM5 | hyperplastic callus, interosseous membrane calcification | Recessive forms involve CRTAP, LEPRE1 and others. Children: IV bisphosphonates (pamidronate, zoledronate) reduce fractures and bone pain. Intramedullary rodding for deformity. Adults: audiology (stapes surgery or hearing aids), fracture-risk management (bisphosphonates, teriparatide or denosumab with specialist input), dental care, physiotherapy. Setrusumab (anti-sclerostin) is in trials.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Paediatric Rheumatology","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":398,"s":"A 13-year-old overweight boy has a 3-week limp and pain in the left knee. On examination, the left hip rotates externally when it is passively flexed, and internal rotation is reduced. What is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Reassurance and discharge, since the knee X-ray is normal","Urgent AP and frog-leg hip radiographs; same-day orthopaedic referral","MRI of the knee alone, without hip imaging","Physiotherapy alone, without any imaging","Education with a progressive gluteal tendon loading exercise programme"],"a":1,"e":"Slipped capital femoral epiphysis often presents with referred knee pain in overweight adolescents. Obligatory external rotation on hip flexion is characteristic. Delay risks further slippage and avascular necrosis. Treatment is surgical fixation. Limp with knee pain in an adolescent: SUFE (overweight, aged 10\u201316, obligatory external rotation on flexion) vs Perthes disease (aged 4\u20138, AVN of the femoral head), septic arthritis or osteomyelitis, transient synovitis (younger), JIA, osteosarcoma (distal femur, proximal tibia), Osgood\u2013Schlatter, patellofemoral pain, osteochondritis dissecans, stress fracture. Always examine the hip in knee pain. Risk factors: obesity, endocrine disease (hypothyroidism, GH therapy, hypogonadism, renal osteodystrophy), Black ethnicity, male sex. It is bilateral in about 20\u201340% (consider prophylactic fixation of the other side in high-risk cases). Klein's line on AP film. An \"unstable\" SUFE (unable to bear weight) carries the highest AVN risk. Make non-weight-bearing immediately and give same-day orthopaedic in-situ screw fixation. Check TFTs if atypical (age <10 or >16, or underweight).","c":"Infection & Immunosuppression","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":399,"s":"A 13-year-old footballer has 2 months of anterior knee pain after training, with a tender, prominent tibial tubercle. There is no effusion and hip examination is normal. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Urgent MRI of the knee to exclude osteochondritis dissecans","Reassure that it is self-limiting; advise load modification and stretching","Joint aspiration to exclude septic arthritis","Referral for surgical excision of the tibial tubercle","Clinically, without imaging"],"a":1,"e":"Osgood-Schlatter disease is a traction apophysitis of the tibial tubercle in active adolescents. It usually settles at skeletal maturity with load management. Enthesitis at multiple sites, or with arthritis or uveitis, should raise the possibility of enthesitis-related arthritis. Anterior knee pain in an adolescent: Osgood\u2013Schlatter (tibial tubercle) vs Sinding-Larsen\u2013Johansson (inferior pole of the patella), patellar tendinopathy, patellofemoral pain syndrome, osteochondritis dissecans, patellar instability, plica, referred hip pain (SUFE), ERA enthesitis, tumour (night pain, swelling). Education and load modification (activity is allowed within pain limits), quadriceps and hamstring stretching, ice, a knee strap, analgesia. Symptoms settle at physeal closure. A persistent ossicle may need excision in adulthood.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":400,"s":"A 5-year-old girl has a limp and a painful right hip. Which combination of features most increases the likelihood of septic arthritis rather than transient synovitis?","o":["Age under 3 years and a normal CRP","A well-appearing child with normal observations","Recent viral illness alone, with a normal ESR","Fever >38.5\u00b0C, non-weight-bearing, ESR >40 mm/h, WCC >12 x 10I/L","Afebrile with a normal WCC and ESR"],"a":3,"e":"Kocher's predictors (fever, non-weight-bearing, raised ESR and raised WCC), together with raised CRP, increase the probability of septic arthritis. With several present, urgent ultrasound-guided aspiration is required. Transient synovitis typically follows a viral illness, and the child is well and afebrile. Painful hip in a young child: transient synovitis (commonest; aged 3\u20138, after a viral illness, afebrile, well), septic arthritis, osteomyelitis (proximal femur, pelvis), Perthes disease, JIA, trauma or NAI, leukaemia, discitis (refuses to walk), psoas abscess, appendicitis. Kocher criteria: fever >38.5 \u00b0C, non-weight-bearing, ESR >40 mm/h, WCC >12 \u00d7 10I/L. Predicted probability of septic arthritis is about 3% with 1 criterion, 40% with 2, 93% with 3 and 99% with 4. Adding CRP >20 mg/L (Caird) improves prediction. Kingella kingae is common under 4 (needs PCR). Ultrasound for effusion, then urgent US-guided aspiration (Gram stain, culture, Kingella PCR) and washout if septic. Transient synovitis: NSAIDs, rest and safety-net advice; review if symptoms persist beyond 1\u20132 weeks (consider Perthes).","c":"Paediatric Rheumatology","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":401,"s":"A 7-year-old boy has episodes of leg pain. Which feature would be inconsistent with benign 'growing pains' and warrant further investigation?","o":["Compression ultrasound of the leg","Bilateral pain confined to the shins, worse at night","Pain relieved by massage, with a normal examination","Intermittent pain that resolves completely by morning","Persistent unilateral pain with a limp and focal bone tenderness"],"a":4,"e":"Growing pains are bilateral, intermittent, nocturnal and leave a normal child by morning. Unilateral or persistent pain, limp, joint swelling, bone tenderness, systemic symptoms or abnormal blood counts suggest infection, malignancy (such as leukaemia or bone tumour) or inflammatory arthritis. Growing pains: aged 3\u201312, bilateral, in the shins, calves or thighs (not joints), evening or night, relieved by massage, no limp, normal examination, and well by morning. Reassurance, massage, stretching, simple analgesia. Investigate red flags with FBC and film, CRP and ESR, LDH, X-ray or MRI, and consider marrow examination if blood counts are abnormal (leukaemia can present with bone pain and arthritis before cytopenias). Consider vitamin D deficiency and benign joint hypermobility.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Paediatric Rheumatology","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":402,"s":"A 17-year-old with a repaired cleft palate has high myopia, a previous retinal detachment, sensorineural hearing loss and early-onset osteoarthritis of the hips. Which diagnosis is most likely?","o":["Marfan syndrome, given the myopia and joint findings","Greater trochanteric pain syndrome","Behcet syndrome","Stickler syndrome (type II collagen disorder)","Hypermobile Ehlers-Danlos syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"Stickler syndrome is most often due to COL2A1 variants. It combines ocular (high myopia, vitreoretinal degeneration, retinal detachment), orofacial (cleft palate, Pierre Robin sequence), auditory and skeletal features, including premature osteoarthritis. Regular ophthalmic surveillance is essential. Stickler syndrome: COL2A1 (type 1; membranous vitreous; commonest), COL11A1 (type 2; beaded vitreous), COL11A2 (non-ocular), and recessive forms. Features: high myopia, vitreoretinal degeneration and retinal detachment, cleft palate or Pierre Robin sequence, midface hypoplasia, sensorineural or mixed hearing loss, hypermobility, spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia, premature OA. Annual ophthalmic review (prophylactic retinopexy or laser to reduce detachment risk); audiology; cleft and airway care; joint protection and early arthroplasty when needed; genetic counselling (AD).","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Osteoarthritis & Mechanical"]},{"id":403,"s":"A young woman has daily spiking fevers, an evanescent salmon-pink rash, sore throat, arthritis, neutrophilia and ferritin 8,000 \u00b5g/L with infection and malignancy excluded. What is the diagnosis, and what is the next treatment if she remains febrile on prednisolone 0.5 mg/kg?","o":["Adult-onset Still's disease; start an IL-1 inhibitor (such as anakinra) or an IL-6 inhibitor, ideally early after diagnosis","IL-1 blockade (canakinumab or anakinra)","Systemic lupus erythematosus with serositis","Lymphoma; refer urgently for biopsy before any immunosuppression","Reactive arthritis following streptococcal pharyngitis"],"a":0,"e":"Adult-onset Still's disease is a diagnosis of exclusion, and very high ferritin is supportive but not specific. Systemic JIA and adult-onset Still's disease are treated as one disease. The 2024 EULAR/PReS recommendations advise starting an IL-1 or IL-6 inhibitor as early as possible after diagnosis to avoid prolonged glucocorticoids, aiming for clinically inactive disease off glucocorticoids by 6 months. MAS must be actively sought. Infection (endocarditis, EBV, CMV, HIV, TB), lymphoma (PET-CT), other malignancy, SLE, vasculitis, DRESS, Schnitzler syndrome, Sweet's syndrome, Kikuchi disease, and in older patients VEXAS and other monogenic or acquired autoinflammatory diseases. Fever \u202139 \u00b0C (quotidian or double-quotidian) with a salmon rash during fever (Koebner phenomenon; atypical persistent pruritic or flagellate lesions occur). Sore throat often precedes fever. Lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly and serositis. Neutrophilia >15,000 (\u202180% neutrophils), abnormal LFTs in about 75%. Glycosylated ferritin \u00a320% plus ferritin >5\u00d7 ULN gives >90% specificity. IL-18 and S100 are very high. RF and ANA are negative. Yamaguchi criteria (\u20215 including \u20212 major, after exclusions; sensitivity 96%); Fautrel criteria (no exclusions needed). Chronic disease causes pericapitate wrist ankylosis. Course: monocyclic (about 30%), polycyclic (about 30%) or chronic articular (35\u201340%; worst prognosis). Methotrexate for chronic arthritis; TNFi for destructive arthritis. Refractory: switch between IL-1 and IL-6, JAKi. MAS (10\u201320%; mortality 10\u201340%): pulse GC plus high-dose anakinra plus ciclosporin; emapalumab. Watch for Still's lung disease and DRESS-like reactions to biologics.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":404,"s":"A young man of Armenian or Mediterranean descent has recurrent 1\u20133-day episodes of fever with peritonitis and pleurisy, and two MEFV mutations. What is the main goal of lifelong colchicine, and what is done if attacks continue (two a month) with raised SAA despite colchicine 2.5 mg daily taken reliably?","o":["To reduce flare frequency only, with no effect on amyloidosis risk","Colchicine prevents AA amyloidosis and reduces attacks; for colchicine-resistant FMF, continue colchicine and add IL-1 blockade","Switch to high-dose NSAIDs instead of escalating colchicine","Add a TNF inhibitor, since IL-1 blockade is ineffective in FMF","Stop colchicine, since IL-1 blockade alone fully prevents amyloidosis"],"a":1,"e":"FMF is an autosomal recessive autoinflammatory disease. Colchicine-resistant FMF (persistent attacks or raised inflammatory markers despite the maximum tolerated dose, up to 3 mg/day in adults) should be treated with IL-1 inhibitors added to colchicine, which continues to protect against amyloidosis. Persistently raised SAA signals a risk of AA amyloidosis, so serum amyloid A and urinary protein are monitored. Recurrent fever with serositis: other periodic fevers (TRAPS has longer attacks; MKD infant onset with nodes), acute intermittent porphyria (no fever), recurrent appendicitis or cholecystitis, endometriosis, IBD, hereditary angioedema (abdominal attacks without fever; low C4), SLE, recurrent idiopathic pericarditis. Exon 10 mutations: M694V is the most severe (amyloid risk); E148Q is low-penetrance and of doubtful significance. About 25\u201330% of patients carry only one mutation. Features: peritonitis (>90%), pleuritis, large lower-limb monoarthritis, erysipelas-like erythema, acute scrotum, exertional leg myalgia, protracted febrile myalgia (responds to GC). Associations: IgA vasculitis, PAN, sacroiliitis. Criteria: Tel-Hashomer or Livneh (adults), Yalcinkaya\u2013Ozen (children). Paediatric colchicine doses: \u00a30.5 mg/day under 5, 0.5\u20131 mg at 5\u201310 and 1\u20131.5 mg over 10; adults up to 3 mg/day. Aim for SAA <10 mg/L. Colchicine is safe in pregnancy and breastfeeding (do not stop for conception in either partner). Watch CYP3A4 and P-gp interactions and statins. Confirm adherence first (resistance affects 5\u201310%). Canakinumab is licensed (CLUSTER), or anakinra. Tocilizumab for AA amyloid refractory to IL-1 blockade.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":405,"s":"A patient with long-standing poorly controlled inflammatory arthritis (for example 30 years of RA) develops nephrotic-range proteinuria. What is the least invasive specific investigation, and what is the key management principle for AA amyloidosis?","o":["Renal biopsy as the first and only test, given the clinical certainty","Abdominal fat aspirate with Congo red staining (with typing and exclusion of AL); suppress the underlying inflammatory driver (target SAA below 10 mg/L) and monitor renal function","Serum amyloid A measurement alone, without any tissue confirmation","Echocardiography, since cardiac amyloid is the dominant concern here","Genetic testing for hereditary amyloidosis as the first-line test"],"a":1,"e":"Abdominal fat aspiration is safe and highly specific, though less sensitive than organ biopsy. Typing (immunohistochemistry or mass spectrometry) distinguishes AA from AL amyloid, and serum free light chains and immunofixation are needed to exclude AL. AA amyloidosis results from sustained serum amyloid A elevation; effective control of inflammation can stabilise or improve renal function. Nephrotic syndrome in a rheumatology patient: AA amyloid; AL amyloid (myeloma or MGUS); membranous nephropathy (SLE class V, gold, penicillamine, NSAIDs, HBV, malignancy, anti-PLA2R); minimal change or FSGS (NSAIDs, lupus podocytopathy); diabetic nephropathy; lupus nephritis; cryoglobulinaemic MPGN. Congo red shows apple-green birefringence. Fat pad sensitivity is 70\u201390% in AA and AL (lower in ATTR). Organs involved: kidney (>90%), GI tract, liver, spleen, adrenals, thyroid; the heart rarely. SAP scintigraphy maps wholebody load. Median survival is now \u202110 years; SAA <4 mg/L gives the best outcome. Treat the cause aggressively. Tocilizumab is especially effective (SAA Rheumatology SCE \u2014 5-option SBA 130 production is IL-6-dependent); also TNFi; IL- 1 blockade in FMF, CAPS and TRAPS; treat infection (TB, bronchiectasis, osteomyelitis); siltuximab or tocilizumab for Castleman disease. ACE inhibitor or ARB, diuretics, dialysis, transplant once inflammation is controlled. Eprodisate and anti-fibril antibodies are unproven. Monitor SAA every 1\u20133 months.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":406,"s":"A man in his 60s has submandibular swelling, autoimmune pancreatitis and retroperitoneal fibrosis with storiform fibrosis, obliterative phlebitis and IgG4+ plasma cells on biopsy. What is first-line treatment, and what maintenance is used after a second relapse below 7.5 mg prednisolone?","o":["Antibiotics alone, since this likely represents chronic sialadenitis","Cyclophosphamide first-line, reserving glucocorticoids for refractory disease","Observation only, since IgG4-related disease is usually self-limiting","Methotrexate monotherapy, without any glucocorticoid","Glucocorticoids first-line; B-cell depletion (rituximab or inebilizumab) for relapse or steroid sparing"],"a":4,"e":"Glucocorticoids induce remission in most patients. Relapse is common on steroid tapering (40\u201360% after withdrawal). A phase 3 trial (MITIGATE) showed that inebilizumab (anti-CD19) markedly reduced flares (by about 87%) and steroid exposure, and it became the first treatment approved for IgG4-RD in some regions in 2025. Rituximab is widely used off-label. Conventional steroid-sparing agents have weaker evidence. Serum IgG4 is neither fully sensitive nor specific, so malignancy and mimics must be excluded. Pancreatic cancer, cholangiocarcinoma, lymphoma, multicentric Castleman disease (high CRP, IL-6, anaemia), Rosai\u2013Dorfman, Erdheim\u2013Chester, GPA (orbit, sinus, meninges), sarcoid, SjD (anti-Ro positive, persistent sicca), PSC, infection. IgG4-RD typically affects men aged 50\u201370; the head and neck phenotype is commoner in women. It is subacute and painless, with rare fever and common atopy. Phenotypes: pancreato-hepatobiliary (sausage pancreas, sclerosing cholangitis); retroperitoneal and aortic; head and necklimited (Mikulicz, K\u00fcttner tumour, orbital pseudotumour, Riedel's thyroiditis, hypophysitis, pachymeningitis); classic Mikulicz with systemic involvement (lung, TIN with low complement). Histology: >10 IgG4+ cells per HPF with an IgG4:IgG ratio >40%. Granulomas or neutrophilic vasculitis argue against it. Serum IgG4 is raised in 60\u201380%; >5\u00d7 ULN is more specific. 2019 ACR/EULAR classification requires \u202120 points. Treat urgently when vital organs are involved: pancreas, bile ducts, kidney, aorta, retroperitoneum with ureteric obstruction, pachymeninges. Asymptomatic lymphadenopathy can be watched. Prednisolone 0.6 mg/kg (30\u201340 mg) for 2\u20134 weeks, then taper over 3\u20136 months; failure to respond should prompt rethinking the diagnosis. Maintenance: low-dose prednisolone 2.5\u20135 mg, MMF, azathioprine or methotrexate, rituximab or inebilizumab. Stent biliary or ureteric obstruction. Established fibrosis responds poorly.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":407,"s":"A 32-year-old woman has painful bilateral ankle periarthritis, erythema nodosum and fever. Chest radiograph shows bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy. What is the most likely diagnosis and outlook?","o":["SAPHO syndrome","Reactive arthritis following a preceding gastrointestinal infection","L\u00f6fgren's syndrome; usually self-limiting, treated with NSAIDs","Tuberculosis with hilar lymphadenopathy, requiring anti-TB therapy","Lymphoma, requiring urgent biopsy of the hilar nodes"],"a":2,"e":"This acute sarcoidosis presentation usually resolves within 6-24 months. In typical cases biopsy is often unnecessary. Erythema nodosum causes: idiopathic, streptococcal infection, sarcoidosis, IBD, drugs (OCP, sulfonamides), TB, Yersinia, Beh\u00e7et's, pregnancy, lymphoma, coccidioidomycosis. Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy: sarcoid, lymphoma, TB, histoplasmosis, berylliosis, metastases. L\u00f6fgren's syndrome: acute fever, bilateral ankle periarthritis (periarticular swelling rather than true synovitis), erythema nodosum and bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy. It is associated with HLA-DRB1*03 and has a good prognosis (>90% remit within 2 years). Biopsy is unnecessary in typical cases. NSAIDs; short low-dose prednisolone if severe; colchicine for arthritis. Baseline sarcoid screen: FBC, calcium, creatinine, LFTs, urinalysis, ECG, eye examination, PFTs with DLCO. Repeat chest imaging to confirm resolution.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":408,"s":"A patient with sarcoidosis and bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy develops a new facial nerve palsy. What is the best approach?","o":["Reassurance only, since facial palsy in sarcoidosis is always benign and self-limiting","Observation with no imaging, since the chest radiograph is already known to be abnormal","Lofgren syndrome","Refer to ENT alone, without any neurological or CSF assessment","Urgent assessment for neurosarcoidosis and other causes, as organ-threatening disease may need systemic treatment"],"a":4,"e":"Neurosarcoidosis needs MRI, CSF analysis and exclusion of infection and malignancy. Facial palsy with BHL: neurosarcoidosis vs Lyme disease, TB meningitis, lymphoma, HIV, GPA, Bell's palsy (coincidental). Bilateral facial palsy suggests sarcoid, Lyme, GBS or HIV. Neurosarcoidosis affects 5\u201310%: facial palsy (Heerfordt's syndrome with parotitis and uveitis), aseptic meningitis, hypothalamic and pituitary disease (DI), myelopathy, smallfibre neuropathy. MRI brain (leptomeningeal enhancement) and CSF (lymphocytosis, raised protein, low glucose; exclude TB, fungi and lymphoma by cytology and flow). GC, then add methotrexate, then infliximab early for severe CNS disease; cyclophosphamide in selected cases. Isolated facial palsy often responds to a short GC course.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":409,"s":"A 58-year-old woman has recurrent bilateral auricular chondritis sparing the lobules, nasal chondritis and new stridor. What is the most serious complication requiring urgent assessment?","o":["Necrotising scleritis","Progressive sensorineural hearing loss alone, without airway risk","Laryngotracheal involvement with airway stenosis or collapse","Saddle-nose deformity alone, which is cosmetic rather than life-threatening","Aortic regurgitation alone, which progresses slowly and rarely needs urgent action"],"a":2,"e":"Relapsing polychondritis can involve the larynx, trachea and bronchi. Dynamic CT and flow-volume loops are needed. Ear inflammation: RP vs infectious perichondritis (Pseudomonas; involves the lobule), trauma, frostbite, cellulitis, insect bite, gout tophus, GPA, VEXAS. Saddle nose: GPA, cocaine, syphilis, leprosy, trauma. Relapsing polychondritis: prevalence 4\u20139 per million; peak onset 40\u201360; F:M about 1:1. About 30% have an associated condition (AAV, RA, SLE, Sj\u00f6gren's, MDS). Features: auricular chondritis in 85\u201390% (spares the lobule; cauliflower ear), nasal chondritis (saddle nose), airway disease in about 50% (subglottic stenosis, tracheobronchomalacia; the leading cause of death), eye 50\u201360%, migratory non-erosive arthritis, costochondritis, cardiovascular disease (AR or MR, aortitis; the second cause of death), audiovestibular damage, GN (poor prognosis). McAdam criteria require \u20213 of 6. Flow\u2013volume loops, dynamic inspiratory and expiratory CT, bronchoscopy, echo, aortic imaging, FDG-PET. Mild disease: NSAIDs, colchicine, dapsone, short GC. Severe disease (airway, eye, heart, kidney, inner ear): pulse GC plus methotrexate, azathioprine or MMF; cyclophosphamide for life-threatening disease. Refractory: TNFi (most data), tocilizumab, abatacept, anakinra, JAKi. Airway management by ENT and respiratory (stent, tracheostomy, CPAP for malacia; intubation is risky). MAGIC syndrome (mouth and genital ulcers with inflamed cartilage) should prompt testing for VEXAS or trisomy 8 MDS.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Crystal Arthropathy","Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":410,"s":"A 67-year-old man has recurrent ear and nasal chondritis, macrocytic anaemia and neutrophilic skin lesions. Bone marrow shows vacuoles in myeloid and erythroid precursors. Which test is most likely to confirm the diagnosis?","o":["68Ga-DOTATATE PET/CT","Anti-topoisomerase I (Scl-70)","Somatic UBA1 mutation testing","Bone marrow trephine biopsy alone, without genetic testing","Anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibody testing alone"],"a":2,"e":"VEXAS syndrome mainly affects men over 50 and features relapsing polychondritis, neutrophilic dermatosis, vasculitis, thrombosis and macrocytic anaemia or MDS. It is often glucocorticoid-dependent. VEXAS (Vacuoles, E1 enzyme, X-linked, Autoinflammatory, Somatic) is caused by somatic UBA1 mutations (p.Met41) and affects about 1 in 4,000 men over 50. Features: relapsing polychondritis (50\u201360%), Sweet's-like dermatosis, vasculitis, pulmonary infiltrates, VTE, macrocytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia, MDS, steroid dependence. Mortality is about 50% at 5\u201310 years. UBA1 sequencing (blood or marrow). GC; JAKi (ruxolitinib is the most effective in cohorts); tocilizumab; azacitidine (especially with MDS). Allogeneic HSCT is the only cure. Give VTE prophylaxis or treatment. Consider VEXAS in any older man with RP, unexplained recurrent inflammation, macrocytosis and steroid dependence.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":411,"s":"A patient with Raynaud's phenomenon, puffy hands, inflammatory arthritis and myositis features has hightitre anti-U1-RNP antibodies. Which organ complication needs active surveillance?","o":["Scleroderma renal crisis, requiring urgent ACE inhibition","Digital ulceration alone, requiring vascular surveillance","Pulmonary arterial hypertension and interstitial lung disease","Cryoglobulinaemic glomerulonephritis, requiring urgent biopsy","Class IV lupus nephritis, requiring induction immunosuppression"],"a":2,"e":"Mixed connective tissue disease overlaps features of SLE, SSc and myositis. PAH is a major cause of death. UCTD (CTD features with ANA for \u20213 years without meeting criteria; 20\u201335% evolve within 5 years, mostly to SLE; predictors are dsDNA, Sm, Scl-70, ACA, homogeneous ANA, an SSc capillaroscopy pattern, serositis, cytopenias, low complement). Overlap syndromes (rhupus, scleromyositis with PM/Scl or Ku, antisynthetase). IPAF (ILD with autoimmune features from \u20212 of clinical, serological and morphological domains; a research classification). MCTD: high-titre anti-U1-RNP (anti-U1-70 kDa is most specific); Raynaud's in >90%, puffy hands, polyarthritis (60\u201390%; can be erosive), mild myositis, ILD (20\u201350%; NSIP), PAH (10\u201325%; the leading cause of death), oesophageal dysmotility, trigeminal neuropathy, aseptic meningitis (sometimes NSAID-triggered). Anti-dsDNA, Sm, Scl-70 and centromere antibodies are absent; their appearance suggests evolution to SLE or SSc. Criteria: Alarc\u00f3n-Segovia, Kasukawa, revised Japanese. HRCT and PFTs at diagnosis; yearly echo, DLCO and NTproBNP for PAH; RHC if suspected. Early MCTD-PAH may partly respond to immunosuppression plus dual vasodilators. Arthritis and myositis: methotrexate and GC. ILD: MMF first. HCQ for mild disease. In pregnancy, test anti-Ro and aPL and avoid pregnancy with PAH.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":412,"s":"A young man has 2-week fever episodes with migratory painful rash over areas of myalgia, periorbital oedema and abdominal pain; his father had similar attacks and developed nephrotic syndrome. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Familial Mediterranean fever, given the recurrent abdominal attacks","TNF receptor-associated periodic syndrome (TRAPS)","Cyclical neutropenia, confirmed by serial blood counts","Lofgren syndrome","Complex regional pain syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"TRAPS (TNFRSF1A, autosomal dominant) causes prolonged attacks, often over a week, with centrifugal migratory rash over myalgia and periorbital oedema. It carries a risk of AA amyloidosis. IL-1 blockade (canakinumab or anakinra) is preferred for maintenance; monoclonal anti-TNF antibodies can paradoxically worsen it. Cysteine mutations are more severe; R92Q and P46L are low-penetrance and milder. Attacks last >7 days (often 1\u20133 weeks), with centrifugal migratory myalgia (fasciitis) and overlying erythema, periorbital oedema, conjunctivitis and abdominal pain. AA amyloid occurs in 10\u201315%. Short GC courses for attacks; IL-1 blockade for maintenance (canakinumab is licensed). Etanercept is partially Rheumatology SCE \u2014 5-option SBA 133 effective. Avoid infliximab and adalimumab (they can paradoxically worsen TRAPS). Colchicine is ineffective. Monitor SAA and urine protein.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":413,"s":"A 3-year-old has had recurrent fever lasting 4-6 days since infancy. Episodes are often triggered by vaccination and accompanied by tender cervical lymphadenopathy, diarrhoea, aphthous ulcers and a maculopapular rash. Which test best supports the diagnosis?","o":["Genetic testing for TNFRSF1A alone, without urinary studies","Serum IgD level alone, as the definitive diagnostic test","Urinary mevalonic acid during an attack, with MVK genetics","A throat swab for group A streptococcus during an attack","NOD2 genetic testing alone, without any urinary or enzyme studies"],"a":2,"e":"Mevalonate kinase deficiency (formerly hyper-IgD syndrome) begins in infancy, and attacks are often triggered by vaccination. Raised IgD is neither sensitive nor specific, contrary to older descriptions. Urinary mevalonic acid during attacks and biallelic MVK variants confirm it. IL-1 blockade (canakinumab) is effective for frequent attacks. MKD spans HIDS (residual enzyme activity 1\u201310%) to mevalonic aciduria (<1%: developmental delay, dysmorphism, cataracts). Attacks are triggered by vaccines, infection and stress, with painful cervical nodes, abdominal pain, diarrhoea, aphthae and rash. There is a risk of MAS (and rarely amyloid). On-demand NSAIDs or GC for mild disease; IL-1 blockade (canakinumab licensed per CLUSTER; anakinra on demand) for frequent attacks. Colchicine and statins are ineffective. HSCT for severe mevalonic aciduria.","c":"Infection & Immunosuppression","t":["Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":414,"s":"A young woman has had a non-itchy urticarial rash since childhood with fever, conjunctivitis and progressive sensorineural deafness; her mother is affected. What is the most appropriate treatment?","o":["Long-term antihistamines, since this is a mast-cell-mediated urticaria","IL-1 blockade (canakinumab or anakinra)","Avoidance of cold exposure alone, as in familial cold urticaria without further treatment","X-linked hypophosphataemia; burosumab","High-dose glucocorticoids alone, without any biologic agent"],"a":1,"e":"Muckle-Wells syndrome is a cryopyrin-associated periodic syndrome caused by gain-of-function NLRP3 variants that drive IL-1-beta release. IL-1 inhibition controls inflammation, can stabilise or improve hearing, and prevents AA amyloidosis. The urticaria-like rash is neutrophilic and does not respond to antihistamines. Chronic non-itchy urticarial rash with fever: CAPS, Schnitzler, urticarial vasculitis, AOSD, SLE, cold urticaria (itchy, mast cell-mediated), PLAID (cold urticaria with antibody deficiency). CAPS phenotypes: FCAS (mild; cold-induced; attacks <24 hours); MWS (near-daily rash, deafness, amyloid about 25%); NOMID/CINCA (neonatal onset, chronic aseptic meningitis, papilloedema, deafness, patellar overgrowth, frontal bossing, developmental delay). Somatic mosaicism occurs in up to a third of NOMID and in late-onset adult CAPS. The rash is neutrophilic urticaria and antihistamineresistant. Anakinra (best CNS penetration; escalate the dose in NOMID), canakinumab or rilonacept, started early to prevent deafness and amyloid. Monitor audiometry, eyes and SAA.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":415,"s":"A 4-year-old has 4-day fevers every 4 weeks with aphthous ulcers, pharyngitis and cervical adenitis, and is well between episodes. Which response would most support the likely diagnosis?","o":["Elevated serum IgD, confirming mevalonate kinase deficiency","Fever resolving promptly after one dose of prednisolone","A biallelic MEFV mutation on genetic testing","Persistently raised inflammatory markers between episodes","Failure to respond to a single dose of glucocorticoid"],"a":1,"e":"PFAPA is the commonest periodic fever syndrome of childhood. Attacks are clock-like, and the child is well between episodes. A single dose of corticosteroid aborts the fever, although it may shorten the interval between attacks. Colchicine or tonsillectomy can reduce recurrence, and it usually resolves by adolescence. Modified Marshall criteria apply. Attacks last 3\u20136 days every 3\u20138 weeks with clockwork regularity; the child is well and grows normally between attacks. It usually resolves by adolescence, though adult cases are recognised. Prednisolone 1\u20132 mg/kg (or 0.5 mg/kg) as a single dose at onset (may shorten intervals). Colchicine prophylaxis (especially in MEFV variant carriers); cimetidine; tonsillectomy (curative in most); anakinra for refractory cases. Eurofever/PRINTO PFAPA criteria help exclude monogenic disease (for example if there are GI symptoms, rash or arthritis).","c":"Paediatric Rheumatology","t":["Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":416,"s":"A 64-year-old man has a chronic non-itchy urticarial rash, recurrent fever, bone pain and a monoclonal IgM paraprotein, unresponsive to antihistamines. What is the diagnosis and treatment?","o":["Schnitzler syndrome; IL-1 blockade (such as anakinra)","A biallelic NLRP3 mutation confirming cryopyrin-associated periodic syndrome","Multiple myeloma, requiring immediate chemotherapy","Waldenstr\u00f6m's macroglobulinaemia, requiring rituximab-based chemotherapy","IL-1 blockade (canakinumab or anakinra)"],"a":0,"e":"Schnitzler syndrome is an acquired autoinflammatory disorder of later life: urticarial rash, monoclonal IgM (or IgG), fever, bone pain and raised inflammatory markers. IL-1 inhibition gives dramatic responses. Patients need monitoring because of the risk of progression to lymphoproliferative disease. Strasbourg criteria: 2 major (urticarial rash plus monoclonal IgM or IgG) plus \u20212 minor (recurrent fever, abnormal bone remodelling or pain, neutrophilic dermal infiltrate, leucocytosis or raised CRP). 15\u201320% progress to Waldenstr\u00f6m's or lymphoma. Bone scintigraphy or MRI shows increased uptake at the distal femur and proximal tibia. Anakinra gives a dramatic, near-universal response; canakinumab and tocilizumab also work. Long-term haematology follow-up of the paraprotein. Risk of AA amyloid if untreated.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":417,"s":"A 9-year-old has livedo racemosa, recurrent fevers, two lacunar strokes and mesenteric aneurysms suggesting polyarteritis nodosa, along with mild hypogammaglobulinaemia. Which test and treatment are most appropriate?","o":["Genetic testing for NLRP3 alone, treating with IL-1 blockade","Antiphospholipid antibody testing alone, treating with anticoagulation","ADA2 enzyme activity and genetics; treat with a TNF inhibitor","Nerve conduction studies alone, treating with physiotherapy","ANCA testing alone, treating with cyclophosphamide"],"a":2,"e":"Deficiency of adenosine deaminase 2 (DADA2) causes early-onset PAN-like vasculitis, lacunar strokes, livedo and immunodeficiency. TNF inhibitors are highly effective at preventing strokes, whereas glucocorticoids and conventional immunosuppressants are less effective. Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is used for severe haematological disease. DADA2 (AR): early-onset PAN-like vasculitis, lacunar strokes (often before age 5), livedo racemosa, hepatosplenomegaly, hypogammaglobulinaemia, cytopenias including pure red cell aplasia, bone marrow failure. TNF inhibitors (etanercept, adalimumab) prevent strokes. Avoid anticoagulants and antiplatelets after strokes (haemorrhage risk). HSCT for the haematological phenotype. Screen siblings. Consider DADA2 in young or familial PAN, and in Sneddon-like presentations.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":418,"s":"A 3-year-old has boggy, painless swelling of the wrists and ankles (tenosynovitis), granulomatous panuveitis and a tan-coloured scaly ichthyosiform rash. A parent had uveitis in childhood. The chest radiograph is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Juvenile idiopathic arthritis with uveitis, unrelated to any rash","Blau syndrome (NOD2-associated granulomatous disease)","Cystic fibrosis with arthropathy","Sarcoidosis, adult-type, with bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy","Langerhans cell histiocytosis with skin and bone involvement"],"a":1,"e":"Blau syndrome (and early-onset sarcoidosis) is caused by gain-of-function NOD2 variants. It presents before age 5 with the triad of granulomatous arthritis or tenosynovitis, uveitis and rash, and the lungs are usually spared. Ocular disease is the main threat, and TNF inhibitors are often needed. Childhood granulomatous disease: Blau, CGD, CVIDassociated granulomas, TB, cat-scratch disease, Crohn's, JIA (non-granulomatous), sarcoid (adult-type is rare in young children). Blau syndrome (familial) and early-onset sarcoidosis (sporadic) are both NOD2 gain-of-function (AD) and present before 5 with a granulomatous triad: boggy synovitis and tenosynovitis with large cystic swelling, panuveitis (sight-threatening), and ichthyosiform or papular rash. Also camptodactyly, fever, vasculitis, hepatic granulomas. The lungs are spared. GC, methotrexate, TNFi (adalimumab or infliximab) for the eyes; ophthalmology surveillance; IL-1 blockade or JAKi for refractory disease.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":419,"s":"A 46-year-old Afro-Caribbean woman with lupus pernio has painful swelling of several fingers. Hand radiographs show 'lace-like' lytic lesions and cysts in the middle and distal phalanges with intact joint spaces. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["SAPHO syndrome","Polyarteritis nodosa","Paget disease of bone","Reactive arthritis","Osseous sarcoidosis"],"a":4,"e":"Bone sarcoid usually affects the phalanges, with a lacy lytic pattern, and strongly correlates with chronic cutaneous disease such as lupus pernio. Asymptomatic lesions need no treatment. Painful or destructive disease is treated with glucocorticoids and steroid-sparing agents such as methotrexate, with TNF inhibitors for refractory cases. Lytic phalangeal lesions: sarcoid, enchondroma, gout (punched-out with overhanging edges), hyperparathyroidism (subperiosteal resorption, acroosteolysis), TB dactylitis, metastasis (lung), osteomyelitis, glomus tumour, PsA acro-osteolysis. Bone sarcoid occurs in up to 15% (phalanges with \"lacelike\" lytic lesions; vertebral or pelvic lytic or sclerotic lesions that mimic metastases on PET or MRI). Muscle granulomas are found in 50\u201380% at biopsy, usually silently. Joints: acute (L\u00f6fgren's) or chronic, oligo- or polyarticular, sometimes destructive; dactylitis. First line: GC (pulmonary 20\u201340 mg, taper over 3\u20136 months; course 6\u201312 months; relapse in a third). Second line: methotrexate (10\u201320 mg/week), azathioprine, MMF, leflunomide. HCQ for skin, joints and hypercalcaemia. Third line: infliximab or adalimumab for refractory lung, cutaneous (lupus pernio), cardiac or neurological disease. Not effective: ciclosporin. Efzofitimod missed its phase 3 primary endpoint. JAKi are promising for skin. Biomarkers: ACE is not diagnostic; sIL-2R and chitotriosidase are better activity markers. Prognosis: spontaneous remission in 60\u201370%; mortality <5%.","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Crystal Arthropathy","Metabolic Bone Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":420,"s":"A 44-year-old man with known pulmonary sarcoidosis reports palpitations and an episode of presyncope. ECG shows new first-degree heart block with right bundle branch block. What is the most appropriate investigation?","o":["Pulmonary function tests alone, without cardiac imaging","Serum ACE level alone, to guide cardiac risk","Chest radiograph alone, repeated serially","Compression ultrasound of the leg","Cardiac MRI and/or FDG-PET with Holter monitoring"],"a":4,"e":"Cardiac sarcoidosis causes conduction block, ventricular arrhythmias and sudden death, and is under-recognised. New conduction abnormalities need cardiac MRI (late gadolinium enhancement) and/or FDG-PET. Treatment includes immunosuppression and, often, device therapy. ACE levels do not guide management. Cardiac sarcoid is clinically evident in about 5% (up to 25% on imaging or at autopsy): conduction block, VT, sudden death, heart failure. Screen everyone with ECG and symptoms, plus Holter and echo if abnormal. CMR (patchy mid-wall or subepicardial LGE, especially basal septum) and cardiac FDG-PET (after a high-fat, lowcarbohydrate preparation). GC plus an early steroidsparing agent Rheumatology SCE \u2014 5-option SBA 136 (methotrexate); infliximab for refractory disease. ICD or pacing for high-degree block or VT (an ICD is often indicated even if the block recovers). Transplant for end-stage disease.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":421,"s":"A man has knee pain, diabetes insipidus, symmetrical long-bone osteosclerosis and 'hairy kidney' perirenal infiltration; biopsy shows CD68-positive, CD1a-negative foamy histiocytes. Which molecular test guides treatment?","o":["BRAF V600E mutation analysis","NSAIDs","Clinically, without imaging","MAPK pathway sequencing only, without BRAF-specific testing","Look for an underlying malignancy"],"a":0,"e":"Erdheim-Chester disease is a histiocytic neoplasm with symmetrical long-bone osteosclerosis, retroperitoneal and perirenal infiltration and diabetes insipidus. About half of patients carry BRAF V600E and respond to BRAF inhibitors such as vemurafenib. Others often have MAPK pathway variants treatable with MEK inhibitors. Histiocytoses: LCH (CD1a+, langerin+, Birbeck granules, lytic lesions), Rosai\u2013Dorfman (emperipolesis), multicentric reticulohistiocytosis, IgG4-RD (retroperitoneal fibrosis), retroperitoneal lymphoma. Erdheim\u2013Chester disease: a non-Langerhans histiocytosis (CD68+, CD1a-, S100 variable) with symmetrical diaphyseal and metaphyseal osteosclerosis of the legs, \"hairy kidney\" perirenal infiltration, \"coated aorta\", retroorbital masses, DI, xanthelasma, right atrial pseudotumour and CNS involvement. BRAF V600E is present in about half. BRAF inhibitors (vemurafenib, dabrafenib) or MEK inhibitors (cobimetinib) for MAPK-pathway variants. Interferon-a historically. Bone scan or PET for staging.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":422,"s":"A woman has a destructive DIP-predominant arthritis and red-brown 'coral bead' papules around the nail folds and on the face. What is the diagnosis, and what must be screened for?","o":["Rheumatoid arthritis, requiring only methotrexate","Multicentric reticulohistiocytosis; underlying malignancy","3 points; he meets the criteria","Psoriatic arthritis mutilans, unrelated to any systemic screening","Gout, confirmed by synovial fluid crystals alone"],"a":1,"e":"Multicentric reticulohistiocytosis combines papulonodular skin lesions (histiocytes and multinucleated giant cells) with an aggressive DIP-predominant arthritis that can progress to arthritis mutilans. Around a quarter of cases are associated with malignancy. Treatment includes methotrexate, bisphosphonates and TNF inhibitors. Screen for malignancy (CT chest, abdomen and pelvis; age-appropriate tests; skin biopsy). Treatment: methotrexate, cyclophosphamide, bisphosphonates, TNFi or tocilizumab, GC; treat the tumour. Early aggressive therapy aims to prevent arthritis mutilans.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Psoriatic Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":423,"s":"After intensive weight training, a man develops woody induration of the forearms and calves with a 'groove sign', sparing hands and face, with no Raynaud's and marked eosinophilia. Which investigation best confirms the diagnosis?","o":["Peripheral blood eosinophil count alone, without tissue sampling","Deep full-thickness biopsy including fascia","Skin biopsy of the epidermis alone, without fascia","Capillaroscopy, since this closely mimics early systemic sclerosis","MRI of the thighs with STIR (fat-suppressed) sequences"],"a":1,"e":"Eosinophilic fasciitis causes fascial inflammation and thickening with prominent eosinophilia. A deep en-bloc biopsy including fascia, or MRI showing fascial enhancement, confirms it. Most patients respond to glucocorticoids, often with methotrexate. Haematological malignancy is an occasional association. Eosinophilic fasciitis (Shulman syndrome) is often triggered by strenuous exercise and sometimes by statins or checkpoint inhibitors. Rapid woody induration of the limbs (\"peau d'orange\", groove sign) spares the hands, feet and face. There is no Raynaud's, capillaroscopy is normal, eosinophilia and hypergammaglobulinaemia are present, and morphoea coexists in about a third. Associations: aplastic anaemia, lymphoma, MDS. MRI (fascial thickening and enhancement) and fullthickness en-bloc biopsy. GC (often 0.5\u20131 mg/kg) plus methotrexate early; MMF, IVIG, tocilizumab, JAKi or sirolimus for refractory disease. Physiotherapy for contractures. FBC surveillance for haematological disease.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":424,"s":"A 30-year-old man has a 2-year history of recurrent atraumatic swelling of the right knee, with blood-stained aspirates. MRI shows nodular synovial proliferation with low signal and 'blooming' on gradient-echo sequences. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Haemophilic arthropathy from an undiagnosed bleeding disorder","Synovial chondromatosis","Spinal tuberculosis (Pott's disease)","Transient osteoporosis of the hip","Diffuse tenosynovial giant cell tumour (PVNS)"],"a":4,"e":"Tenosynovial giant cell tumour (the current name for PVNS) is a CSF1-driven synovial neoplasm. Haemosiderin deposition gives a characteristic 'blooming' artefact on gradient-echo MRI. Treatment is surgical synovectomy. CSF1 receptor inhibitors are now an option for diffuse disease that is not amenable to surgery. Recurrent atraumatic haemarthrosis: TGCT/PVNS, haemophilia or von Willebrand disease, anticoagulation, synovial haemangioma, CPPD, Charcot joint, synovial sarcoma, scurvy. Gradient-echo \"blooming\" (haemosiderin) also occurs in haemophilic arthropathy. Localised (nodular) vs diffuse TGCT; the knee is the commonest site in diffuse disease. CSF1 translocation causes CSF1 overexpression. X-ray may show erosions or cysts with preserved joint space. Surgical synovectomy (open or arthroscopic), with high recurrence in diffuse disease. CSF1R inhibitors (pexidartinib, with hepatotoxicity; vimseltinib) for disease not amenable to surgery. Radiosynovectomy is an adjunct. Arthroplasty for end-stage disease.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":425,"s":"A man being investigated for myelodysplasia develops fever, neutrophilia and tender red plaques; skin biopsy shows a dense neutrophilic infiltrate without vasculitis. What is the most appropriate treatment?","o":["Systemic glucocorticoids, while investigating the marrow","Colchicine alone, reserving glucocorticoids for refractory cases","Broad-spectrum antibiotics alone, pending culture results","Observation alone, since the lesions are expected to resolve spontaneously","Potassium iodide alone, reserving glucocorticoids for treatment failure"],"a":0,"e":"Sweet syndrome (acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis) responds quickly to glucocorticoids, with colchicine, dapsone or potassium iodide as alternatives. It may be idiopathic, drug-induced (for example, by G-CSF) or associated with malignancy, especially AML and MDS. VEXAS syndrome should also be considered in older men with Sweet-like lesions and macrocytosis. Neutrophilic dermatoses: Sweet's, pyoderma gangrenosum, Beh\u00e7et's, bowel-associated dermatosis\u2013 arthritis syndrome, neutrophilic urticarial dermatosis (Still's, CAPS, Schnitzler, SLE), erythema elevatum diutinum, infection (cellulitis, mycobacteria), leukaemia cutis. Sweet's syndrome criteria: abrupt tender red plaques or nodules with a dense neutrophilic infiltrate without vasculitis (major), plus fever, an association (malignancy, infection, IBD, pregnancy, drugs), neutrophilia and raised ESR, and a rapid GC response. Associations: AML, MDS, G- CSF, azacitidine, ATRA, vaccination, IBD, Beh\u00e7et's, VEXAS. Prednisolone 0.5\u20131 mg/kg (rapid response). Alternatives: colchicine, dapsone, potassium iodide, ciclosporin, IL-1 blockade. Treat the underlying cause, and do FBC, film and marrow examination in older patients. Check for macrocytosis and UBA1 in older men.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Infection & Immunosuppression","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":426,"s":"A woman with ulcerative colitis has a rapidly enlarging, very painful leg ulcer with a violaceous undermined border that began after minor trauma. What is the key management principle?","o":["Early surgical debridement to remove necrotic tissue","Compression bandaging alone, without any systemic treatment","Topical antibiotics alone, since this likely represents a secondary skin infection","Avoid debridement (pathergy); give systemic immunosuppression","Urgent vascular surgery referral for revascularisation"],"a":3,"e":"Pyoderma gangrenosum is a neutrophilic dermatosis associated with IBD, inflammatory arthritis and haematological disease. Surgical trauma can worsen it through pathergy. First-line treatment for extensive disease is systemic glucocorticoids or ciclosporin, with TNF inhibitors as effective options, particularly with IBD. Leg ulcers in rheumatology patients: pyoderma gangrenosum (IBD, RA, PsA, myeloma or IgA paraprotein, AML, PAPA), vasculitic ulcers (RA vasculitis, PAN, cryoglobulinaemia, AAV), Felty's leg ulcers, APS or livedoid vasculopathy, calciphylaxis, venous or arterial ulcers, infection (atypical mycobacteria, fungi, ecthyma gangrenosum), malignancy (SCC), hydroxyurea ulcers, Martorell ulcer (hypertensive). PG is a diagnosis of exclusion (Delphi or PARACELSUS criteria). Biopsy the edge to exclude infection and malignancy, and expect pathergy. Local: potent topical steroid or tacrolimus, moist dressings, no sharp debridement. Systemic: prednisolone or ciclosporin (STOP GAP trial showed equivalence). TNFi (infliximab, adalimumab), especially with IBD; IL-1, IL-23 or IL-17 blockade, JAKi or IVIG for refractory disease. Treat the associated disease. Plan any surgery with immunosuppression cover. malignancy","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":427,"s":"A man with long-standing type 1 diabetes cannot press his palms flat together and has thick, waxy finger skin, without Raynaud's and with normal capillaroscopy. What is the diagnosis?","o":["The A1 pulley at the metacarpal head","Osteosarcoma arising in pagetic bone","Systemic sclerosis, limited cutaneous subtype","Diabetic cheiroarthropathy (limited joint mobility)","Scleredema diabeticorum"],"a":3,"e":"Limited joint mobility in diabetes produces the 'prayer sign' and waxy skin thickening from glycation of connective tissue. It is associated with microvascular complications. Absent Raynaud's, normal capillaroscopy and no autoantibodies distinguish it from systemic sclerosis. Limited hand mobility with thick skin: diabetic cheiroarthropathy vs SSc (Raynaud's, capillaroscopy, antibodies), scleredema diabeticorum (upper back), Dupuytren's, palmar fasciitis and polyarthritis (paraneoplastic, rapid onset), eosinophilic fasciitis (spares hands), hypothyroid myxoedema, amyloid, stiff skin syndrome, chronic GVHD. Optimise glycaemic control, stretching and hand therapy. Screen for microvascular complications (retinopathy, nephropathy), which are associated.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Metabolic Bone Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":428,"s":"A woman with diabetic neuropathy has a warm, swollen, red midfoot, 3\u00b0C warmer than the other foot, with no ulcer and normal radiographs. What is the most appropriate immediate management?","o":["Oral antibiotics for presumed cellulitis, without any offloading","Urgent surgical debridement, given the suspicion of deep infection","Reassurance and routine follow-up in 2 weeks, since radiographs are normal","Compression bandaging alone, continuing normal weight-bearing","Acute Charcot foot: immediate offloading in a total contact cast and urgent MDT referral"],"a":4,"e":"Acute Charcot foot can present with normal radiographs. A temperature difference over 2\u00b0C in a neuropathic foot should be treated as Charcot until proven otherwise. Immediate offloading prevents collapse and rocker-bottom deformity. MRI shows early bone marrow oedema, and infection is unlikely without an ulcer. Warm swollen neuropathic foot: acute Charcot vs cellulitis, osteomyelitis (usually with an ulcer; forefoot), gout, DVT, fracture, septic arthritis. Other neuropathic arthropathies: tabes dorsalis (knee), syringomyelia (shoulder, elbow), leprosy, alcohol, congenital insensitivity to pain, amyloid neuropathy. Charcot occurs in 0.1\u20131% of people with diabetes, mostly at the midfoot (Lisfranc), then the ankle. The temperature difference is >2 \u00b0C. X-ray progresses through the \"6 Ds\": distension, density, debris, disorganisation, dislocation, destruction. Rocker-bottom deformity follows. Total contact cast or removable walker until the temperature difference is <2 \u00b0C (often 3\u20136+ months), then custom footwear. Diabetic foot MDT. Surgery for unstable deformity. Bisphosphonates have limited evidence.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":429,"s":"A 49-year-old man has bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome, knee pain, increasing shoe and ring size and coarse facial features. Hand radiographs show widened MCP joint spaces and the heel pad is thickened. Which test is most appropriate first?","o":["Anti-Jo-1","MRI of both hips","CT of the chest","High-resolution CT chest","Serum IGF-1"],"a":4,"e":"Acromegalic arthropathy causes cartilage hypertrophy with widened joint spaces, then secondary OA. Carpal tunnel syndrome is common. Serum IGF-1 is the screening test (older texts suggested a skull radiograph), confirmed by failure of GH suppression on an oral glucose tolerance test and by pituitary MRI. Acromegaly arthropathy affects about 70%: early joint space widening (cartilage hypertrophy), then premature OA with large osteophytes. Also CTS, Raynaud's, proximal myopathy, back pain with DISH-like changes and posterior vertebral scalloping, spade-like hands, heel pad >22\u201323 mm, prognathism, sleep apnoea, cardiomyopathy, colonic polyps. IGF-1 (screen), then OGTT GH suppression (confirm), then pituitary MRI. Transsphenoidal surgery, somatostatin analogues, pegvisomant. Joint changes are only partly reversible.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":430,"s":"A 61-year-old woman has 4 months of proximal muscle aching, cramps and weakness, with slow-relaxing ankle reflexes. CK is 1,400 U/L. She is not taking a statin. What is the most important next investigation?","o":["Anti-Jo-1","Thyroid function tests","MRI of both hips","68Ga-DOTATATE PET/CT","Anti-SRP"],"a":1,"e":"Hypothyroid myopathy can mimic inflammatory myositis, with raised CK and proximal weakness, and is reversible with thyroxine. It should be excluded before invasive investigation for myositis. Muscle hypertrophy with stiffness (Hoffmann's syndrome) is a rare variant. Raised CK with weakness: myositis, hypothyroidism, statin or other drug myopathy (colchicine, HCQ, zidovudine, alcohol), muscular dystrophy, metabolic myopathy (McArdle, Pompe), hypokalaemia, hypophosphataemia, rhabdomyolysis, exercise, IM injection, macro-CK, African ancestry (higher baseline CK), cardiac troponin confusion. Hypothyroid myopathy occurs in 30\u201380% of untreated patients: proximal weakness, cramps, myalgia, slowrelaxing reflexes, CK 2\u201310\u00d7 (sometimes higher). EMG is near-normal, MRI shows little oedema, and biopsy shows type II atrophy without inflammation. Hypothyroidism also raises statin myopathy risk. ANA and RF are often positive in autoimmune thyroid disease without CTD. Levothyroxine (myopathy resolves over weeks to months). Recheck CK and strength. Investigate for myositis only if weakness persists when euthyroid.","c":"Connective Tissue Disease","t":["Connective Tissue Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":431,"s":"A 45-year-old man has early large-joint osteoarthritis, disc calcification, blue-black ear cartilage and urine that darkens on standing. Which treatment targets the metabolic defect?","o":["Allopurinol, to reduce urate-driven cartilage deposition","A TNF inhibitor","Colchicine, to reduce pigment deposition","Nitisinone","Clinically, without imaging"],"a":3,"e":"Alkaptonuria (homogentisate dioxygenase deficiency) causes ochronosis: homogentisic acid deposits in cartilage, causing pigmentation and premature arthropathy. Nitisinone inhibits an upstream enzyme and markedly lowers homogentisic acid, slowing progression. Alkaptonuria (AR, HGD deficiency): dark urine on standing or alkalinisation, blue-black pigmentation of the ear and scleral cartilage, disc calcification with fusion (mimics AS), early spinal and large-joint arthropathy, tendon rupture, aortic stenosis, renal and prostatic stones. Diagnose with urinary homogentisic acid. Nitisinone (SONIA 2 trial; it raises tyrosine, so use a lowprotein diet and eye monitoring for keratopathy). Joint replacement, physiotherapy, cardiac surveillance.","c":"Crystal Arthropathy","t":["Crystal Arthropathy","Osteoarthritis & Mechanical"]},{"id":432,"s":"A 19-year-old man with severe haemophilia A has an acutely painful, warm, swollen right knee after minor trauma. What is the most appropriate immediate management?","o":["Aspiration of the joint as the first step, before any factor treatment","Oral NSAIDs alone, without factor replacement","Compression bandaging alone, deferring factor replacement until swelling increases","Observation only, since haemarthrosis in haemophilia typically resolves spontaneously","Immediate factor VIII replacement with rest, ice and analgesia"],"a":4,"e":"Prompt factor replacement is the key treatment for haemarthrosis in haemophilia. Aspiration is not routine and is done only with factor cover if infection or a tense effusion is suspected. Recurrent bleeds lead to synovitis and haemophilic arthropathy, so prophylaxis is important. Recurrent haemarthroses cause synovitis and haemophilic arthropathy (knees, elbows, ankles), with \"target joints\" and pseudotumours. X-ray shows epiphyseal overgrowth, squared patella and widened intercondylar notch. Factor replacement or emicizumab prophylaxis, physiotherapy, radiosynovectomy for chronic synovitis, arthroplasty with factor cover. Avoid NSAIDs (use COX-2 cautiously); avoid IM injections. HCV and HIV screening in older patients (historical transfusion).","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":433,"s":"A teenager with sickle cell disease has fever and a painful swollen tibia. Apart from Staphylococcus aureus, which organism characteristically causes osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease?","o":["Scheuermann's disease","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Gram stain and bacterial culture","An IL-17 inhibitor","Salmonella species"],"a":4,"e":"Functional hyposplenism and bowel infarction predispose people with sickle cell disease to Salmonella osteomyelitis, although S. aureus remains common. Bone infarction is far more frequent than osteomyelitis, so blood cultures and, where possible, bone or subperiosteal aspirate help guide therapy. Bone pain in sickle cell disease: vaso-occlusive bone infarction (far commoner) vs osteomyelitis (Salmonella, S. aureus), septic arthritis, AVN of the femoral or humeral head, dactylitis (hand\u2013foot syndrome in infants), gout (increased turnover). Blood cultures and MRI (osteomyelitis vs infarct is difficult to separate), with aspirate or biopsy where feasible. Empirical cover for Salmonella and S. aureus (for example a third-generation cephalosporin plus an antistaphylococcal agent). Treat 6 weeks or more.","c":"Psoriatic Arthritis","t":["Psoriatic Arthritis","Crystal Arthropathy","Infection & Immunosuppression","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":434,"s":"A 67-year-old man has back pain, weight loss, anaemia, hypercalcaemia, raised creatinine, normal ALP and a vertebral compression fracture. What is the most appropriate next investigation?","o":["Compression ultrasound of the leg","Whole-body isotope bone scan","MRI of both hips","Serum protein electrophoresis, immunofixation and free light chains","Somatic UBA1 mutation testing"],"a":3,"e":"Anaemia, hypercalcaemia, renal impairment and bone lesions suggest myeloma. ALP is usually normal and bone scans are often falsely negative, because myeloma lesions are purely lytic. Serum protein electrophoresis, immunofixation and free light chains are the key tests, followed by whole-body low-dose CT or MRI. Back pain with hypercalcaemia and anaemia: myeloma, metastases (breast, prostate, lung, kidney), lymphoma or ATLL, hyperparathyroidism with osteoporotic fracture, sarcoid with vertebral fracture, TB spondylitis. CRAB: calcium >2.75 mmol/L (or >0.25 above ULN), renal (creatinine >177 \u00b5mol/L or CrCl <40), anaemia (Hb <100 g/L), bone (\u20211 lytic lesion \u20215 mm). SLiM: \u202160% marrow plasma cells, FLC ratio \u2021100, >1 MRI focal lesion. SPEP + immunofixation + FLC detects >97%. Renal disease is mainly cast nephropathy, plus AL or LCDD and Fanconi syndrome. Whole-body low-dose CT (has replaced the skeletal survey), PET-CT or whole-body MRI, bone marrow with FISH, R2-ISS staging. Urgent haematology referral. Hydrate and avoid NSAIDs and contrast. Bisphosphonate or denosumab for all treated patients. Vertebral augmentation for painful fractures. Daratumumab-based quadruplets; CAR-T and bispecifics for relapse.","c":"Metabolic Bone Disease","t":["Metabolic Bone Disease","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":435,"s":"A 63-year-old smoker has painful swelling of the ankles and wrists, finger clubbing, and tenderness over the distal tibiae. Radiographs show smooth periosteal new bone along the tibial and fibular diaphyses. What is the most appropriate next investigation?","o":["68Ga-DOTATATE PET/CT","CT of the chest","MRI of both hips","Compression ultrasound of the leg","High-resolution CT chest"],"a":1,"e":"Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy (clubbing, periostitis and arthropathy) is strongly associated with intrathoracic malignancy, particularly non-small cell lung cancer, and also with chronic lung suppuration and cyanotic heart disease. Bone scintigraphy shows parallel 'tram-line' cortical uptake. Symptoms may improve after tumour resection. Secondary HOA causes: NSCLC (commonest), mesothelioma, metastases; bronchiectasis, CF, lung abscess, ILD; cyanotic heart disease, infective endocarditis; IBD, cirrhosis, coeliac disease; thyroid acropachy (Graves'); localised forms (infected vascular grafts, aneurysms). Clubbing without periostitis: the same list. Primary HOA (pachydermoperiostosis; HPGD or SLCO2A1; adolescent boys) features pachydermia, cutis verticis gyrata and hyperhidrosis. Secondary HOA (about 90% of adult cases) is caused by megakaryocyte or platelet clumps bypassing the lung, releasing VEGF and PDGF, plus raised PGE2. Bone pain is worse on dependency and eased by elevation. Synovial fluid is non-inflammatory. Treat the cause (tumour resection can produce dramatic improvement). NSAIDs or COX-2 inhibitors. IV bisphosphonates (pamidronate, zoledronate) for pain. Octreotide or anti-VEGF in refractory cases.","c":"Investigations & Imaging","t":["Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":436,"s":"A 79-year-old man develops sudden, symmetrical synovitis of both wrists and hands with marked pitting oedema of the dorsum of both hands. RF and anti-CCP are negative. He improves rapidly on low-dose prednisolone. Which additional step is most appropriate?","o":["Look for an underlying malignancy","Nerve conduction studies of both hands","A further, longer course of oral prednisolone without any screening","A short course of oral prednisolone","Anticoagulation, given the pitting oedema"],"a":0,"e":"RS3PE (remitting seronegative symmetrical synovitis with pitting oedema) typically affects older men and responds well to low-dose glucocorticoids. It can be paraneoplastic, with solid tumours and haematological malignancies reported. A poor steroid response or systemic features should prompt a search for cancer. Elderly-onset symmetrical synovitis with oedema: RS3PE vs PMR with distal oedema (overlapping), elderly-onset RA (ACPA), CPPD, PsA, SpA, paraneoplastic arthritis, DPP-4 inhibitor-associated RS3PE, heart failure, nephrotic syndrome, hypothyroidism. Prednisolone 10\u201315 mg gives a rapid response (taper over months). Screen for malignancy (CT, SPEP, PSA, ageappropriate tests), especially with a poor steroid response, constitutional symptoms or very high ESR. HCQ for steroid sparing. Associated cancers are found in up to about 25%.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":437,"s":"A patient on pembrolizumab (for example for melanoma, tumour responding) develops seronegative inflammatory polyarthritis that persists and limits function despite NSAIDs. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Stop the checkpoint inhibitor immediately and start cyclophosphamide","High-dose glucocorticoids alone, continued indefinitely at induction dose","Permanently discontinue the checkpoint inhibitor regardless of tumour response","Glucocorticoids at the lowest effective dose (moderate-dose prednisolone), usually continuing immunotherapy in discussion with oncology, with an early csDMARD or a biologic (TNF or IL-6 inhibition) if steroid- dependent","Observation only, since arthritis from immunotherapy always resolves spontaneously"],"a":3,"e":"Immune checkpoint inhibitor-induced arthritis is often persistent. Rheumatology input, glucocorticoids at the lowest effective dose and early steroid-sparing csDMARDs are recommended; TNF or IL-6 receptor inhibitors are options for refractory disease. Close collaboration with oncology aims to control arthritis while continuing cancer immunotherapy where possible, and immunotherapy can usually continue for arthritis that is not life-threatening, through shared decision with oncology. New arthritis during cancer therapy: ICI arthritis vs aromatase inhibitor arthralgia, paraneoplastic arthritis, bone metastases, G-CSF bone pain, crystal arthritis (tumour lysis), septic arthritis (neutropenia), BRAF or MEK inhibitor arthralgia. Rheumatic irAEs affect about 5\u201310% of ICI recipients: RAlike, PMR-like or SpA-like arthritis (usually seronegative, sometimes persisting after stopping the ICI), sicca, myositis (with myocarditis and myasthenia), GCA-like vasculitis, sarcoid-like reactions, lupus-like disease. Anti-CTLA-4 plus anti-PD-1 combinations carry the highest risk. Pre-existing autoimmune disease is not an absolute contraindication, but flares are common. Grade by CTCAE. Grade 1: NSAIDs, IA steroid. Grade 2: prednisolone 10\u201320 mg. Grade 3\u20134: 0.5\u20131 mg/kg, hold the ICI, then steroid-sparing methotrexate, SSZ or HCQ, then IL-6R or TNFi. Check troponin and CK if myalgia or weakness occurs (3M overlap). Screen for HBV, HCV and TB before biologics.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Vasculitis","Crystal Arthropathy","Connective Tissue Disease","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":438,"s":"A 59-year-old woman develops rapidly progressive bilateral palmar thickening with flexion contractures of the fingers ('woody hands') and an inflammatory polyarthritis. Nailfold capillaroscopy is normal. Which investigation is most important?","o":["Pelvic imaging and CA-125 (ovarian cancer)","Nailfold capillaroscopy repeated at 3 months","Anti-MDA5","Anti-Scl-70 and anti-centromere antibody testing","Rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibody testing"],"a":0,"e":"Palmar fasciitis and polyarthritis syndrome is a paraneoplastic disorder strongly associated with ovarian carcinoma, and also with other adenocarcinomas. It can precede the diagnosis of cancer and responds poorly to immunosuppression. It can resemble Dupuytren's disease or scleroderma, but onset is rapid and inflammatory. PFPAS (palmar fasciitis and polyarthritis syndrome): rapid \"woody\" palmar thickening with flexion contractures, polyarthritis, swollen hands, nodules. It is strongly associated with ovarian cancer, and also pancreatic, breast, lung and gastric adenocarcinoma. It may precede the cancer and responds poorly to immunosuppression. Pelvic and transvaginal ultrasound, CA-125, CT chest, abdomen and pelvis or PET-CT. Treating the tumour may improve the hands. Hand therapy. Other paraneoplastic syndromes to remember: carcinomatous polyarthritis, panniculitis\u2013arthritis\u2013pancreatitis syndrome (lipase, pancreatic acinar carcinoma), Lambert\u2013Eaton (VGCC; SCLC), erythromelalgia (MPN), paraneoplastic Raynaud's or digital ischaemia.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Connective Tissue Disease","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":439,"s":"A woman with inflammatory arthritis plans pregnancy in the next year. What is the best overall plan?","o":["Switching to a biologic DMARD only after conception is confirmed","Continuing all current medications unchanged until pregnancy is confirmed","Stopping all DMARDs now, restarting only after delivery","Delaying conception until disease activity scores reach zero on current therapy","Preconception counselling aiming for low disease activity on pregnancy-compatible medicines for several months before conception"],"a":4,"e":"Active disease itself increases risks such as pre-eclampsia, preterm birth and growth restriction, so planned transition to compatible therapy before conception is ideal. Why outcomes go wrong in inflammatory arthritis pregnancy: active disease (the main driver; DAS28-CRP >3.2), prednisolone >7.5 mg, NSAID-related subfertility, unplanned conception on a teratogen, undiagnosed aPL or anti-Ro, unrecognised cervical spine instability (anaesthesia), postpartum flare after stopping treatment. RA improves in about 50\u201365% (more in seronegative disease) but often with moderate residual activity; 40\u201360% flare within 3\u20136 months postpartum. axSpA usually does not improve (worse night pain in the second trimester; 50\u2013 80% flare postpartum). Psoriasis often improves; PsA behaves more like RA. Time to pregnancy is longer with active disease, NSAIDs and prednisolone >7.5 mg/day; methotrexate does not prolong it once stopped. 1. Before conception: treat to remission; switch MTX or leflunomide to sulfasalazine and/or HCQ \u00b1 a TNF inhibitor (certolizumab if needed throughout); screen aPL and anti-Ro/La where relevant; folic acid; rubella and varicella immunity; review cervical spine in longstanding RA. 2. During pregnancy: assess activity each trimester with DAS28-CRP (ESR rises physiologically); avoid NSAIDs late; lowest-dose prednisolone or intra-articular steroid for flares. 3. Third trimester: anaesthetic review (cervical spine, hips, lumbar ankylosis). 4. Postpartum: review at 6\u201312 weeks and restart treatment promptly; breastfeeding does not worsen RA. Disease activity, not the diagnosis, drives risk of pre-eclampsia, preterm birth, SGA and caesarean section. A Danish registry linked miscarriage to younger women with recently diagnosed (active) RA. Continuing TNF inhibitors late in pregnancy, or restarting promptly, lowers postpartum flare risk.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Psoriatic Arthritis","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":440,"s":"Which DMARDs are generally considered compatible with pregnancy?","o":["Methotrexate at low dose throughout pregnancy, given its long safety record","Leflunomide without washout, since levels fall naturally in pregnancy","Hydroxychloroquine, sulfasalazine and azathioprine","Mycophenolate mofetil, given its established pregnancy safety data","JAK inhibitors, given their targeted mechanism and short half-life"],"a":2,"e":"The 2024 EULAR recommendations also list colchicine, ciclosporin and tacrolimus as compatible, and TNF inhibitors can be used throughout pregnancy; prednisolone is tapered to 5 mg/day or less where possible. Sulfasalazine should be given with folic acid 5 mg daily. Not compatible: methotrexate, leflunomide (unless washed out), mycophenolate, cyclophosphamide (except lifethreatening disease after the first trimester), JAK inhibitors, thalidomide, warfarin (switch before week 6), ACE inhibitors and ARBs, endothelin receptor antagonists (bosentan, ambrisentan, macitentan). Limited data: rituximab, tocilizumab, abatacept, anakinra, IL-17 and IL-23 inhibitors, belimumab \u2014 use only if no compatible alternative controls disease. Compatible in pregnancy (2024 EULAR and the course agree): HCQ, sulfasalazine (with folic acid 5 mg), azathioprine (\u00a32 mg/kg), ciclosporin, tacrolimus, colchicine, IVIG, LMWH, low-dose aspirin, prednisolone and TNF inhibitors. Prednisolone target: the source explanation cites tapering to \u00a35 mg/day where possible; the EULAR course table gives \u00a37.5\u201310 mg/day. Long-term doses above 10\u201315 mg/day raise GDM, hypertension, preeclampsia, PROM, infection and preterm birth. Use prednisolone (not dexamethasone or betamethasone) for maternal disease, because placental 11b-HSD2 inactivates most of it. Give stress-dose hydrocortisone at delivery after prolonged use above 5 mg/day. IV methylprednisolone pulses are acceptable for severe flares. Among biologics with limited data, anakinra and rituximab (given before conception) have the most experience. Belimumab may be continued in SLE when needed for disease control; registry data are reassuring. Accidental first-trimester biologic exposure is not a reason to terminate.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":441,"s":"A 30-year-old woman with RA on methotrexate 20 mg weekly plans to conceive. When should methotrexate be stopped?","o":["Immediately upon a positive pregnancy test, with no lead time","At least 1 month before conception, continuing folic acid","Before conception, after the post-treatment contraception interval in the product information","Only after a formal washout procedure with cholestyramine","There is no need to stop, provided folic acid is continued"],"a":1,"e":"Methotrexate is teratogenic and is stopped before conception; UK (BSR) guidance advises at least 1 month before, replaced by a compatible drug, and continuing folic acid. Inadvertent first-trimester exposure warrants stopping, high-dose folic acid and specialist fetal assessment. Timing advice varies: BSR \u2014 stop \u20211 month before conception; 2024 EULAR and the EULAR course \u2014 stop 1\u20133 months before. Many clinicians use 3 months as the cautious figure. Continue high-dose folic acid (5 mg daily) through the first trimester. Methotrexate is teratogenic and abortifacient (aminopterin or methotrexate embryopathy: craniofacial, limb and CNS anomalies; miscarriage). This is why pregnancy is its absolute contraindication. Relative contraindications or cautions include moderate renal impairment (dose reduction), heavy alcohol use, significant liver disease, active serious infection, and pre-existing significant lung disease; older age alone is not a contraindication. Severe renal impairment (eGFR <30) is usually treated as a contraindication. Plan the switch (sulfasalazine, HCQ, TNF inhibitor) and confirm control before stopping contraception. Inadvertent exposure: stop the drug immediately; folic acid 5 mg; early fetal assessment and detailed anomaly scan; report to a pregnancy registry; switch to a compatible drug to prevent flare. Absolute malformation risk with low-dose MTX exposure is about 6\u201310% vs a background of 3\u20134%, often lower than patients fear \u2014 exposure is not by itself an indication for termination.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":442,"s":"A 34-year-old man with PsA on methotrexate 15 mg weekly asks whether he must stop it before trying for a baby. What is the correct advice?","o":["Paternal low-dose methotrexate exposure is considered compatible with conception","Switch to leflunomide, which is considered safer for male fertility","Reduce to 7.5 mg weekly but continue trying to conceive","At least 1 month before conception, continuing folic acid","Stop methotrexate now and wait 3 months before trying to conceive"],"a":0,"e":"The 2024 EULAR recommendations state that men trying to conceive may continue methotrexate at 25 mg/week or less; data show no increase in miscarriage or malformations. Cyclophosphamide is the main drug where paternal exposure is a concern (dose-related irreversible infertility). Male subfertility in rheumatic disease: sulfasalazine oligospermia, cyclophosphamide gonadotoxicity, active inflammation, erectile dysfunction (vascular disease, depression, drugs), hypogonadism from chronic glucocorticoids or opioids, varicocele, Beh\u00e7et's epididymoorchitis, mumps orchitis, unrelated female-partner factors. Paternal exposure \u2014 EULAR course summary: Drug Advice for fathers Stop \u20213 months before Cyclophosphamide conception; offer sperm banking Can be continued (2024 Methotrexate EULAR: \u00a325 mg/week); no increase in miscarriage or malformations Mycophenolate Can be continued Azathioprine, HCQ, TNF Can be continued inhibitors, most biologics Can be continued; reversible Sulfasalazine oligospermia (recovers ~3 months after stopping) Limited data, no harm signal JAK inhibitors (2024 EULAR suggests considering a switch) Thalidomide Condoms required Reassure for most drugs. If conception is delayed beyond 12 months on sulfasalazine, perform semen analysis, consider switching, and investigate both partners. Offer sperm banking before cyclophosphamide.","c":"Vasculitis","t":["Vasculitis","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":443,"s":"A 31-year-old woman on leflunomide wishes to conceive within 6 months. What is the most appropriate advice?","o":["Stop leflunomide and perform cholestyramine washout, confirming low plasma levels, while switching to a pregnancy-compatible DMARD","Continue leflunomide unchanged, since teratogenicity data are reassuring","Stop leflunomide and wait 2 years without any washout procedure","Switch directly to methotrexate while leflunomide washout is completed","Continue leflunomide and simply avoid conception until it is naturally cleared"],"a":0,"e":"The active metabolite persists for up to 2 years. Washout is cholestyramine 8 g three times daily for 11 days, with two plasma levels below 0.02 mg/L 14 days apart. Teriflunomide (active metabolite) has enterohepatic recirculation and can persist for up to 2 years. Washout: cholestyramine 8 g three times daily for 11 days (alternative: activated charcoal 50 g four times daily for 11 days), then confirm two plasma levels <0.02 mg/L at least 14 days apart. Switch to a compatible DMARD during or before washout. Use effective contraception until levels are confirmed. Cholestyramine reduces absorption of other drugs (including oral contraceptives), so use additional contraception during washout. The EULAR course notes that leflunomide can be continued until conception if washout is started promptly once pregnancy is confirmed; exposed pregnancies with prompt washout show no excess malformations, so the old 2-year wait without washout is no longer needed. This is a more permissive approach than many UK practices, which still prefer pre-conception washout.","c":"Pregnancy & Reproductive","t":["Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":444,"s":"A woman with lupus nephritis on mycophenolate plans pregnancy. What is the most appropriate approach?","o":["Switch to azathioprine (with or without tacrolimus), stopping mycophenolate at least 6 weeks before conception, and confirm stable disease before conceiving","Continue mycophenolate unchanged, provided disease remains quiescent","Switch to cyclophosphamide instead of azathioprine before conception","Stop mycophenolate with no replacement immunosuppressant","Switch to methotrexate instead of azathioprine before conception"],"a":0,"e":"Mycophenolate causes orofacial clefts, ear and limb anomalies. Disease should be quiescent for at least 6 months on the new regimen, and hydroxychloroquine continued. Predictors of poor outcome in SLE pregnancy (PROMISSE and others): active disease or LN in the 6 months before conception, lupus anticoagulant, hypertension, thrombocytopenia, prior LN, prednisolone >10 mg, non- White ethnicity. Advise against pregnancy with severe PAH, severe restrictive lung disease, advanced CKD, heart failure, or severe flare or stroke within 6 months. Mycophenolate embryopathy: microtia or ear canal atresia, cleft lip and palate, micrognathia, hypertelorism, limb and heart defects, and first-trimester miscarriage (up to ~45%). Pregnancy prevention programme with two forms of contraception. Switch MMF to azathioprine \u00b1 tacrolimus, stop MMF \u20216 weeks before conception, and confirm stability on the new regimen (EULAR course: ~3 months; LN inactive \u20216 months overall). Continue HCQ; start low-dose aspirin; stop ACEi or ARB at conception and use labetalol or nifedipine; baseline creatinine, uPCR, complement, anti-dsDNA, aPL and anti- Ro/La. Renal risk is highest with creatinine >1.5 mg/dL (~130 \u00b5mol/L), proteinuria >1 g/day or hypertension. About 80% of women with stable mild-to-moderate SLE in PROMISSE had no adverse outcome.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":445,"s":"A woman with RA on tofacitinib wants to conceive. When should tofacitinib be stopped?","o":["Immediately on confirmation of pregnancy, with no lead time beforehand","There is no need to stop, since human pregnancy data are reassuring","Only after delivery, since tofacitinib does not cross the placenta","At least 1 year before conception, regardless of the product information","Before conception, after the post-treatment contraception interval in the product information"],"a":4,"e":"JAK inhibitors have animal teratogenicity and insufficient human data, so the 2024 EULAR recommendations advise avoiding them in pregnancy and breastfeeding. They should be stopped before conception, observing the contraception interval in the product information. For men, data are limited and EULAR suggests considering a switch when trying to conceive. JAK inhibitors are small molecules that cross the placenta and are teratogenic in animals; human data remain insufficient. They are also avoided during breastfeeding. Stop 1\u20133 months before conception (follow the product's contraception interval; it varies by agent). Bridge to a compatible strategy before stopping: TNF inhibitor (certolizumab if continued throughout) \u00b1 sulfasalazine or HCQ. Inadvertent exposure: stop, specialist fetal assessment, registry report.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":446,"s":"A 28-year-old woman with active axial SpA needs a TNF inhibitor and wants to continue it throughout pregnancy and breastfeeding with minimal placental transfer. Which drug is most suitable?","o":["Mycophenolate mofetil","Infliximab","A TNF inhibitor","Certolizumab pegol","Monoclonal-antibody TNF inhibitor"],"a":3,"e":"Certolizumab lacks an Fc region, so active placental transfer is minimal. Other TNF inhibitors are also compatible, often with consideration of stopping later in pregnancy if disease is quiescent to reduce infant exposure. Certolizumab pegol is a PEGylated Fab' fragment with no Fc region, so it is not actively transported by the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn). The CRIB study found no or minimal cord blood levels, and CRADLE showed negligible transfer into milk. Infliximab, adalimumab and golimumab (IgG1) cross actively in the third trimester; etanercept (fusion protein with Fc) crosses less than infliximab or adalimumab. In stable disease, other TNF inhibitors are often stopped at about 20\u201332 weeks to limit infant exposure, but can be continued if needed (risk of postpartum flare if stopped). Certolizumab is preferred when active treatment is needed in late pregnancy. In axSpA: NSAIDs only before 20 weeks; sulfasalazine for peripheral disease; switch IL-17 or IL-23 inhibitors to a TNF inhibitor when planning, unless refractory. axSpA flares in the second trimester and 50\u201380% flare at 4\u201312 weeks postpartum; uveitis can flare. Lumbar ankylosis may make neuraxial anaesthesia difficult and hip disease may limit vaginal delivery, so arrange anaesthetic review in the third trimester.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":447,"s":"A woman with spondyloarthritis received adalimumab throughout pregnancy, with the last dose at 36 weeks. Her baby is now 6 weeks old. What vaccination advice is correct?","o":["Give all vaccines, live and non-live, on the routine schedule with no deferral","Defer all non-live vaccines until 12 months of age, in addition to live vaccines","Give live vaccines early, before 6 months, to maximise protection","No vaccines are needed before 12 months given passive maternal immunity","Give routine non-live vaccines as scheduled, but defer live vaccines until 6 months of age"],"a":4,"e":"Infants exposed to TNF inhibitors (other than certolizumab) in later pregnancy may have detectable drug for months. National advice on specific live vaccines, such as rotavirus, should be checked. Fc-containing monoclonal antibodies given in the third trimester can be detectable in the infant for 6\u201312 months (longest with infliximab). Inactivated vaccines should be given on schedule. Certolizumab exposure does not require a delay. Defer live vaccines for the first 6 months after thirdtrimester exposure; in practice in the UK this mainly affects BCG (deferred) and oral rotavirus vaccine (withheld or given only on specialist advice) \u2014 check the current national schedule (Green Book). After late-pregnancy rituximab exposure, check the infant's B-cell count before live vaccines. Tell the paediatrician and health visitor about the exposure. Neonatal infection rates in exposed infants are similar to unexposed infants.","c":"Spondyloarthritis & AS","t":["Spondyloarthritis & AS","Infection & Immunosuppression","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Paediatric Rheumatology","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":448,"s":"A 27-year-old woman with SLE on hydroxychloroquine 200 mg twice daily is 6 weeks pregnant. Her disease is quiescent. What is the correct advice?","o":["Stop hydroxychloroquine now and restart only if disease flares","Switch hydroxychloroquine to chloroquine for the remainder of pregnancy","Reduce the hydroxychloroquine dose by half for the duration of pregnancy","Continue hydroxychloroquine throughout pregnancy and breastfeeding","Stop hydroxychloroquine before breastfeeding, even if continued through pregnancy"],"a":3,"e":"Stopping hydroxychloroquine increases flare risk. It also reduces the risk of congenital heart block in anti-Ro-positive women. SLE pregnancy complications to anticipate: flare (second and third trimesters, postpartum), pre-eclampsia (10\u201330%) and HELLP, fetal growth restriction, preterm birth (~20\u2013 30%), miscarriage and stillbirth, maternal thrombosis, neonatal lupus. HCQ in SLE pregnancy reduces flares, pre-eclampsia, preterm birth and congenital heart block (anti-Ro-positive women). Withdrawal increases flare risk. It is compatible with breastfeeding. Continue screening for retinopathy as usual (doses \u00a35 mg/kg/day actual body weight). Continue HCQ; low-dose aspirin from before 16 weeks (ideally ~12 weeks); azathioprine, ciclosporin or tacrolimus as steroid-sparing agents; IVIG for severe thrombocytopenia or refractory disease; calcium and vitamin D (especially on steroids or heparin); monitor BP, urinalysis, platelets, complement and anti-dsDNA each trimester; uterine artery Doppler at 20\u201324 weeks; serial growth scans.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Metabolic Bone Disease","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":449,"s":"A pregnant woman with RA at 33 weeks' gestation has a flare and asks to take naproxen. What is the correct advice?","o":["Naproxen is safe throughout pregnancy at any gestation","Switch to a COX-2 selective inhibitor instead, which is better studied in pregnancy","Naproxen can be used safely provided it is stopped immediately after delivery","Avoid NSAIDs in the third trimester because of the risk of premature ductus arteriosus closure and oligohydramnios","Naproxen is contraindicated throughout pregnancy, not only in the third trimester"],"a":3,"e":"The 2024 EULAR recommendations advise using NSAIDs only intermittently in pregnancy, preferring short-acting non-selective agents such as ibuprofen, and stopping them after 28 weeks because of premature ductus closure and oligohydramnios. Prednisolone or intra-articular steroid are safer late in pregnancy. Non-selective NSAIDs are compatible with breastfeeding. Cut-offs differ: 2024 EULAR \u2014 stop after 28 weeks; EULAR course \u2014 avoid from 20 weeks and stop by 28\u201332 weeks. Selective COX-2 inhibitors have insufficient pregnancy data. NSAIDs inhibit prostaglandin synthesis: premature closure of the ductus arteriosus (third trimester), fetal renal dysfunction and oligohydramnios (from about 20 weeks; FDA 2020 warning), and possibly miscarriage if used around conception. They also impair ovulation (luteinised unruptured follicle). Late-pregnancy flare: oral prednisolone at the lowest effective dose, intra-articular steroid for monoarticular flares, paracetamol, physiotherapy, or a TNF inhibitor if sustained. Ibuprofen is compatible with breastfeeding.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":450,"s":"A 34-year-old woman with SLE and hypertension takes ramipril and wishes to conceive. What is the most appropriate advice?","o":["Continue ramipril unchanged, since it is compatible with early pregnancy","Switch to losartan or a calcium-channel blocker","Stop ramipril with no replacement antihypertensive, accepting higher blood pressure during pregnancy","Switch to a pregnancy-compatible antihypertensive such as labetalol or nifedipine before conception or","Switch to an ACE inhibitor from a different class, since class effects vary"],"a":3,"e":"as soon as pregnancy is confirmed ACE inhibitors and ARBs are fetotoxic in the second and third trimesters. Enalapril and captopril are compatible with breastfeeding. ACE inhibitors and ARBs are fetotoxic in the second and third trimesters (fetal renal failure, oligohydramnios, skull hypoplasia, growth restriction, neonatal hypotension). Firsttrimester exposure carries lower risk; stop on confirmation of pregnancy. Pregnancy-compatible antihypertensives: labetalol (first line), nifedipine (modified release), methyldopa (avoid postpartum because of depression). Postpartum: enalapril or captopril are compatible with breastfeeding and useful for proteinuria. Target BP \u00a3135/85 mmHg in pregnancy (NICE). Exception: scleroderma renal crisis \u2014 ACE inhibitors are still used in pregnancy because they save the mother's life. Risk is highest in early diffuse disease (<4 years), anti-RNA polymerase III positivity and steroids >15 mg/day, and renal crisis can mimic pre-eclampsia.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Pregnancy & Reproductive"]},{"id":451,"s":"A woman with rheumatoid arthritis controlled on etanercept has just given birth and wishes to breastfeed. Which advice is most appropriate regarding etanercept?","o":["Etanercept should be stopped before breastfeeding can begin","Etanercept is contraindicated during breastfeeding due to high milk transfer","Etanercept is generally compatible with breastfeeding","Etanercept requires a 6-week washout before breastfeeding can start","Breastfeeding should be deferred until etanercept has been stopped for 3 months"],"a":2,"e":"TNF inhibitors such as etanercept are generally considered compatible with breastfeeding because transfer into breast milk and infant gastrointestinal absorption are low. This conclusion is drug-specific and does not automatically apply to methotrexate or other antirheumatic medicines.","c":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","t":["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":452,"s":"A 29-year-old woman with SLE and previous lupus nephritis is planning pregnancy. Her disease has been quiescent for 9 months on hydroxychloroquine and azathioprine. Which additional medication should be started from 12 weeks of pregnancy?","o":["Increase the azathioprine dose rather than adding any new medication","Add a short course of oral prednisolone instead of any additional drug","Low-dose aspirin to reduce the risk of pre-eclampsia","Add therapeutic-dose LMWH rather than aspirin, regardless of APS status","No additional medication is needed, since disease has been quiescent for 9 months"],"a":2,"e":"SLE, particularly with renal disease or aPL, increases pre-eclampsia risk. Low-dose aspirin (75\u2013150 mg nightly; NICE uses 75\u2013150 mg) from 12 weeks (before 16 weeks) until delivery or about 36 weeks reduces pre-eclampsia. Indications include SLE, APS or aPL, CKD, chronic hypertension, diabetes, previous hypertensive disease of pregnancy, and moderate risk factors combined (first pregnancy, age \u202140, BMI \u202135, multiple pregnancy, family history). Add LMWH if APS (prophylactic for obstetric APS; therapeutic for thrombotic APS) or other thrombotic risk. Monitor BP, urine protein, platelets and renal function; uterine artery Doppler at 20\u201324 weeks; sFlt-1/PlGF ratio when pre-eclampsia is suspected. Continue HCQ and azathioprine. APS in pregnancy: aPL-positive without events fi aspirin (\u00b1 6 weeks' postpartum LMWH if high-risk profile); obstetric APS fi aspirin + prophylactic LMWH from a positive test, LMWH for 6 weeks postpartum; thrombotic APS fi therapeutic LMWH (switch warfarin before week 6) + aspirin, restart warfarin postpartum; refractory obstetric APS fi add HCQ, low-dose prednisolone in the first trimester, IVIG or higher-dose LMWH. Live-birth rates with standard therapy are about 70\u201380%.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety"]},{"id":453,"s":"A pregnant woman with SLE is positive for anti-Ro/SSA antibodies. Which fetal complication is specifically associated with these antibodies?","o":["Neonatal lupus, including congenital heart block","Pulmonary hypoplasia from oligohydramnios","B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma","Maternal pre-eclampsia alone, unrelated to any fetal antibody effect","Fetal growth restriction alone, without any cardiac risk"],"a":0,"e":"Anti-Ro and anti-La cross the placenta. Heart block typically develops between 16 and 26 weeks and is often irreversible; neonatal lupus rash is transient. Fetal bradycardia: CHB (anti-Ro), structural heart disease (left atrial isomerism, congenitally corrected transposition), long QT, fetal distress, maternal b-blockers, sinus node dysfunction. Anti-Ro (Ro52 and Ro60) and anti-La IgG cross the placenta from about 16 weeks. The mother may have SLE, Sj\u00f6gren's, UCTD, or no disease. Cutaneous neonatal lupus affects 15\u201325% of exposed infants (annular periorbital \"raccoon eye\" rash, cytopenias, transaminitis), clearing by 6\u20138 months. Congenital heart block: ~1\u20132% in a first pregnancy, ~15\u201318% after one affected child, ~50% after two; higher anti-Ro60 titres carry more risk. Mortality is ~15\u201320% and 60\u201370% need pacing; endocardial fibroelastosis and cardiomyopathy can also occur. 1. HCQ for every anti-Ro-positive pregnancy, started before 10 weeks (PATCH: CHB recurrence fell from ~18% to ~7.5%). 2. Fetal echocardiography (mechanical PR interval) weekly or every 2 weeks from 16 to 26 weeks; twice-daily home handheld Doppler allows detection within hours. 3. Complete block is irreversible: fluorinated steroids do not reverse it and cause fetal harm, so they are not routine. First- or second-degree block or myocarditis may be treated with dexamethasone \u00b1 IVIG in a fetal medicine centre (limited evidence). 4. Deliver where neonatal cardiology and pacing are available; check the baby's ECG, skin, FBC and LFTs.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Connective Tissue Disease","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Paediatric Rheumatology"]},{"id":454,"s":"A pregnant woman with SLE develops new hypertension and proteinuria at 30 weeks. Which feature favours a lupus flare over pre-eclampsia?","o":["New hypertension alone, regardless of complement or anti-dsDNA trend","Rising anti-dsDNA with falling complement and an active urinary sediment","Low platelets and deranged liver enzymes alone","Macrophage activation syndrome","Resolution of hypertension within 48 hours of delivery"],"a":1,"e":"The two can be hard to distinguish and may coexist. Active serology and red cell casts favour a flare, whereas new hypertension, abnormal LFTs, low platelets and stable complement favour pre-eclampsia. New hypertension and proteinuria in SLE pregnancy: LN flare, pre-eclampsia (can coexist), HELLP, APS nephropathy or catastrophic APS, TTP or atypical HUS, scleroderma renal crisis (overlap), acute fatty liver of pregnancy, pre-existing CKD revealed by pregnancy. Distinguishing features: Feature LN flare Pre-eclampsia Any time, After 20 weeks Timing including before 20 weeks Active (red cells, Urinary Bland red cell casts) sediment Falling C3/C4 Complement Normal or rising (physiological rise) Anti-dsDNA Rising Stable Uric acid Normal Raised LFTs, Raised transaminases, Usually normal (unless SLE low platelets (HELLP) platelets cytopenia) sFlt-1/PlGF Normal High ratio Rash, arthritis, Absent; headache, Other SLE visual disturbance, features serositis epigastric pain After delivery Persists Improves Joint rheumatology\u2013obstetric\u2013renal care. Renal biopsy is possible before about 28 weeks if the result would change management. LN flare: steroids (including IV pulses), azathioprine, tacrolimus. Pre-eclampsia: BP control (labetalol, nifedipine), magnesium sulfate for severe disease, and delivery timing.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Crystal Arthropathy","Connective Tissue Disease","Pregnancy & Reproductive","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]},{"id":455,"s":"A patient with SLE on mycophenolate and prednisolone asks about cervical screening. What is the most appropriate advice?","o":["Avoid cervical screening entirely while immunosuppressed","Take part in cervical screening, with consideration of more frequent screening and HPV vaccination because of increased risk","Defer screening until mycophenolate and prednisolone are stopped","Screening is only needed if symptoms such as bleeding develop","HPV vaccination is contraindicated while taking mycophenolate"],"a":1,"e":"Women with SLE, especially on immunosuppression, have more HPV infection and cervical dysplasia. Other malignancy risks in SLE: non-Hodgkin lymphoma, vulvovaginal, anal and lung cancers, and bladder cancer after cyclophosphamide; lower risk of breast, endometrial and possibly prostate cancer. Women with SLE have more persistent high-risk HPV infection, cervical dysplasia and cervical cancer than the general population; risk is higher with immunosuppression (cyclophosphamide, mycophenolate, azathioprine, glucocorticoids). Similar concerns apply to other heavily immunosuppressed patients (e.g. after transplant, or on long-term biologics). Encourage participation in the national cervical screening programme; some guidance advises more frequent screening for heavily immunosuppressed women (local policy varies \u2014 UK NHS screening for most immunosuppressed women follows the standard schedule, with annual screening for women with HIV). Offer HPV vaccination (non-live, safe on immunosuppression; EULAR 2019 vaccination recommendations support it in SLE), ideally before or early in immunosuppression. Treat CIN promptly and refer suspicious lesions.","c":"SLE & Lupus","t":["SLE & Lupus","Infection & Immunosuppression","Drug Monitoring & Safety","Investigations & Imaging"]}];
